window.__ARCHAIC_MD__="# THE ARCHAIC ERA (III century BC -- beginning of I century BC)\n\nRoman literature does not begin with a moment of spontaneous inspiration. It begins with a practical problem: Rome has conquered the Greek world, or most of it, and now has to figure out what to do with all this Greek culture. The answer, for the first two centuries of Latin literature, is essentially: translate it, adapt it, and quietly pretend it was your idea all along.\n\nThe earliest Latin authors are almost entirely occupied with importing Greek literary forms into Latin. Tragedy, comedy, epic -- all arrive in Rome via Greece, carried by writers who were often themselves Greek or bilingual. The native Italian tradition, most notably the Saturnian meter and the improvised *fescennine* verses, survives in fragments or in occasional use, but it does not set the tone. Greek models do. This is not a sign of cultural weakness: it is a deliberate act of literary construction, the creation of a literature from scratch by a civilization that had the ambition to match Greece and the pragmatic intelligence to borrow what it needed.\n\nStylistically, the Archaic Era is defined by features that classical authors would later view as rough or unpolished: archaic morphology, pre-classical spelling and vocabulary, the Saturnian meter (whose exact rules remain debated to this day), and a directness of expression that has more in common with oral poetry than with the studied elegance of the Augustan age. This is not a defect -- it is the texture of a literature still finding its feet. Reading these authors means reading Latin before it became \"Latin\" in the classical sense. The language is recognizable but foreign in ways that go beyond mere vocabulary.\n\nBy the middle of the era the picture shifts. With Plautus and Terence, Roman comedy establishes a fully Latin voice -- colloquial, energetic, theatrically sophisticated -- even while drawing on Greek originals. With Cato, Latin prose finds its first great practitioner, and for the first time a major author is not adapting Greek material but writing about Rome directly, in a Latin he consciously shapes as a political and cultural instrument. With Lucilius, Latin satire is invented -- a genre the Romans would later claim as entirely their own.\n\nPractically speaking, the surviving corpus of the Archaic Era is almost entirely fragmentary, with two major exceptions: the comedies of Plautus and Terence, and the prose of Cato the Elder. For most other authors in this era, we have at best scattered lines preserved by later grammarians who quoted them precisely because they were already archaic and strange. This means that most difficulty ratings for this era carry an asterisk, and NC (Not Comparable) is a frequent and honest outcome.\n\n---\n\n# LIVIUS ANDRONICUS (c. 284-204 BC)\n\n\n![Livius Andronicus](./images/livius_andronicus.jpg)\n*Livius Andronicus *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n## Biography\n\nLivius Andronicus is the founding father of Latin literature -- practically the Big Bang of Roman letters. Born around 284 BC in Tarentum (modern Taranto), a Greek colony in southern Italy, he was brought to Rome as a slave after the Roman conquest of the city in 272 BC. Acquired by the Livii family, he took the name Livius Andronicus and worked as a tutor. He was eventually freed, likely because of his exceptional intellectual abilities.\n\nIn 240 BC he did something remarkable: he staged the first theatrical performance in Latin at Rome, a comedy and a tragedy, both adapted from Greek originals. That date is conventionally treated as the birth of Latin literature. He founded an entire literary tradition. As a former slave.\n\nAs if that were not enough, in 207 BC -- with the Second Punic War still raging and Hannibal still in Italy -- the Senate commissioned him to compose a propitiatory hymn to Juno to appease the gods. The hymn worked well enough that the guild of writers and actors (*collegium scribarum histrionumque*) received official recognition from the Roman state, partly in his honor. Not bad for a Greek ex-slave.\n\n## Main Works\n\n- **Odusia** -- Latin translation of Homer's Odyssey in Saturnian meter. Only fragments survive, but it served as a school text in Rome for generations. Cicero recalled studying it as a boy -- not with particular enthusiasm.\n- **Tragedies** (*Achilles*, *Ajax Mastigophorus*, *Andromeda*, *Equos Troianus*, *Tereus*, among others) -- adaptations from Greek models. All lost; only scattered lines survive.\n- **Comedies** -- adaptations from Greek models. Almost entirely lost.\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nLivius Andronicus writes in archaic Latin. If you come to his text expecting the classical clarity of Caesar, you are in for a surprise. Words like *insece* (for *narra*), *topper* (for *statim*), *dacrimas* (for *lacrimas*), and *nequinont* (for *nequeunt*) are the norm, not the exception. The text is not obscure by stylistic choice -- it is archaic by historical necessity. Livius is writing before literary Latin exists as a standard. He is essentially inventing the rules as he goes.\n\nThe Odusia uses the **Saturnian meter**, the native Italic verse form. The problem is that nobody today -- and already few people in antiquity -- fully agrees on how this meter works. Horace already found it rough (*\"horridus ille / defluxit numerus Saturnius\"*). Using it as a reading aid is like navigating with a medieval map.\n\nThe main sources of difficulty are: archaic lexicon with pre-classical morphology; irregular Saturnian meter that cannot be used to guide comprehension the way classical meters can; the fragmentary nature of the corpus (surviving lines are isolated from their context); and Greek-influenced constructions, since the text is a direct translation of Homer.\n\n## Latin Excerpt, Translation and Analysis\n\n**Latin text** *(The Latin Library, thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> Virum mihi, Camena, insece versutum\n> *(Odusia I, 1 -- rendering of Odyssey 1.1)*\n\n> Igitur demum Ulixi cor frixit prae pavore.\n> *(Odusia V, 297 -- rendering of Odyssey 5.297)*\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> Tell me, O Camena, of the man of manifold cunning.\n\n> And then at last the heart of Ulysses froze with dread.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThe first line is the heart of the Odusia: Livius's rendering of Homer's immortal opening (*\"Ἄνδρα μοι ἔννεπε, Μοῦσα, πολύτροπον\"*). Every word repays attention.\n\n*Virum* translates *ἄνδρα*, though in Latin the emphatic force of Homer's opening is slightly diluted -- in Greek, \"the man\" is the absolute first word of the poem, thrown at the reader immediately. *Camena* replaces the Greek Muse: the Camenae were Italic water divinities, and the substitution is a deliberate act of cultural translation, not a failure of imagination. *Insece* is an archaic imperative form of *insequor* (narrate, follow); it was already obscure in the classical period. *Versutum* renders *πολύτροπον* (\"of many turns, of many wiles\"): a happy choice, since *versutus* carries exactly the right connotation of slippery, fox-like intelligence.\n\nThe second line corresponds to the moment when Odysseus, alone at sea, sees the whirlpool of Charybdis open before him. *Cor frixit* (\"his heart froze\") is a vivid archaic expression -- *frigesco* applied to the heart to indicate terror. The construction *prae pavore* (cause expressed with *prae* + ablative) is fully classical syntax; one of the moments where Livius anticipates rather than lags behind the norm.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: NC (Not Comparable)**\n\nThe intrinsic per-line difficulty of the Odusia would place Livius solidly in Tier A: archaic lexicon, pre-classical morphology, and a metrically opaque verse form all converge. However, the surviving corpus is far too sparse for a reliable rating. No complete work survives. The fragments are isolated lines preserved by grammarians who quoted them precisely because they were already strange and old -- roughly 80 verses in total. Applying a full difficulty rating to this material would be like rating a mountain by the color of three pebbles found at its base. NC is the honest call. If pressed, Tier B would be the scaled-down equivalent.\n\n---\n\n# GNAEUS NAEVIUS (c. 270-201 BC)\n\n\n![Gnaeus Naevius](./images/naevius.jpg)\n*Gnaeus Naevius *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n## Biography\n\nIf Livius Andronicus is the founding father of Latin literature, Naevius is its first troublemaker -- and the first Latin author we know of who was actually Roman. Born around 270 BC, probably in Campania, he fought in the First Punic War as a Roman citizen and soldier. This is not a detail: Naevius was the first Latin poet who could write about Rome's wars because he had been in them.\n\nHe worked primarily as a playwright, producing both tragedies adapted from Greek models and comedies that veered increasingly toward topical political satire. This second tendency got him into serious trouble. Naevius had a gift for the cutting remark, and he used it freely against Rome's most powerful families. His most famous provocation was a single line aimed at the Metelli: *\"Fato Metelli Romae fiunt consules\"* -- \"The Metelli become consuls at Rome by fate.\" The word *fato* carries a double edge: fate, yes, but also stupidity (from *fatuus*). The Metelli responded with equal economy: *\"Dabunt malum Metelli Naevio poetae\"* -- \"The Metelli will give the poet Naevius what he deserves.\" They were as good as their word. Naevius was imprisoned, and eventually exiled to Utica in North Africa, where he died around 201 BC.\n\nHe also invented a genre: the *fabula praetexta*, a tragedy built around Roman historical or legendary subjects rather than Greek ones. Rome writing about Rome, on a stage. That was new.\n\n## Main Works\n\n- **Bellum Poenicum** -- a historical epic on the First Punic War, written in Saturnian meter. The only Latin epic before Ennius's Annales. Survives in about 67 fragments; later divided into seven books by the grammarian Octavius Lampadio.\n- **Comedies** -- a substantial output, some titles survive (*Acontizomenos*, *Ariolus*, *Carbonaria*, among others). Almost entirely lost.\n- **Tragedies** -- including *fabula praetexta* works (*Romulus*, *Clastidium*). Lost.\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nNaevius writes in a Latin that is archaic but already slightly more confident than Livius Andronicus. His vocabulary still features pre-classical forms and constructions, but there is a directness to his expression -- a soldier's bluntness -- that is oddly readable once you adjust to the archaic surface. Where Livius can feel like pure translationese from Homer, Naevius feels like a Roman writing about Roman things in a Latin that is still figuring itself out.\n\nThe *Bellum Poenicum* uses the Saturnian meter, with all the usual difficulty that brings. The epitaph (fragment 67), however, is metrically cleaner and serves as a useful introduction to his voice. The comedies, judging by their titles and the few surviving lines, show a more colloquial register -- faster, looser, closer to spoken Latin.\n\nThe main difficulties are the same as for Livius: archaic lexicon, Saturnian meter, and fragmentary context. Naevius adds one complication of his own: his political and satirical content requires familiarity with the Roman public figures and events of the mid-third century BC, many of whom are obscure even to specialists.\n\n## Latin Excerpts, Translations and Analyses\n\n### Bellum Poenicum -- Book I, Fragment 8\n\n**Latin text** *(The Latin Library, thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> amborum uxores\n> noctu Troiad exibant capitibus opertis,\n> flentes ambae, abeuntes lacrimis cum multis.\n\n*(Bellum Poenicum, Liber I, fragment 8)*\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> The wives of both\n> went out from Troy at night with covered heads,\n> both weeping, departing with many tears.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThis fragment comes from Book I of the *Bellum Poenicum*, where Naevius connected the Punic War narrative back to the fall of Troy and the departure of Aeneas -- an innovation that shaped all subsequent Roman epic, and that Virgil would develop into the *Aeneid*. The subject of *amborum* (of both) is disputed: most scholars read it as the wives of Anchises and Aeneas, departing together from the burning city.\n\nWhat is striking is how much Naevius achieves in three short lines. The midnight departure (*noctu*), the covered heads (*capitibus opertis* -- a Roman mourning gesture any contemporary audience would recognize), and the careful doubling of *amborum* / *ambae* (both/both) across the passage create a quietly devastating effect. This is not the heroic register of departure: it is the register of grief and displacement, and it belongs to the women left to walk out while the men steer the ships. It is also the most cinematic moment in archaic Latin epic.\n\nMetrically, this is Saturnian verse. The archaic form *Troiad* (from Troy -- an old ablative construction) and the adverb *noctu* (at night) are characteristic of the register; *capitibus opertis* is an ablative absolute. The syntax is clean and paratactic: three images in sequence, no subordination. This is Naevius at his most direct, and his most human.\n\n---\n\n### Epitaphium Naevii -- Fragment 67\n\n**Latin text** *(The Latin Library, thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> immortales mortales si foret fas flere,\n> flerent divae Camenae Naevium poetam.\n> itaque postquam est Orcho traditus thesauro,\n> obliti sunt Romae loquier lingua Latina.\n\n*(Epitaphium Naevii -- fragment 67)*\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> If it were right for immortals to weep for mortals,\n> the divine Camenae would weep for the poet Naevius.\n> And so, since he was handed over to the treasury of Orcus,\n> Rome has forgotten how to speak the Latin tongue.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThis epitaph -- either written by Naevius himself (which would be entirely in character) or by admirers shortly after his death -- is one of the most striking self-assessments in all of Latin literature. The arrogance is magnificent and entirely deliberate.\n\nThe opening conditional, *\"si foret fas\"*, sets up a reversal of the cosmic order: it is not the mortals who mourn, but the immortals. The *Camenae* -- the same Roman divinities Livius used to translate Homer's Muse -- weep for a poet, elevating him to the status of a loss felt by the divine. *Orcho traditus thesauro* is the striking expression for death: Orcus (the underworld) treated as a treasure vault that has received a deposit. The word *thesauro* implies that Naevius was a treasure, not a casualty. Then the closing blow: since his death, Rome has forgotten how to speak Latin. Not just how to write it -- how to speak it.\n\nMetrically, this is Saturnian verse, but relatively regular by the standards of the meter. The four lines show characteristic features: the heavy end-weight of each line, the slightly irregular stress pattern, and the paratactic flow (conjunctions do the work, subordination is minimal). *Loquier* is the archaic infinitive form for *loqui*, a useful reminder that even in this relatively polished piece the archaic surface is always present.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: B -- Difficult**\n\nNaevius has more surviving material than Livius Andronicus -- roughly 67 fragments of the *Bellum Poenicum* plus comedy titles and a handful of other lines -- enough to attempt a rating, if cautiously. The intrinsic per-line difficulty would place him at Tier A: archaic lexicon, Saturnian meter, and content that presupposes knowledge of mid-third century Roman politics. Scaled down one step for the fragmentary corpus, Tier B is the appropriate call. The epitaph above, being metrically cleaner and self-contained, is actually closer to Tier C in isolation -- which is part of why it makes a good entry point.\n\n---\n\n# QUINTUS ENNIUS (239-169 BC)\n\n\n![Quintus Ennius](./images/ennius.jpg)\n*Quintus Ennius *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n## Biography\n\nQuintus Ennius is the most important Latin author nobody reads anymore. In antiquity he was the great poet: Lucretius called him \"our Ennius\"; Virgil quietly borrowed from him on almost every page of the Aeneid; Cicero quoted him constantly and from memory, for pleasure. Today he survives in about six hundred lines of fragmentary epic and scattered other pieces, and most students encounter him only as a footnote. This is a shame.\n\nBorn in 239 BC in Rudiae, a small town in Calabria at the crossroads of Latin, Greek, and Oscan cultures, Ennius was trilingual from birth. He famously claimed to have \"three hearts\" (*tria corda*) because he could think and speak in three languages -- which, depending on how you look at it, is either the most cosmopolitan thing a Roman ever said, or an early and very Latin form of showing off. He served as a centurion during the Second Punic War and was brought to Rome around 204 BC by none other than Cato the Elder -- which tells you something about Cato, and rather more about Ennius. Once in Rome he became a close friend of Scipio Africanus, moving in the most powerful circles of the Republic while living modestly on the Aventine hill. He died in 169 BC at seventy, reportedly from gout. For a poet of his standing, a disappointingly undramatic end.\n\nHis single greatest contribution is metrical: he replaced the Saturnian meter with the Greek dactylic hexameter for Latin epic. This was not merely a technical choice. It was a cultural declaration -- that Latin poetry could compete with Homer on Homer's own terms.\n\n## Main Works\n\n- **Annales** -- an epic in 18 books covering Roman history from the fall of Troy to Ennius's own time. The foundational monument of Archaic Latin poetry. About 600 lines survive, preserved mainly by later grammarians. [Fragmentary]\n- **Tragedies** -- adaptations from Greek, primarily Euripides (*Medea*, *Thyestes*, *Iphigenia*, among others). All lost except fragments.\n- **Saturae** -- a miscellaneous collection, the earliest Latin work to carry the name *satura*. Lost.\n- **Epicharmus** and **Euhemerus** -- philosophical works in prose and verse. Lost.\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nEnnius writes at a crossroads. He is archaic by our standards -- his Latin still has forms like *induperator* (for *imperator*), *ollis* (for *illis*), and *postque magisque* -- but for Romans of the classical period he already represented a kind of founding grandeur. The dactylic hexameter he introduced makes him considerably more accessible than Livius or Naevius for one simple reason: the meter is learnable and regular enough to assist comprehension. Once you can read hexameter, you have a framework. You do not have that with Saturnian verse.\n\nThat said, archaic vocabulary remains the core difficulty, with morphological forms that disappeared before Cicero was born. The syntax is largely paratactic -- events placed side by side rather than subordinated -- which creates a staccato energy but is not, in itself, syntactically intricate. What is complex is allusion density: the Annales is saturated with Homeric echoes, epic conventions, and references to Roman historical figures that require constant contextual knowledge.\n\n## Latin Excerpt, Translation and Analysis\n\n**Latin text** *(The Latin Library, www.thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> Unus homo nobis cunctando restituit rem.\n> Non enim rumores ponebat ante salutem.\n> Ergo postque magisque viri nunc gloria claret.\n>\n> *(Annales IX -- on Fabius Maximus Cunctator)*\n\n> Nos sumus Romani qui fuimus ante Rudini.\n>\n> *(Annales XVIII -- de se ipso)*\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> One man by his delaying restored our fortunes.\n> He did not put rumor before the safety of the state.\n> And so, more and more, that man's glory shines.\n\n> We are Romans now, we who were once men of Rudiae.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThe three-line passage on Fabius Maximus -- the dictator who famously refused open battle with Hannibal during the Second Punic War, earning the nickname *Cunctator* (the Delayer) and the contempt of the Roman public until his strategy was eventually proved correct -- is one of the most quoted fragments of Latin literature. Its power lies in compression.\n\n*Unus homo*: one single man, emphasis on the individual against the mass. *Cunctando*: the ablative gerund embeds the strategy itself into the syntax. *Restituit rem*: three words that contain the entire achievement -- *res* is not just \"the situation\" but the Roman state, Roman reality, the thing that matters. The second line wraps a moral judgment in an antithesis: *rumores* (popular noise) against *salutem* (survival). In the third line *postque magisque* -- an archaic double construction for \"more and more afterward\" -- gives historical validation its cumulative rhythm.\n\nThe single autobiographical line *de se ipso* rewards attention: \"We are Romans now, we who were once men of Rudiae.\" Ennius writes it near the end of his life, looking back at his journey from a Calabrian town where nobody had heard of Latin poetry. He has spent his career making Rome great in verse. He has also, in doing so, become Roman. The line says both things at once.\n\nMetrically, these are the first lines in this document where the dactylic hexameter can be scanned with reasonable confidence. Notice the spondaic weight in *Unus homo nobis cunctando restituit rem*: the rhythm itself delays.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: B (borderline A) -- Difficult**\n\nEnnius has more surviving material than any other author in the Archaic Era -- about 600 lines of the Annales plus dramatic fragments -- enough for a confident rating. Per-line difficulty is high: archaic vocabulary and morphology, epic diction, dense historical allusion. Without the fragmentary adjustment this would be a firm A. With it, Tier B stands as the overall rating, but the hardest passages -- the battle scenes, the dream sequences in Book I -- genuinely touch A. Think of it as high B / low A: the tier reflects the average reading experience, not the ceiling.\n\n---\n\n# TITUS MACCIUS PLAUTUS (c. 254-184 BC)\n\n\n![Titus Maccius Plautus](./images/plautus.jpg)\n*Titus Maccius Plautus *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n## Biography\n\nEven the name is a joke. \"Titus Maccius Plautus\" is almost certainly not the name this man was born with. *Maccius* is the Latinized form of Maccus, the stock clown of the Atellan farce -- the slapstick theatrical tradition from Atella in Campania, where a dim-witted buffoon named Maccus was roughly the Roman equivalent of Punchinello. *Plautus* means \"flat-footed\" or possibly \"floppy-eared,\" both animal-derived insults that double as theatrical nicknames. So what we have is: Titus the Clown, the Flat-Footed. Either this is a stage name he adopted deliberately, or his parents had an unusual approach to child-rearing. Scholars lean toward the former.\n\nWhat we know about his life comes almost entirely from a single paragraph in Aulus Gellius's *Noctes Atticae* (III.3), written more than three centuries after Plautus's death, and citing the scholar Varro as the source. According to this tradition, Plautus was born around 254 BC in Sarsina, a hill town in Umbria. He came to Rome and made money in some theatrical capacity -- acting, possibly, or carpentry, or building stage sets -- then lost everything in overseas trading ventures and ended up working in a grain mill, grinding by hand, the lowest form of labor available to a free man. There, between flour sacks, he allegedly wrote his first three plays. The story has the satisfying shape of a fable rather than biography, and may be approximately true.\n\nHe went on to become the most popular playwright in Roman history. In the second century BC, the scholar Varro sifted through the 130 plays attributed to him and identified 21 as certainly authentic -- and those 21 are precisely the ones that survive, making Plautus, alongside Terence, the best-preserved dramatist of the Roman Republic. He died around 184 BC, almost exactly when Cato the Elder was at the height of his powers -- a nice collision of Rome's two very different voices.\n\nPlautus's comedies are all *fabulae palliatae*: Greek-costumed comedies, adaptations of Greek New Comedy (primarily Menander and Diphilus). He takes Greek plots, Greek character types -- the clever slave, the braggart soldier, the young man in love, the miserly father, the pimp -- and translates them not just into Latin but into something distinctly Roman and distinctly loud. Where his Greek sources were relatively restrained, Plautus is exuberant: more puns, more physical slapstick, more wordplay, more improvisation-style asides, more songs. The result is not a translation. It is a transformation.\n\nTo understand what happens on a Plautine stage, it helps to think of the Saturnalia -- that December festival when masters served their slaves at table, social ranks were publicly reversed, and almost any joke was permissible. Plautine comedy breathes the same air. The *servus callidus* -- the clever slave who spends an entire play running circles around his masters, deceiving them for money and laughing last -- is not just a theatrical type inherited from Menander. He is a temporary demolition of the Roman social order, licensed by the stage and by laughter. The pimp gets robbed. The old man gets humiliated. The young man wins the girl he was not supposed to have. Everyone in the audience knew the slave would return to his usual position the next morning. That was the point: the reversal was delicious precisely because it was bounded and brief. Two hours of official disorder, then home to dinner.\n\nA small biographical postscript that says everything about the man: Plautus reportedly requested that *Pseudolus* be performed at his funeral. He spent his life writing jokes and then, at the end, asked for more jokes. There is a kind of dignity in that.\n\n## Main Works\n\n- **Menaechmi** -- twin brothers, mistaken identity, chaos. Shakespeare's *Comedy of Errors* is based on this. [Complete]\n- **Amphitryon** -- Jupiter impersonates a mortal husband; Mercury disguises himself as the slave Sosia. The only surviving Roman comedy on a mythological subject, and the first text to use the word *tragicomoedia*. [Complete]\n- **Miles Gloriosus** -- the braggart soldier, one of the most copied characters in Western comedy. [Complete]\n- **Pseudolus** -- Plautus's own favorite; the clever slave outsmarts a pimp, a soldier, and his own master in one afternoon. Reportedly performed at Plautus's funeral. [Complete]\n- **Aulularia** -- the miser and his pot of gold. Model for Moliere's *L'Avare*. [Complete, with lacuna at the end]\n\nTwenty-one plays survive in total.\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nHere is the trap Plautus sets for the unwary student: he looks easy. The vocabulary is everyday, the syntax is paratactic, the characters speak rapidly in short bursts. No dense philosophy, no Ciceronian periods, no archaic epic register. Plautus, apparently, is just people talking.\n\nHe is not just people talking. He is the most linguistically demanding author in the Archaic Era, and a serious contender for the hardest author in all of Latin literature.\n\nThe core difficulty is the *sermo cotidianus* -- the everyday speech register. Classical Latin as taught in schools is the formal register of the first century BC: Cicero, Caesar, Livy. Plautus wrote 150 years before that register existed. His Latin is colloquial, rapid, theatrical, and full of elements that appear nowhere else in surviving Latin simply because nobody else bothered to write down how people actually talked. When Pseudolus calls a love letter unreadable because *gallina scripsit* -- a chicken wrote it -- that is a Roman street joke, and unpacking it requires cultural fluency that no dictionary entry provides. Some Plautine jokes are no longer fully recoverable; the context that made them land has been lost.\n\nArchaic morphology adds another layer. Forms like *Sibulla* (for *Sibylla*), *pellegam* (for *perlegam*), *opsecro* (for *obsecro*) appear constantly -- the student trained on Caesar will find a strange form on almost every line. On top of this sits an enormous colloquial vocabulary: slang, diminutives, Plautine coinages, Greek loanwords used for punchlines, obscenity euphemisms. These are in the footnotes of the dictionary, not the main entries. **Lexicon: HIGH.**\n\nThe wordplay is structural, not decorative. The puns are the architecture. Missing one can break comprehension of the next three lines, and the joke density is relentless -- four different registers of humor can appear in fourteen consecutive lines. **Stylistic figures: HIGH.**\n\nMeter is more varied than it looks. The iambic senarii of spoken dialogue are learnable and actually help comprehension once mastered; but Plautus shifts to trochaic septenarii for emotional scenes and into lyric *cantica* -- cretic and bacchiac rhythms -- for musical numbers. The *cantica* are among the hardest passages in Latin verse. Syntax, paradoxically, is the one forgiving element: rapid parataxis and ellipsis, but not Tacitean compression. **Medium: MEDIUM-HIGH; syntax: MEDIUM; allusion density: LOW-MEDIUM** (the Greek originals lurk behind each play, but the dialogue itself carries little unmarked mythological allusion).\n\nThe *sermo cotidianus* constitutes its own genre burden: it demands cultural immersion that grammar study alone cannot supply. **Genre burden: HIGH.** Three criteria at HIGH -- lexicon, figures, genre -- clears the technical threshold for Tier S, which requires more than two criteria at maximum difficulty simultaneously. The reason for stopping at high A is that syntactic complexity and allusion density stay at medium: S tier should feel like total resistance on every front. Plautus is brutal on specific dimensions but offers footholds. Individual *cantica* scenes may touch S in isolation.\n\n## Latin Excerpt, Translation and Analysis\n\n**Latin text** *(The Latin Library, www.thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> PSEVDOLVS: Mos tibi geretur. sed quid hoc, quaeso?\n> CALIDORVS: Quid est?\n> PS: Vt opinor, quaerunt litterae hae sibi liberos:\n> alia aliam scandit.\n> CAL: Ludis iam ludo tuo?\n> PS: Has quidem pol credo nisi Sibulla legerit,\n> interpretari alium posse neminem.\n> CAL: Cur inclementer dicis lepidis litteris\n> lepidis tabellis lepida conscriptis manu?\n> PS: An, opsecro hercle, habent quas gallinae manus?\n> nam has quidem gallina scripsit.\n> CAL: Odiosus mihi es.\n> lege vel tabellas redde.\n> PS: Immo enim pellegam.\n> advortito animum.\n> CAL: Non adest.\n> PS: At tu cita.\n> CAL: Immo ego tacebo, tu istinc ex cera cita;\n> nam istic meus animus nunc est, non in pectore.\n> PS: Tuam amicam video, Calidore.\n> CAL: Vbi ea est, opsecro?\n> PS: Eccam in tabellis porrectam: in cera cubat.\n>\n> *(Pseudolus I.i, vv. 22-36)*\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> PSEUDOLUS: As you wish. But what in the world is this?\n> CALIDORUS: What is it?\n> PS: As I see it, these letters are looking to have children:\n> one is climbing on top of another.\n> CAL: Starting with your usual games already?\n> PS: By Pollux, I truly believe that unless the Sibyl were to read them,\n> nobody else could make any sense of them.\n> CAL: How can you speak so harshly of beautiful letters\n> written on a beautiful tablet in a beautiful hand?\n> PS: By Hercules, do chickens have hands?\n> Because a chicken wrote this.\n> CAL: You are insufferable.\n> Read it or give it back.\n> PS: No, no, I will read it all the way through.\n> Pay attention.\n> CAL: I am not here.\n> PS: Then make an effort.\n> CAL: No, I will be quiet; you call her out of the wax;\n> because my soul is there right now, not in my chest.\n> PS: I can see your girlfriend, Calidorus.\n> CAL: Where is she? Please.\n> PS: There she is, stretched out on the tablet: she is lying in the wax.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThis passage is from the opening scene of *Pseudolus* -- the first extended exchange between the clever slave Pseudolus and his lovesick young master Calidorus. Calidorus has been walking around for days clutching a wax tablet (a letter from his beloved Phoenicium, sold by a pimp to a soldier) and weeping over it. Pseudolus finally takes the tablet and attempts to read it.\n\nMetrically, these lines are iambic senarii -- the dialogue register of Roman comedy, relatively approachable by the standards of Latin verse. The meter here does not fight the reader. Everything else does.\n\nThe first joke operates on two levels simultaneously. *Quaerunt litterae hae sibi liberos: alia aliam scandit* -- \"these letters are looking to have children: one is climbing on top of another.\" The surface meaning is visual: the handwriting is so cramped and tangled that individual letters appear to be piled on top of each other. But *scandere* is the technical verb for scanning verse -- \"to climb\" a meter. Plautus is making a self-referential joke about his own written text. The letters of the love letter cannot be scanned; the letters of the play you are watching can. Whether this is deliberate or incidental is, with Plautus, the wrong question.\n\n*Sibulla* is the archaic spelling of *Sibylla*, the prophetess of Cumae. Pseudolus is comparing illegible handwriting to prophetic inscription -- the Sibyl's oracles were famously written on leaves, scattered by the wind, and notoriously difficult to reassemble. It is also a joke about the grandiosity of calling in divine interpreters for a teenage love letter.\n\nThe gallina joke -- *An, opsecro hercle, habent quas gallinae manus?* -- is structured as a rhetorical question followed by a concluding blow: *nam has quidem gallina scripsit*. The oath *opsecro hercle* (\"by Hercules, I beg you\") is pure Plautine verbal tic: *opsecro* (archaic for *obsecro*) and *hercle* appear dozens of times across the comedies as conversational filler and emphasis markers. They are the Roman equivalent of \"I mean, come on.\"\n\n*Pellegam* (for *perlegam*, \"I will read through\") and *advortito* (for *advertito*, \"pay attention\") are further archaic forms, unremarkable in Plautus but invisible in school Latin.\n\nThe emotional high point of the exchange is *nam istic meus animus nunc est, non in pectore*: \"my soul is there right now, not in my chest.\" Calidorus is saying that his whole being has followed Phoenicium into the wax tablet she wrote. It is a genuinely touching line -- a real moment of feeling in a comic scene. Pseudolus immediately punctures it: *Eccam in tabellis porrectam: in cera cubat* -- \"there she is, stretched out on the tablet: she is lying in the wax.\" *Porrectam* (from *porrigo*, \"to stretch out\") carries the additional connotation of a corpse laid out for viewing. Pseudolus is being simultaneously affectionate and brutal, which is his entire character in a single line.\n\nNote what is happening structurally: four different registers of humor -- physical (the chicken), intellectual (the Sibyl), metatextual (scanning verse), and emotional (the soul in the wax) -- appear in fourteen lines of dialogue. This density is not unusual for Plautus. It is the baseline.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: A (high) -- Very Difficult. Borderline S.**\n\nBrutal on lexicon, wordplay, and colloquial register; partially offset by clear syntax and low allusion density. The *sermo cotidianus* makes Plautus uniquely hard in a way the tier scale does not fully capture: not uniformly opaque, but requiring cultural fluency that cannot be studied into. *Cantica* scenes in isolation may touch S.\n\n---\n\n# MARCUS PORCIUS CATO -- CATO THE ELDER (234-149 BC)\n\n\n![Marcus Porcius Cato](./images/cato.jpg)\n*Marcus Porcius Cato*\n## Biography\n\nMarcus Porcius Cato is the Roman whom every Roman tried to imitate and nobody actually wanted to be. Austere, combative, obsessively frugal, deeply suspicious of anything that smelled of Greece -- he was the self-appointed conscience of the Republic, and he never stopped reminding you of this.\n\nBorn in 234 BC in Tusculum, a hill town in the hills outside Rome, Cato was a man of equestrian origins who built his career through military service and sheer force of personality rather than aristocratic connections. He fought under Fabius Maximus during the Second Punic War -- yes, the same Fabius Maximus whose delaying strategy Ennius had just immortalized. The war made him, and he never stopped thinking of himself as a soldier first.\n\nHere is the contradiction at the heart of Cato: he is the man who brought Ennius to Rome from Sardinia in 204 BC. Let that sink in. The most ferociously anti-Greek voice in Roman public life personally imported the poet who would inject Greek dactylic hexameter into Latin literature. He later learned Greek privately, in his seventies, while continuing to deliver speeches warning Romans against the corrupting influence of Greek culture. He apparently saw no contradiction in this. Winning the argument was more important than being consistent.\n\nHis censorship of 184 BC is legendary. He expelled senators on moral grounds, taxed luxury goods heavily, cut off private citizens who had illegally tapped the public aqueducts, and in general made himself the most hated and most admired man in Rome simultaneously. The two things frequently go together.\n\nHis most famous habit: in the years leading up to the Third Punic War, he reportedly ended every speech he gave in the Senate -- regardless of the topic -- with the phrase *Ceterum censeo Carthaginem esse delendam*: \"Furthermore, I move that Carthage must be destroyed.\" Whether he was discussing grain prices, road repairs, or the election of magistrates, the speech ended with Carthage. He once brought a fresh fig from Carthage into the Senate chamber to demonstrate how close and fertile the enemy city was, and therefore how dangerous. Carthage was destroyed in 146 BC. Cato died in 149 BC and did not quite live to see it, but he had spent the last two decades of his life making absolutely sure it would happen. Some people plant trees. Cato planted wars.\n\n## Main Works\n\n- **De Agri Cultura** (also called *De Re Rustica*) -- a practical handbook on running a Roman farm: how to plant vineyards, manage slaves, press olive oil, cure hams, and keep oxen healthy. The only complete prose work to survive from before Cicero -- the oldest surviving Latin prose text. [Complete]\n- **Origines** -- Roman history in 7 books, from the origins of Rome through Cato's own time. The first Roman history written in Latin; previous historians had written in Greek. [Fragmentary]\n- **Speeches** -- over 80 are known from ancient sources; substantial fragments survive. Cicero studied them as a young man. [Fragmentary]\n- **Ad Marcum filium** -- precepts addressed to his son on medicine, oratory, law, and farming. [Fragmentary]\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nCato is a gift to the Latin student who happens to know what *oleum*, *vinum*, *frumentum*, *vilicus*, and *fundus* mean. If you show up to *De Agri Cultura* with a cheat sheet of agricultural vocabulary -- and this is not a hypothetical, this is a genuine study strategy -- you have cleared the main linguistic obstacle. What remains is archaic morphology, and for that there is no cheat sheet, but there is good news: the archaisms are finite.\n\nThe style of *De Agri Cultura* is utilitarian to the point of self-parody. Cato is not trying to write beautifully. He is trying to be useful. Sentences are short, direct, and structured as commands or factual statements. He writes the same sentence pattern repeatedly without apology, because the point is to convey information, not to demonstrate range. A representative passage runs: *do this. then do this. if you fail to do this, here is what happens. do this.* The repetition is not a stylistic flaw; it is the style.\n\nArchaic forms are the one difficulty that does not yield to preparation. *Quom* for *cum*, *siet* for *sit*, *posiverunt* for *posuerunt*, *foenerari* for *faenerari* -- these surface regularly and are invisible to anyone trained purely on classical Latin. The morphology is roughly a century older than Cicero's, and the gap shows. But unlike Plautus, where the colloquial vocabulary is effectively bottomless, Cato's archaisms follow recognizable patterns. Once catalogued, they stop being surprises.\n\nBy the criteria: lexicon is MEDIUM (agricultural technical vocabulary plus archaic forms, but repetitive and finite); medium is LOW (prose, and the most unadorned prose in the corpus); syntactic complexity is LOW (short paratactic sentences, commands and declarations, almost no subordination); stylistic figures are nearly absent; allusion density is very low; genre burden is LOW (practical manual -- the lowest category on the scale). Tier C is the honest call, and there is nothing embarrassing about it. Cato was not writing for posterity. He was writing for farmers.\n\n## Latin Excerpt, Translation and Analysis\n\n**Latin text** *(The Latin Library, www.thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> Est interdum praestare mercaturis rem quaerere, nisi tam periculosum sit, et item foenerari, si tam honestum. Maiores nostri sic habuerunt et ita in legibus posiverunt: furem dupli condemnari, foeneratorem quadrupli. Quanto peiorem civem existimarint foeneratorem quam furem, hinc licet existimare. Et virum bonum quom laudabant, ita laudabant: bonum agricolam bonumque colonum; amplissime laudari existimabatur qui ita laudabatur. Mercatorem autem strenuum studiosumque rei quaerendae existimo, verum, ut supra dixi, periculosum et calamitosum. At ex agricolis et viri fortissimi et milites strenuissimi gignuntur, maximeque pius quaestus stabilissimusque consequitur minimeque invidiosus, minimeque male cogitantes sunt qui in eo studio occupati sunt.\n>\n> *(De Agri Cultura, Praefatio)*\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> Sometimes it pays better to seek profit through trade -- if only the life were not so risky -- or through lending at interest, if that were not so dishonorable. Our ancestors thought as much, and wrote it into law: a thief to be fined double, a moneylender fourfold. How much worse they judged a moneylender than a thief, you can judge from this. And when they praised a virtuous man, they praised him thus: good farmer and good tiller of the soil; whoever was praised in those terms was held to be praised in the highest possible way. I consider the merchant vigorous and keen in pursuit of profit -- but, as I said above, the life is risky and prone to ruin. From farmers come the strongest men and the most capable soldiers; the profit earned from the land is the most honorable, the most stable, the least resented; and those devoted to this pursuit are the least inclined to think ill.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThis is the only passage in *De Agri Cultura* where Cato steps back from practical instruction and makes something like a moral argument. It is worth reading carefully, because it reveals how the whole book is meant to be understood: not merely as a technical handbook, but as a statement of Roman values.\n\nThe structure is characteristically Catonian: three short declarative sentences establishing the case against commerce and moneylending; then the law cited as authority. Note *posiverunt* (archaic for *posuerunt*) and *foeneratorem* -- the archaic surface appears immediately, in the very first paragraph. Then the clinching observation is delivered as an invitation rather than a declaration: *hinc licet existimare*, \"from this one may judge.\" Cato does not spell out the conclusion. He lets the argument do its work.\n\n*Quom laudabant, ita laudabant* -- \"when they praised, they praised thus\" -- is the rhetorical high point: a doubled construction that mimics the weight of inherited habit. The superlative chain that closes the passage -- *fortissimi... strenuissimi... stabilissimusque... minimeque invidiosus... minimeque male cogitantes* -- is almost the only extended flourish in the prologue, and it is earned. Cato has been building toward it with short sentences. When the superlatives arrive, they land with cumulative force precisely because everything before them was so compressed.\n\nSyntactically, this is as accessible as archaic Latin gets. The sentences are self-contained, the logic is sequential, and the vocabulary, while including archaic forms (*quom*, *posiverunt*, *foenerari*), makes no technical demands beyond agricultural context. A student with a glossary and an afternoon of preparation can read this passage without difficulty. What they get out of it is, as with all of Cato, something they were not necessarily expecting.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: C -- Manageable.**\n\nAgricultural vocabulary is the main barrier, and it is a barrier you can build in advance. The archaic forms are the one obstacle a glossary cannot solve, but they are finite, patterned, and learnable. Syntax is paratactic and nearly free of subordination. This is the author you assign to a Latin student who has just survived Caesar and wants to feel competent again -- and who also happens to like olives.\n\n---\n\n# CAECILIUS STATIUS (c. 220-166 BC)\n\n\n![Caecilius Statius](./images/caecilius_statius.jpg)\n*Caecilius Statius *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n## Biography\n\nCaecilius Statius is the essential middle link in a chain almost everyone has heard of -- Plautus to Terence -- and almost no one has read. He is the man who was ranked the greatest Roman comic poet by one ancient critic, called mediocre by another, praised for his moral seriousness by a third, and accused of butchering Menander by a fourth. He survived as roughly 280 lines of fragments and a collection of anecdotes. In some ways this is a more honest legacy than a polished complete corpus.\n\nHe was born around 220 BC in Cisalpine Gaul, among the Insubres -- a Celtic tribe in what is now Lombardy, somewhere in the vicinity of modern Milan. He came to Rome not as a traveler but as a prisoner of war, enslaved during the Roman campaigns against the Insubrian Gauls in the early second century BC. Acquired by a member of the *gens Caecilia*, he took the family name on manumission, keeping *Statius* -- probably his original Gaulish or Oscan personal name -- as his cognomen. So Caecilius Statius was, like Livius Andronicus before him, a freed slave from a conquered people who became one of the foundational figures of Roman literature. Rome had a habit of doing this.\n\nHe was a contemporary and friend of Ennius. When Ennius died in 169 BC, Caecilius died within a year -- perhaps the same year; the sources are slightly confused on the timing. Later Romans spoke of the two men as a literary generation passing together, the torch handed on to Terence.\n\nThat handover is the subject of the most famous story about Caecilius, preserved in Suetonius and retold in Donatus's *Life of Terence*. The young Terence -- poor, shabbily dressed, completely unknown -- came to the elderly Caecilius to read him his first play, the *Andria*. Being poorly dressed, he was told to wait and given a stool near the door while Caecilius finished his dinner. But after the first few lines, Caecilius put down his fork, got up, and invited the young man to sit at the table and eat with him. The story may be apocryphal, but it is the right story: the passing of a comic tradition from one generation to the next, over a dinner the younger man was not initially invited to join.\n\nThen there is the Volcacius Sedigitus problem. In his *Carmen de Poetis* (c. 100 BC), Volcacius ranked the Roman comic poets in order of excellence:\n\n1. Caecilius\n2. Plautus\n3. Naevius\n4. Licinius\n5. Atilius\n6. Terence\n\nTerence. In sixth place. Behind Atilius, who is otherwise a footnote in literary history. Cicero thought this ranking was absurd, Horace quietly ignored it, and modern scholars have spent considerable energy explaining what Volcacius could possibly have meant. The most charitable interpretation is that Volcacius was evaluating stage entertainment for Roman audiences of his own time, in which register Caecilius's broader humor and more elaborate plotting may have been genuinely preferred over Terence's restrained Hellenism. The less charitable interpretation is that Volcacius had terrible taste.\n\nVarro offered a more useful three-way comparison: Caecilius excelled in *argumenta* (plot construction), Plautus in *eloquium* (language and wit), Terence in *ethos* (character and moral texture). This probably captures something real. Quintilian, writing much later, praised Caecilius specifically for *gravitas* -- a moral seriousness unusual in comedy -- and called him the most effective of the Roman comic writers at moving an audience's emotions.\n\nAulus Gellius, in *Noctes Atticae* II.23, sat down with Caecilius's *Plocium* and Menander's *Plocium* side by side and reported feeling depressed by the comparison. After reading Menander's original, Caecilius looked shabby to him: changing things that did not need changing, adding lowbrow jokes where Menander had subtlety, dropping the best moments of the Greek entirely. His verdict is probably unfair -- he was comparing a fragmentarily transmitted adaptation in Latin to a well-preserved original in Greek -- but it stuck, and it is the reason most people who know anything about Caecilius know him primarily as the man who failed to be Menander.\n\nHe did not fail to be Menander. He chose not to be Menander, just as Plautus had. That is a different thing entirely.\n\n## Main Works\n\nAround 42 comedies are attributed to him; none survive complete. Titles known include *Plocium* (The Necklace), *Synephebi* (The Young Men Together), *Epiclerus* (The Heiress), *Hypobolimaeus* (The Changeling), *Triumpus*, *Andria*, *Aethrio*, *Imbrii*. The *Plocium* is the best-attested work, thanks to Gellius. [All fragmentary]\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nWhat the fragments suggest: Caecilius wrote closer to Plautus in energy and register than to the restrained Terence, but with more attention to plot architecture and emotional sequence. The *gravitas* Quintilian mentions probably reflects a willingness to let scenes develop feeling rather than rushing to the next joke. The verse is iambic and trochaic, the standard registers of Roman comedy.\n\nBeyond this, reliable assessment is impossible. The NC criteria are met precisely: fragments exist in sufficient quantity to identify a voice, but not to assess difficulty consistently across multiple criteria. What can be said with confidence is that archaic morphology and comic register would have applied, and that the verse -- like all comic verse of the period -- would have required metrical competence to read well. The linguistic distance from Plautus was probably not large.\n\n## Latin Excerpt, Translation and Analysis\n\n**Latin text** *(Caecilius Statius, Plocium, fr. preserved in Aulus Gellius, Noctes Atticae II.23, www.thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> is demum miser est, qui aerumnam suam nescit occultare\n> ferre: ita me uxor forma et factis facit, si taceam, tamen indicium.\n> Quae nisi dotem, omnia, quae nolis, habet: qui sapiet, de me discet,\n> qui quasi ad hostes captus liber servio salva urbe atque arce.\n> Quae mihi, quidquid placet, eo privatu vim me servatum.\n> Dum ego eius mortem inhio, egomet vivo mortuus inter vivos.\n> Ea me clam se cum mea ancilla ait consuetum, id me arguit,\n> ita plorando, orando, instando atque obiurgando me obtudit,\n> eam uti venderem; nunc credo inter suas\n> aequalis et cognatas sermonem serit:\n> \"quis vestrarum fuit integra aetatula,\n> quae hoc idem a viro\n> impetrarit suo, quod ego anus modo\n> effeci, paelice ut meum privarem virum?\"\n> haec erunt concilia hodie, differor sermone miser.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> Truly wretched is the man who cannot keep his misery hidden:\n> my wife, by her looks and behavior, denounces me even when I say nothing.\n> Apart from her dowry, she has everything you would not want: let the wise man learn from my example --\n> I, a free man, serving like a prisoner of war while the city and citadel stand safe.\n> Whatever pleases me, she deprives me of by force, for my own good.\n> While I eagerly wait for her death, I myself live as a dead man among the living.\n> She accused me of carrying on in secret with my slave girl,\n> and wore me down with weeping, begging, nagging, and scolding until I sold her.\n> Now, I imagine, she is spreading word among her friends\n> and relations of the same age: \"which of you, in the prime of your youth,\n> ever managed to get from her husband\n> what I, old woman that I am, just managed to get --\n> depriving my husband of his mistress?\"\n> That will be the talk today, and I, poor wretch, am being torn apart by gossip.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThis passage is the husband's monologue from Caecilius's *Plocium*, quoted in full by Gellius in *Noctes Atticae* II.23, immediately alongside the corresponding section of Menander's Greek original. Gellius prefers Menander. A reader encountering Caecilius alone, without the comparison, may find themselves less disappointed than Gellius.\n\nThe opening line -- *is demum miser est, qui aerumnam suam nescit occultare* -- circulated in antiquity as a standalone maxim and was quoted independently for its compression and accuracy. \"He alone is truly wretched who cannot conceal his wretchedness\" is both a moral observation and a piece of practical advice for surviving a bad marriage.\n\nThe imagery escalates into comic hyperbole that brushes against something real. *Quasi ad hostes captus liber servio salva urbe atque arce* -- \"I serve like a prisoner of war while the city and citadel stand safe\" -- describes the domestic condition of a Roman man married to a wealthy woman whose dowry gives her leverage he cannot match. Caecilius makes this explicit: *Quae nisi dotem, omnia, quae nolis, habet* -- \"apart from the dowry, she has everything you would not want.\" The *dos* is the trap.\n\n*Dum ego eius mortem inhio, egomet vivo mortuus inter vivos* -- \"while I eagerly await her death, I myself live as a dead man among the living\" -- is probably the best single line in surviving Caecilius. The reversal is clean: the man waiting for his wife to die has, in the waiting, already died first.\n\nNote the fifth line -- *Quae mihi, quidquid placet, eo privatu vim me servatum* -- which is metrically and syntactically troubled. Editors have proposed emendations; the transmitted text is likely corrupt. This is not unusual for fragmentary authors transmitted through quotation, and it is a reminder of the conditions under which we read Caecilius: through a narrow window of other people's books, on a text that has accumulated damage over centuries of copying.\n\nThe metrical form throughout is the trochaic septenarius -- the seven-foot trochaic line Plautus and Caecilius both used for heightened emotional scenes. The rhythm is rapid and insistent, appropriate to the husband's frantic internal inventory of his domestic situation.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: NC -- Not Comparable.**\n\nAbout 280 lines survive, almost entirely through quotation in other authors. The voice is identifiable and the dramatic approach partially reconstructable, but no complete work exists and the fragments are too sparse to assess difficulty reliably across the full set of criteria. Per-line difficulty would likely place him close to Plautus -- archaic, comic, metrically varied -- but a tier rating built on fifteen-line excerpts in other people's books would be a guess, not an assessment.\n\n---\n\n# PUBLIUS TERENTIUS AFER -- TERENCE (c. 185-159 BC)\n\n\n![Publius Terentius Afer -- Terence](./images/terence.jpg)\n*Publius Terentius Afer -- Terence*\n## Biography\n\nTerence is the only author in this entire document whose complete surviving works you can actually read. Six comedies. All of them. Not \"six out of an estimated forty.\" Not \"six with gaps and lacunae.\" Six complete plays, in order, start to finish, from the first line of the prologue to the closing *plaudite*. In the archaic era, that is a minor miracle, and it matters more than it might seem: it is the only author of the period for whom we can talk about style, development, and technique across a whole body of work rather than guessing from rubble. (Plautus comes close -- twenty-one complete comedies survive, all of them authentic by Varro's reckoning, though scattered fragments from non-Varronian attributions remain incomplete.)\n\nHe was born around 185 BC in Carthage -- hence *Afer*, \"the African\" -- and came to Rome as a slave in the household of the senator Terentius Lucanus. The senator recognized his intelligence, had him educated alongside his own children, and eventually freed him. Terence took his patron's name and kept his cognomen. He was young, he was educated, he was well-connected, and he was probably very good company. Within a few years, he had written six comedies. By around 160 BC, he had apparently left for Greece -- possibly to collect more Menander to translate, possibly just to travel. He never came back. He died sometime around 159 BC, somewhere in Greece or at sea; the ancient sources offer several theories (shipwreck, illness, grief at losing his manuscripts in transit) without agreeing on any of them. He was in his mid-twenties. The six plays were already written. Whatever he was planning to do next, we will never know.\n\nThe accusations against him come in two flavors, both addressed in his prologues. The first was *contaminatio* -- combining multiple Greek originals into a single Latin play. His main critic, the playwright Luscius Lanuvinus, called it literary dishonesty; Terence responded that Naevius, Plautus, and Ennius had done the same and considered them excellent precedents. He did not deny the practice; he denied it was wrong. The second accusation was more personal: that Terence did not write his own plays at all -- that Gaius Laelius and Scipio Aemilianus, the inner circle of the \"Scipionic circle,\" were the real authors. His response in the *Adelphoe* prologue is a masterpiece of non-denial: he considers the association with such admired men no disgrace, and leaves the question open. Modern consensus is that Terence wrote his own plays but genuinely collaborated intellectually with the circle -- which would explain both the accusation and his studied refusal to flatly deny it.\n\n## Main Works\n\nAll six comedies survive complete:\n\n- **Andria** (166 BC) -- The Woman from Andros. His first play; the one he read to Caecilius. A young man in love with an unsuitable girl, a scheming slave, a father who eventually comes around.\n- **Hecyra** (165 and 160 BC) -- The Mother-in-Law. Notoriously flopped twice in a row at its first performances -- the audience left to watch a tightrope walker, and then a gladiatorial show -- and succeeded only on its third staging. A comedy about a marriage that almost didn't happen for reasons nobody will explain.\n- **Heautontimorumenos** (163 BC) -- The Self-Tormentor. A father who punishes himself for driving his son away. Contains *homo sum*.\n- **Eunuchus** (161 BC) -- The Eunuch. His biggest commercial success; reportedly earned more money on its first day than any previous Latin comedy.\n- **Phormio** (161 BC) -- the scheming parasite Phormio manipulates two fathers into accepting their sons' unsuitable attachments.\n- **Adelphoe** (160 BC) -- The Brothers. Two contrasting fathers, one strict and one permissive, and what their sons turn out to be. Performed at the funeral games of Aemilius Paullus, almost certainly at the direct request of the Scipionic circle.\n\n## Style, Difficulty, and the Concept of *Humanitas*\n\nThe most important thing about Terence, stylistically and philosophically, is a word he did not invent but almost single-handedly defined for Latin literature: *humanitas*.\n\nThe word resists clean translation. It means something like: the quality of being fully and recognizably human -- which in the Roman upper-class context of the 160s BC meant educated, refined, emotionally intelligent, capable of empathy, and freed from the crude reflexes of the merely powerful. It is Cicero's intellectual ideal, Seneca's moral aspiration, and Augustine's theological target. And it is already there, fully formed, in a line of Terence's comedy written in 163 BC.\n\n*homo sum: humani nil a me alienum puto.* \"I am human; I consider nothing human foreign to me.\"\n\nChremes says this to Menedemus to justify why he is sticking his nose into a neighbor's business. It is, on the surface, a joke about being a busybody. But Terence means it structurally. The entire moral architecture of his comedy rests on this principle: that other people's pain is not someone else's problem, that understanding what another person is going through is not sentimental but rational, and that the rigid application of rules to human situations -- by fathers, by law, by social convention -- produces worse outcomes than careful attention to the actual people involved.\n\nThis is what separates Terence from Plautus at the deepest level, and it is worth being precise about the differences.\n\nPlautus writes comedy of situation: the collision of types, each maximally themselves -- the miser as miserly as possible, the braggart soldier as vain as possible, the clever slave as relentlessly clever as possible. The energy is carnivalesque, the vocabulary colloquial and archaically unruly, the cantica (elaborate sung passages in cretic and bacchiac meters) pure spectacle. Everything is built for immediate theatrical impact.\n\nTerence writes comedy of character. A Terentian father is not simply wrong to be strict; he has reasons, and part of the comedy is that everyone, including the strict father, eventually has to acknowledge everyone else's perspective. His Latin is deliberately pure -- Caesar praised his *puri sermonis amor* -- with no deep colloquial register and almost no cantica. His plays are essentially sustained dialogue, written for an educated upper-class audience, which made him commercially less successful than Plautus and considerably more durably influential.\n\nFrom a difficulty standpoint, Terence is more controlled than any other archaic author -- but \"controlled\" is not the same as \"easy.\" The Latin is still from 160 BC. Contracted and archaic forms (*usu'*, *opu'*, *quoiquam* for *cuiquam*, *nil* for *nihil*) appear constantly, and the dialogue is peppered with colloquial interjections -- *eheu!*, *hui!*, *ah!*, *hem!* -- that classical Latin training leaves a reader entirely unprepared for. Syntax is transparent and allusion density is low; the cultural challenge of Roman institutions (patria potestas, the legal status of a freedman, dowry leverage) is real but resolvable with preparation. The archaic sediment, however, is consistent across six complete plays, and it earns him a step above the \"read after Caesar\" comfort zone.\n\n## Latin Excerpt, Translation and Analysis\n\n**Latin text** *(Terence, Heautontimorumenos, I.i, vv. 75-87, www.thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> ME. Chreme, tantumne ab re tuast oti tibi\n> aliena ut cures ea quae nil ad te attinent?\n> CH. homo sum: humani nil a me alienum puto.\n> vel me monere hoc vel percontari puta:\n> rectumst ego ut faciam; non est te ut deterream.\n> ME. mihi sic est usu'; tibi ut opu' factost face.\n> CH. an quoiquamst usus homini se ut cruciet? ME. mihi.\n> CH. si quid laborist nollem. sed quid istuc malist?\n> quaeso, quid de te tantum meruisti? ME. eheu!\n> CH. ne lacruma atque istuc, quidquid est, fac me ut sciam:\n> ne retice, ne verere, crede inquam mihi:\n> aut consolando aut consilio aut re iuvero.\n> ME. scire hoc vis? CH. hac quidem causa qua dixi tibi.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> ME. Chremes, have you really got so much free time that you concern yourself with other people's affairs, things that have nothing to do with you?\n> CH. I am human: I consider nothing human foreign to me.\n> Take it as advice or as simple curiosity on my part:\n> it is right for me to do this; I am not trying to put you off.\n> ME. My ways are my own; you do as you see fit.\n> CH. Is there anyone for whom tormenting himself is a good idea? ME. For me.\n> CH. I am sorry if there is something painful. But what is this trouble?\n> Tell me, what have you done to deserve this from yourself? ME. Oh.\n> CH. Do not weep -- whatever it is, let me know:\n> do not stay silent, do not be afraid, trust me:\n> I will help you with comfort, with counsel, or with something concrete.\n> ME. Do you really want to know? CH. For the reason I already told you.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThe scene opens with Menedemus -- a neighbor Chremes barely knows -- performing hard manual labor in his fields despite being a wealthy old man of sixty. Chremes cannot leave it alone. His justification, when Menedemus asks why he is meddling: *homo sum: humani nil a me alienum puto.*\n\nThe line is iambic senarius -- the workhorse meter of Terentian dialogue, which mimics the natural stress patterns of spoken Latin well enough that Cicero cited Terence as evidence that good prose and good iambic verse share the same rhythmic instincts. The syntax is clean and direct. There is no difficult vocabulary, no hyperbaton, no ellipsis. Even the compressed *nil* for *nihil* is standard colloquial Latin, not an archaic form. This is, by the standards of the archaic period, exceptionally readable Latin.\n\nThe content, however, is doing philosophical work. *Humani nil a me alienum puto* is not just an expression of nosiness. It is a claim about the scope of moral attention: everything a human being experiences is, by virtue of that shared humanity, also something I am capable of understanding and therefore something I have some obligation to engage with. The Stoic underpinning -- that all humans share a common rational nature that makes them mutually intelligible and mutually responsible -- was already circulating in the Roman Hellenism of the Scipionic circle. Terence puts it in the mouth of a comedy character justifying why he is pestering a man he met last week.\n\nNote that Menedemus's answer is not a refutation. He simply says *mihi sic est usu'* -- \"those are my ways.\" He is not arguing that Chremes is wrong to care; he is asserting the right to privacy. The comedy that follows -- his story of driving his son away and punishing himself for it by working like a slave -- proves Chremes right to ask. The *humanitas* principle wins.\n\nThe scene also models Terentian dialogue: two voices, each grammatically complete, trading control of the conversation in short exchanges. This is not the voluble verbal flooding of Plautus. It is measured, almost Socratic. The effect is that the characters feel like people thinking in real time rather than performers delivering prepared material.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: B -- Difficult.**\n\nTerence is the most controlled Latin writer of the archaic era, but control is not the same as accessibility. The archaic morphology is consistent -- contracted forms, pre-classical spellings, colloquial interjections scattered throughout the dialogue -- and it is the kind of difficulty that compounds over a long read: one *eheu!* or *usu'* is a footnote; six complete plays of them is a genuine lexical and phonological adjustment. Syntax is transparent and allusion density is low, but the cultural context (patria potestas, dowry law, the social ladder from slave to freedman) needs to be in place before the comedies make full sense. Placed above Naevius for the sustained archaic register across a complete and extensive corpus; well below Plautus, whose colloquial depth and cantica represent a separate category of difficulty entirely.\n\n---\n\n# MARCUS PACUVIUS AND LUCIUS ACCIUS\n\n### Marcus Pacuvius (c. 220-130 BC) and Lucius Accius (170-86 BC)\n\nRoman tragedy had two giants, and we have almost nothing by either of them. This is one of antiquity's more irritating losses: the Greeks gave us Aeschylus, Sophocles, and Euripides in large and mostly readable quantities; Rome gave us Pacuvius and Accius and then, over several centuries of copying and neglect, quietly lost the plays. What survives are fragments, critical assessments, and one anecdote that neatly captures the relationship between the two men and, through them, the whole tradition they represented.\n\n## Biographies\n\n\n![Marcus Pacuvius](./images/pacuvius.jpg)\n*Marcus Pacuvius *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n**Marcus Pacuvius** was born around 220 BC in Brundisium -- modern Brindisi, at the heel of Italy. He was the nephew of Ennius: their connection was through Ennius's sister, making Pacuvius the closest thing we have to a family lineage in early Latin literature. He came to Rome sometime in the second century BC, where he worked first as a painter -- Pliny the Elder mentions a picture he made in the Temple of Hercules in the Forum Boarium, the cattle market, which was still visible in Pliny's time, four centuries later -- before establishing himself as the greatest tragic playwright of his generation. He was admitted to the circles of the Roman aristocratic elite: a friend of Gaius Laelius, the same Laelius connected to the Scipionic circle and to Terence.\n\nHe wrote thirteen tragedies known by name, all adapted from Greek models (*fabulae cothurnatae* -- \"high-boot plays,\" after the footwear of Greek tragic actors), plus at least one *fabula praetexta*, the Paulus, a Roman historical tragedy about Aemilius Paullus and the Battle of Pydna. He lived to approximately ninety-one years old, which, for the ancient world, is practically supernatural. In his extreme old age he retired to Tarentum in southern Italy, where he spent his last years. He was still writing, or at least receiving visitors, when a much younger Accius came through.\n\n\n![Lucius Accius](./images/accius.jpg)\n*Lucius Accius *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n**Lucius Accius** was born in 170 BC in Pisaurum -- modern Pesaro, on the Adriatic coast. The date is unusually precise for an ancient author: Accius himself apparently mentioned being born during the ludi Romani of 170 BC. His father was a freedman -- a former slave -- which made Accius the son of someone who had himself been property. He used this detail, or at least did not hide from it: he was one of the most combative and self-assured literary figures of the Roman republic.\n\nHe was, by any measure, extraordinarily prolific. More than forty-five tragedies are attributed to him, plus historical plays, grammatical treatises, and a major work on Roman theatrical history and chronology called the *Didascalica*. He was a member of the *collegium poetarum*, the official guild of Roman poets and playwrights. The collegium had a hall where members met, and there is a famous story about what happened when Julius Caesar's father -- the praetor, not the dictator -- entered that hall. Every other member stood. Accius did not. When asked why, he explained that he had not stood when he entered the room, and therefore saw no reason to stand for others entering it. The social calculus here is extraordinary: a man whose father had been a slave, refusing to rise for a Roman magistrate of consular family, in a room full of poets, on a point of artistic dignity. Nobody, apparently, threw him out.\n\nHe outlived almost everyone. He was still alive, elderly and nearly deaf, when Cicero was a young man in the 80s BC -- Cicero says he knew Accius personally and attended readings of his plays. This means Accius's career stretched across a century of Roman history, from Ennius's death to Sulla's dictatorship.\n\nThe anecdote that best captures both men at once is preserved by Aulus Gellius. When Accius was on his way to Asia, he stopped to visit the very elderly Pacuvius in Tarentum. Pacuvius asked him to read from his new tragedy, the *Atreus*. Accius read it. Pacuvius praised the language as powerful and elevated but found it perhaps too harsh, too rough -- *truculentiora*, too savage. Accius responded that he intended to keep writing that way and expected to improve with age. \"You are right,\" said Pacuvius; \"for I notice that in my case the fruit has ripened, but in yours it is still forming.\" (*mea, inquit, matura, tua, ut video, viridisunt*.) It is, in miniature, the whole story of literary generations: the older writer who has arrived at mastery warning the younger one that ambition alone is not mellowness, while the younger one cheerfully ignores the advice.\n\n## Main Works\n\n**Pacuvius:** Thirteen tragedies known by name including *Niptra* (Ulysses Wounded), *Teucer*, *Antiopa*, *Hermiona*, *Dulorestes*, *Chryses*, *Iliona*; one *fabula praetexta* (*Paulus*). [All fragmentary]\n\n**Accius:** Over 45 tragedies including *Atreus*, *Medea*, *Antigona*, *Clytaemestra*, *Philocteta*, *Brutus* (*praetexta*), *Decius* (*praetexta*); also *Didascalica* (literary history) and grammatical works. [All dramatic works fragmentary; *Didascalica* survives only in citations]\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nQuintilian's paired assessment in *Institutio Oratoria* X.1 is the most useful starting point: Pacuvius, he says, is the most learned (*doctissimus*) of the Roman tragic poets, with genuine weight and solemnity in his verse -- but if he were only clearer and more concise, he would be comparable to Sophocles. Accius he rates as more powerful in sheer force, with a grandeur and vigor that some readers prefer to any other tragic poet -- but rough and uneven in execution. The shorthand that stuck in antiquity was: Pacuvius for *gravitas* and *doctrina*, Accius for *vis* and *ardor*.\n\nBoth wrote what Romans called *fabulae cothurnatae* -- adaptations of Greek tragic plots, often from Euripides or Sophocles, given Roman Latin costumes. The literary challenge was translating not just the stories but the tone: Greek tragedy's elevated diction, its choral passages, its metaphysical weight, into a language and culture that had no indigenous tragic tradition before the 240s BC. Both managed it at a high level, if Quintilian is to be believed.\n\nNeither is assessable for difficulty in any reliable way. The fragments are sufficient to confirm that both wrote elevated, archaic dramatic verse -- dense with allusion, grammatically demanding, metrically complex (the iambic senarius of comedy gives way to longer and more elaborate meters in tragedy) -- but the surviving material is too sparse and too decontextualized (single lines torn out of plays we cannot read) to rate them consistently across multiple criteria.\n\n## Latin Excerpts, Translations and Analyses\n\n### Pacuvius -- *Niptra* (Ulysses Wounded)\n\n**Latin text** *(Pacuvius, Niptra, fr. preserved in Cicero, Tusculanae Disputationes II.50, www.thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> Conqueri fortunam adversam, non lamentari decet.\n> Id viri est officium, fletus muliebri ingenio additus.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> To complain of adverse fortune is fitting; to lament it is not.\n> That is a man's duty; weeping belongs to a woman's nature.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThese two lines close the *Niptra* -- \"The Washing\" or \"The Foot-Washing,\" the name referring to the scene in which Ulysses, wounded by a spear thrown by his own son Telegonus (who did not know his father's identity), is being carried by attendants. The play adapted a story from the lost Greek epic tradition. Cicero quotes the passage in *Tusculanae Disputationes* II to illustrate the proper philosophical response to pain: not stoic silence, not theatrical weeping, but the controlled articulation of complaint without collapse into grief.\n\nThe lines are in iambic senarii. The syntax is clean -- a parallel construction (*conqueri... non lamentari*) resolved by two defining statements. The language is unambiguous, almost aphoristic. This is precisely why Cicero found them quotable: they state a Roman moral position in Roman dramatic verse with complete economy.\n\nThe gendered framing -- weeping as feminine, controlled complaint as masculine -- is entirely conventional for the period and for Roman tragedy generally. What is Pacuvian is the precision: *conqueri* (to complain, to voice grievance) is explicitly permitted; *lamentari* (to lament, to give way to grief) is not. The distinction is fine enough to be philosophically interesting rather than merely macho.\n\n---\n\n### Accius -- *Atreus*\n\n**Latin text** *(Accius, Atreus, fr. preserved in Cicero, De Officiis I.97, www.thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> oderint, dum metuant.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> Let them hate, so long as they fear.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThree words. One of the most consequential three-word phrases in Latin literature.\n\nCicero quotes it in *De Officiis* I.97 while discussing theatrical decorum: it would be outrageous, he says, to hear a just ruler like Aeacus or Minos say this. But spoken by Atreus -- the murderer of his brother's children, who cooked them and served them at a feast -- it is exactly right. The line fits the character, which is what Accius intended. Tragedy is not obliged to put good thoughts in the mouths of evil people.\n\nThe line became famous long before Caligula. It circulated as the definition of tyrannical government throughout the late Republic. Cicero treats it as self-evidently wrong: a ruler who governs through fear rather than love has already failed as a political figure and as a human being. The Stoic argument is that *metuant* produces compliance, not loyalty, and that compliance based on fear collapses the moment the fear source is removed. *Oderint, dum metuant* is not a governing philosophy; it is a confession that you have no legitimacy.\n\nThen, approximately two centuries after Accius wrote it, Caligula made it his favorite saying. Suetonius records this in *Caligula* 30. The joke that history kept playing -- that the most explicit theatrical statement of what a tyrant sounds like would be adopted enthusiastically by an actual Roman emperor -- would probably not have surprised Accius. He had, after all, refused to stand up for a magistrate. He understood exactly what kind of men ended up in positions of power.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: NC -- Not Comparable (both).**\n\nThe fragments of Pacuvius and Accius together amount to a few hundred lines, almost entirely isolated from their dramatic contexts. The per-line difficulty is assessable -- archaic elevated tragic verse, metrically varied, allusion-dense, with Pacuvian obscurity compounding the challenge -- but there is no basis for a reliable cross-criteria rating without extended connected texts. Both are NC. The anecdotes are better preserved than the plays.\n\n---\n\n# GAIUS LUCILIUS (c. 180-102 BC)\n\n\n![Gaius Lucilius](./images/lucilius.jpg)\n*Gaius Lucilius *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n## Biography\n\nGaius Lucilius was born around 180 BC in Suessa Aurunca, a small town in Campania, and died around 102 BC in Naples. He was of equestrian rank -- wealthy, well-connected, and emphatically not obliged to worry about what anyone thought of him. But even among equestrians, Lucilius had a particular kind of insurance: he was the personal friend, and for a time the companion-in-arms, of Publius Cornelius Scipio Aemilianus.\n\nScipio Aemilianus is the man who destroyed Carthage in 146 BC. He is also the man who destroyed Numantia in 133 BC. In the 130s BC he was the single most powerful figure in Rome, commanding a circle that included the historian Polybius, the philosopher Panaetius, the statesman Laelius, and in a more junior capacity a young writer from Campania with opinions about everyone. Lucilius served with Scipio at Numantia; Velleius Paterculus mentions him in this context, and the experience apparently confirmed his view that with the right connections you could say essentially whatever you wanted about whoever you liked.\n\nThis view turned out to be correct. Lucilius wrote thirty books of *Saturae*, in which he attacked sitting senators, consuls, generals, and public figures by name, described their failings in specific and often unflattering terms, and generally treated Rome's political class as raw material for entertainment. Nobody took him to court. Nobody had him exiled like Naevius. The relationship with Scipio was presumably part of the reason; the equestrian rank provided further insulation; and it is also possible that the satires were simply so entertaining that their targets preferred not to draw additional attention to themselves by complaining. In any case, the genre of Roman personal satire -- the one Quintilian would later call *satura tota nostra est*, entirely ours and not borrowed from the Greeks -- was built on Lucilius's combination of genuine literary talent and exceptionally convenient social position.\n\nAfter Scipio's death in 129 BC (sudden, unexplained, and widely suspected to be political murder), Lucilius continued writing for another twenty-five years. He died in Naples around 102 BC at a considerable age, and Jerome records that he received a public funeral. The city had apparently made peace with the idea of a man who spent his entire career being rude about everyone in verse.\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n**Saturae** (30 books; fragmentary, approximately 1,300 lines surviving across hundreds of isolated fragments)\n\nThe thirty books of *Saturae* are the founding document of Roman satirical literature. Subject matter ranges widely: philosophy (Lucilius had Stoic sympathies, probably absorbed from the Scipionic circle), literary criticism, gastronomy, travel, erotic content, and above all direct personal attacks on named contemporaries. Lucilius named names, described specific actions, and drew character portraits of real people in ways that would have been legally and physically risky for anyone without his connections.\n\nThe metrical organization of the books is counterintuitive: books 26-30 (composed first, despite the numbering) use dactylic hexameter; the later books use trochaic septenarii, iambic senarii, and other meters. Horace would eventually establish hexameter as the default meter for Latin satire, following Lucilius's early precedent and setting aside his later experimentalism.\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nLucilius is the inventor of a genre, which means reading him involves meeting Latin at an early, rough, pre-canonical moment in that genre's history.\n\nThe lexical difficulty is real. His Latin is archaic and colloquial simultaneously -- a specific combination quite different from Cato's archaic prose (formal, compressed) or Plautus's colloquial Latin (theatrical, exuberant). Lucilius sounds like an educated Roman talking to equals at dinner in the second century BC, while inserting Greek words whenever the Latin equivalent seems insufficient or less precise. He did this constantly. Horace found it irritating, criticizing the habit explicitly in *Satires* I.10: mixing Greek and Latin, he says, is no great achievement, and a serious Roman author should commit to one language. Lucilius, composing about a century earlier, evidently found the code-switching perfectly natural. For the modern reader, the result is that working through his fragments requires at minimum a basic familiarity with Greek vocabulary on top of archaic Latin morphology and pre-classical spelling conventions.\n\nThe deeper difficulty, however, is topicality. Lucilius's satires attacked real people, by name, for specific and contemporary reasons. The humor depended on the audience knowing who these people were and what exactly they had done. We do not. Almost none of the named targets can be securely identified, and even where identification is possible, the specific offense motivating the attack is usually lost. A line that was devastatingly accurate about a particular senator in 130 BC now requires three paragraphs of scholarly apparatus to explain why it might once have been funny.\n\nThen there is Horace's verdict, which has shaped every subsequent reading of Lucilius and is worth engaging with directly. In *Satires* I.4, Horace traces Lucilius's descent from Greek Old Comedy (Eupolis, Cratinus, Aristophanes) and then delivers his characteristically double assessment: Lucilius was *emunctae naris* -- keen-nosed, quick to smell out a target -- but also *durus conponere versus*, rough and hard to write well. And: he dictated two hundred verses per hour *stans pede in uno*, standing on one foot. The *stans pede in uno* was probably proverbial for doing something effortlessly, without pausing to think. Two hundred verses an hour, on one foot, *ut magnum* -- as if that were some great achievement. The result: *cum flueret lutulentus, erat quod tollere velles* -- when he flowed muddy, there was always something you would want to cut.\n\nHorace modeled his entire satirical career on Lucilius. He also could not stop pointing out everything he would have done differently. This is what literary admiration looks like when practiced by someone with very firm opinions about craft.\n\n## Latin Excerpt and Analysis\n\n**Latin text** *(Lucilius, Saturae, frr. 1342-1354 Krenkel, preserved in Lactantius, Divinae Institutiones VI.5)*\n\n> Virtus, Albine, est pretium persolvere verum\n> quis in versamur, quis vivimus rebus, potesse,\n> virtus est homini scire id quod quaeque habeat res,\n> virtus scire homini rectum, utile quid sit, honestum,\n> quae bona, quae mala item quid inutile, turpe, inhonestum,\n> virtus quaerendae finem re scire modumque,\n> virtus divitiis pretium persolvere posse,\n> virtus id dare quod re ipsa debetur honori,\n> hostem esse atque inimicum hominum morumque malorum\n> contra defensorem hominum morumque bonorum,\n> hos magni facere, his bene velle, his vivere amicum,\n> commoda praeterea patriai prima putare,\n> deinde parentum, tertia iam postremaque nostra.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> Virtue, Albinus, is being able to pay the fair price in the things we deal in, the things we live by; virtue is knowing for a man what each thing contains within itself; virtue is knowing what is right, useful, and honourable; what things are good and what are equally bad, useless, base, dishonourable; virtue is knowing the limit and measure of what is sought; virtue is being able to give wealth its proper value; virtue is giving what is in truth owed to honour; being enemy and adversary to bad men and bad customs, and on the contrary defender of good men and good customs; holding these in high regard, wishing them well, living as their friend; and moreover putting the advantages of the fatherland first, then those of our parents, and our own interests third and last.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThis is the virtus fragment -- the most philosophically substantial passage to survive from Lucilius's thirty books, and one of the most discussed texts in all of archaic Latin. It is preserved by Lactantius in his *Divinae Institutiones* (VI.5), who quotes it as a model pagan definition of virtue before arguing that it falls short of the Christian one. That Lactantius kept it is the reason we have it at all. Neither The Latin Library nor Splash Latino carries a dedicated Lucilius page; the text here follows the standard scholarly edition of Krenkel (1970), which reconstructs the fragments from their ancient sources.\n\nThe fragment opens with a direct address to \"Albine\" -- almost certainly a friend or associate of Lucilius, possibly a member of the Scipionic circle. The conversational frame is characteristic: satire as dinner-table philosophy between equals, not public oration. We are in the middle of an argument, not at a lectern.\n\nThe dominant rhetorical figure is anaphora: *virtus* repeated six times, each instance opening a new clause in what becomes a catalogue definition. The structure is broadly tripartite: *virtus* as practical knowledge (lines 1-5), *virtus* as practical conduct (lines 6-8), and *virtus* as social and civic duty (lines 9-13).\n\nThe syntax of the opening lines is an immediate index of the difficulty. Line 2 -- *quis in versamur, quis vivimus rebus, potesse* -- is one of the most compressed in the fragment: *quis* here is the locative/dative plural of the relative pronoun (equivalent to *in quibus*), and *potesse* is the archaic infinitive of *posse*. The clause means \"being able [to pay the fair price] in the things in which we move, in the things by which we live\" -- but reaching that reading requires recognizing the archaic infinitive form, resolving the ellipsis carried over from line 1, and understanding *versamur* in its idiomatic sense of \"to be engaged in, to move in a sphere.\" None of these is individually fatal; all at once, in two lines, without surrounding context, this is representative of what the rest of the fragments will do to you.\n\n*Patriai* in line 12 is the archaic genitive of *patria* (-ai for -ae), inherited from the oldest Latin declension patterns and shared with Lucretius. It is a deliberate register marker: old-fashioned, solemn, Roman in the deepest sense of the word.\n\nThe closing three lines deliver the climax. After the long catalogue of intellectual and social virtues, Lucilius ends with a priority ranking: the fatherland first (*patriai prima*), then parents (*parentum*), our own interests last (*postremaque nostra*). The compression of the final line -- *tertia iam postremaque nostra* -- lands with unusual force. *Iam* is idiomatic here (\"and in fact,\" \"yes, and\"), and *postremaque* (last of all) doubles the weight of *tertia* (third). Lucilius is not inventing a new ethics; he is articulating, with remarkable precision, the inherited Roman hierarchy of obligations -- and making absolutely clear where the individual stands in relation to it.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: A -- Very Difficult.**\n\nThree criteria push Lucilius into A territory simultaneously. The lexicon is genuinely demanding: archaic pre-classical morphology, colloquial contractions, and Greek insertions throughout without advance notice. The allusion density is high in a way specific to personal satire: the targets are topical and contemporary, meaning the humor depends on knowledge that is now almost entirely lost and cannot be recovered by preparation alone. The fragmentary state of the corpus eliminates the contextual support that normally makes sustained reading easier -- virtually every passage must be interpreted without surrounding text. The surviving material is substantial enough in total (around 1,300 lines) that NC is not warranted; the difficulty is assessable and consistently high. But the absence of extended connected text means there is nowhere to find firm footing, and the difficulty never lets up.\n\n---\n\n# POMPONIUS BONONIENSIS AND QUINTUS NOVIUS\n\n### Lucius Pomponius Bononiensis (fl. c. 90 BC) and Quintus Novius (fl. c. 90-80 BC)\n\n*Lesser-Known Authors*\n\n---\n\nThe Atellan farce had two fathers, and we have almost nothing by either of them. This is somewhat less devastating than the loss of Pacuvius and Accius, because the *fabulae Atellanae* were never aiming for literary immortality in the first place -- they were the low comedy of festivals and market days, performed by actors in exaggerated masks for audiences who wanted to laugh at the same disasters happening to the same characters they had always laughed at. But the loss still stings, because from the fragments it is clear that both men were genuinely funny in ways that do not require scholarly apparatus to appreciate. The jokes land. That is rarer than it sounds across two thousand years.\n\nPomponius and Novius are always mentioned together by ancient critics, because they did the same thing at the same time: they took the Atellan farce from an improvised performance tradition in Oscan into a scripted Latin literary genre. Before them, the *fabulae Atellanae* were unwritten and performed in the native language of Campania; after them, they had fixed Latin texts. This is not a minor contribution. It is the difference between folk music and a published score.\n\n## Biographies\n\n\n![Lucius Pomponius Bononiensis](./images/pomponius.jpg)\n*Lucius Pomponius Bononiensis *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n**Lucius Pomponius Bononiensis** -- the cognomen tells you everything that is known about his origins: he was from Bononia, the city on the Via Aemilia that is now Bologna. This makes him, to the best of our knowledge, the only significant Latin literary figure from what was then technically Cisalpine Gaul, a region that would not receive full Roman citizenship until 49 BC under Caesar. Whether this made him an outsider in Rome's literary circles, or whether nobody particularly cared, we do not know. What we do know is that he was active around the time of Sulla, worked alongside Quintus Novius, and proceeded to do something no one had done before: he wrote down the Atellan farce.\n\nThe *fabulae Atellanae* were one of the oldest forms of popular Italian entertainment. They took their name from Atella, a town in Campania between Capua and Naples, originally performed in Oscan by improvising actors wearing fixed masks who played stock characters in loose comic scenarios. Rome had been importing and watching *Atellanae* for generations before Pomponius's time. They were the ancient equivalent of television: everyone had seen them, nobody would have claimed they were high art, and the audience knew exactly who was going to do what to whom because the characters never changed.\n\nThe four stock characters were: **Maccus**, the fool, the glutton, the perpetually unsuccessful schemer -- a man who enters every situation with a plan and leaves it without his pants; **Bucco**, the loud-mouthed braggart with puffed-out cheeks (*buccae*), all talk and no thought; **Pappus**, the elderly man, inevitably rich, inevitably cuckolded, and inevitably convinced that his young wife is faithful; and **Dossennus**, the hunchback trickster, a figure of sly intelligence who is funnier than he is clever and cleverer than he looks.\n\nWhat Pomponius did -- and this is his specific literary contribution -- was write it down. He gave the *Atellanae* a fixed script, a predetermined plot, and formal metrical structure. He transformed an improvised folk entertainment into a scripted literary genre. Approximately seventy play titles are attributed to him; around two hundred verses survive.\n\nAncient critics recognized the achievement. Fronto, writing to Marcus Aurelius, singles out Novius and Pomponius for their mastery of *verba rusticana et jocularia ac ridicularia* -- rustic, comic, and absurd vocabulary. Seneca the Elder (*Controversiae* 7.3.9) credits Pomponius specifically with inventing wordplay based on the double meaning of a single word. Velleius Paterculus places him among notable writers of the Sullan period. Macrobius quotes the *Kalendae Martiae* in the *Saturnalia*. As for what Pomponius actually looked like, thought, or cared about: unknown. He is, appropriately for a genre built on stock characters, something of a stock character himself in the history of literature -- present only in other people's footnotes and his own fragments.\n\n\n![Quintus Novius](./images/novius.jpg)\n*Quintus Novius *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n**Quintus Novius** is an even greater ghost than Pomponius. No ancient source tells us where he was born, who his family was, or how he died. We know he was active at roughly the same time as Pomponius -- the late Republic, around 90-80 BC -- and that the two men are consistently named together as the founders of literary Atellan comedy. Beyond that, biography is silence.\n\nWhat the fragments reveal is a writer who was, if anything, even sharper with verbal wit than Pomponius. Cicero quotes Novius directly in *De Oratore* (2.279) -- a scene from an unnamed play in which a father is weeping and someone asks why:\n\n> quid ploras, pater? -- mirum ni canem / condemnatus sum.\n> \"Why are you crying, father?\" -- \"No wonder -- I have been convicted. A thousand sestertii.\"\n\nThe joke is constructed with a professional's timing: the question is asked, the emotional register is established, the explanation is delayed, and when it arrives it is simultaneously trivial and entirely sufficient. Cicero uses it as a textbook example of how comedy produces laughter through surprise and reversal -- the same mechanisms he is analyzing in a treatise on oratory. That Cicero, with his elaborate theory of wit, chose a Novius fragment to illustrate his point is no minor endorsement.\n\nFronto also quotes Novius separately from Pomponius (*Epistulae ad M. Caes.* 4.6.1H), praising a fragment about the throat (*fauces*) as a prime example of rustic vocabulary. Macrobius quotes Novius in the same *Saturnalia* discussion where he quotes Pomponius's *Kalendae Martiae*, suggesting both wrote comedy associated with seasonal celebrations. Approximately forty-three play titles are attributed to Novius; roughly one hundred lines survive.\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n**Pomponius:** Around seventy plays, none surviving complete. The major groupings are:\n\nThe **Maccus** series: *Maccus*, *Macci Gemini* (The Twin Maccuses -- a premise that sounds like a recipe for structural chaos), *Maccus Miles* (Maccus as Soldier), *Maccus Sequester* (Maccus as Intermediary), and *Maccus Virgo* (Maccus as Virgin -- which went exactly as well as the title suggests).\n\nThe **Pappus** series: *Pappus Agricola* (Pappus the Farmer), *Pappus Praeteritus* (Pappus Passed Over), *Sponsa Pappi* (Pappus's Bride), and *Hirnea Pappi* (Pappus's Wine Jug).\n\nThe **Bucco** series: *Bucco Adoptatus* (Bucco the Adopted Son) and *Bucco Auctoratus* (Bucco Under Contract).\n\nStandalone titles: *Galli Transalpini* (The Transalpine Gauls), *Fullones* (The Cloth-Fullers), *Philosophia*, *Dotata* (The Woman with a Dowry), *Leno* (The Pimp), and *Pannuceati* (The Ragged Men). [All fragmentary]\n\n**Novius:** Around forty-three plays, none surviving complete. The stock-character series includes *Maccus*, *Maccus Copo* (Maccus the Innkeeper), *Maccus Exul* (Maccus the Exile), *Pappus Praeteritus* (Pappus Passed Over -- the same title as one of Pomponius's, which tells you something about genre conventions), and *Duo Dossenni* (The Two Dossenni).\n\nStandalone titles: *Agricola* (The Farmer), *Asinus* (The Donkey), *Exodium* (The Finale -- possibly a generic term for the Atellan piece performed at the end of a theatrical program), *Fullones* (also the same title as Pomponius's -- the fulling workshop was evidently irresistible for both of them), *Funus* (The Funeral), *Gallinaria* (The Chicken Coop), *Gemini* (The Twins), *Milites Pometinenses* (The Soldiers from Pometia), *Parcus* (The Miser), *Surdus* (The Deaf Man), *Tabellaria* (The Tablet Carrier), *Vindemiatores* (The Grape-Harvesters), and *Virgo Praegnans* (The Pregnant Virgin). [All fragmentary]\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nThe ancient critics who mention Pomponius and Novius tend to mention them in the same breath, and for good reason: they were working in the same genre for the same audience with the same stock of characters. Fronto's *verba rusticana et jocularia ac ridicularia* applies equally to both. Both wrote predominantly in iambic senarii and trochaic septenarii. Both preferred short lines, imperative-heavy syntax, rapid dialogue, and jokes that land on the last word.\n\nWhere a distinction can be attempted, it is this: Pomponius seems to have leaned toward situational and physical comedy -- the humor of Maccus in a disguise, of Pappus comprehensively deceived, of workers in a fulling shop giving each other contradictory instructions. Novius had a sharper tongue. The condemned-man fragment Cicero quotes is purely verbal; the joke is entirely in the structure of the disclosure. Seneca's attribution of wordplay invention to Pomponius is Pomponius-specific; Cicero's endorsement of Novius as a model for well-constructed comic reversal is Novius-specific. The difference, if real, is roughly the difference between a pratfall and a punchline. In the Atellan farce, both were necessary.\n\nThe stock character structure means that very little context is needed to grasp what is happening in any given scene. When Pappus Agricola learns that someone is driving his wife \"like a donkey at the millstone,\" the situation is immediately legible. Atellan comedy is not subtle. It is not trying to be.\n\nMetrically, the fragments are predominantly iambic senarii and trochaic septenarii -- the same meters used by Plautus and Terence, making the rhythmic structure familiar to anyone who has read those authors. Syntactically, both writers operate with great simplicity: imperatives, short questions, direct insults. There is no room for periodic sentences or hyperbaton when performance demands immediate comprehension.\n\nThe main difficulty for a modern student is vocabulary. The rustic and colloquial words in Pomponius and Novius do not appear in standard Latin courses, and many are hapax legomena -- single attestations preserved only because a grammarian needed an example. Nonius Marcellus is the most important ancient source for both, preserving fragments in his lexicographical *De Compendiosa Doctrina* not as literary monuments but as illustrations of unusual words. Context is almost entirely absent: we know the lines but rarely who is speaking, to whom, or why.\n\n## Latin Excerpts, Translations and Analyses\n\n### Pomponius Bononiensis -- *Fullones* (The Cloth-Fullers)\n\n**Latin text** *(Pomponius Bononiensis, Fullones, fr. 48-50 Ribbeck, preserved by Nonius Marcellus 472M)*\n\n*Source note: Neither The Latin Library nor Splash Latino has a dedicated Pomponius Bononiensis page. The text is drawn from PHI Latin Texts (phi0618.phi001), which uses the Ribbeck edition as its base text. The ancient source is Nonius Marcellus, the 4th-century AD grammarian, who preserves this passage under his entry for the word* conplectite *in* De Compendiosa Doctrina.\n\n> Quin ergo, quando conuenit, conplectite?\n> Mi frater, salueto! -- O soror, salue, mea!\n> Facite ut ignis feruat, ligna insipite, far concidite.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> Come on then, since it is the right moment, go ahead and embrace!\n> My brother, hello! -- Oh my sister, hello!\n> Make sure the fire boils, feed in the wood, chop up the grain.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThe play is the *Fullones* -- the Cloth-Fullers. A *fullo* was a laundry worker who cleaned and finished woolen cloth by treading it in large vats filled with water, clay, and stale urine (the ammonia content being essential to the process). Roman fulling workshops were a fixture of city life; the fullers of Pompeii were famous enough to paint political campaign slogans on walls. Choosing them as the subject of an Atellan farce is characteristic: this is comedy from the world of physical, smelly, manual labor, the exact milieu where Maccus and Bucco would feel most at home.\n\nThe fragment was preserved because Nonius Marcellus needed to cite the imperative form *conplectite* -- second-person plural present of *complecti* (to embrace). It was saved not as a literary monument but as a grammar example.\n\nLine 1: *Quin ergo, quando conuenit, conplectite?* The particle *quin* with a direct imperative is an urgent colloquial construction -- \"come on then,\" \"why not go ahead and.\" *Quando conuenit* means \"when it is fitting\" -- someone is prompting two characters to greet each other, as though watching from outside the scene.\n\nLine 2: The greeting is the joke. One character says *mi frater, salueto* (\"my brother, hello\"), using the archaic salutation form *salueto* (more old-fashioned than simple *salue*). The other responds *o soror, salue, mea* (\"oh my sister, hello\"). One says \"brother\"; the other says \"sister.\" This asymmetry is the comic kernel. In Atellan comedy, gender confusion points almost certainly to a disguise scenario -- *Maccus Virgo* is an entire play -- or to the kind of willful absurdity that the genre treats as a feature rather than a bug.\n\nLine 3: The comedy lands back in the workshop: *facite ut ignis feruat* (see to it that the fire boils), *ligna insipite* (feed in the wood), *far concidite* (chop up the grain). Three imperatives in rapid sequence, no explanatory framing. The spelt grain is surprising for a fulling workshop -- it is food, not part of the process -- suggesting the scene may take place partly in a kitchen, or that Pomponius is simply enjoying the incongruity. The line ends and the scene continues somewhere we cannot follow.\n\n---\n\n### Quintus Novius -- *Maccus Exul* (Maccus the Exile)\n\n**Latin text** *(Novius, Maccus Exul, fr. 48-50 Ribbeck, preserved by Nonius Marcellus 4M)*\n\n*Source note: Neither The Latin Library nor Splash Latino has a dedicated Novius page. The text is drawn from PHI Latin Texts (phi0592.phi001), which uses the Ribbeck edition as its base text. The ancient source is Nonius Marcellus, who preserves these lines under his entry for the demonstrative particle* em *in* De Compendiosa Doctrina.\n\n> Em: díxin itúrum hominem in Túscos tolútim?\n> Límen superum, quód mei misero saépe confregít caput,\n> Ínferum autem, dígitos omnis úbi ego diffregí meos.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> There! Did I not say that man would be off to Tuscany at a gallop?\n> The top threshold, which has so often smashed this wretch's head in,\n> and the bottom one, where I have broken all my toes.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThe play is *Maccus Exul* -- Maccus the Exile. The title suggests a scenario in which Maccus has been driven out of somewhere, presumably following the kind of comprehensive disaster that defines his character. Exile -- from home, city, or household -- was a standard comic starting point, placing the fool in an unfamiliar situation with no safety net.\n\nLine 48: *Em: díxin itúrum hominem in Túscos tolútim?* The fragment opens with the demonstrative particle *em* -- \"there!\" or \"look!\" -- which is what Nonius Marcellus was citing it to illustrate. *Dixin* is a syncopated form of *dixine* (= *dixistine*, \"did I not say...?\"), a colloquial contraction found elsewhere in Plautus. *Tolutim* means moving at a trot or gallop, borrowed from the vocabulary of horse-gaits and used here to describe a rapid and undignified departure. Tuscany -- Etruria -- carried associations of provincial foreignness in the Roman comic imagination. Whether the destination is funny in itself, or whether the speed of the departure is the joke, depends on context we no longer have.\n\nLines 49-50: The scene shifts to a monologue of physical complaint. *Limen superum* is the top threshold -- the lintel above a doorway, at head height for a tall man and slightly above it for anyone moving at speed. *Limen inferum* is the raised stone at the base of the doorframe, where feet trip. Maccus's relationship with this doorway is characterized by one word: *saepe* -- often. He has often hit his head on the top threshold. He has broken all his toes on the bottom one. This is not a one-time accident; it is a pattern. The comedy is precisely in the *saepe*: Maccus is a man who, after multiple encounters with the same doorway, has still not learned to duck or step carefully. The exaggerated mask of the fool, with its large features, would have made the image immediately visible to any audience familiar with the character.\n\nThe fragment was preserved not for the image or the joke but because Nonius needed a citation for *em*. Maccus's chronic battle with his own doorframe survived two thousand years as a footnote to a particle. That is Atellan comedy's version of literary immortality.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: NC -- Not Comparable (both).**\n\nThe fragments of Pomponius and Novius together amount to roughly three hundred lines distributed across more than one hundred identified play titles -- an average of two to three lines per play. For Novius specifically, the surviving material is even sparser than Pomponius's: approximately one hundred lines across forty-three titles, averaging barely two lines per play. No complete scene survives for either author. The voice is identifiable in both cases -- colloquial, rustic, punchy, occasionally obscene -- and the per-line difficulty is assessable (roughly C to low B in intrinsic terms). But reliable multi-criteria rating requires connected text, and connected text is precisely what does not exist. Both are rated NC.\n\nAs a closing note: the genre they invented proved more durable than either of them. The same basic structure, the same characters, and many of the same jokes resurfaced in the *commedia dell'arte* fifteen centuries later. Maccus's mask and his tendency to collide with every doorframe he encountered did not die with the Roman Empire -- they just changed their name to Pulcinella.\n\n";
window.__CAESAR_MD__="# CAESAR'S ERA (100--44 BC)\n\nThe first century BC is Rome's most turbulent, and the turbulence is inseparable from its literature. The Republic spent the century destroying itself: Sulla's march on Rome, the Catilinarian conspiracy, the First Triumvirate, Caesar's civil war, and finally his assassination in 44 BC. For the authors of this era, this is not background noise -- it is the subject. Cicero was executed on Antony's orders. Sallust wrote history as a post-mortem on a republic he had watched corrupt itself from the inside. Caesar narrated his own conquests in the third person, as calmly as if someone else had done them. The political crisis and the literary output are the same phenomenon.\n\nGreek philosophy arrived in Rome in this era with the force of a delayed import finally cleared through customs. Epicureanism, Stoicism, and Academic skepticism were no longer foreign curiosities: they were the intellectual air educated Romans breathed. Lucretius built his entire epic on Epicurean physics, arguing in verse that the gods do not intervene in human affairs and that death is simply nothing to worry about. Cicero, who was skeptical of certainty in all its forms, ransacked the Academy and the Stoa alike to produce Latin versions of Greek philosophy -- coining words like *qualitas* and *essentia* because Latin had no equivalents, and in doing so permanently shaped the vocabulary of Western thought.\n\nThe most consequential development of this era is the stabilization of Latin itself. The archaic language of Plautus and Ennius -- irregular, exuberant, full of forms that later readers would find barbaric -- gave way to the *sermo urbanus*, the polished urban prose that schoolchildren still parse today. This transition was not accidental: it was argued over. The *Atticists*, led by the orator Calvus, advocated a plain, spare style modeled on classical Greek, stripped of ornament. Against them, Cicero defended a fuller, rhythmical prose -- neither Attic austerity nor Asiatic excess, but a middle road that used periodic sentences, rhetorical figures, and careful cadence to make Latin a pleasure to read aloud. Cicero won, in the sense that his prose became the model for the next two millennia.\n\nThe Big 5 of this era -- Cicero, Caesar, Lucretius, Sallust, Catullus -- shaped not just Roman literature but the entire Western tradition. Cicero defined what an orator and a philosophical writer could do in Latin. Caesar demonstrated that absolute clarity could be its own kind of power. Lucretius proved that a technical argument about physics could also be great poetry. Sallust invented the compressed, moralistic historical monograph. Catullus made Roman lyric poetry personal in a way it had never been before. Any of the five, taken alone, would be enough to make this era remarkable. Together, they represent a convergence that has no real parallel in the history of Latin literature.\n\nThe works produced in Caesar's era fall into strikingly different categories, reflecting how many things Latin could now do. At one end, Varro and Lucretius write *didactic* works -- encyclopaedic and technical, one about farming and language, the other about the nature of the universe. At the other, Catullus writes lyric poetry of radical brevity and emotional directness. Between them: Caesar and Sallust in historical prose; Cicero oscillating between oratory (the *Verrine* orations, the *Philippics*), philosophical dialogue (*De Re Publica*, *Tusculanae Disputationes*), and deeply personal letters that were never meant to be published but were. The era has no single literary mode. It has, instead, an embarrassment of them.\n\n---\n\n# MARCUS TERENTIUS VARRO (116--27 BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nMarcus Terentius Varro was born in 116 BC in Reate, a Sabine town in the hills northeast of Rome (modern Rieti), and died in 27 BC at eighty-nine. What he did with those years staggers the imagination: according to Jerome's count, seventy-four separate works comprising approximately six hundred and twenty books. No other Latin prose author in antiquity comes close. The man was not merely prolific -- he was encyclopaedic in the literal sense, filling volumes the way other Romans filled storehouses. Cicero dedicated the *Academica* to him and wrote that Romans had been wandering as strangers in their own city until Varro's books showed them their history, their customs, and who they actually were. That is not a dedication. It is a civic debt notice.\n\nHe was educated in Rome under the distinguished philologist Aelius Stilo, and later in Athens under the Academic philosopher Antiochus of Ascalon -- the same Antiochus whose lectures Cicero attended, a detail that places both men in the same intellectual current even as they pursued very different literary careers. Varro's practical side was equally developed: he served as a naval commander under Pompey during the campaign against the pirates (67 BC), earning the *corona rostrata*, the naval crown, one of the rarest military decorations in Roman history, awarded for victory in a sea battle. A scholar who could also run a fleet is an unusual combination, but Varro managed it without apparent difficulty.\n\nIn the civil war he sided with Pompey, commanding troops in Spain, where things went badly and he eventually surrendered to Caesar. Caesar, who was shrewder than his enemies about when to be generous, pardoned him completely -- and then gave him one of the most remarkable assignments in Roman cultural history: planning Rome's first great public library. For a man who had spent his life producing books, the prospect of building a place to house them must have seemed like the logical culmination of everything. It was not to be. Caesar was assassinated in 44 BC, the library project died with him, and Varro lost his most powerful patron.\n\nWorse followed. Mark Antony proscribed him after the formation of the Second Triumvirate -- Varro's name went on the list, his estates were seized, and soldiers ransacked his private library. He survived, reportedly hiding in the villa of Calpurnius Piso, and was eventually removed from the proscription list, possibly through Octavian's intervention. He got back enough of his property and peace of mind to keep writing. He was still completing new works at the age of eighty-eight.\n\nPetrarch called him *\"tertium Romanae lucis lumen\"* -- the third great light of Rome, placing him alongside Cicero and Virgil as one of antiquity's supreme luminaries. Augustine, who quarried Varro's *Antiquitates* exhaustively for the *City of God* (disagreeing with it so passionately that he cited it on almost every page), called him *\"vir doctissimus\"* -- the most learned of men. The joke -- because there is always a joke with Varro -- is that he opens *De Re Rustica* by explaining to his wife that he is writing in a hurry because his eightieth year is reminding him to pack his bags before departing life. He then lived nine more years and kept writing through all of them.\n\n## Main Works\n\n- **De Re Rustica** (*On Agriculture*, 37 BC) -- three books on farming, animal husbandry, and villa management, cast in the form of a philosophical dialogue. The most readable of Varro's surviving works, partly because the dialogue frame provides movement and the occasional dry joke. [Complete]\n- **De Lingua Latina** (*On the Latin Language*) -- originally twenty-five books; a systematic investigation of Latin etymology, inflection, and word-formation. Books V through X survive, with gaps. Essential for the history of Latin linguistics, and frequently maddening. [Fragmentary -- Books V-X of XXV]\n- **Saturae Menippeae** (*Menippean Satires*, c. 80--67 BC) -- roughly one hundred and fifty books of Cynic-style satirical writing in a mixture of prose and verse, modelled on the philosopher Menippus. Survive only as approximately six hundred fragments preserved by later grammarians. Were apparently very funny and unlike anything else in Latin literature. Their loss is one of antiquity's most lamented. [Fragmentary -- c. 600 fragments from 150 books]\n- **Antiquitates Rerum Humanarum et Divinarum** (*Antiquities of Human and Divine Matters*) -- forty-one books. A systematic account of Roman history, institutions, religious rites, and theology. The most important work Varro ever wrote and arguably the most important work of Roman cultural history ever attempted. Known primarily through Augustine's exhaustive and combative citations in the *City of God*. [Lost]\n- **Disciplinarum Libri IX** (*Nine Books of Disciplines*) -- an encyclopaedia of the nine liberal arts (grammar, logic, rhetoric, geometry, arithmetic, astronomy, music, medicine, architecture). The work that gave the Middle Ages most of its educational framework, including the distinction between trivium and quadrivium. [Lost]\n- **Imagines** (*Portraits*, also called *Hebdomades*) -- seven hundred paired portraits of famous Greeks and Romans, each accompanied by a verse epigram. Possibly the first illustrated book in Western history. [Lost]\n\nThe pattern should by now be clear. Varro is the most widely influential author in the history of Latin prose and among the most thoroughly lost.\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\n*De Re Rustica* is the entry point into Varro, and in some ways the most deceptive. It looks easy: dialogue form, named interlocutors, a conversational frame, a subject (farming) whose vocabulary is at least predictable. A reader with solid intermediate Latin and a good agricultural glossary can navigate it without being overwhelmed. The periodic sentences are real -- Varro was a classically trained orator and it shows -- but they are controlled rather than labyrinthine, and the dialogue structure provides constant contextual anchoring. **Lexicon: MEDIUM** (technical agricultural vocabulary, but repetitive and learnable); **Syntactic complexity: LOW-MEDIUM**; **Allusion density: LOW**.\n\n*De Lingua Latina* is a different creature. The subject is Latin itself, which means the vocabulary is abstract and meta-linguistic: Varro discusses inflection, word-formation, etymology, and grammatical category using philosophical terms borrowed from Greek that he himself is not entirely consistent in applying. Reading it requires not just Latin but the ability to think analytically *about* Latin while reading Latin -- a recursive demand that wrong-foots even experienced readers. His etymologies, furthermore, are frequently wrong by modern standards (he derives *lucus*, \"sacred grove,\" from *lucere*, \"to shine,\" on the grounds that it is shaded and therefore dark: the opposite of shining, as he himself acknowledges, which he considers etymologically significant). This requires a degree of patient detachment. **Lexicon: HIGH**; **Syntactic complexity: MEDIUM**.\n\nThe *Saturae Menippeae* present a third mode entirely: the mixture of prose and verse, the Cynic satirical register, the rapid code-switching between philosophical abstraction and street-level humor. What survives is fragments of fragments, which means the most allusive and elliptical passages tend to survive while the explanatory tissue around them is gone. Based on what can be read, they were linguistically adventurous -- mixing archaic Latin, Greek loanwords, colloquialisms, neologisms, and technical vocabulary as the satirical mood demanded. **Lexicon: HIGH; Stylistic figures: HIGH** (for what survives).\n\nAcross his surviving work, the main obstacles are: (1) technical vocabulary that shifts register between works, requiring different preparation for each; (2) an organizational style that privileges completeness over argument -- Varro lists, catalogues, and enumerates where a more rhetorically minded author would synthesize; (3) in *De Lingua Latina* specifically, the abstractness of the subject. **Genre burden: MEDIUM-HIGH** (encyclopaedic and didactic, with philosophical elements in the linguistic work). As a composite assessment, *De Re Rustica* sits squarely at Tier C; *De Lingua Latina* pushes into Tier B. The overall rating reflects the bulk of surviving material.\n\n## Latin Excerpt, Translation and Analysis\n\n**Latin text** *(The Latin Library, thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> Otium si essem consecutus, Fundania, commodius tibi haec scriberem, quae nunc, ut potero, exponam cogitans esse properandum, quod, ut dicitur, si est homo bulla, eo magis senex. Annus enim octogesimus admonet me ut sarcinas conligam, antequam proficiscar e vita. Quare, quoniam emisti fundum, quem bene colendo fructuosum cum facere velis, meque ut id mihi habeam curare roges, experiar; et non solum, ut ipse quoad vivam, quid fieri oporteat ut te moneam, sed etiam post mortem.\n\n> Nec, ut Homerus et Ennius, Musas, sed duodecim deos Consentis [...] illos XII deos, qui maxime agricolarum duces sunt. Primum, qui omnis fructos agri culturae caelo et terra continent, Iovem et Tellurem: itaque, quod ii parentes, magni dicuntur, Iuppiter pater appellatur, Tellus terra mater. Secundo Solem et Lunam, quorum tempora observantur, cum quaedam seruntur et conduntur. Tertio Cererem et Liberum, quod horum fructus maxime necessari ad victum: ab his enim cibus et potio venit e fundo. Quarto Robigum ac Floram, quibus propitiis neque robigo frumenta atque arbores corrumpit, neque non tempestive florent. Itaque publice Robigo feriae Robigalia, Florae ludi Floralia instituti. Item adveneror Minervam et Venerem, quarum unius procuratio oliveti, alterius hortorum; quo nomine rustica Vinalia instituta. Nec non etiam precor Lympham ac Bonum Eventum, quoniam sine aqua omnis arida ac misera agri cultura, sine successu ac bono eventu frustratio est, non cultura.\n\n*(De Re Rustica I.1 -- opening dedication and invocation)*\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> If I had managed to find some leisure, Fundania, I would have written these things for you more comfortably; as it stands, I will set them out as best I can, bearing in mind that haste is necessary -- because, as they say, if a man is a bubble, an old man is all the more so. My eightieth year is reminding me to pack my bags before I set out from life. Since, then, you have bought an estate that you wish to make productive by farming it well, and you are asking me to take responsibility for advising you on it, I will try; and not only will I advise you on what should be done for as long as I live, but also after my death.\n\n> Not the Muses, as Homer and Ennius did, but the twelve Consentes gods [...] those twelve gods who are above all the guides of farmers. First, Jupiter and Tellus, who hold together all the fruits of agriculture through sky and earth: and because they are parents and are called great ones, Jupiter is called Father, Tellus is called Earth Mother. Second, Sol and Luna, whose seasons are watched when certain things are sown and stored. Third, Ceres and Liber, because their fruits are most necessary for sustenance: from them come the food and drink that the farm provides. Fourth, Robigus and Flora, by whose favour neither does mildew ruin the grain and the trees, nor do they fail to flower in season - and so the state founded the Robigalia festival for Robigus and the Floralia games for Flora. Likewise I revere Minerva and Venus, the care of the olive grove belonging to one and of the gardens to the other - on whose account the rustic Vinalia was founded. And I pray too to Lympha and Bonus Eventus, since without water all farming is dry and wretched, and without success and a good outcome there is only frustration, not cultivation.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThe opening of *De Re Rustica* sets Varro's tone immediately: dry wit inside a frame of genuine erudition. Fundania is his wife, so his first move in a farming manual is to apologize to her for writing it in a hurry because he is about to die. The *bulla* -- the amulet-bubble worn by Roman boys -- is applied to human life in general, old age in particular. *Sarcinas conligam, antequam proficiscar e vita* (\"pack my bags before setting out from life\") is probably a proverbial phrase, but Varro anchors it to his eightieth year with the precision of a man who kept meticulous records of everything.\n\nThe invocation that follows is learned wit in action: Varro bypasses Homer's Muses and Ennius's Muses in favor of a full enumeration of agricultural deities -- Jupiter and Tellus, Sol and Luna, Ceres and Liber, and on down the list. A man who cannot open a farming manual without first cataloguing the correct gods for the occasion is a man whose encyclopaedic compulsion is structural, not ornamental. Note also the hyperbaton in *quem bene colendo fructuosum cum facere velis*: the subordinate clause wraps around its own object in a way that demands the reader hold the syntax in suspension -- characteristic of Varro's prose at its denser moments.\n\n## Proposed Tier\n\n**Tier: C (Manageable)**\n\nThe core of Varro's accessible prose -- *De Re Rustica* in particular -- sits at the manageable end of the difficulty scale. The language is classical, the vocabulary, though technical, is concrete, and the dialogue form provides structural clarity that prevents the denser passages from becoming truly opaque. *De Lingua Latina* pushes toward Tier B in its more analytical sections, due to abstract vocabulary and compressed argumentation. As a composite rating for what survives, Tier C reflects the fact that a well-prepared reader with good vocabulary support can navigate Varro's work without being overwhelmed. The NC designation does not apply: what survives is substantial enough to sustain a meaningful rating.\n\n---\n\n# CORNELIUS NEPOS (c. 110--24 BC)\n\n![Cornelius Nepos](./images/cornelius_nepos.jpg)\n*Cornelius Nepos (imaginary reconstruction)*\n\n## Biography\n\nCornelius Nepos was born around 110 BC somewhere in Cisalpine Gaul -- the ancient sources are vague on the exact town, and Nepos himself apparently saw no reason to clear the matter up. What is certain is that he came to Rome, moved in the highest literary circles of the late Republic, and managed to die in his bed in 24 BC at a respectable old age, which in the first century BC was no mean achievement. He knew Cicero, he knew Catullus, and he was one of the closest friends of Titus Pomponius Atticus, the man who maintained intimate correspondence with virtually every literate Roman of the era. To be a friend of Atticus was to be connected to everyone. Nepos played his cards quietly and well.\n\nThe most famous thing about Nepos is not something he wrote -- it is something written about him. Catullus opens his collected poems with a dedication to Cornelio, his fellow Cisalpine Gaul, describing him as the man who \"used to think my trifles were something\" (*meas esse aliquid putare nugas*) and who had dared, alone among Italians, to set out all of history in three scholarly and laborious little books (*omne aevum tribus explicare chartis / doctis, Iuppiter, et laboriosis*). The affection is real, the teasing is gentle, and the reference to the three-book *Chronica* tells us something important: Nepos was known to his contemporaries not as a biographer but as a chronographer, a man who had tried to organize universal history into a compact framework. The biographical works came later and outlasted everything else.\n\nNepos was also the biographer of Atticus -- and this is the genuinely unusual part: he wrote the life while Atticus was still alive. Atticus died in 32 BC; sections of the surviving *Vita Attici* clearly refer to events before his death and were apparently read by the subject himself. Writing a panegyric biography of a living friend and submitting it to him for his approval is an act that requires either great tact or great friendship, and Nepos and Atticus had enough of both. The life of Atticus is the most personal thing Nepos wrote: the portrait of a man he admired deeply, written for no occasion except admiration. After Atticus's death, Nepos apparently added a final chapter bringing the account to its close.\n\nHis life was spent at a careful distance from the violence of his era. He did not hold magistracies, did not command armies, and was not assassinated. While Cicero was being hunted down by Antony's soldiers, while Caesar was crossing rubicons and being stabbed in theatre lobbies, while Sallust was getting expelled from the Senate on morality charges, Nepos was writing biographies of dead Greek generals and keeping his head down. This is not cowardice -- it is the entirely defensible position of a man who understood that literature survives better than politics. He was right. His biographies are still read. Most of his contemporaries' political careers are footnotes.\n\n## Main Works\n\n- **De Viris Illustribus** (*On Famous Men*) -- originally at least sixteen books covering generals, historians, kings, orators, poets, and philosophers, comparing Greek and Roman exemplars in parallel. The vast majority is lost. What survives: the complete (or nearly complete) *Liber de Excellentibus Ducibus Exterarum Gentium* (twenty-three lives of foreign, mainly Greek, commanders, including Miltiades, Themistocles, Alcibiades, Hannibal, and Epaminondas); the *Vita Catonis* and *Vita Attici* from the book on Latin historians. [Fragmentary -- roughly 25 lives survive of what was originally a much larger work]\n- **Chronica** (*Chronicles*) -- three books of universal history from earliest times to Nepos's own era, the work Catullus mentions in his dedication. The first sustained attempt in Latin to place Roman history in a global chronological framework. [Lost]\n- **Exempla** (*Examples*) -- at least five books of collected anecdotes illustrating moral and practical lessons, probably similar in spirit to *De Viris Illustribus*. Cited by later authors. [Lost]\n- **Love poems** -- mentioned by Pliny the Elder, who seems to have found them rather racy. [Lost]\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nNepos is one of the most accessible Latin prose authors that survives, which is partly why he has been a school text for centuries and partly why literary critics have never known quite what to do with him. His sentences are short, his vocabulary is standard classical, his syntax is paratactic: subject, verb, object, next sentence. There is almost no periodic architecture, almost no subordination for its own sake, very little of the rhetorical elaboration that makes Cicero demanding. A student who has worked through Caesar and wants something with more biographical interest and less military logistics will find Nepos a comfortable next step. **Lexicon: LOW; Syntactic complexity: LOW; Stylistic figures: LOW; Allusion density: LOW.**\n\nThis does not mean Nepos is entirely without surprises. His Latin has been described by ancient and modern critics alike as occasionally provincial -- a slight Gallic tinge, some non-classical constructions, choices that classical purists would flag. Quintilian notes his usage without enthusiasm. None of this rises to the level of genuine difficulty; it is more a reminder that \"correct\" classical Latin was a prestige standard centered on Rome, and that writers from the provinces sometimes diverged from it in ways that were not yet fully ironed out. For a modern reader, the oddities are minor and rarely impede comprehension.\n\nWhat makes Nepos genuinely worth reading -- and what the tier system does not fully capture -- is the quality of his subject matter. The lives are short, sometimes frustratingly so (he is aware of this and apologizes for it in the preface), but they are carefully chosen and economically told. The life of Epaminondas is the clearest example: Nepos's Latin is as transparent as glass, and what you see through it is one of the most attractive characters in all of ancient biography -- a man of complete integrity, no money, no wife, no children, whose only legacy was a single battle that freed Greece. The simplicity of the prose suits the simplicity of the man. It is not clear whether that is art or luck, but the effect is real.\n\n## Latin Excerpt, Translation and Analysis\n\n**Latin text** *(The Latin Library, thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> At Epaminondas cum animadverteret mortiferum se vulnus accepisse simulque, si ferrum, quod ex hastili in corpore remanserat, extraxisset, animam statim emissurum, usque eo retinuit, quoad renuntiatum est vicisse Boeotios. Id postquam audivit, 'Satis' inquit 'vixi; invictus enim morior.' Tum ferro extracto confestim exanimatus est.\n>\n> Hic uxorem numquam duxit. In quo cum reprehenderetur, quod liberos non relinqueret, a Pelopida, qui filium habebat infamem, maleque eum in eo patriae consulere diceret, 'Vide', inquit 'ne tu peius consulas, qui talem ex te natum relicturus sis. Neque vero stirps potest mihi deesse. Namque ex me natam relinquo pugnam Leuctricam, quae non modo mihi superstes, sed etiam immortalis sit necesse est.'\n\n*(Epaminondas IX.3--4 and X.1--2)*\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> But Epaminondas, realizing he had received a mortal wound and knowing that if he drew out the iron from the spear-shaft that remained lodged in his body he would immediately breathe his last, held it in place until word was brought that the Boeotians had won. When he heard this, he said: \"I have lived long enough; for I die undefeated.\" Then, the iron drawn out, he died at once.\n>\n> He never married. When he was reproached for this on the grounds that he left no children -- by Pelopidas, who had a son of bad reputation and said that Epaminondas was failing his duty to his country -- he replied: \"Take care that it is not you who fail it worse, since you will leave behind a son like that. Nor can I lack for descendants. I leave behind me, born of me, the Battle of Leuctra, which will not only outlast me but must of necessity be immortal.\"\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThese two passages contain the most quoted lines in Nepos and together illustrate both what he does well and how he does it. *Satis vixi; invictus enim morior* -- \"I have lived long enough; for I die undefeated\" -- is one of the great exits in ancient literature, and Nepos delivers it in six words of utterly plain Latin. No rhetorical figures, no subordination, no period: a subject, a verb, a connective, another subject, an adverb, another verb. The power is entirely in the content. Nepos understood, better than many more ambitious writers, that some moments do not need ornamentation.\n\nThe second passage shows a different skill: the reporting of a witty comeback. *Ex me natam relinquo pugnam Leuctricam* -- \"I leave behind me, born of me, the Battle of Leuctra\" -- is a brilliant retort to Pelopidas, whose own son was notorious enough to make the reproach somewhat reckless. Nepos sets up the exchange economically: the charge, the parry, the counter-blow. The *necesse est* closing the period is the only moment the syntax reaches toward something more elaborate, and it earns its place: immortality needs a logical connective. Note also the gentle sting buried in the setup -- Nepos identifies Pelopidas's son as *infamem* in a single word and moves on, letting the reader do the arithmetic.\n\nFor the reader studying Latin, these passages offer a useful counterpoint to Caesar: the same clarity, the same economy, a completely different subject matter. The main analytical tool needed here is not grammatical but contextual: knowing that Leuctra (371 BC) was the moment Thebes broke Sparta's military dominance for good changes the weight of *pugnam Leuctricam* from a proper noun to a monument. Nepos's prose gives you the words; you supply the history.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: D (Good Exercise)**\n\nNepos is Latin without obstacles: standard classical vocabulary, short paratactic sentences, no archaisms, no compressed philosophical argument, no allusion requiring specialist knowledge to unpack. He is the author you assign when the goal is confident reading rather than demanding translation. The biographical genre is the most accessible prose register in the ancient world, and Nepos inhabits it with complete ease. The occasional provincial oddity noted by ancient critics is a footnote, not a difficulty. Pair him with Caesar for a course on clean, unpretentious Latin -- the contrast in subject matter (campaigns vs. character portraits) keeps both authors interesting.\n\n---\n\n# QUINTUS HORTENSIUS HORTALUS (114--50 BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nQuintus Hortensius Hortalus was born in 114 BC, probably in Rome, into an equestrian family of no exceptional distinction. That did not slow him down. He delivered his first speech in the Forum at the age of nineteen, and it caused an immediate sensation. Cicero, who was fifteen at the time and still collecting cases for his rhetorical education, would later recall that Hortensius debuted to the unanimous admiration of everyone present -- not a promising beginning, but a fully formed performance. For the next two decades, Hortensius was the undisputed leading advocate at the Roman bar. No one could compete with him. This is the context that makes his later rivalry with Cicero worth understanding: when Cicero arrived, Hortensius was not a champion being challenged. He was an institution being dismantled.\n\nThe style that made Hortensius dominant was what Rome called *Asianism* -- the florid, theatrical, rhythmically elaborate school of Greek rhetoric developed in the cities of Asia Minor and imported into Latin advocacy. Asianist oratory was everything that spare Attic prose was not: long periodic sentences with carefully wrought endings, elaborate word-coloring, vivid metaphor, and emotional intensity calibrated to maximum effect. Hortensius handled all of this with a performer's instinct. He modulated his voice like a musician moving through registers, arranged his toga before each appearance with the deliberate care of an actor, and gestured so expressively that contemporaries compared the experience of watching him to watching a theatrical dance. This last quality provoked the most famous insult ever directed at a Roman advocate.\n\nDuring shared oratorical exercises in their youth, Lucius Torquatus, watching Hortensius perform yet another sweeping gesture, called out that he was not an orator at all but \"Dionysia\" -- the stage name of a famous female dancer of the time. It was meant to be devastating. Hortensius's reply was instant: he said he would far rather be Dionysia than what Torquatus was -- a man utterly unknown to the Muses, to Venus, and to Bacchus. In other words: uncultivated, loveless, and sober. The audience apparently loved this, and the anecdote circulated for generations. Cicero included it in the *Brutus*. The nickname \"Dionysia\" stuck to Hortensius in Roman gossip, though whether as a compliment or an insult seems to have depended on who was telling the story.\n\nThe collapse of his supremacy came in 70 BC, during the trial of Gaius Verres, the former governor of Sicily, who had spent his tenure looting the island with systematic thoroughness -- its temples, its private collections, its treasury. Cicero was brought in as prosecutor; Hortensius, the finest advocate money could retain, appeared for the defense. Hortensius was confident, reasonably so: the trial was set to run late in the year, when courts would close, forcing a retrial with a potentially more favorable jury. His plan was to talk. Cicero did not let him. Instead of opening with a long prosecutorial oration -- which would have eaten time and given Hortensius his stage -- Cicero spent five days presenting nothing but evidence: witnesses from Sicily, ledger books, sworn inventories of looted goods, one devastating document after another. Hortensius found himself with nothing to argue against. Verres read the room, did the arithmetic, and fled into voluntary exile before the second actio could begin. Cicero published the second actio speeches anyway -- five enormous orations on art theft and provincial corruption that Hortensius never had to answer, which may be why they survive in such good condition. The career of the most celebrated orator of his generation had been ended not by a more brilliant argument but by more efficient preparation.\n\nWhat followed is, in its way, more interesting than the rivalry itself. Hortensius accepted the verdict gracefully, and the two men became genuine friends. They appeared on the same side in subsequent cases, exchanged books, and maintained what Cicero in the *Brutus* describes as a relationship of mutual support and encouragement rather than competitive antagonism. Hortensius even turned to writing erotic poetry in his later years, presumably under the influence of the neoteric poets whose aesthetic he had no business sharing. A few fragments survive -- barely. One imagines Catullus reading them and being diplomatically silent. He died in 50 BC, two years before the civil war that would kill so many of his contemporaries, and was mourned by Cicero with what seems to have been genuine grief. He was also mourned, it is reported, far less sincerely at home, where he had been known to weep at the death of one of his ornamental fish without displaying comparable emotion at the death of his wife. He kept elaborate *piscinae* -- fish ponds -- at his Bauli villa near Baiae, and Cicero, who found this combination of luxury and emotional misalignment reliably absurd, gave him and his fellow fish-pond enthusiasts the collective nickname *piscinarii*.\n\nThe most remarkable chapter in the family history came after his death. In 43 BC, the Second Triumvirate -- Antony, Octavian, and Lepidus -- needed funds to prosecute the war against Brutus and Cassius, and decreed that the fourteen hundred wealthiest women in Rome would be subject to a special war tax. The women organized. They sent delegations to Octavia and Livia, who received them politely; they then tried Antony's mother Fulvia, who turned them away from the door. At that point Hortensia, daughter of Hortensius, walked to the Rostra in the Forum and gave a speech. She argued that women could not be made to fund a civil war in which they had no vote, no political role, and no voice -- that if the state wanted their property, it owed them, at minimum, the right to object. The triumvirs ordered their lictors to remove her. The crowd reacted badly enough that the lictors backed off. The next day the tax was reduced from fourteen hundred women to four hundred. Quintilian read Hortensia's speech and praised it not as a curiosity but as genuinely worth reading on its own merits. The speech has not survived. Her father, who made his reputation by speaking for clients who could pay, might have found something to admire in a daughter who spoke for herself.\n\n## Main Works\n\n- **Speeches** [Lost]: Hortensius argued hundreds of cases across a forty-year career, but no speech survived antiquity in complete form. Cicero notes in the *Brutus* that his written texts were disappointing compared to his performances -- the Asianist style depended so heavily on delivery, voice, and gesture that the words on the page lost most of their effect when the man was not there to animate them. This is the most eloquent verdict possible on both his talent and his limitations.\n- **Erotic poetry** [Fragmentary, c. three fragments]: In his later years Hortensius wrote verse in the neoteric manner. Ovid mentions it in the *Tristia*, apparently aware of it. What survives is almost nothing -- a few lines cited by grammarians for linguistic rather than literary reasons.\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\n**Lexicon and Medium:** The problem with assessing Hortensius's style is immediately obvious: we cannot assess Hortensius's style. No complete text survives. What we have is Cicero's description of it in the *Brutus*, and Cicero was both his most sympathetic critic and his successful replacement, which makes him a complicated witness. What Cicero tells us is that Hortensius worked in the Asianist tradition: elaborate sentence-structures with studied rhythmic endings (*clausulae*), rich *colores* of verbal ornament, and periodic architecture designed to build emotional pressure and release it with precisely timed cadences. This was technically demanding oratory, and readers who encountered his texts without him apparently found them less compelling than audiences who had watched him perform.\n\n**The delivery problem:** Cicero identifies this directly and without malice. Some orators, he writes, depend on the inflaming of the judge rather than the mere instruction of the judge -- their power is essentially performative. When the fire of delivery dies down, what remains is cold. Hortensius was, by this analysis, the most extreme case of a great advocate whose words only half-existed on the page. His poetry, similarly, exists as a rumor. NC is not a rating of difficulty but of inaccessibility: what we cannot read, we cannot rank.\n\n## Latin Excerpt\n\nThe passage below is not by Hortensius -- nothing by him survives. It is from the opening of Cicero's *Brutus* (6), a dialogue on Roman oratory written in 46 BC as a deliberate memorial to his dead rival and colleague. Cicero wrote this line to explain why Hortensius, had he lived, would have grieved more than anyone else at the state of Rome.\n\n**Source:** Cicero, *Brutus* 6, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/cicero/brut.shtml)\n\n**Latin text**\n\n> Etenim si viveret Q. Hortensius, cetera fortasse desideraret una cum reliquis bonis et fortibus civibus, hunc autem aut praeter ceteros aut cum paucis sustineret dolorem, cum forum populi Romani, quod fuisset quasi theatrum illius ingeni, voce erudita et Romanis Graecisque auribus digna spoliatum atque orbatum videret.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> For indeed, if Quintus Hortensius were still alive, he would perhaps share all the other losses with the rest of Rome's good and brave citizens -- but this grief he would bear more than all the others, or alongside very few: to see the Forum of the Roman people, which had been, as it were, the theater of his genius, stripped and bereft of an educated voice worthy of Roman and Greek ears.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nCicero opens the *Brutus* with this line for a reason: the entire dialogue is an act of grief for Hortensius, combined with a sustained argument that the tradition of Roman eloquence cannot die with him. The phrase *quasi theatrum illius ingeni* -- \"as it were, the theater of his genius\" -- is the most elegant tribute in Latin critical prose to an orator's relationship with his stage. The Forum was not merely where Hortensius worked; it was the space that gave his talent meaning and scale. Without an audience, without the physical space of advocacy, his kind of oratory could not exist at all. Cicero understood this, because he had beaten him in that same theater.\n\nThe sentence is a single period built on a conditional (*si viveret*) that immediately modulates: not a simple protasis-apodosis, but a conditional that becomes increasingly specific as it unfolds, adding *praeter ceteros aut cum paucis* (more than the rest, or with only a few) as a refinement of the pain, then the *cum*-clause that drives to the actual grief. The ablative of separation -- *spoliatum atque orbatum voce erudita* -- is emphatic and final. The doubling of the participles (*spoliatum atque orbatum*) is Ciceronian symmetry, reinforcing the totality of the loss. For the student of Latin, this sentence is a model of classical periodic style operating at full power with no room for misunderstanding: every clause is clear, every transition is marked, and the emotional weight is distributed precisely across the structure.\n\nThe point of including it here is that this is the closest we can get to Hortensius -- through the most famous pair of eyes that ever watched him speak.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: NC (Not Comparable)**\n\nNo complete work by Hortensius survives in any genre. His speeches existed in written form in the early Empire -- Cicero quotes from some of them in the *Brutus* -- but none reached us. His poetry is a handful of lines cited for grammatical purposes. The NC rating is not a comment on his ability, which by ancient testimony was exceptional, but on the impossibility of reading him: you cannot rank what you cannot read. What we can say is that the intrinsic difficulty of Asianist oratory -- complex periodic structure, elaborate rhythmic endings, dense verbal ornament -- would have placed him somewhere in the B-A range, had any full text survived. As it is, Hortensius exists in Latin literary history as an absence: the voice that made the Forum its theater, and left no transcript.\n\n---\n\n# PUBLIUS NIGIDIUS FIGULUS (c. 98--45 BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nPublius Nigidius Figulus was born around 98 BC and died in exile around 45 BC. He was an equestrian from a respectable but not distinguished family, who rose to the praetorship in 58 BC and became one of the most learned men in Rome -- or, depending on your point of view, one of the most alarming. Aulus Gellius, writing in the second century AD, called him \"second in learning only to Varro,\" and if you know how Gellius felt about Varro that is not a minor compliment. What set Nigidius apart from other learned Romans of his era was not the breadth of his knowledge -- Varro had more of that -- but its texture. Where Varro catalogued, Nigidius divined. Where Varro explained institutions, Nigidius explained the hidden relationships between words and things, the religious functions of Etruscan lightning, the astrological significance of the positions of stars, and the philosophical implications of the Pythagorean tradition that he was determined to revive in Rome. He was a scholar the way some people are prophets: with conviction that knowledge is not merely useful but dangerous.\n\nThe range of his intellectual interests was staggering and, in polite Roman society, slightly unsettling. He wrote extensively on Latin grammar -- a major work in at least twenty-eight books of *Commentarii grammatici*, still being mined by grammarians in the second century AD. He wrote on Etruscan religious discipline (*disciplina Etrusca*): the interpretation of lightning, of prodigies, of haruspicy. He wrote on astronomy and astrology, producing works on the Greek and \"barbarian\" (non-Greek) celestial spheres. He wrote on theology, on animals, and on Pythagorean philosophy. This combination -- rigorous grammar alongside astrological prediction and Pythagorean mysticism -- would have seemed perfectly coherent to Nigidius himself and deeply eccentric to almost everyone else. Cicero, who was his friend and respected his learning, nevertheless clearly found him somewhat bewildering.\n\nThe Pythagorean angle deserves its own paragraph. Pythagoreanism had been a force in southern Italy since the sixth century BC, but by Nigidius's time it had dwindled in Rome to an exotic curiosity. Nigidius tried to revive it as a living philosophical tradition, which in practice meant advocating for things that struck ordinary Romans as deeply strange: vegetarianism (or at minimum dietary restrictions), belief in the transmigration of souls, mathematical mysticism, and a set of ritual practices and taboos that Pythagorean communities observed. Cicero's dialogue *Hortensius* (now lost) has Nigidius as a character, and later writers referred to him as the founder of a neo-Pythagorean movement in Rome. Whether his attempt at a genuine revival gained any real following is doubtful. What is certain is that it gave him a reputation as a man who took the occult seriously, at a time when educated Romans were supposed to treat divination as a public institution useful for civic purposes, not as a genuine means of accessing hidden knowledge. Nigidius appears to have believed the latter.\n\nThe most famous anecdote about him concerns not grammar or Pythagoreanism but a birth. In 63 BC, during the Catilinarian crisis -- the most fraught moment in Cicero's consulship -- the senator Gaius Octavius arrived late to a Senate meeting because his wife had just given birth. The future emperor Augustus had entered the world. When the lateness of Octavius was noted and the cause explained, Nigidius Figulus, who was present and who had made something of a specialty of astrological calculation, asked for the exact hour of the birth. He received it, did his calculations, and announced to the assembled senators that a master of the world had been born. Suetonius, who preserves this story in the *Life of Augustus*, adds that the alarm this caused was considerable -- Octavius apparently attempted to expose the infant, and Nigidius had to dissuade him by pointing out that the child's destiny was already fixed and could not be undone by human intervention. Augustus eventually heard the story and, reportedly, was pleased by it. Whether Nigidius actually said this or whether the story was retroactively attributed to the most conveniently prophetic scholar present is impossible to determine, but the fact that it attached to Nigidius at all tells us something about his reputation.\n\nHis political end was the mirror image of Varro's. Both were learned polymaths, both Pompeian in sympathy, both caught on the wrong side of the civil war. Varro survived: he surrendered to Caesar in Spain, was pardoned, was given the library commission, outlived Caesar, outlived the proscriptions, and wrote into his nineties. Nigidius did not. He was exiled after Pharsalus, attempted through various intermediaries to obtain Caesar's clemency, and received no answer that was helpful. Cicero wrote to Caesar on his behalf -- a surviving letter (*Ad Familiares* IV.13) attests to the friendship and to the effort -- but Caesar did not recall him. He died in exile around 45 BC, a year before Caesar's assassination and decades before the master of the world whose birth he had supposedly predicted took power. It is one of history's more pointed ironies. He predicted Augustus and was killed, in effect, by the civil war that Augustus eventually ended.\n\n## Main Works\n\n- ***Commentarii grammatici*** [Fragmentary, c. 28 books]: The major grammatical work; extensively quoted by Gellius, Priscian, and other grammarians. Dealt with etymology, phonetics, morphology, and the meaning of words with a distinctively philosophical bent -- Nigidius was interested not just in how words worked but in why they worked that way.\n- **Works on Etruscan discipline** [Lost or fragmentary]: Covered haruspicy, the interpretation of lightning (*fulgurales libri*), and prodigies. He was considered one of Rome's leading authorities on Etruscan religious knowledge.\n- **Works on divination and astrology** (*Sphaera Graecanica*, *Sphaera Barbarica*) [Lost]: Astronomical and astrological works on the Greek and non-Greek celestial spheres; probably the basis for his reputation as an astrologer.\n- **Philosophical and religious works** [Lost]: Pythagorean philosophy and theology; also works on animals (*De animalibus*) and on the gods (*De diis*).\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\n**Lexicon and archaic interest:** What the Gellius quotations show us is a mind consistently drawn to the archaic layer of Latin -- old words, old forms, old distinctions that classical usage had smoothed over. In the passage on *religiosus*, Nigidius does not simply explain the word; he finds it in an ancient verse no one else bothered to preserve, then unpacks the morphological pattern (*vinosus*, *mulierosus*, *religiosus*) to show that the suffix carries a meaning that standard usage had forgotten. His grammar is not descriptive but archaeological: he digs into language looking for what it used to mean.\n\n**The overall situation:** NC is the only honest rating. The fragments we have are almost all preserved by grammarians who quoted Nigidius for a specific point and moved on; we have no continuous text, no complete argument, no sense of how a full Nigidian exposition actually unfolded over pages rather than sentences. The *Commentarii grammatici* in particular would have been demanding -- the philosophical weight behind individual grammatical observations suggests a dense, allusion-rich prose -- but this is inference, not reading.\n\n## Latin Excerpt\n\nThe passage below is from Aulus Gellius, *Noctes Atticae* X.9.1--2, where Gellius discusses the word *religiosus* and quotes Nigidius directly: first a verse from an old poem that Nigidius had preserved in his *Commentarii grammatici*, then Nigidius's own explanation of the morphological pattern.\n\n**Source:** Aulus Gellius, *Noctes Atticae* X.9.1--2, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/gellius/gellius10.shtml)\n\n**Latin text**\n\n> Nigidius Figulus, homo, ut ego arbitror, iuxta M. Varronem doctissimus, in undecimo commentariorum grammaticorum versum ex antiquo carmine refert memoria hercle dignum:\n>\n> *religentem esse oportet, religiosus ne fuas,*\n>\n> cuius autem id carmen sit, non scribit. Atque in eodem loco Nigidius: \"Hoc\" inquit \"inclinamentum semper huiuscemodi verborum, ut 'vinosus', 'mulierosus', 'religiosus', significat copiam quandam inmodicam rei, super qua dicitur. Quocirca 'religiosus' is appellabatur, qui nimia et superstitiosa religione sese alligaverat, eaque res vitio assignabatur.\"\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> Nigidius Figulus, a man who was, in my judgment, second in learning only to Marcus Varro, records in the eleventh book of his *Grammatical Commentaries* a verse from an ancient poem that is, by Hercules, worth remembering:\n>\n> *one ought to be scrupulous in religion, but must not become overly religious,*\n>\n> though he does not write whose poem it is. And in the same passage Nigidius says: \"This suffix, in words of this kind -- such as 'vinosus', 'mulierosus', 'religiosus' -- always signifies a kind of immoderate excess of the thing in question. For this reason, the person called 'religiosus' was one who had bound himself to religion with excessive and superstitious devotion, and this was counted as a fault.\"\n\n**Analysis**\n\nGellius introduces Nigidius with one of the most pointed descriptions in ancient scholarship: *iuxta M. Varronem doctissimus* -- \"second only to Varro in learning.\" This is the ancient world's equivalent of a Wikipedia \"See also.\" Then Nigidius does something that perfectly illustrates why he was remarkable: he preserves a line of ancient Latin verse (*religentem esse oportet, religiosus ne fuas*) that appears nowhere else in surviving literature, and he uses it to make a grammatical and theological argument simultaneously. The verse distinguishes *religens* (the participle: one who is observant, scrupulous, careful in religious duties) from *religiosus* (the adjective with the suffix *-osus*: one who is excessively, dangerously devoted). The suffix pattern -- *vinosus* is addicted to wine, *mulierosus* is obsessed with women, *religiosus* is a religious fanatic -- is Nigidius's key observation, and it is entirely correct.\n\nThe theological implication is quietly significant. To a Roman reader, the idea that *religiosus* was a pejorative -- that being excessively pious was a *vitium*, a fault, not a virtue -- was genuinely counterintuitive in one direction (the word had already begun shifting toward its later neutral or positive meaning, as Gellius himself notes) and utterly Roman in another. Roman religion was civic and practical; excess in either direction was suspect. Nigidius, the Pythagorean mystic who took the occult more seriously than any respectable Roman was supposed to, was here arguing on behalf of religious moderation. The irony is its own small portrait.\n\nFor the student encountering this passage, the main work is syntactic: the *ut ego arbitror* parenthetical, the *versum refert* construction with the verse itself, and the indirect statement in Nigidius's quoted words (*significat copiam... quocirca... eaque res vitio assignabatur*). The vocabulary is classical throughout, with the single archaic touch of *religens* itself -- which, as Nigidius would have noted with satisfaction, is exactly the point.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: NC (Not Comparable)**\n\nNothing by Nigidius survives in complete form. His *Commentarii grammatici* existed in late antiquity and was mined by grammarians until it eventually disappeared; the fragments preserved in Gellius and Priscian are tantalizing but discontinuous. His other works are known only by title and reputation. Per-line difficulty of what survives in Gellius suggests a B range -- archaizing vocabulary, philosophical density, awareness of older Latin forms -- but there is simply not enough to rank. The NC rating is also appropriate for a more fundamental reason: Nigidius's most characteristic work was probably his religious and astrological writing, which combined Latin prose with Etruscan technical terminology and Pythagorean philosophical language in ways we cannot reconstruct at all. What we are rating, if we were to rate him, would be a shadow of a corner of the actual text.\n\n---\n\n# MARCUS TULLIUS CICERO (106--43 BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nMarcus Tullius Cicero was born on January 3, 106 BC in Arpinum -- a small hill town in Latium, also (as he enjoyed pointing out) the birthplace of Gaius Marius, the general who had saved Rome from the Cimbri. Whether Cicero enjoyed the comparison because it gave him a distinguished local precedent for ambition, or because it allowed him to associate himself with a genuine military hero in an era when he had none of his own, is a question his biographers have debated. The answer is probably: both.\n\nThe family was equestrian, respectable, not distinguished. No consuls in his ancestry, no famous generals, no one who had held power in Rome. Cicero would be the first, and he never forgot it, and he never let anyone else forget it either. He was what Romans called a *homo novus* -- a new man, someone who had reached the consulship on talent alone with no family name to open doors. He found this a genuine source of pride and an inexhaustible source of anxiety, and the two emotions never quite separated in him.\n\nHis cognomen \"Cicero\" came, according to Plutarch, from an ancestor who had a wart-like protrusion on the tip of his nose shaped like a chickpea (*cicer*). When Cicero entered public life, his friends advised him to change the name -- a chickpea was no kind of name for a Roman statesman. Cicero declined. He said he would make the name Cicero more famous than Scaurus or Catulus (two of the most distinguished names in Roman politics). He was right. This is the first piece of evidence that Marcus Tullius Cicero had an ego the size of the Capitoline hill.\n\nHe was educated in Rome alongside Titus Pomponius (later known as Atticus, his lifelong friend) under the poet Archias, whose citizenship Cicero would later defend in a famous oration. He studied rhetoric and philosophy under the best teachers available: Philo of Larissa, head of the Platonic Academy, who gave him his lifelong attachment to Academic skepticism; Apollonius Molon of Rhodes, the most famous rhetorical teacher of the age; and the great jurist Quintus Mucius Scaevola, for Roman law. Cicero was not just diligent -- he was compulsive. He studied the way some people eat: constantly, with no apparent sense of being full.\n\nHis first major case, *Pro Roscio Amerino* (80 BC), was an act of courage that easily could have been an act of suicide. A man named Sextus Roscius of Ameria was accused of murdering his own father -- the charge backed by Chrysogonus, a freedman of Sulla, who had his own reasons for wanting the conviction (he had purchased Roscius's estate during the proscriptions and needed the son out of the way). Defending anyone against a Sullan freedman while Sulla was still dictator was genuinely dangerous. Cicero was twenty-six years old and took the case anyway. He won. Shortly thereafter, perhaps for health reasons, certainly also for safety reasons, he left for Athens and Rhodes to study for two years with Apollonius Molon and others.\n\nHe served as quaestor in Sicily in 75 BC and was, by his own account, an outstanding administrator of the grain supply -- fair, energetic, admired. He was so pleased with his performance that he expected Rome to be talking about him when he returned. He arrived at Puteoli and met a Roman acquaintance, who asked him cheerfully where he had been. \"Sicily,\" said Cicero. The man seemed confused. \"Oh -- Sicily? No one in Rome mentioned it.\" Cicero recorded his own mortification, and Plutarch preserved it for us, because sometimes the gods are kind. The experience humbled him. For a while.\n\nSicily came back productively four years later. In 70 BC, the Sicilians hired Cicero to prosecute Gaius Verres, the former governor who had spent three years stripping the island of everything portable and several things that weren't. Verres's defense counsel was Hortensius, the greatest orator in Rome. Cicero was supposed to lose. Instead, he prepared his evidence so thoroughly and presented it with such efficiency that Verres went into voluntary exile before Hortensius even had a chance to speak at the first session. Cicero published the five books of the *Actio Secunda* anyway, even though they were never delivered. There was already a visible pattern in his relationship with posterity.\n\nHe also discovered, during his time as quaestor in Sicily, the tomb of Archimedes in Syracuse. The Syracusans themselves had forgotten where it was. Cicero identified it by the sphere and cylinder carved on the stone -- the symbols of the mathematician's greatest theorem. The tomb was completely overgrown. He cleared it, read the inscription, and was very pleased with himself. He recounted this story in his *Tusculanae Disputationes* (V.64) with the air of a man who has found something the locals should have taken better care of.\n\nThe consulship of 63 BC was the peak of his public life, and he knew it, and he wrote about it for the next twenty years in a way that eventually began to irritate even his friends. When the Catilinarian conspiracy was revealed -- Lucius Sergius Catilina plotting to overthrow the Republic with an armed insurrection -- Cicero had Catiline denounced in the Senate four times, in four of the most famous speeches ever delivered in Latin. He executed the arrested conspirators without trial, which was legally questionable (they were Roman citizens) but politically popular at the time. He was hailed as *pater patriae*, father of the fatherland, in the Senate. He was forty-three years old and this was the greatest moment of his life.\n\nTo mark the occasion, he wrote a poem. He wrote a poem about his own consulship, which included the line *O fortunatam natam me consule Romam!* -- \"O fortunate Rome, born in my consulship!\" -- a verse so bad that it was mocked even in antiquity. It rhymes *natam* with *Romam*, which is the Latin equivalent of rhyming \"nation\" with \"station\" in a poem you have composed about yourself. The man who would write some of the finest Latin prose ever committed to papyrus also wrote some of the worst Latin verse, and chose to write it about himself. He apparently composed an epic poem about his own life as well, now lost. Its loss is one of antiquity's few mercies.\n\nThe political downfall came through a scandal that was not his fault but for which he paid anyway. In December 62 BC, the tribune Publius Clodius Pulcher was caught infiltrating the women-only festival of the Bona Dea, held at Caesar's house, disguised as a female lute-player. This was a religious sacrilege and a social scandal simultaneously. Clodius was put on trial and claimed to have been in Interamna at the time. Cicero testified that Clodius had dined with him in Rome just hours before the festival. The jury acquitted Clodius anyway: they had been bribed. Cicero was furious and made no secret of it. Clodius was furious and made even less secret of it.\n\nIn 58 BC, Clodius (now a tribune) passed a law targeting Cicero retroactively for the execution of the Catilinarians without trial. Cicero went into exile. His Palatine mansion was demolished and a temple to Liberty built on the site. He wrote letters from exile that were so painfully self-pitying that his friends eventually had to tell him to stop. He was recalled in 57 BC with public demonstrations of popular support that he described, with his usual restraint, as the greatest reception any Roman had ever received. His house was rebuilt at public expense.\n\nHe was now fifty years old and had survived Sulla's dictatorship, the Catilinarian crisis, and exile. The decade of the fifties was difficult: Caesar's rise, the First Triumvirate, the growing sense that the Republic was not going to survive. He wrote the *De Oratore*, which is about his own craft rather than about how to live, and he wrote philosophy: the *De Re Publica*, the *De Legibus*. He wrote orations. He wrote letters to Atticus that are among the most intimate and revealing documents to survive from the ancient world. He governed Cilicia (as proconsul, 51-50 BC) and did an honorable job; he wanted a triumph but never got one.\nUnderneath all of it lay a single idea, held with a consistency his enemies mistook for vanity: that Rome was held together not by written law but by the *mos maiorum*, the custom of the ancestors. This was an unwritten constitution, accumulated by practice rather than voted into existence, and it governed how a magistrate behaved, how the Senate was consulted, how a man treated his clients and his opponents. In the *De Re Publica* Cicero built his argument around a line of Ennius, *moribus antiquis res stat Romana virisque*, \"the Roman state stands on its ancient customs and its men,\" and then made the uncomfortable observation that Rome was running short of both. The difficulty was structural. The *mos maiorum* had been shaped by a city-state that governed itself, and it was now being asked to govern an empire, in a world of armies loyal to their generals rather than to the Senate, of provincial fortunes large enough to buy an election, and of street violence used as an ordinary political instrument. Cicero spent his career insisting that the old rules still applied to a game whose pieces had changed.\n\nThere is an obvious oddity in a man from Arpinum appointing himself the guardian of ancestral tradition, since he had no ancestors inside it: no consuls, no triumphs, no wax masks in the atrium. He knew it, and he had a model. Cato the Elder had also come from a country town, had also been a *novus homo*, and had made himself into the living definition of the ancestral virtue that the old families assumed they owned. Cicero admired him enough to list him in the *Pro Archia* among the Romans worth imitating, and later to hand him the leading role in the *De Senectute*, which is the literary equivalent of letting the *mos maiorum* speak in its own voice. The irony is the one that ended his career. In 63 BC, in defence of that tradition, he had Roman citizens executed without trial, for which the tradition offered no clear precedent, and five years later a tribune used the tradition's own protections to drive him out of Italy for doing it.\n\n\nCaesar's victory in the civil war left Cicero stranded: too implicated with Pompey to be welcome at Caesar's court, too prudent to resist openly. He retreated to philosophy again. Then, in 46 BC, he divorced Terentia -- his wife of thirty years -- under disputed circumstances (some ancient sources blame financial mismanagement during the exile, some blame his desire for a younger woman). He married Publilia almost immediately: a much younger woman, reportedly his former ward. His daughter Tullia died in February 45 BC. The blow was devastating. Cicero was inconsolable. He proposed building a temple to Tullia to deify her -- a project that his friends, carefully and persistently, talked him out of. When Publilia seemed insufficiently moved by Tullia's death, he divorced her too. He was fifty-nine years old, had been married twice and divorced twice in less than a year, and was channeling his grief into an enormous outpouring of philosophical writing. The period 46-44 BC produced the *De Finibus*, the *Tusculanae Disputationes*, the *De Natura Deorum*, the *De Amicitia*, the *De Senectute*, and several other works. If Cicero had not lost his daughter, Latin philosophy would be much poorer.\n\nThe obvious question about those two years is how anybody writes that much that fast, and the answer has a name. Marcus Tullius Tiro was Cicero's slave, freed in 53 BC, and he ran the whole literary machinery of the household: he took dictation, kept the archive, corrected the drafts, and is credited with devising the shorthand that made it possible. The *notae Tironianae* were signs standing for whole words and endings rather than for letters, which meant a writer could keep up with a speaking voice. Plutarch says that in December 63 BC, while the Senate was debating what to do with the Catilinarian conspirators, Cicero stationed clerks trained in such signs around the chamber to take down Cato's speech -- the earliest recorded use of shorthand at Rome, and the reason anyone knows what was actually said. Cicero worked by talking. He dictated, Tiro wrote, and the philosophical works of 45 and 44 BC were produced at a rate that only that arrangement makes plausible. Book XVI of the *Ad Familiares* is the file of letters to Tiro, and it contains the warmest things Cicero ever wrote to anybody.\n\nCaesar's assassination in 44 BC briefly reignited his political life. He had not been part of the conspiracy -- Brutus and Cassius knew he would be unreliable with a secret -- but he supported it enthusiastically afterward. He then delivered the fourteen Philippic orations against Mark Antony: the most sustained campaign of personal rhetorical destruction in Latin literature. The Second Philippic, never delivered but published as a pamphlet, is probably the most annihilating personal attack ever written in Latin prose. Antony reciprocated in the only way available to him: when the Second Triumvirate was formed in 43 BC, Cicero's name went on the proscription list.\n\nHe tried to flee by sea. The winds turned against him. He returned to his Formian villa and met the soldiers on December 7, 43 BC. His head and hands were cut off and displayed on the Rostra -- the speaker's platform from which he had addressed Rome for decades. Antony's wife Fulvia (formerly Clodius's wife, a small circle of revenge) reportedly drove a pin through his tongue. The story may not be true. It is the kind of story that sticks.\n\nTwenty-three years later, Cicero's son -- also named Marcus -- was consul. He announced the defeat of Antony and Cleopatra to the Senate. The man who had ordered his father's death was dead, and it was Cicero's son who said so. The gods, occasionally, have a sense of structure.\n\n## Main Works\n\nCicero wrote in four modes: orator, philosopher, teacher of rhetoric, and correspondent. He was excellent at all four, and the scope of his output is staggering. What follows is selective. The philosophical works and the rhetorical works are listed apart because they are different projects: the first ask what a good life and a good state are, the second ask how a Roman learns to speak, and only the second are about the craft Cicero actually practised for a living.\n\n**Orations** (more than 50 survive of perhaps 100 or more delivered)\n\n- *Verrine Orations* (70 BC): seven speeches against the corrupt Sicilian governor Gaius Verres. Only the first was delivered in court; the vast *Actio Secunda* in five books was published after Verres fled into exile. A monument of invective and evidence-marshaling. [Complete]\n- *In Catilinam I-IV* (63 BC): the four orations against Catiline. The most famous speeches in Latin literature. \"Quo usque tandem...\" [Complete]\n- *Pro Milone* (52 BC): defense of Titus Annius Milo for the killing of Clodius on the Appian Way. Milo was convicted anyway; the published version (significantly revised) is considered Cicero's finest forensic achievement. [Complete]\n- *Philippics I-XIV* (44-43 BC): against Mark Antony. The Second Philippic is the most ferocious personal attack in Latin literature. [Complete]\n\n**Philosophical Works** (a selection from a very large catalogue, almost all of it written in two frantic years)\n\n- *De Re Publica* (54-51 BC): on the ideal state. Badly damaged in transmission -- most of what we have was recovered in 1819 from a palimpsest, a manuscript scraped clean and written over with a commentary on the Psalms. Its sixth and final book, the *Somnium Scipionis*, survived by a completely different route, quoted whole inside Macrobius's commentary, and is usually read on its own.\n- *Paradoxa Stoicorum* (46 BC): six Stoic paradoxes (\"only the wise man is free\", \"only the good man is rich\") argued as rhetorical set-pieces. Short, and the easiest philosophical Latin he wrote.\n- *Tusculanae Disputationes* (45 BC): five dialogues on how to achieve happiness, written in the weeks after Tullia's death. Book I is on the fear of death, and it is not a detached performance.\n- *De Finibus Bonorum et Malorum* (45 BC): a comprehensive survey of Epicurean, Stoic, and Peripatetic ethics. Dense and demanding.\n- *De Natura Deorum* (45 BC): three books presenting Epicurean, Stoic, and Academic views on the gods, and refusing at the end to say which is right.\n- *De Divinatione* (44 BC): whether prophecy, omens and dreams mean anything. Cicero the augur argues, in his own voice, that they do not.\n- *De Fato* (44 BC): the sequel to *De Divinatione* and the last panel of the theological trilogy, on fate, free will and what is left up to us. Damaged at both ends. Its interlocutor is Aulus Hirtius, who has his own entry here.\n- *De Senectute* (44 BC): on old age. Warm, witty, and genuinely felt.\n- *De Amicitia* (44 BC): on friendship. Contains the famous definition of friendship as *consensio* of all divine and human things.\n- *De Officiis* (44 BC): on duty, written for his son and finished as Antony was destroying him. The most influential prose work of antiquity in later Europe.\n\n**Rhetorical Works** (the craft he actually practised, described from the inside)\n\n- *De Oratore* (55 BC): on the ideal orator, in three books, and the best thing he wrote in any genre. A conversation set in 91 BC among the great orators of his youth. The most sophisticated treatment of rhetoric in Latin.\n- *Brutus* (46 BC): a history of Roman oratory from its beginnings to Cicero himself, in the form of a dialogue. Our single most important source for the early Latin orators, most of whom survive nowhere else.\n- *Orator* (46 BC): the portrait of the perfect orator, addressed to Brutus. Contains Cicero's fullest account of prose rhythm, the thing his own sentences are built on.\n- *De Optimo Genere Oratorum* (46 BC): a short preface to a translation, and the origin of the argument about translating sense rather than words -- *non verbum pro verbo*.\n- *Topica* (44 BC): on where to find arguments. Written from memory on a sea voyage, for a lawyer who had asked.\n\n**Letters** (about 900 survive in four collections, most of them never meant for publication)\n\n- *Epistulae ad Atticum* (16 books): to his closest friend Titus Pomponius Atticus. The most personal and candid body of correspondence in all of Latin.\n- *Epistulae ad Familiares* (16 books): to various friends, associates, and political figures.\n- *Epistulae ad Quintum Fratrem* (3 books): to his brother Quintus.\n- *Epistulae ad Brutum* (2 books): to Marcus Brutus; the letters from the final months of Cicero's life.\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\n**The Periodic Sentence**\n\nCicero is the master of the *oratio perpetua*: sustained periodic style in which a sentence builds through successive subordinate clauses until the final main verb resolves the accumulated tension. Think of it as musical composition. The period opens a theme, adds complications, creates counterpoint through relative clauses and participial phrases, and eventually lands on a *clausula* -- a rhythmically patterned ending (*esse videatur*, *posse videri*, and their relatives) that was the audible signature of elevated Latin prose. Educated Romans could hear these endings the way musicians hear a perfect cadence.\n\nThis commitment to hypotaxis -- the architectural principle of subordination -- is what most defines Cicero against his contemporaries. Where Caesar coordinates (*venit, vidit, vicit*: three main verbs, three separate actions), Cicero subordinates. Causal clauses, temporal clauses, indirect statement, purpose clauses, result clauses all nest within each other, creating a hierarchy of information the reader must hold suspended until the main clause arrives and organizes everything.\n\nCicero also practices *amplificatio*: saying the same thing two, three, or four times in different words. *Synonymia* (accumulation of near-synonyms) and *anaphora* (repetition of the same word or phrase at the start of successive clauses) are the most visible signs of his rhetorical training, and they are everywhere in the orations.\n\n**The Difficulty Question**\n\nStudents approach Cicero with fear, and this is not entirely unreasonable. The sentences are long, the vocabulary is enormous, and his frequent use of indirect discourse and legal technicality can feel like a circular highway with no exits.\n\nBut here is the essential insight: Cicero's prose is one of the most *learnable* in all of Latin, once you understand how his sentences work. Translating Cicero is like assembling a puzzle where every piece is guaranteed to fit and fall into the correct position -- if you are slow and careful. The periodic structure, far from being arbitrary complexity, is a system. The subordinate clauses set up the main clause. The participial phrases attach to the nearest appropriate noun. The relative *qui* clause refers back to the nearest plausible antecedent. Patience and a solid command of Latin subordination are 80% of what you need. The remaining 20% is vocabulary.\n\nBy genre:\n\nThe **letters** -- especially the *Ad Atticum* -- are the most accessible: colloquial, often short-sentenced, full of Greek loan-words signaling informal register, written at conversational pace. Many of them are effectively text messages from antiquity. Tier C.\n\nThe **orations** are where most students encounter Cicero, and the Catilinarians and *Pro Caelio* are standard entry points for good reason. Hypotactic complexity is real but tractable: Cicero wants to persuade, which means he wants to be understood. Longer, denser speeches (*Verrine* set-pieces, *Second Philippic*) push higher. Overall: B to low A.\n\nThe **rhetorical works** sit between the two. They are dialogues, so the give and take keeps the sentences shorter than a treatise would, and the subject is one the reader is already inside: this is Cicero describing the job he did every day. The technical vocabulary is real but finite and worth learning, since it is the vocabulary these notes use. *De Oratore* is the most demanding of them, *Topica* the least. Overall: B to low A.\n\nThe **philosophical works** are a different register entirely. Cicero had to invent Latin philosophical vocabulary -- *qualitas*, *essentia*, *beatitudo*, *moralis*, *humanitas* are Ciceronian coinages or adaptations -- meaning that even an advanced reader will encounter lexicon that cannot be found in the usual dictionaries. The argumentation requires tracking multiple Greek philosophical schools simultaneously, and the allusion density to Greek originals is high and increasing. *De Finibus* and *Tusculanae Disputationes* are genuinely difficult. Overall: A.\n\n## Latin Excerpts\n\n### Excerpt 1: Oration -- *In Catilinam* I.1-2\n\n**Source:** Cicero, *Oratio in L. Catilinam Prima*, sections [1]-[2], from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/cicero/cat1.shtml)\n\n**Context:** November 8, 63 BC. Cicero as consul has uncovered the details of the Catilinarian conspiracy. He summons Catiline to appear before the Senate in the Temple of Jupiter Stator -- and Catiline actually shows up. Cicero addresses the conspirator to his face, in front of the entire Senate.\n\n**Latin text**\n\n> [1] Quo usque tandem abutere, Catilina, patientia nostra? quam diu etiam furor iste tuus nos eludet? quem ad finem sese effrenata iactabit audacia? Nihilne te nocturnum praesidium Palati, nihil urbis vigiliae, nihil timor populi, nihil concursus bonorum omnium, nihil hic munitissimus habendi senatus locus, nihil horum ora voltusque moverunt? Patere tua consilia non sentis, constrictam iam horum omnium scientia teneri coniurationem tuam non vides? Quid proxima, quid superiore nocte egeris, ubi fueris, quos convocaveris, quid consilii ceperis, quem nostrum ignorare arbitraris? [2] O tempora, o mores! Senatus haec intellegit. Consul videt; hic tamen vivit. Vivit? immo vero etiam in senatum venit, fit publici consilii particeps, notat et designat oculis ad caedem unum quemque nostrum. Nos autem fortes viri satis facere rei publicae videmur, si istius furorem ac tela vitemus. Ad mortem te, Catilina, duci iussu consulis iam pridem oportebat, in te conferri pestem, quam tu in nos machinaris.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> [1] How long, Catilina, will you go on abusing our patience? How much longer will your madness mock us? Where will your unbridled arrogance push itself? Has nothing moved you -- not the nightly guard on the Palatine, not the city watches, not the people's fear, not the assembly of all good citizens, not this most heavily fortified place of the Senate's meeting, not the faces and expressions of these men? Do you not feel that your plans have been exposed, do you not see that your conspiracy is already gripped and held fast by the knowledge of everyone here? What you did last night and the night before, where you were, who you summoned, what decision you took: is there anyone among us you think does not know? [2] O what times, O what customs! The Senate knows this. The consul sees it; and yet this man lives. Lives? He does more -- he comes to the Senate, he takes part in public deliberations, he marks out each one of us with his eyes for murder. We, brave men, seem to do enough for the Republic if we dodge his rage and his weapons. Long since, Catilina, you should have been led to death by order of the consul; the ruin you are plotting against us should by now have fallen on you.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThe three opening words -- *Quo usque tandem* -- are among the most famous in Latin prose. *Quo usque* means \"how far\" or \"how long\"; *tandem*, usually \"at last\" or \"finally,\" here functions as an intensifier implying that the patience of Rome has been stretched past any reasonable limit. The question is not really a question; it is an accusation in interrogative clothing.\n\nThe sentence builds through triple anaphora: *quo usque... quam diu... quem ad finem* -- three parallel questions, each synonymous, each escalating in emotional temperature. This is Cicero's characteristic technique of *tricolon crescens*, saying the same thing three ways so that the meaning approaches from multiple angles and lands harder.\n\nWhat follows is the rhetorical climax of the opening: the seven *nihil* clauses. *Nihilne te... nihil... nihil... nihil... nihil... nihil... nihil moverunt?* Seven things that should have moved Catiline, listed in strict parallel series, each a further witness to the conspirator's brazenness. No pause, no explanatory gloss, seven consecutive items hammered home. Then: *moverunt?* -- the single verb and the question mark. Cicero's deadpan shrug.\n\n*O tempora, o mores!* -- after the controlled anaphora of the opening, this sudden shift to a two-part exclamation is a shock of simplicity. Two brief exclamations, both vocative, addressing not Catiline but the age itself. In context, the change of register -- from hammering accumulation to a two-item outcry -- is a genuine rhetorical stroke. The sentence has been rubbed smooth by repetition, but it lands differently when you have just waded through the seven *nihil* clauses before it.\n\nFor vocabulary: *abutere* (from *abutor*, to misuse, abuse; a deponent taking the ablative: \"you abuse our patience\"); *effrenata* (unbridled, unrestrained); *praesidium* (guard, garrison, protection); *voltus* (archaic variant of *vultus*, face, expression); *constrictam... teneri* (held gripped, held fast; *teneri* is passive infinitive of *teneo*); *coniurationem* (conspiracy, from *coniuro*, to swear together); *particeps* (participant, partner; takes the genitive: *publici consilii*); *pestem* (plague, ruin, destruction -- loaded political vocabulary).\n\nThe sentence lengths themselves teach the strategy: the three opening questions are each a few words; the seven *nihil* clauses are medium length and strictly parallel; the *Patere... non vides* section switches to statements; then *O tempora, o mores!* is tiny; then the periodic climax. Length and structure are rhetoric.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 2: Letter -- *Ad Atticum* I.16 (excerpt)\n\n**Source:** Cicero, *Epistulae ad Atticum* I.16, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/cicero/att1.shtml)\n\n**Context:** June/July 61 BC. Clodius has just been acquitted of the charge of sacrilege -- entering the Bona Dea festival disguised as a woman -- despite Cicero having testified against his alibi. The acquittal was obtained by bribery. Cicero writes to Atticus, furious and sardonically amused at the same time. The passage is one of the sharpest accounts of judicial corruption in ancient literature, and also one of the funniest.\n\n**Latin text**\n\n> Biduo per unum servum et eum ex ludo gladiatorio confecit totum negotium; arcessivit ad se, promisit, intercessit, dedit. Iam vero (o di boni, rem perditam!) etiam noctes certarum mulierum atque adulescentulorum nobilium introductiones non nullis iudicibus pro mercedis cumulo fuerunt. Ita summo discessu bonorum, pleno foro servorum, XXV iudices ita fortes tamen fuerunt, ut summo proposito periculo vel perire maluerint quam perdere omnia. XXXI fuerunt, quos fames magis quam fama commoverit. Quorum Catulus cum vidisset quendam, \"Quid vos,\" inquit, \"praesidium a nobis postulabatis? an, ne nummi vobis eriperentur, timebatis?\"\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> In two days, through a single slave -- and one from a gladiatorial school, at that -- he wrapped up the whole business: summoned them to him, made promises, interceded, paid out. And what is more -- O good gods, what a wretched affair! -- even evenings spent with certain women and with noble young men were laid on for some of the jurors as a bonus on top of their fee. And so, with every decent man gone and the Forum full of slaves, twenty-five jurors were nonetheless brave enough to prefer, at the gravest personal risk, to die rather than sell everything out. Thirty-one were moved more by hunger than by reputation. When Catulus caught sight of one of them afterward, he said: \"Why did you ask us for a guard? Were you afraid someone would steal your money?\"\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThe contrast with the oration excerpt is almost violent in its completeness. This is Cicero writing to his closest friend, at speed, with no performance in mind. The sentences are short and punchy. The word order is colloquial. The parenthetical interjection -- *o di boni, rem perditam!* -- is not rhetoric; it is a private cry. \"O good gods, a ruined affair!\" There is no periodic structure, no anaphora, no clausula. This is a man in his study telling his friend what happened, using the vocabulary of controlled outrage.\n\n*Biduo per unum servum et eum ex ludo gladiatorio*: the compression is itself the indignation. In two days. Through a single slave. A slave from a *ludus gladiatorius* -- a gladiatorial training school. The moral quality of the agent is encoded in the noun: this is who you use when you need to buy a jury. *Confecit totum negotium*: wrapped up the whole business. The bureaucratic phrase (*negotium conficere*) applied to judicial corruption is Ciceronian irony at its most understated.\n\nThe vote count -- XXV and XXXI -- is exact and sour. Twenty-five honest jurors, thirty-one corrupt ones. *Fames magis quam fama commoverit*: hunger moved them more than reputation. The alliterative pair (*fames/fama*) is both phonetically precise and morally devastating. The word *fama* is almost self-satirizing here from a man famously obsessed with his own reputation: these thirty-one judges simply did not care about theirs.\n\nCatulus's parting remark -- \"Were you afraid someone would steal your money?\" -- is the kind of line you can only write in a private letter when you are still shaking with fury. It is the exact rejoinder to a jury that had requested a guard: the guard was presumably to protect their bribe money, since their judicial integrity was evidently not the thing at risk. Two days, one gladiatorial-school slave, and the justice system of the Roman Republic was for sale.\n\nGrammar notes: *arcessivit* (from *arcesso*, to summon; perfect indicative); *pro mercedis cumulo* (as a supplement to the fee -- *cumulus* literally means a heap or addition piled on top); *summo discessu bonorum* (ablative absolute: with the complete departure of good men); *vel perire maluerint quam perdere omnia* (they preferred to die rather than to lose everything -- *malo + infinitive* construction); *quos fames... commoverit* (relative clause with perfect subjunctive, characterizing the thirty-one: people whom hunger moved more than reputation).\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 3: Philosophical Work -- *De Amicitia* 20\n\n**Source:** Cicero, *Laelius de Amicitia* [20], from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/cicero/amic.shtml)\n\n**Context:** Written in 44 BC, the final year of Cicero's life. The dialogue is presented as Laelius's reflections on friendship, set after the death of Scipio Aemilianus. This passage contains the most famous definition of friendship in Western literature. It is worth noting that Cicero wrote it at sixty-two years old, recently widowed of his daughter, twice divorced in a year, and watching the Republic disintegrate. Under the circumstances, what he says about friendship is not just philosophy.\n\n**Latin text**\n\n> Est enim amicitia nihil aliud nisi omnium divinarum humanarumque rerum cum benevolentia et caritate consensio; qua quidem haud scio an excepta sapientia nihil melius homini sit a dis immortalibus datum.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> Friendship is nothing other than agreement on all divine and human things, bound together by goodwill and affection; and, with wisdom perhaps excepted, I am not sure whether anything better has been given to mankind by the immortal gods.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nOne sentence. Thirty-four Latin words. Cicero needed exactly one sentence, and this is what he did with it.\n\n*Est enim amicitia nihil aliud nisi...* is the classical definition formula: friendship is nothing other than X. What follows the *nisi* is one of the most carefully balanced formulations in Cicero's philosophical prose.\n\n*Omnium divinarum humanarumque rerum*: of all divine and human things. The chiastic double genitive (*divinarum humanarumque*) gestures at totality -- everything above and below, everything in the cosmos and in daily life. *Cum benevolentia et caritate consensio*: agreement, or harmony, reached through goodwill (*benevolentia*) and deep affection (*caritas*). The word *consensio* deserves close attention: it is not merely \"agreement\" in the propositional sense, but a deeper resonance -- a co-feeling, a harmony of minds responding to the same things.\n\nThe second clause performs the Ciceronian Academic hedge: *haud scio an* (I do not know whether -- a formula that typically introduces a confident claim in polite disguise) *excepta sapientia* (with wisdom perhaps excepted -- an ablative absolute doing the quiet work of qualification) *nihil melius homini sit a dis immortalibus datum*. Nothing better has been given to mankind by the immortal gods. The effect places friendship just below wisdom in the entire catalogue of divine gifts: second in the universe, with a caveat that even first place is not quite certain.\n\nThe difficulty of this sentence lies not in its length but in its compression: thirty-four words carry a complete ethical argument. Students accustomed to long periodic sentences sometimes find the compressed philosophical aphorism harder, because the syntactic machinery is less visible. The work here is: recognizing *nisi* as introducing an exception after *nihil aliud*; understanding *consensio* as the predicate noun completing *amicitia est*; reading *cum benevolentia et caritate* as the ablative of accompaniment defining how the *consensio* holds; and then unpacking the relative clause (*qua*) that follows the definition.\n\nVocabulary: *nisi* here = \"other than,\" after *nihil aliud*; *consensio* (harmony, resonance, agreement of souls -- stronger than *consensus*); *benevolentia* (goodwill, warm stable inclination toward another's good); *caritas* (deep affection, regard -- not romantic love but warm, stable regard); *haud scio an* (I am inclined to think, I suspect -- a softening formula preceding what is actually a confident claim); *excepta sapientia* (ablative absolute: with wisdom excepted); *a dis immortalibus datum* (given by the immortal gods).\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nCicero was dead before he had any idea what he had made. His letters, published after his execution by his freedman Tiro, were the first private correspondence ever presented as literature in the Western tradition. They created a genre: the epistle as self-revelation, the letter as the truest version of the person who wrote it. And in creating it, they preserved Cicero whole -- not the polished orator of the Catilinarians, but the frightened exile, the grieving father, the man who divorced two wives in a year, the writer who could not stop talking about his consulship, the politician who was vain, terrified, brilliant, petty, courageous, and calculating sometimes in the same letter on the same day.\n\nTiro's shorthand outlasted everything else that came out of that household. The *notae* were expanded by later hands -- Isidore of Seville reports that the collection eventually reached some five thousand signs -- and they became the working script first of the imperial chancery and then of the medieval scriptorium, where Merovingian and Carolingian scribes used them for notes, glosses and subscriptions; the surviving lexica of them run to thousands of entries. They faded after the eleventh century, by which time scribes who could no longer read them had started to suspect they were a cipher, or magic. Two fossils are still in daily use. One is the word *notarius*, which originally meant not a man of law but a shorthand writer, a man who wrote *notae*, and which drifted across the Middle Ages into the modern notary. The other is a single sign: the Tironian *et*, written as ⁊, which is still printed on Irish road signs in place of *agus*, \"and\". Modern shorthand -- Bright in 1588, Willis in 1602, then Pitman in 1837 and Gregg in 1888 -- was invented afresh rather than inherited, but its authors knew exactly whose precedent they were claiming. Tiro is the reason the idea of writing at the speed of a voice was thinkable in the first place.\n\nThe philosophical works performed a different miracle: they became the primary channel through which Greek philosophy entered the Latin-speaking world. The vocabulary problem was severe -- Greek had a rich philosophical language; Latin had almost none, because the Romans had not traditionally done Greek-style abstract philosophy. Cicero invented the missing words. *Qualitas* (quality), *quantitas* (quantity), *essentia* (essence), *moralis* (moral), *humanitas* (a characteristically Ciceronian concept: the cultivation of our human qualities through education and culture), *beatitudo* (happiness, blessedness) -- all Ciceronian coinages or adaptations. The vocabulary of Western philosophy and ethics, in Latin and in all the languages derived from it, is substantially Ciceronian. When Thomas Aquinas writes about virtue, when the founders of the American Republic write about *res publica*, they are using Cicero's language whether they know it or not.\n\nThen there is Petrarch. In 1345, the humanist poet Francesco Petrarch was searching the cathedral library in Verona when he found a manuscript of Cicero's *Epistulae ad Atticum*. He had known the orations and some of the philosophical works. He had not known the letters. He read them and found a Cicero he had not expected: anxious, emotional, obsessively engaged with day-to-day politics, deeply human. He wrote Cicero a letter in response -- in Latin, addressed to the dead man -- both praising his genius and gently reproving him for having sacrificed philosophical retirement to political entanglement. Petrarch was not just reading a dead author; he was having a conversation with him.\n\nThis act is often taken as one of the founding moments of Renaissance humanism. What Petrarch discovered in the *Ad Atticum* was that the past was not a museum; it was a conversation interrupted by death. Cicero had written those letters to a specific friend about specific events, and they could still be read, still argued with, still felt. The discovery energized Renaissance scholarship and Latin writing for the next century and a half. Erasmus wrote a dialogue mocking the most extreme Ciceronians (*Ciceronianus*, 1528) for refusing to use any word not found in Cicero. Even the mockery is a testament to how central Cicero had become.\n\nEven today, the standard school trajectory in classical Latin passes through Caesar and Cicero in sequence. Caesar teaches the learner to read Latin that is radically clear. Cicero teaches the learner what Latin can do when someone is trying to move an audience, define a concept, or tell a friend what happened. In the most literal sense, Cicero is still the author who tells most readers what Latin is for.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: A (Very Difficult) -- Low End**\n\nThe placement reflects the aggregate of all Cicero wrote, with the philosophical works pulling the rating upward from where the orations alone would place him.\n\nThe **letters** are C tier: colloquial, often short-sentenced, immediate, and accessible to an intermediate reader. The *Ad Atticum* is the most candid and in many ways the most human text in the entire Latin corpus, and also one of the easier ones.\n\nThe **orations** sit in the B to low A range. Hypotactic complexity is real but tractable: Cicero wants to be understood, which means the structure, however elaborate, always serves comprehension rather than defeating it. The major Catilinarians and *Pro Caelio* are manageable entry points. Longer, denser speeches push higher.\n\nThe **philosophical works** justify the A rating decisively. Cicero's coined philosophical vocabulary is non-standard and cannot be easily looked up. The argumentation requires tracking multiple philosophical schools simultaneously. Allusion density to Greek originals is high. *De Finibus* and *Tusculanae Disputationes* are A-tier texts by any measure.\n\nThe \"low A\" modifier matters: Cicero, even at his most demanding, is never gratuitously difficult. He is always trying to reach the reader, always signposting his structure, always placing his clausulae to signal where the sentence is going. The puzzle always has the right number of pieces. The work is learning to see them.\n\n---\n\n# GAIUS JULIUS CAESAR (100--44 BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nBorn 100 BC in Rome to a patrician family of the gens Julia, which traced its lineage back to Aeneas and, through him, to the goddess Venus herself. Caesar was not born a god, but he spent his career making a reasonable case for the upgrade.\n\nHis family was aristocratic but not particularly wealthy or powerful. His aunt Julia was married to Gaius Marius, the great populist general and six-time consul; his father was a praetor who died suddenly when Caesar was sixteen. These connections -- to the popular faction, to the reform tradition -- shaped Caesar's politics for life.\n\n**Pirates, ransoms, and revenge:** Around 75 BC, on his way to study rhetoric in Rhodes, Caesar was captured by Cilician pirates. They demanded a ransom of twenty talents. Caesar laughed at them. He told them they had dramatically underestimated his worth and insisted they demand fifty. While waiting to be ransomed, he mingled with his captors like a friend, recited his own poetry to them (complaining when they did not applaud), and told them, cheerfully, that once he was free he would come back and have them all crucified. The pirates thought he was joking. He was not. He returned with a fleet, captured them, and had them crucified -- though, as Plutarch notes, he did first cut their throats, \"out of consideration for them,\" since he had grown rather fond of them. This story is not allegory. This is just what happened.\n\n**The Nicomedes problem:** In his youth Caesar served in Asia and spent considerable time at the court of Nicomedes IV of Bithynia. Rumors circulated immediately and never entirely died: his enemies whispered for decades that there had been something between them. His soldiers, who adored him but maintained a cheerful tradition of insulting their general, sang during his Gallic triumph: *Gallias Caesar subegit, Nicomedes Caesarem* (\"Caesar conquered the Gauls, Nicomedes conquered Caesar\"). Caesar apparently took this with good humor, as a man who had successfully conquered Gaul can afford to do.\n\n**Baldness:** Caesar was deeply self-conscious about his receding hairline, which is extremely relatable. He reportedly combed his thin hair forward to cover it and was so grateful for the privilege of wearing the laurel wreath at all times -- granted as an honor -- that he wore it everywhere, on every possible occasion. Ancient sources are united on one point: he would have preferred the wreath.\n\n**Climbing the ladder:** Caesar's political career followed the standard Roman *cursus honorum*, though with more borrowing than usual. He was consistently in debt -- his campaigns to win popular support were expensive -- but his ability to attract financial backers, notably Crassus, compensated for personal finances that were always spectacular in their negative numbers. As aedile he staged games of such extravagance that Plutarch says the Roman people considered giving him the consulship on the spot, which would have saved everyone a great deal of time.\n\n**\"Caesar's wife must be above suspicion\":** In 62 BC, during the rites of the Bona Dea festival -- a ceremony exclusively for women, from which all men were excluded -- a young nobleman named Clodius Pulcher was discovered inside the house where the ceremony was being held, reportedly disguised as a woman. The house belonged to Caesar. Clodius was allegedly there to see Caesar's wife Pompeia. Caesar himself was not present. Nevertheless, he divorced Pompeia immediately. When asked why, since there was no proof of wrongdoing, he gave the answer that has survived ever since: \"Caesar's wife must be above suspicion.\" Whether this was genuine high-mindedness or convenient political cover has been debated ever since. Cicero, who testified against Clodius at his trial, sided firmly with the second interpretation.\n\n**The First Triumvirate:** Around 60 BC, Caesar formed the political alliance known today as the First Triumvirate with Pompey and Crassus. It was not a formal institution -- Rome had no such thing -- but a private arrangement of mutual support among the three most powerful men in Rome. It got Caesar his consulship in 59 BC and, from there, the proconsular command in Gaul that would make him the most famous man in Western history.\n\n**Nine years in Gaul:** Caesar's Gallic campaigns (58-50 BC) are the subject of the *Bellum Gallicum*. In brief: he conquered most of what is now France, Belgium, and parts of Switzerland and Germany, made two expeditions to Britain, crossed the Rhine twice to intimidate the Germans, and suppressed a massive Gallic revolt led by Vercingetorix in 52 BC. The campaigns resulted in roughly one million dead and one million enslaved Gauls, by ancient estimates. They also produced an incomparable military reputation, enormous wealth, and the undying loyalty of a professional army that had spent nine years under his command. Pompey, watching from Rome, grew increasingly nervous.\n\n**The Rubicon:** In 49 BC, the Senate ordered Caesar to disband his army before returning to Rome. The tribunes who tried to veto the motion were physically expelled from the Senate. Caesar's response was not a written complaint. On the night of January 10-11, 49 BC, he led the XIII Legion across the Rubicon, the small river that marked the boundary between Roman Italy and his province. To cross armed was to commit an act of war against the state. The moment was legally simple and politically incandescent. What Caesar actually said at the crossing is disputed; the most famous version -- *iacta alea esto*, \"let the die be cast,\" a line from the Greek comedian Menander -- comes from Suetonius. What all sources agree on is that he crossed.\n\n**Clemency and \"Veni, vidi, vici\":** The civil war against Pompey (49-48 BC) saw Caesar consistently pursue a policy of *clementia* -- mercy -- toward defeated enemies, releasing Pompeian officers rather than executing them. It was good propaganda and also, apparently, sincere. Pompey fled to Greece, then to Egypt, where he was assassinated by agents of Ptolemy XIII before Caesar arrived. Caesar, by all accounts, was genuinely distressed. In 47 BC, after defeating Pharnaces II of Pontus at the battle of Zela -- a campaign so quick it barely qualified as a campaign -- Caesar sent Rome the three-word dispatch that ranks among history's most economical communiques: *veni, vidi, vici*. I came, I saw, I conquered. Each word is a perfect first-person singular past tense verb. The war had lasted five days. The dispatch required three words.\n\n**Not an emperor -- this needs to be said clearly:** Caesar was not an emperor. The Roman Empire did not yet exist; it was created by Augustus after Caesar's death. Caesar's constitutional position was that of *dictator* -- an extraordinary magistracy with sweeping powers, legally recognized but always understood to be temporary. In February 44 BC, just weeks before his assassination, Caesar was appointed *dictator perpetuus*: dictator for life. This was the crossing of another line. The dictatorship had always been a magistracy with a time limit; a permanent dictatorship was kingship by another name. It was this appointment, more than anything else, that hardened the conspiracy against him.\n\nThe distinction matters. \"Emperor\" is the title of the *princeps*, a position created by Augustus after the civil wars, carefully designed to look like a restored republic. Caesar gave his name to the whole enterprise -- *Kaiser*, *Czar*, and \"Caesar\" in a dozen other languages all descend from him -- but he died before the thing he was building had a name.\n\n**The Ides of March and his will:** On March 15, 44 BC, Caesar was stabbed twenty-three times in the Theatre of Pompey by senators calling themselves *Liberatores*. Medical examination established that only one wound was fatal. Whether he said anything as he fell is uncertain; \"Et tu, Brute?\" is Shakespeare's, not history's.\n\nThree days later, his will was read publicly by Mark Antony. Two clauses shook Rome. First: Caesar left his private gardens on the Tiber (*Horti Caesaris*) to the Roman people as a public park, and bequeathed 75 denarii to every Roman citizen. Second: his great-nephew Gaius Octavius was posthumously adopted as his son and primary heir. Octavius would become Gaius Julius Caesar Octavianus and eventually, as Augustus, the first Roman emperor. Caesar's last political act was to designate his successor in a world that did not yet know it was going to need one.\n\n---\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n**The Corpus Caesarianum**\n\nCaesar's surviving works all fall under the umbrella of the *Corpus Caesarianum*, a collection of military memoirs covering his campaigns in Gaul and his civil war against Pompey, along with subsequent campaigns in Alexandria, Africa, and Spain written by his officers. Not all of these are by Caesar himself.\n\n***De Bello Gallico*** (\"On the Gallic War\") is Caesar's account of his campaigns in Gaul from 58 to 52 BC. It is the primary Latin text encountered by first- and second-year students almost everywhere Latin is taught. The work consists of **eight books**, but Caesar wrote only **the first seven**, covering the campaigns year by year. **Book VIII was written by Aulus Hirtius**, one of Caesar's officers, who completed the account after Caesar's death up to 50 BC. Hirtius says so openly in his preface. His style -- somewhat more ornate and slower -- is noticeably different from Caesar's; the seam is visible to a trained reader.\n\nOne thing textbooks often leave implicit must be said explicitly: the *Bellum Gallicum* is propaganda. Extremely well-crafted, strategically shaped propaganda. Caesar wrote it while the campaigns were still in progress, releasing books roughly year by year, and its purpose was political: to justify his actions in Gaul to a Roman audience, to maintain his name and military reputation before the public, and to counter his enemies' accusations that he was exceeding his mandate. Every campaign is presented as a response to a provocation or a defensive necessity. The Gauls are always the aggressors, or the troublemakers, or the treacherous. Caesar is always the reasonable party who offers fair terms before resorting to force. The third-person narration -- \"Caesar did this,\" \"Caesar ordered that\" -- is not humility. It is the prose equivalent of wearing a toga to your own trial: it signals objectivity, distance, the voice of a man calmly reporting what happened rather than an interested party justifying himself. Do not be fooled.\n\n***De Bello Civili*** (\"On the Civil War\") is Caesar's account of the civil war against Pompey, covering 49-48 BC in three books. It is almost certainly **unfinished** -- it breaks off in the middle of the Alexandrian campaign -- and was completed by others. The *Bellum Alexandrinum* is generally attributed to Hirtius; the *Bellum Africum* and *Bellum Hispaniense* are by unknown hands.\n\nThe BC is a different experience from the BG. Where the BG takes time to explain who the Gallic tribes are, what their social structure looks like, and what the geography of the Rhine means for military operations, the BC assumes you already know who Pompey is, what the tribunes of the plebs are, what the *Senatus Consultum Ultimum* means, and when it has been invoked before. Caesar is writing for Romans who lived through these events, not for posterity.\n\nThe propaganda is, if anything, more visible in the BC than in the BG, because the stakes are higher. In the BG Caesar justifies conquest. In the BC he justifies civil war. His line of defense is threefold: he tried every diplomatic channel and Pompey refused each time; the constitutional order was violated not by him but by the Pompeians, who illegally silenced the tribunes; and he is fighting not for personal power but for his *dignitas* and for the freedom of the Roman people from \"the faction of the few.\" Meanwhile, in Caesar's telling, Pompey consistently refuses to negotiate, consistently avoids pitched battle, and consistently runs away when pressed. Caesar presents himself as the responsible adult in a room full of panicking optimates. The verdict of history, such as it is, may vary.\n\n**Lost Works**\n\nCaesar wrote far more than survives. Among the attested losses:\n\n*Anticato*: a prose response to Cicero's laudation of Cato the Younger after his suicide in 46 BC. Caesar clearly felt that Cato -- who died refusing to accept his clemency -- deserved less of the last word than he was being given. Lost.\n\n*De Analogia*: a grammatical treatise in two books, dedicated to Cicero, arguing for principled and regular Latin usage. Caesar wrote it during a crossing of the Alps in 54 BC. Writing grammar textbooks while crossing the Alps is, again, very Caesar.\n\n*Iter*: a poem about his journey from Rome to Spain in 46 BC. Lost. Augustus apparently suppressed the *Oedipus* tragedy Caesar had written, reportedly saying that Caesar's genius was better contemplated in the twelve months of his dictatorship than in his poetry. Whether this was diplomacy or aesthetic judgment is unclear.\n\n---\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nCaesar's style is, at its core, a weapon. He developed the most radically transparent Latin prose in the entire tradition -- not because he could not do more, but because clarity was exactly what the political situation required.\n\nThe dominant technical feature of Caesarian prose is *brevitas*: compression, the refusal of ornament. Where Cicero builds a period across five subordinate clauses and lands on a resonant clausula, Caesar coordinates. *Veni, vidi, vici*: three main verbs, three completed actions, nothing between them. He uses parataxis far more than hypotaxis. The result reads like military dispatches because that is, in essence, what the *Bellum Gallicum* is. This is not accidental. Caesar wrote the lost *De Analogia* arguing for precise and regular Latin, and he practiced what he preached: *tamquam scopulum sic fugias inauditum atque insolens verbum* -- \"flee the unusual and foreign word as you would a reef.\"\n\nThe third-person narration deserves particular notice. Caesar never writes \"I.\" He writes \"Caesar.\" \"Caesar ordered,\" \"Caesar decided,\" \"Caesar advanced.\" The *commentarius* genre permitted this convention, and Caesar adopted it so completely that it seems inseparable from his work. The effect positions Caesar as an object of history rather than a subject of advocacy. He is not telling you what he did and why; he is reporting, in measured impersonal terms, what happened. The person who happens to be called Caesar happens to be the protagonist, but that is simply how events unfolded. The technique is so effective that generations of students have read the BG as a historical document and only later been told that it is political advocacy by the advocate himself.\n\n**De Bello Gallico** is the starting point for almost every Latin student, and the choice is well made. The Latin is principled in its simplicity. Sentences are structured logically, with clear subordination and minimal ambiguity. The vocabulary is military and administrative -- not wide, not rare. There are no lyric meters, no philosophical abstractions, no dense allusive texture. The main challenge for the beginning student is not Caesar's Latin but Latin itself: the ablative absolute, indirect statement, the gerundive, the sequence of tenses. Caesar uses all of these, but he uses them in clean, logical contexts, as if he designed the sentences to teach the constructions. He did not, of course; but the effect is the same.\n\n**De Bello Civili** is a step up, and the step is more significant than it might appear. The language itself is not dramatically harder -- same clarity, same compression, same measured pace. The difficulty is contextual. The BC was not finished and not revised to the same degree as the BG; there are passages that feel raw and hurried, as if Caesar meant to come back and polish later. More critically, it assumes a politically literate Roman audience that needs no explanation of the SCU, the tribunes' constitutional history, or the events of the Sullan proscriptions. For a modern reader, this demands heavy annotation. You can parse every word of BC I.7 and still not understand what Caesar is arguing unless you know what the *tribunicia intercessio* was and what happened to the Gracchi. The Latin is manageable; the political subtext is dense.\n\nThere is also the propaganda dimension to hold in mind, which changes the reading once you are aware of it. Caesar's careful framing, his consistent presentation of himself as the reasonable party, his portrait of Pompey as a man who talked big and ran -- these are not neutral features of the text. Reading the BC well means reading it against itself: noticing what Caesar does not explain, what he glides over quickly, what he frames with particular care. That is a more sophisticated exercise than it looks.\n\n---\n\n## Latin Excerpts\n\n### Excerpt 1: De Bello Gallico VI.13-14 -- The Druids\n\n**Source:** Caesar, *Commentarii de Bello Gallico*, Book VI, chapters 13-14, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/caesar/gall6.shtml)\n\n**Context:** Book VI contains one of Caesar's most famous ethnographic digressions -- an extended description of Gaulish and German society inserted between two years of military campaigning. Chapters 13-20 describe the social structure of Gaul: two privileged classes (druids and knights), their customs, their religion, their relationship to the common people. The digression is typical of the BG's strategy: by presenting Gaul as a coherent civilization with its own institutions, Caesar implicitly positions Rome as the superior civilization whose conquest of these lands is a natural and legitimate process. What appears to be anthropology is also, quietly, justification.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n[13] In omni Gallia eorum hominum, qui aliquo sunt numero atque honore, genera sunt duo. Nam plebes paene servorum habetur loco, quae nihil audet per se, nullo adhibetur consilio. Plerique, cum aut aere alieno aut magnitudine tributorum aut iniuria potentiorum premuntur, sese in servitutem dicant nobilibus: in hos eadem omnia sunt iura, quae dominis in servos. Sed de his duobus generibus alterum est druidum, alterum equitum. Illi rebus divinis intersunt, sacrificia publica ac privata procurant, religiones interpretantur: ad hos magnus adulescentium numerus disciplinae causa concurrit, magnoque hi sunt apud eos honore. Nam fere de omnibus controversiis publicis privatisque constituunt, et, si quod est admissum facinus, si caedes facta, si de hereditate, de finibus controversia est, idem decernunt, praemia poenasque constituunt; si qui aut privatus aut populus eorum decreto non stetit, sacrificiis interdicunt. Haec poena apud eos est gravissima. Quibus ita est interdictum, hi numero impiorum ac sceleratorum habentur, his omnes decedunt, aditum sermonemque defugiunt, ne quid ex contagione incommodi accipiant, neque his petentibus ius redditur neque honos ullus communicatur. His autem omnibus druidibus praeest unus, qui summam inter eos habet auctoritatem. Hoc mortuo aut si qui ex reliquis excellit dignitate succedit, aut, si sunt plures pares, suffragio druidum, nonnumquam etiam armis de principatu contendunt. Hi certo anni tempore in finibus Carnutum, quae regio totius Galliae media habetur, considunt in loco consecrato. Huc omnes undique, qui controversias habent, conveniunt eorumque decretis iudiciisque parent. Disciplina in Britannia reperta atque inde in Galliam translata esse existimatur, et nunc, qui diligentius eam rem cognoscere volunt, plerumque illo discendi causa proficiscuntur.\n\n[14] Druides a bello abesse consuerunt neque tributa una cum reliquis pendunt; militiae vacationem omniumque rerum habent immunitatem. Tantis excitati praemiis et sua sponte multi in disciplinam conveniunt et a parentibus propinquisque mittuntur. Magnum ibi numerum versuum ediscere dicuntur. Itaque annos nonnulli vicenos in disciplina permanent. Neque fas esse existimant ea litteris mandare, cum in reliquis fere rebus, publicis privatisque rationibus Graecis litteris utantur. Id mihi duabus de causis instituisse videntur, quod neque in vulgum disciplinam efferri velint neque eos, qui discunt, litteris confisos minus memoriae studere: quod fere plerisque accidit, ut praesidio litterarum diligentiam in perdiscendo ac memoriam remittant. In primis hoc volunt persuadere, non interire animas, sed ab aliis post mortem transire ad alios, atque hoc maxime ad virtutem excitari putant metu mortis neglecto. Multa praeterea de sideribus atque eorum motu, de mundi ac terrarum magnitudine, de rerum natura, de deorum immortalium vi ac potestate disputant et iuventuti tradunt.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nIn all of Gaul, among those men who are of some rank and prestige, there are two classes. For the common people are regarded as almost the equivalent of slaves: they dare nothing on their own authority and are called into no council. Most of them, pressed down by debt or the weight of taxes or the injustice of the more powerful, commit themselves into the service of the nobility; over these men, the nobles hold the same rights as masters over slaves. Of these two classes, one is that of the druids, the other that of the knights. The druids take part in divine matters, perform public and private sacrifices, and interpret religious observances; a great number of young men come to them for instruction, and they are held in great honor. For they decide on almost all public and private disputes: if any crime has been committed, if a murder has been done, if there is a dispute over an inheritance or a boundary, it is these same men who decide and set out rewards and punishments; if any private person or community does not abide by their decree, they forbid them from sacrifice. This is the heaviest punishment among them. Those on whom this has been imposed are counted among the number of the impious and the criminal; all men give way before them and shun their approach and conversation, lest they incur some harm from the contact; neither is justice granted to them when they seek it, nor is any honor shared with them. Over all these druids there presides a single man, who holds the highest authority among them. When he dies, whoever stands out above the rest in dignity succeeds him; if several are equal, the succession is settled by vote of the druids, though sometimes they contend for the leadership with arms. At a fixed time of year they assemble in a consecrated place in the territory of the Carnutes, which is held to be the center of all Gaul. From every direction all who have disputes come together and submit to their decrees and judgments. The system is believed to have been discovered in Britain and imported from there into Gaul; and today, those who wish to study it more carefully generally travel there for the purpose of learning.\n\nThe druids are accustomed to abstain from war and do not pay taxes alongside the rest; they enjoy exemption from military service and immunity from all obligations. Attracted by such great rewards, many come of their own accord to study under them, and others are sent by parents and kinsmen. They are said to learn a great number of verses there; accordingly some remain in their course of study for twenty years. They do not think it right to commit these things to writing, even though for virtually everything else -- for public and private business -- they use the Greek alphabet. They seem to have adopted this practice for two reasons: they do not wish the system to spread among the common people, and they do not want those who study to rely on writing and give less attention to memory, which is generally what happens when people have the resource of writing: they relax their effort and application in learning by heart. First and foremost, they wish to establish the conviction that souls do not perish but after death pass from one person to another, and they believe that by this teaching, with the fear of death set aside, valor is most powerfully encouraged. In addition, they hold many debates among their students about the stars and their motion, the size of the world and of our lands, the nature of things, and the power and might of the immortal gods.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThis passage is a showcase of Caesar's ethnographic method at its most characteristic. The prose is measured and confident, the sentences mostly short to moderate in length, the vocabulary precise and administrative. Notice how chapter 13 opens with a near-definition: *genera sunt duo* -- there are two classes. Caesar the taxonomist, organizing Gaulish society the way a Roman magistrate would organize a provincial census.\n\nThe social picture Caesar paints is notable for its Roman frame. He maps Gaulish society onto Roman categories: the *plebes* who are *paene servorum loco* (almost like slaves), the *nobiles* who hold *iura* over them analogous to *dominus* over *servus*, the druids who handle *res divinae* and serve functions equivalent to Roman pontiffs and judges combined. This is not pure anthropology: it is translation, which always involves interpretation. Understanding Gaul in Roman terms makes it legible to Caesar's Roman audience, and makes it comprehensible as something Rome can and should administer.\n\nThe druids passage is substantively fascinating. Caesar notes that they function as judges and arbiters, that their highest penalty is excommunication (being forbidden from sacrifice, cut off from all civic interaction), and that their leader is elected -- or in disputed cases, the leadership is settled by armed contest. The detail about the druidic school in Britain is one of the earliest attestations of any kind of British-Continental cultural exchange, treated here as a given.\n\nThe chapter 14 passage on the oral tradition is particularly rich. Caesar observes -- as a trained Roman rhetorician himself, with apparent admiration -- that the druids memorize an enormous number of verses and deliberately refuse to write any of them down. His explanation of the two reasons for this is analytical and sympathetic. He reports their doctrine of the transmigration of souls without comment. He lists their curriculum -- astronomy, cosmology, natural philosophy, theology -- topics that would not have surprised a Roman Stoic. The paragraph functions, quietly, as a reminder that the people Caesar is conquering are not simple barbarians: they have an intellectual class with a sophisticated educational program. His enemies must be worthy of conquest, not trivial.\n\nSyntactically, the passage is clean and instructional. The dominant constructions are the ablative absolute (*metu mortis neglecto*, \"with the fear of death set aside\") and the accusative and infinitive in indirect statement (*non interire animas, sed ab aliis post mortem transire ad alios*). These are exactly the constructions that appear in every first-year Latin grammar in the clearest possible contexts. Caesar did not design them as practice exercises; they are just how the constructions appear when someone writes correct, measured Latin prose.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 2: De Bello Civili I.7 -- Caesar Addresses the Thirteenth Legion\n\n**Source:** Caesar, *Commentarii de Bello Civili*, Book I, chapter 7, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/caesar/bc1.shtml)\n\n**Context:** January 49 BC. Caesar is at Ravenna, having just received word that his final diplomatic offers have been rejected, the tribunes who tried to veto the Senate's ultimatum have been physically expelled from Rome, and the *Senatus Consultum Ultimum* has been passed. He has the XIII Legion with him. Before crossing the Rubicon, Caesar addresses the troops. What follows is Caesar's account of Caesar's speech. Read that sentence again.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n[7] Quibus rebus cognitis Caesar apud milites contionatur. Omnium temporum iniurias inimicorum in se commemorat; a quibus deductum ac depravatum Pompeium queritur invidia atque obtrectatione laudis suae, cuius ipse honori et dignitati semper faverit adiutorque fuerit. Novum in re publica introductum exemplum queritur, ut tribunicia intercessio armis notaretur atque opprimeretur, quae superioribus annis armis esset restituta. Sullam nudata omnibus rebus tribunicia potestate tamen intercessionem liberam reliquisse. Pompeium, qui amissa restituisse videatur, dona etiam, quae ante habuerint, ademisse. Quotienscumque sit decretum, darent operam magistratus, ne quid res publica detrimenti caperet (qua voce et quo senatus consulto populus Romanus ad arma sit vocatus), factum in perniciosis legibus, in vi tribunicia, in secessione populi templis locisque editioribus occupatis: atque haec superioris aetatis exempla expiata Saturnini atque Gracchorum casibus docet; quarum rerum illo tempore nihil factum, ne cogitatum quidem. Nulla lex promulgata, non cum populo agi coeptum, nulla secessio facta. Hortatur, cuius imperatoris ductu VIIII annis rem publicam felicissime gesserint plurimaque proelia secunda fecerint, omnem Galliam Germaniamque pacaverint, ut eius existimationem dignitatemque ab inimicis defendant. Conclamant legionis XIII, quae aderat, milites -- hanc enim initio tumultus evocaverat, reliquae nondum convenerant -- sese paratos esse imperatoris sui tribunorumque plebis iniurias defendere.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nHaving learned of these developments, Caesar addressed his soldiers. He recalled the wrongs done to him by his enemies over all the years; he complained that Pompey had been led astray and corrupted by their envy and their constant efforts to undermine his reputation -- Pompey, whose own honor and position Caesar had always supported and promoted. He complained that a new precedent had been introduced in the state: that the tribunician veto should be silenced and crushed by armed force, the very right that had been restored by armed force in earlier years. He pointed out that Sulla, though he had stripped the tribunate of everything, had still left the right of veto free. Pompey, who had seemed to restore what was lost, had in fact taken away even the rights they previously held. Whenever the decree had been passed that magistrates should ensure the state suffer no harm -- the formula by which the Roman people had been summoned to arms by that voice and that senatorial resolution -- it had been used against dangerous legislation, against violence by the tribunes, against secessions of the people who had occupied the temples and the high ground; and he showed that examples of this kind from earlier generations had been atoned for by the fate of Saturninus and the Gracchi. Yet none of these things had happened in the present case -- not even been thought of. No law had been proposed, no action had been initiated with the people, no secession had occurred. He urged them -- in whose service, under his generalship over nine years, they had managed the affairs of the state with the greatest success, won very many battles, and brought all of Gaul and Germany to peace -- to defend his reputation and dignity from his enemies. The soldiers of the Thirteenth Legion, which was present -- for he had summoned it at the beginning of the crisis; the remaining legions had not yet assembled -- shouted out that they were ready to defend their general and the tribunes of the people from injustice.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nPut this passage next to BG VI.13 and the difference is immediately visible. The fundamental machinery is the same -- short to medium sentences, coordinated or lightly subordinated, third-person narration -- but the texture is denser, the political assumptions thicker on the ground, the compression more aggressive.\n\nBegin with the grammar. Most of the passage is in indirect discourse: not \"he said that Pompey...\" but the accusative and infinitive construction embedded throughout. *Deductum ac depravatum Pompeium* -- Pompey to have been led astray and corrupted. *Sullam intercessionem liberam reliquisse* -- Sulla to have left the veto free. *Pompeium dona ademisse* -- Pompey to have taken away the rights. Each is a compressed indirect statement, accusative subject plus perfect infinitive, running line after line. The reader must hold the reporting verb (*queritur*, *docet*, *hortatur*) in mind while parsing five or six consecutive accusations in the same construction. In the BG this structure appears briefly and in clear contexts. In the BC it is the dominant mode of much of the text.\n\nNow consider what Caesar does not explain. He refers to the *tribunicia intercessio* -- the tribunician veto -- as a constitutional right that was suppressed by Sulla and later restored. For this to make sense, a reader must know: who the tribunes of the plebs were and what their powers included; what Sulla's dictatorship did to the tribunate in the 80s BC; what later reforms restored; and that in January 49 BC, two tribunes -- Mark Antony and Quintus Cassius -- attempted to veto the SCU and were expelled from the Senate. Caesar assumes all of this without explanation. Similarly, the references to Saturninus and the Gracchi assume the reader knows these were populist politicians killed by senatorial violence, and that in each case the SCU was invoked to justify the killing. The argument Caesar is making -- that the SCU was designed for genuine emergencies, not for use against a general who had done nothing illegal -- is sophisticated and potentially persuasive, but you have to know the history to follow it.\n\nFinally, look at the propaganda structure. Caesar positions himself as (1) a victim of years of orchestrated enmity, (2) a defender of constitutional norms that his enemies have violated, and (3) a servant of his soldiers and the Roman people. Pompey is the villain -- led astray by envy, the man who took away rights he seemed to restore. The speech ends with the soldiers' response: they are ready to defend not just Caesar but \"the tribunes of the people\" -- meaning they are defending the Roman constitution, not just their general. The framing transforms a military revolt into an act of civic duty. Caesar wrote this. Keep that in mind every time you translate it.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nCaesar's legacy is so pervasive that it has become almost invisible: it is the furniture of Western civilization. His name became a title. *Caesar* gave us *Kaiser* in German, *Czar* in Russian, and equivalents in a dozen other languages, used for centuries to designate supreme rulers who had nothing to do with Rome. The month of July is named after him. The Cesarean section probably is not, despite the popular belief, but the name stuck anyway.\n\nIn the history of Latin prose, the *Bellum Gallicum* occupies an almost paradoxical position: it is simultaneously the most widely read Latin text in the world and among the most underread, in the sense of being most frequently read without being seriously analyzed. It is the training text, the warm-up before the real authors -- and yet it is a masterwork of deliberate literary craft, every apparent simplicity a calculated effect. The military historian Adrian Goldsworthy called it one of the most brilliantly sustained pieces of political advocacy ever written in any language. He was right. Most first-year students have no idea.\n\nThe BC had less immediate literary influence -- it was incomplete, unrevised, and Pompey's faction lost, which reduced the readership of texts that made Pompey look bad -- but it established a template for politically engaged historical writing that Sallust, Tacitus, and many others would develop. Caesar's self-exculpatory narrative structure, the way he mobilizes constitutional argument in service of a predetermined conclusion, became a model for how the winning side writes civil war history. This tradition runs from the BC through Sallust's *Bellum Catilinae*, through Tacitus's careful management of the Tiberian record, and arguably all the way to political memoirs in the present day.\n\nCaesar also mattered to the rhetorical tradition as an exemplar of the Atticist style -- spare, precise, unornamented. Cicero praised his Latin as pure to the point of elegance: *genus sermonis purus et inlustris*, a pure and brilliant manner of speaking (*Brutus* 261-262). Quintilian, writing a century later, said that if Caesar had devoted himself to oratory instead of military and political life, he would have been Cicero's only real rival.\n\nPerhaps the most interesting aspect of Caesar's afterlife is the question of what he was trying to build. He did not call himself king. He did not abolish the Senate. He moved toward something -- a permanent personal supremacy, an end to the cycle of aristocratic competition that had been tearing Rome apart -- but the Ides of March came before he could define what it was. Augustus, in the end, did it for him: created the Principate, one-man rule disguised as a restored republic, and gave it Caesar's name.\n\nOne final detail, recorded by Suetonius: on a visit to a temple in Spain in his early thirties, Caesar wept at a statue of Alexander the Great. He said that Alexander at his age had already conquered the world, while he himself had done nothing of note. He was already praetor at the time. He seems to have recovered.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: D (Good Exercise)**\n\nThe D rating reflects primarily the *Bellum Gallicum*, which is and remains the most pedagogically accessible extended prose text in classical Latin. Its vocabulary is controlled, its syntax clean, its sentences logically built. A student with two solid semesters of Latin can open BG Book I and move through it with reasonable confidence, dictionary in hand. This is not an accident; it is Caesar's Latin doing exactly what Caesar's Latin was designed to do.\n\nThe *Bellum Civili* is harder. The political background demands annotation, the indirect discourse is denser and more sustained, and the unfinished state of the text means there are passages that lack the smooth coherence of the BG. Taken alone, the BC would sit at a low C -- accessible with context, frustrating without it. But the aggregate of Caesar's output, in which the BG is both the larger and the more characteristic work, keeps the rating at D.\n\nThe caveat that always applies: reading Caesar *well* -- not just parsing him, but understanding what he is doing and why every stylistic choice is a political choice -- is harder than the tier suggests. A D for difficulty is not a D for complexity. The *Bellum Gallicum* rewards a second and third reading in a way that few \"easy\" texts do. The students who only read it to pass a test are missing the point, which is itself rather Caesarian.\n\n---\n\n# AULUS HIRTIUS (c. 90--43 BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nAulus Hirtius is the rare figure in Roman literary history who owes his place in the canon entirely to finishing someone else's work -- and whose own life ended before he could finish his own. He is the literary world's eternal second act.\n\nBorn around 90 BC, Hirtius came from an equestrian family and attached himself early to Caesar's staff. He served as a legate in Gaul from approximately 54 BC onward and became one of Caesar's most trusted officers and diplomatic intermediaries. He handled negotiations with foreign powers, managed correspondence, and apparently spent enough time around Caesar's table to develop a reputation as a committed epicure. Cicero, who knew him well and corresponded with him regularly, mentions in his letters that they had an informal exchange arrangement: Cicero would give Hirtius rhetoric lessons, Hirtius would teach Cicero how to eat well. The surviving evidence suggests Hirtius held up his end of the bargain considerably better.\n\nCicero's letters paint a vivid portrait of Hirtius in the years after Caesar's death: politically moderate, genuinely loyal to the Republican institutions, distressed by the chaos following the Ides of March. He was not an ideologue, and he was not a Caesarian hardliner -- he was an officer who had served his general with competence and was now trying to figure out, like most of Rome, what happened next.\n\n**The boss's work:** The anecdote that defines Hirtius's literary career is not really an anecdote at all -- it is the opening paragraph of his own preface to BG Book VIII. He writes that he was pestered (*coactus assiduis tuis vocibus*) by Cornelius Balbus, one of Caesar's secretaries and political fixers, into completing Caesar's unfinished commentaries. He had been refusing. He writes, with transparent anxiety, that he wishes readers could know how *unwillingly* he took on the task -- because that would make it easier for him to avoid the accusation of stupidity and arrogance for inserting himself between Caesar's texts. He admits that nothing written by anyone else can match the elegance of Caesar's commentaries. He notes that while others can judge how well and correctly Caesar wrote, he himself additionally knows how *fast* Caesar wrote.\n\nThis is a man who understood exactly what he was getting himself into and did it anyway because a powerful friend would not leave him alone. The result is Book VIII of the *Bellum Gallicum* and, almost certainly, the *Bellum Alexandrinum* -- a sequel covering the Alexandrian War of 47 BC that picks up exactly where Caesar's *Bellum Civili* breaks off. Whether Hirtius would have gone further, completing the historical record of Caesar's entire career, is a question that was never answered.\n\n**Consul and soldier:** In 43 BC, Hirtius was elected consul alongside Gaius Vibius Pansa -- a remarkable piece of Cicero-backed positioning designed to create two reliably anti-Antonian consuls who would defend the Republic against Mark Antony's encroachments. They succeeded militarily and failed biologically. Both consuls raised armies, marched north against Antony, and defeated him at the battles of Forum Gallorum and Mutina in April 43 BC. Then both consuls died -- Hirtius killed in the fighting at Mutina, Pansa dying of his wounds a few days later. Mark Antony escaped with his army. The young Octavian, technically a subordinate in these campaigns, emerged as the only surviving Roman commander with troops. Ancient gossip -- unfounded, probably -- suggested Octavian had engineered the outcome. What is not in doubt is that within the year Octavian had allied with Antony, and Cicero (who had been the intellectual architect of the anti-Antonian strategy) was dead.\n\nHirtius was fifty-something, a competent general, a reliable ally, a decent writer, and someone who liked good food. He deserved a more dignified exit than dying at Mutina to set up the Second Triumvirate.\n\n**A footnote on food:** Some later sources credit Hirtius with writing a cookbook. This is almost certainly apocryphal, but the image of Caesar's legate composing recipes between battles has a certain appeal. It would at least explain why the *Bellum Alexandrinum*'s prose, while military in content, occasionally pauses to describe things with rather more sensory precision than strictly necessary.\n\n---\n\n## Most Important Works\n\nHirtius's place in the *Corpus Caesarianum* rests on two attributions, one certain and one probable.\n\n***De Bello Gallico, Book VIII*** is Hirtius's definite contribution. He says so himself in the preface, naming himself, his correspondent Balbus, and his sources (including Caesar's unfinished notes). The book covers the campaigns of 51-50 BC -- the mopping-up operations after the great Gallic revolt of Vercingetorix had been crushed. It is a competent continuation, sometimes quite good, but it reads differently from Books I-VII, as even a moderately experienced reader will notice within a few pages.\n\n***De Bello Alexandrino*** (\"On the Alexandrian War\") picks up precisely where Caesar's *Bellum Civili* stops -- mid-sentence, essentially -- and covers the war Caesar fought in Alexandria (48-47 BC) after pursuing Pompey to Egypt, the subsequent campaign against Pharnaces II of Pontus (*veni vidi vici* territory), and operations in Illyricum and Spain. The attribution to Hirtius is widely accepted but not entirely settled. The Latin is more polished than the *Bellum Africum* and *Bellum Hispaniense*, which are generally thought to be by other, less skilled hands; it has thematic and stylistic continuities with BG VIII; and the only ancient reference that comes close to identifying an author points toward someone in Caesar's inner circle, which Hirtius certainly was. The majority scholarly opinion is that Hirtius wrote it, or at minimum supervised its compilation. For practical purposes, it is his.\n\nThe *Bellum Africum* and *Bellum Hispaniense* -- covering campaigns in Africa (47-46 BC) and Spain (45 BC) -- are NOT attributed to Hirtius by modern scholarship. They are rougher, more grammatically irregular texts, probably written by lower-ranking officers who were present for those campaigns. They are fascinating documents for exactly that reason, but they belong elsewhere in this project.\n\n---\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nThe most instructive way to read Hirtius is directly against Caesar -- which is precisely what the *Bellum Gallicum*'s editorial structure forces you to do. Book VII ends with Caesar's crisp, measured account of Vercingetorix's surrender at Alesia. Book VIII opens with Hirtius talking about himself. The contrast is immediate.\n\n**The first-person problem:** Caesar never writes \"I.\" As established in the Caesar entry, the third-person narration is a deliberate political and rhetorical choice: Caesar = object of history, not subject of advocacy. Hirtius, from the very first word of his preface (*coactus* -- \"having been compelled\"), puts himself in the text. He addresses Balbus by name. He writes *me interposuerim* -- \"I have inserted myself.\" He worries about his reputation. He admires his general. He is, in short, a person, not a camera. This is not worse than Caesar; it is simply a different register -- the register of a man writing literary Latin rather than political weaponry.\n\n**Sentence structure:** Hirtius's sentences are longer and more hypotactically complex than Caesar's. Where Caesar packs information into compact coordinated clauses, Hirtius tends to subordinate more heavily, embedding relative clauses, participial phrases, and purpose constructions within each other. The praefatio is the most visible example, but the same pattern holds in the military narrative sections. This is not a radical difference -- Hirtius is still writing what is recognizably Caesarian Latin -- but the register is higher, the architecture denser.\n\n**Vocabulary:** Hirtius allows himself words Caesar would not use. His descriptive passages in the *Bellum Alexandrinum* use technical military engineering vocabulary (*testudinines*, *musculi*, *arietes*) in greater density and with more explanatory detail than is typical of Caesar. He occasionally pauses to explain things -- the geography of Alexandria, the properties of the Nile flood -- in ways that recall Caesar's ethnographic digressions but are rather less efficiently executed.\n\n**Psychological observation:** One of the more interesting features of the *Bellum Alexandrinum* is its occasional forays into the mentality of Caesar's opponents. The Alexandrians are portrayed with a complexity Caesar rarely extends to his enemies: they are clever, adaptable, aware of Roman intentions, and capable of rational political analysis. Chapter 3 gives the Alexandrians' own interpretation of the situation, and it is remarkably lucid as an account of imperial politics. Whether this reflects Hirtius's broader sensibility or simply the demands of the subject matter is difficult to say.\n\n**Narrative distance:** Caesar's narration is impersonal even when describing events at which he was present. Hirtius, especially in the *Bellum Alexandrinum*, occasionally hedges -- *ut coniectura consequi possumus* (\"as we can infer from conjecture,\" BA 23), a phrase that would be unthinkable in Caesar's text. Caesar knows. Hirtius sometimes only thinks he knows, and says so. This intellectual modesty is, in its way, more honest than Caesar's omniscient narration; it is also a measure of how differently the two men understood the genre.\n\n**The bottom line:** BG VIII is close to Caesar in style and is approachable at roughly the same level, though students who move from BG I-VII to BG VIII will notice the gear-change. The *Bellum Alexandrinum* asks more: its subject matter is geographically and politically complex (Egyptian succession crises, Alexandrian urban warfare, naval operations in a city they have never seen), its sentence structure is more demanding, and its vocabulary includes technical and geographical terms not found in the earlier works. The BA rewards the reader who brings context; it frustrates the reader who does not.\n\n---\n\n## Latin Excerpts\n\n### Excerpt 1: De Bello Gallico VIII, Praefatio 1-7 -- The General Finishes the Book\n\n**Source:** Hirtius, *Commentarii de Bello Gallico*, Book VIII, Praefatio, sections 1-7, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/caesar/gall8.shtml)\n\n**Context:** This is the preface Hirtius wrote for Book VIII, addressed to Lucius Cornelius Balbus, one of Caesar's most influential political secretaries. It is the only moment in the entire Corpus Caesarianum where an author writes in the first person, names himself, and discusses the project as a literary undertaking. Reading it after seven books of Caesar's impersonal third-person narration produces a mild vertigo: suddenly there is a *person* here, with anxieties and opinions and a named friend he is writing to.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n[Praefatio] 1 Coactus assiduis tuis vocibus, Balbe, cum cotidiana mea recusatio non difficultatis excusationem, sed inertiae videretur deprecationem habere, rem difficillimam suscepi. 2 Caesaris nostri commentarios rerum gestarum Galliae, non comparantibus superioribus atque insequentibus eius scriptis, contexui novissimumque imperfectum ab rebus gestis Alexandriae confeci usque ad exitum non quidem civilis dissensionis, cuius finem nullum videmus, sed vitae Caesaris. 3 Quos utinam qui legent scire possint quam invitus susceperim scribendos, qua facilius caream stultitiae atque arrogantiae crimine, qui me mediis interposuerim Caesaris scriptis. 4 Constat enim inter omnes nihil tam operose ab aliis esse perfectum, quod non horum elegantia commentariorum superetur: 5 qui sunt editi, ne scientia tantarum rerum scriptoribus deesset, adeoque probantur omnium iudicio ut praerepta, non praebita, facultas scriptoribus videatur. 6 Cuius tamen rei maior nostra quam reliquorum est admiratio: ceteri enim, quam bene atque emendate, nos etiam, quam facile atque celeriter eos perfecerit scimus. 7 Erat autem in Caesare cum facultas atque elegantia summa scribendi, tum verissima scientia suorum consiliorum explicandorum.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nCompelled by your relentless insistence, Balbus -- since my daily refusals were beginning to look less like an excuse of difficulty and more like an apology for laziness -- I have undertaken a most difficult task. I have connected Caesar's commentaries on the campaigns in Gaul (since the books that precede and follow his account do not provide comparison), and I have completed the last unfinished one from the Alexandrian campaign up to the end -- not of the civil war, whose end we cannot yet see, but of Caesar's life. I only wish that those who read them could know how unwillingly I took on the writing of these books, so that I might more easily escape the charge of stupidity and arrogance for having inserted myself into the middle of Caesar's texts. For it is universally agreed that nothing worked over so carefully by others fails to be surpassed by the elegance of these commentaries, which were published so that writers should not lack knowledge of events so important -- and they meet with such universal approval that they seem to have robbed writers of an opportunity rather than provided one. My own admiration for this is, however, greater than that of others: while the rest can judge how well and correctly he wrote them, I also know how quickly and easily he finished them. Caesar possessed not only the highest ability and elegance in writing, but also the most precise understanding of setting out his own plans.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nOpen Caesar's BG Book I: *Gallia est omnis divisa in partes tres*. Three words in, you know where you stand. It is an utterance, not a sentence; a fact, not a framing. Now open Book VIII: *Coactus assiduis tuis vocibus, Balbe*. The first word is a perfect passive participle modifying a subject still to be identified; the second is an adverb modifying an adjective modifying a noun (*assiduis vocibus*) in the ablative; and the whole thing is addressed to a named person. Seven words in, Hirtius has done more grammatical work than Caesar would do in a paragraph.\n\nThe stylistic differences are immediately apparent. Hirtius uses subordination where Caesar would coordinate. His sentence rhythms are longer and more periodic. He deploys the type of rhetorical hedging -- *utinam qui legent scire possint* (\"I only wish those who read could know\") -- that is entirely absent from Caesar's impersonal narration. He worries about how he will be perceived. Caesar never worried about how he would be perceived, at least not in the text; he simply arranged events so that the narration was self-evidently favorable.\n\nThe passage is also a remarkable piece of literary self-consciousness. Hirtius says outright that Caesar's commentaries have *robbed* other writers of the ability to write about these events -- not just surpassed them, but preempted them (*praerepta, non praebita, facultas scriptoribus videatur*: \"the opportunity seems to have been taken from writers, not given to them\"). This is Hirtius's elegant way of explaining why no other historian immediately tackled Caesar's Gallic campaigns: the commentaries were so comprehensive and so authoritative that there was nothing left to do. The observation is correct, and it has remained correct for two thousand years. Historians writing about the Gallic Wars still work around Caesar rather than independently of him.\n\nThe detail in paragraph 6 -- that Hirtius, unlike other readers, knows not only how *well* Caesar wrote but how *fast* -- is the intimate touch of someone who actually watched Caesar compose. Caesar, who wrote the *De Analogia* while crossing the Alps, was apparently as swift at the desk as he was on the march. Hirtius's admiration is not performed modesty; it is the testimony of a witness.\n\nSyntactically, the passage is denser than Caesar but not forbidding. Students who have read BG I-VII will find themselves parsing slightly more carefully, holding subordinate clauses open slightly longer before the main verb resolves them. The vocabulary is standard; the difficulty is architectural.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 2: De Bello Alexandrino 1-2 -- Alexandria at War\n\n**Source:** [Hirtius?], *De Bello Alexandrino*, chapters 1-2, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/caesar/alex.shtml)\n\n**Context:** This is the opening of the *Bellum Alexandrinum*, which picks up exactly where Caesar's *Bellum Civili* breaks off. Caesar has pursued Pompey to Egypt, arrived to find him already dead, and become embroiled in the succession crisis between Ptolemy XIII and his sister Cleopatra. The work opens *in medias res* -- \"with the Alexandrian War having broken out\" (*bello Alexandrino conflato*) -- assuming the reader knows everything that happened in the BC and has followed along. There is no explanation, no orientation. You are already in Alexandria. Keep up.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n[1] Bello Alexandrino conflato Caesar Rhodo atque ex Syria Ciliciaque omnem classem arcessit; Creta sagittarios, equites ab rege Nabataeorum Malcho evocat; tormenta undique conquiri et frumentum mitti, auxilia adduci iubet. Interim munitiones cotidie operibus augentur atque omnes oppidi partes, quae minus esse firmae videntur, testudinibus ac musculis aptantur; ex aedificiis autem per foramina in proxima aedificia arietes immittuntur, quantumque aut ruinis deicitur aut per vim recipitur loci, in tantum munitiones proferuntur. Nam incendio fere tuta est Alexandrea, quod sine contignatione ac materia sunt aedificia et structuris ac fornicibus continentur tectaque sunt rudere aut pavimentis. Caesar maxime studebat ut, quam angustissimam partem oppidi palus a meridie interiecta efficiebat, hanc operibus vineisque agendis ab reliqua parte urbis excluderet, illud spectans primum ut, cum in duas partis esset urbis divisa acies, uno consilio atque imperio administraretur, deinde ut laborantibus succurri atque ex altera oppidi parte auxilium ferri posset, in primis vero ut aqua pabuloque abundaret, quarum alterius rei copiam exiguam, alterius nullam omnino facultatem habebat; quod utrumque large palus praebere poterat.\n\n[2] Neque vero Alexandrinis in gerendis negotiis cunctatio ulla aut mora inferebatur. Nam in omnis partis, per quas fines Aegypti regnumque pertinet, legatos conquisitoresque dilectus habendi causa miserant magnumque numerum in oppidum telorum atque tormentorum convexerant et innumerabilem multitudinem adduxerant. Nec minus in urbe maximae armorum erant institutae officinae. Servos praeterea puberes armaverant; quibus domini locupletiores victum cotidianum stipendiumque praebebant. Hac multitudine disposita munitiones semotarum partium tuebantur; veteranas cohortis vacuas in celeberrimis urbis locis habebant, ut quacumque regione pugnaretur integris viribus ad auxilium ferendum opponi possent. Omnibus viis atque angiportis triplicem vallum obduxerant -- erat autem quadrato exstructus saxo neque minus XL pedes altitudinis habebat -- quaeque partes urbis inferiores erant, has altissimis turribus denorum tabulatorum munierant. Praeterea alias ambulatorias totidem tabulatorum confixerant subiectisque eas rotis funibus iumentisque obiectis derectis plateis in quamcumque erat visum partem movebant.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\n[1] With the Alexandrian War having broken out, Caesar summoned the entire fleet from Rhodes and from Syria and Cilicia; he called up archers from Crete and cavalry from Malchus, king of the Nabataeans; he ordered artillery to be gathered from everywhere, grain to be sent, and reinforcements to be brought up. In the meantime the fortifications were being strengthened daily by construction work, and all the parts of the city that appeared to be less than solid were being fitted with mantlets and protective sheds; battering rams were driven through openings in buildings into the adjacent buildings, and as much ground as was cleared by demolition or taken by force, by that much the fortifications were extended. For Alexandria is largely safe from fire, since its buildings lack timber framing and beams and are held together by courses of masonry and arches, and their roofs are of rubble or tile. Caesar was chiefly concerned to cut off, by means of siege-works and siege screens, the narrowest part of the city -- a strip created by a marsh lying to the south -- from the rest of the urban area, aiming first that, since his battle line was divided across two parts of the city, it should be directed by a single plan and command; second, that he could send help to hard-pressed sectors and bring support from one part of the city to another; and most of all that he might have ample supplies of water and fodder, of the first of which he had too little, and of the second none at all -- both of which the marsh could supply in abundance.\n\n[2] Nor were the Alexandrians suffering any delay or slowdown in the management of their affairs. For they had sent envoys and recruiting officers to every part of the territory over which Egypt's borders and kingdom extended, and had brought into the city a great quantity of projectiles and artillery, and had assembled an innumerable multitude. No less great were the arms workshops established inside the city. They had additionally armed all their male slaves of fighting age, whose wealthier owners provided them with daily food and pay. This multitude disposed, they kept guard over the fortifications on the more distant parts of the perimeter; they held veteran cohorts on standby in the most frequented parts of the city, so that wherever in the city fighting broke out, they could be deployed at full strength to bring aid. In all the streets and alleyways they had built a triple rampart -- it was constructed of squared stone and stood no less than forty feet high -- and the lower parts of the city they had fortified with very tall towers ten stories high. In addition they had built other movable towers of the same height, and by placing them on wheels and hauling them with ropes and draft animals along the straight streets, they could move them to whatever position seemed necessary.\n\n**Analysis: Caesar vs. Hirtius, head to head**\n\nCompare the opening of BA chapter 1 with the opening of BG Book I. Caesar: *Gallia est omnis divisa in partes tres.* Hirtius: *Bello Alexandrino conflato Caesar Rhodo atque ex Syria Ciliciaque omnem classem arcessit.* Both open with a statement of situation. But where Caesar's first sentence is a self-contained geographical orientation (five words, one main verb, complete), Hirtius opens with an ablative absolute (*bello Alexandrino conflato*) that presupposes you know what the Alexandrian War is, and then immediately launches into a list of troop-gathering activities across multiple provinces. The structural assumptions are different: Caesar orients the reader; Hirtius assumes the reader is already oriented.\n\nThe sentence at the end of chapter 1 -- from *Caesar maxime studebat* to *palus praebere poterat* -- is a good example of Hirtius at his most Hirtian. It is a single long sentence articulated through three nested purpose constructions (*ut*... *ut*... *ut*...), each explaining a different reason for Caesar's strategic objective, with an ablative absolute (*quarum alterius rei copiam exiguam, alterius nullam omnino facultatem habebat*) embedded inside the third. This is the sort of sentence Caesar would have broken into two or three shorter ones. Hirtius prefers to hold the whole argument in suspension until the final clause resolves it. The sentence is correct, elegant, and slightly harder to parse than anything in the first seven books.\n\nChapter 2's description of the Alexandrian defenses is the other side of the coin: Hirtius writing in a mode that is actually closer to Caesar's than the praefatio is. Short declarative statements, military precision, a preference for factual enumeration over elaboration. *Omnibus viis atque angiportis triplicem vallum obduxerant* -- \"in all the streets and alleyways they had built a triple rampart\" -- is very Caesarian in its compression. But the parenthetical explanation that follows (*erat autem quadrato exstructus saxo neque minus XL pedes altitudinis habebat*) -- describing exactly what kind of stone and exactly how tall -- is more than Caesar would typically supply. Caesar tells you what exists and what it does. Hirtius tells you what it is made of.\n\nThe technical vocabulary in these passages (*testudinibus*, *musculis*, *arietes* -- mantlets, protective sheds, battering rams; *ambulatorias turris* -- movable towers) is denser than anything in the standard BG text. Students who have only read BG I-VII will need a good dictionary for this material, or a good commentary. The concepts are straightforward once explained; the vocabulary itself is the obstacle.\n\nThe *in medias res* opening deserves a final note. Caesar's BC also begins abruptly, but it does provide some political context in its opening chapters. The BA provides none: it simply begins with the war already in progress, the reader presumably having read the BC, and the political situation around Ptolemy XIII and Cleopatra entirely unexplained. To understand what is happening, why Caesar is trapped in Alexandria, who the factions are, and what the stakes are, a reader needs either a good introduction or prior knowledge of Hellenistic Egypt. This contextual demand -- more than any grammatical feature -- is what makes the BA harder than BG.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nHirtius matters to the tradition primarily as a preserver and completer. Without BG Book VIII, we would lack the account of the final stages of Caesar's Gallic campaigns, including the mopping-up operations that permanently secured Roman control of the province. Without the *Bellum Alexandrinum* (if it is indeed his), we would have no coherent account of the war that kept Caesar pinned in Egypt for several months and produced the *veni vidi vici* episode. These are not small gaps.\n\nAs a writer, Hirtius served an important documentary function precisely because he was not quite as good as Caesar. The praefatio's self-conscious humility -- real or performed -- ensured that the Corpus Caesarianum was understood as a collaborative project: Caesar's design, Hirtius's completion. This framing preserved the commentaries' integrity as a literary corpus rather than allowing them to circulate as disconnected fragments.\n\nThe BA's authorship question has generated more scholarly ink than the text perhaps merits, but it has also produced some genuinely illuminating stylistic analysis of what distinguishes Caesarian from post-Caesarian military Latin. The answer, broadly, is this: Caesar wrote for a political audience that knew the players and needed to be persuaded. Hirtius wrote for a historical audience that needed the record completed.\n\nOne final irony: Hirtius died completing someone else's military campaign (the war against Antony) just as he had made his literary name completing someone else's literary campaign. He was a consistently excellent second-in-command who never got the chance to be first at anything. Given what happened to most of the people who tried to be first in 43 BC, this might not be entirely bad luck.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: C (Manageable)**\n\nBG Book VIII sits at the easier end of C, close to the Caesar D that surrounds it in the curriculum. A student who has successfully read BG I-VII will find Book VIII more demanding but not dramatically so; the main adjustment is the sentence architecture and the occasional shift in vocabulary register. With solid preparation it is thoroughly approachable.\n\nThe *Bellum Alexandrinum* earns the C more decisively. The technical vocabulary of urban siege warfare, the unfamiliar geography of Alexandria and the Nile Delta, the assumed knowledge of Hellenistic court politics, and the *in medias res* narrative structure all add up to a text that requires more preparation than any of Caesar's own books. It is not difficult in the way that Tacitus or Sallust are difficult -- the syntax is never gratuitously dense, the vocabulary never deliberately obscure -- but it asks more from the reader than it gives in return in terms of contextual support.\n\nThe tier is C rather than D because Hirtius, unlike Caesar, cannot be read cold. He assumes you know where you are.\n\n---\n\n# TITUS LUCRETIUS CARUS (c. 99--55 BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nAlmost nothing is known about the life of Titus Lucretius Carus. His birth date is uncertain, his death date is uncertain, his social class is uncertain, his hometown is unknown, and he left no letters, no recorded friendships, and no portraits. He exists entirely in his poem. This is, for an Epicurean philosopher, a perfectly appropriate outcome. Epicurus's famous maxim was *lathe biosas* -- \"live hidden.\" Lucretius followed it so thoroughly that two thousand years of scholarship have failed to locate him. He achieved in life exactly what he recommended in philosophy: radical, total, successful obscurity.\n\nThe one source that claims to know something specific about his life is also the one we should trust least. Jerome's *Chronicle* (written in the 380s AD, roughly four centuries after Lucretius's death) reports that Lucretius was driven mad by a love potion administered by his wife; that he wrote several books of poetry during intervals of lucidity; that Cicero \"emended\" them; and that he died by his own hand at the age of forty-three. This story has everything a hostile late-antique Christian writer would want from an Epicurean atheist: madness, a scheming woman, and a suicide. It has almost no corroborating evidence. Cicero does mention the *De Rerum Natura* in a letter to his brother Quintus in February 54 BC, writing that the poem shows \"many flashes of genius, but also much art.\" This suggests he had read it and thought well of it. Whether he \"emended\" it in the editorial sense or merely discussed it with someone is entirely unclear. The love potion, the madness, and the suicide are almost certainly invented -- or at minimum colored by the tradition's hostility to Epicurean materialism. Jerome is not a disinterested source on a poet who argued that the soul is made of atoms and death is simply nothing.\n\nWhat we can say: Lucretius lived sometime in the first century BC, probably died around 55-50 BC, and addressed his poem to Gaius Memmius, a Roman aristocrat, patron of letters, and sometime politician (also, incidentally, the dedicatee of Catullus's poem collection -- Memmius apparently attracted poets the way other Romans attracted clients). The poem itself, at approximately 7,400 lines across six books, shows signs of being unfinished: some books end abruptly, certain passages are marked as requiring revision, and the structural balance between the books is slightly off. It looks like a work that was still being polished when its author died. Whoever published it posthumously did the world a significant service.\n\nThe man remains invisible. The poem remains extraordinary. For once, this seems fitting.\n\n---\n\n## Most Important Works\n\nLucretius wrote, as far as anyone knows, exactly one thing: the *De Rerum Natura* (\"On the Nature of Things\"). It is a philosophical poem in six books of dactylic hexameter, presenting the complete Epicurean physical system in Latin verse. It is also one of the most ambitious literary projects in the history of the Latin language -- which is saying something in a century that also produced Caesar, Cicero, and Catullus.\n\nThe six books are organized as a sequence of interlocking arguments, each building on what came before:\n\n**Book I** establishes the foundational principles: everything that exists is composed of *corpora* (bodies) and *inane* (void). Nothing comes from nothing (*nil posse creari de nihilo*), and nothing can be reduced to nothing. Matter is eternal, indestructible, and invisible at the fundamental level. The atoms exist; the void exists; everything else is a compound of the two.\n\n**Book II** covers the motion of atoms -- they fall through the void, collide, and form compounds through an unpredictable swerve called the *clinamen*. The variety of atomic shapes accounts for the variety of things in the world. The world, and all worlds, are neither specially created nor uniquely permanent.\n\n**Book III** is the book that should terrify the pious and comfort the wise: the soul (*anima* and *animus*) is a physical compound, made of especially fine and mobile atoms. When the body dies, the soul-compound dissolves too. There is no survival, no afterlife, no punishment -- and therefore nothing to fear from death. *Nil igitur mors est ad nos* -- \"death, therefore, is nothing to us.\" When we exist, death is not present; when death is present, we no longer exist.\n\n**Book IV** handles perception, imagination, and -- at considerable length -- the pathology of sexual love, which Lucretius regards as a particularly vicious form of self-delusion. He does not recommend celibacy, exactly; he recommends not falling in love. This is the Epicurean position, and Lucretius argues it with the enthusiasm of a man who has strong opinions on the matter.\n\n**Book V** describes how the world came to be (not by divine creation but through the chance collision of atoms), how celestial phenomena work (in very un-modern ways, but rationally), and how human civilization developed from primitive beginnings to its current state. It is the closest thing in classical Latin literature to evolutionary social history.\n\n**Book VI** tackles the natural phenomena most likely to inspire religious terror: thunder and lightning (not Jupiter's anger -- atmospheric electricity), earthquakes, volcanoes, and the plague. The book ends with a long description of the plague of Athens in 430 BC, adapted from Thucydides, presenting the ultimate natural catastrophe without a shred of divine explanation. It is one of the most powerful passages in all of Latin poetry and may also be the passage Lucretius never got to revise.\n\n---\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\n### On choosing poetry, or: the honey gambit\n\nEpicurus, the philosophical father of everything Lucretius is doing, distrusted poetry. Poetry deceives, distorts, and appeals to the emotions rather than to reason. For a system dedicated to clear-eyed understanding of reality, verse seemed counterproductive.\n\nLucretius disagreed. His argument, stated with characteristic candor at DRN I, vv. 926-950, is that Epicurean philosophy is genuinely hard to swallow -- dry, abstract, and counterintuitive. So he coats it in verse the way doctors coat bitter medicine in honey, to make it palatable:\n\n> *sed vel uti pueris absinthia taetra medentes*\n> *cum dare conantur, prius oras pocula circum*\n> *contingunt mellis dulci flavoque liquore,*\n> *ut puerorum aetas inprovida ludificetur*\n> *labrorum tenus, interea perpotet amarum*\n> *absinthi laticem deceptaque non capiatur,*\n> *sed potius tali facto recreata valescat,*\n> *sic ego nunc, quoniam haec ratio plerumque videtur*\n> *tristior esse quibus non est tractata, retroque*\n> *volgus abhorret ab hac, volui tibi suaviloquenti*\n> *carmine Pierio rationem exponere nostram*\n> *et quasi musaeo dulci contingere melle*\n\nJust as doctors, when they try to give foul wormwood to children, first coat the rim of the cup around with sweet golden honey, so that the unsuspecting age of childhood is fooled at the lip -- meanwhile they drink down the bitter draught of wormwood and though deceived are not harmed, but rather by this treatment are restored to health; so I now, since this theory generally seems too grim to those not familiar with it, and the crowd shrinks back from it, wished to set out our philosophy for you in sweet-tongued Pierian song, and as it were to touch it with the sweet honey of the Muses.\n\nThe irony is glorious: Lucretius chose poetry to make Epicurus easier, and in doing so produced one of the most demanding poems in the Latin language. This was not entirely his fault.\n\n### The vocabulary problem: inventing a language\n\nLatin had no philosophical vocabulary when Lucretius sat down to write. Greek philosophy had spent four centuries developing precise technical terms. Latin had nothing equivalent. Lucretius solved this in two ways: by coining new Latin words, and by repurposing existing ones.\n\n*Primordia* (primordial elements, i.e., atoms) -- Lucretius's coinage, combining *primus* + *oriri*. *Genitalia corpora* (generative bodies -- his most common periphrasis for atoms). *Inane* (the void -- a normal Latin adjective meaning \"empty\" promoted to a technical noun). *Clinamen* (the atomic swerve -- from *clinare*, \"to bend\" -- the word barely exists in Latin outside Lucretius). *Concilium* (assembly of atoms, from a word that usually means a political gathering). Even *rerum natura* itself, the title of the poem, is a deliberate translation of the Greek *physika*.\n\nThe result is that reading Lucretius requires not just a Latin dictionary but a willingness to follow how he has redefined common words into technical instruments, and to track coinages that appear nowhere else in the language.\n\n### Archaic vocabulary: Ennius's ghost\n\nLucretius admired Quintus Ennius, the great second-century BC epic poet whose *Annales* established the tradition of Latin hexameter before Virgil swept the field. He says so explicitly, calling Ennius \"our Ennius\" and placing him among the philosophical visionaries. Stylistically, he imitates Ennius's archaic texture deliberately: genitive singular forms like *aulai* (for *aulae*), *materiai* (for *materiae*), *Iphianassai* (the Homeric form of Iphigenia's name, rather than the Latinized *Iphigenia*), old endings, old rhythms.\n\nThis archaism serves two purposes: it elevates the tone toward the heroic tradition, and it creates a deliberate distance from Ciceronian classical Latin. Lucretius is not writing the clean, calibrated prose of Caesar's era. He is reaching back toward an older, rougher, more muscular tradition. For a reader trained on Cicero or even Caesar, the lexical texture comes as a mild but persistent shock.\n\n### The image sequences\n\nPerhaps Lucretius's most distinctive technique is what we might call the philosophical snapshot: extended visual descriptions embedded in the argument to make an abstract point concrete and vivid. The most famous is the motes of dust in a sunbeam (II.114-128): \"Often in sunny spots when sunbeams filter through the darkness of a shuttered room, you see a multitude of tiny bodies mingling in a multitude of ways through the empty space in the light of the beam...\" He uses this image to make visible the invisible movement of atoms. He describes a flock of sheep on a distant hill -- all white, all motionless from a distance -- to explain why distant objects lose their individual properties (II.317-322). He describes the armor of a warrior abandoned on a battlefield, rusting into the earth (II.61-79), to illustrate atomic dissolution.\n\nThese passages are among the most beautiful in Latin poetry. They are also, for the translator and student, a significant additional challenge: Lucretius expects you to hold the philosophical argument in your head while processing an extended visual sequence that is doing philosophical work in disguise.\n\n### The difficulty composite\n\nThe result of all this is a text that is difficult on almost every axis simultaneously: poetic form (hexameter, with all its metrical demands), archaic vocabulary (requiring a specialized glossary), philosophical neologisms (requiring systematic learning of the Epicurean system), dense arguments (requiring that you follow a six-book chain of reasoning), and elaborate image sequences (requiring that you see what Lucretius is describing and understand why he is describing it). It is a poem that rewards both the close reader and the patient one -- and that has very little patience for any other kind.\n\n---\n\n### The Lucretian Myth: On Pessimism\n\nBefore the excerpt, a necessary detour.\n\nLucretius is frequently described as a \"pessimistic philosopher\" -- someone who finds comfort in the bleakness of materialism, who tells us that we are just temporary configurations of atoms that will eventually dissolve, and that nothing ultimately matters. This reading is wrong. It is wrong in the way that matters most: it gets the emotional register backwards.\n\nLucretius is furious. He is furious on behalf of humanity, which has been terrorized for centuries by *religio* -- and it is crucial to understand what that word means in his usage. Not \"religion\" in any modern confessional sense. *Religio* for Lucretius is superstition: the irrational fear that the gods are watching, judging, and punishing human beings; that thunderstorms are divine anger; that plague is retribution; that death is followed by eternal torment. This fear, he argues, has caused incalculable suffering. It drove Agamemnon to slit his daughter's throat at Aulis. It keeps human beings in a state of chronic anxiety that prevents them from ever simply living.\n\nEpicurean physics -- the atoms, the void, the soul's dissolution at death -- is not nihilism. It is liberation. If you understand that the gods do not intervene in human affairs, you cannot be terrorized by their supposed anger. If you understand that death is simply the dissolution of the atomic compound that is you, you cannot be paralyzed by the fear of punishment afterward. *Nil igitur mors est ad nos* -- \"death is nothing to us.\" This is not resignation. It is the removal of the largest source of unnecessary suffering in human life.\n\nThe goal is *ataraxia* (tranquility of mind, the absence of anxiety) and *aponia* (freedom from bodily pain). Together they constitute the Epicurean ideal -- not pleasure in the hedonistic sense, but the peaceful condition of a person who has understood how the universe actually works and is no longer afraid of it. Living in harmony with the rhythm of the universe, not fighting shadows.\n\nLucretius is not telling Memmius that nothing matters. He is telling Memmius that the things he has been taught to fear do not exist, and that the things that do exist -- the beauty of the world, the fact of being alive at all, the capacity for calm rational thought -- are worth having precisely because they are finite and real. He writes about atoms with the intensity of a man who finds them beautiful.\n\nThis is not pessimism. This is the most committed form of optimism: the kind that refuses to flinch from the truth.\n\n---\n\n## Latin Excerpt\n\n### De Rerum Natura I, vv. 80-101 -- The Sacrifice of Iphigenia\n\n**Source:** Lucretius, *De Rerum Natura*, Book I, lines 80-101, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/lucretius/lucretius1.shtml)\n\n**Context:** This passage comes near the beginning of Book I. Lucretius has introduced his subject and addressed Memmius. He then anticipates an objection: won't this godless, materialist philosophy lead Memmius to impiety and wickedness? His answer is a pre-emptive reversal -- he inverts the accusation completely. It is not Epicurean physics that leads to *scelera* (crimes, impiety). It is *religio* -- superstitious religious fear -- that does. And to demonstrate what *religio* is capable of, he tells the story of the sacrifice of Iphigenia at Aulis.\n\nThe story, from Greek mythology: the Greek fleet was becalmed at Aulis, unable to sail to Troy. The seer Calchas declared that the goddess Diana (Artemis) was angry and could only be appeased by the sacrifice of Iphigenia, daughter of the commander Agamemnon. Agamemnon complied. The fleet sailed.\n\nNote that Lucretius calls the girl *Iphianassa*, her archaic Homeric name rather than the usual Latin *Iphigenia* -- a deliberate choice that activates the heroic epic register. The altar is described as belonging to *Trivia* (Diana as goddess of crossroads), and the Greek commanders are the *ductores Danaum delecti, prima virorum* -- \"chosen leaders of the Danaans, first among men.\" The framing elevates the scene to emphasize the full horror of what \"first among men\" are capable of, in the name of *religio*.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Illud in his rebus vereor, ne forte rearis\n> impia te rationis inire elementa viamque\n> indugredi sceleris. quod contra saepius illa\n> religio peperit scelerosa atque impia facta.\n> Aulide quo pacto Triviai virginis aram\n> Iphianassai turparunt sanguine foede\n> ductores Danaum delecti, prima virorum.\n> cui simul infula virgineos circum data comptus\n> ex utraque pari malarum parte profusast,\n> et maestum simul ante aras adstare parentem\n> sensit et hunc propter ferrum celare ministros\n> aspectuque suo lacrimas effundere civis,\n> muta metu terram genibus summissa petebat.\n> nec miserae prodesse in tali tempore quibat,\n> quod patrio princeps donarat nomine regem;\n> nam sublata virum manibus tremibundaque ad aras\n> deductast, non ut sollemni more sacrorum\n> perfecto posset claro comitari Hymenaeo,\n> sed casta inceste nubendi tempore in ipso\n> hostia concideret mactatu maesta parentis,\n> exitus ut classi felix faustusque daretur.\n> tantum religio potuit suadere malorum.\n\n*(DRN I.80-101)*\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nIn these matters I fear lest perhaps you suppose that you are entering upon the first principles of impious reasoning and setting out on a path of wickedness. But on the contrary, it is religion that has more often given birth to deeds wicked and impious. Consider how at Aulis the chosen leaders of the Danaans, first among men, foully stained the altar of the Trivian virgin with the blood of Iphianassa. The moment the ribbon was placed around her girl's tresses, streaming equally down both sides of her cheeks, and she noticed her father standing grief-stricken before the altars, and the attendants nearby concealing the iron blade, and her fellow citizens pouring out tears at the sight of her -- struck mute with terror, she sank to her knees seeking the ground. It could not help the poor girl at that moment that she had been the first to give the king the name of father; for she was lifted in the hands of men and led trembling to the altars, not so that after the solemn rite of sacrifice was complete she might be escorted by the bright wedding hymn, but so that, chaste, in the very season of marriage, she might fall as a sorrowful victim in an unholy slaughter by her father's hand -- so that a fortunate and auspicious departure might be given to the fleet. Such great evils could religion persuade.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe passage works in two movements. The first (lines 80-84) is the logical setup: you fear that atomism leads to impiety? Let me show you what impiety actually looks like. The second (lines 84-101) is the narrative demonstration, and it is one of the most controlled pieces of emotional argumentation in Latin poetry.\n\nNotice how Lucretius positions the girl. She is named in the archaic Greek form (*Iphianassa*), which removes her from the safe distance of myth into the heroic tradition's direct light. She is *prima virorum* -- she is surrounded by the best of men, the leaders of the Greek world. And she is, in the most brutal detail, a frightened child: the ribbon in her hair, the sight of her father's grief, the iron blade half-hidden by the attendants. *Muta metu terram genibus summissa petebat* -- \"mute with fear, sinking to her knees, she sought the ground.\" Five words in Latin. There is no verbal elaboration, no pathetic fallacy, no rhetorical cushioning. Just the action, and the silence.\n\nThe detail *quod patrio princeps donarat nomine regem* (\"because she had been the first to call the king by the name of father\") is devastating. She was the first to call him *father*. She cannot help herself now, because this very relationship -- daughter to father, the relationship that should offer the most protection -- is what is killing her. Agamemnon is not a monster; he is a man who has weighed his daughter against his fleet, and the fleet won. That is exactly what religion asked of him, and he complied.\n\nThe grammatical turn in lines 95-100 is worth parsing carefully. *Non ut* ... *sed ut*: \"not so that she might be escorted with the wedding hymn... but so that she might fall as a victim in unholy slaughter.\" The *non ut / sed ut* contrast introduces two purpose clauses, one imagined (the proper outcome of a girl at the appropriate age -- marriage, the *Hymenaeus*) and one real (sacrifice). The word *inceste* -- \"impurely, in an unholy manner\" -- modifies what is supposed to be a *casta* (pure, chaste) action. It is the virginal sacrifice of a virgin, sacred and obscene simultaneously.\n\nThen: *tantum religio potuit suadere malorum.* \"So much evil could religion persuade.\" The line is end-stopped, metrically clean, and comes after a long, syntactically complex narrative. The sudden simplicity is like a hammer blow. Eleven Latin words -- one sentence -- and the entire case against superstition is made.\n\nThe archaic vocabulary throughout is significant: *malarum* (cheeks, an archaism), *Aulide* and *Iphianassai* (archaic Greek-derived forms), *Triviai* (archaic genitive of *Trivia*). Lucretius is deliberately writing in the register of Ennius's epic, the tradition of Rome's oldest poets, to show that the critique of *religio* is not novelty -- it is as old as poetry itself.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nAfter the DRN was apparently published posthumously -- by Cicero, by his brother Quintus, or by some unknown hand; no one knows for certain -- it entered the world to a somewhat muted reception. Cicero, as noted, valued it. Virgil read it with exceptional attention: the echoes run throughout the Georgics, which borrows Lucretius's hexameter register, his extended image sequences, and his philosophical ambition, and through the underworld scenes of the Aeneid. Ovid mentions Lucretius in a famous line that is also, inadvertently, almost accurate: *carmina sublimis tunc sunt peritura Lucreti / exitio terras cum dabit una dies* -- \"the songs of lofty Lucretius will perish only when one day delivers the earth to its end.\" The DRN came genuinely close to perishing.\n\nWith the rise of Christianity, an Epicurean poem denying the afterlife, dismissing divine intervention, and treating death as simple annihilation was not a text that monasteries were eager to copy. Only a handful of manuscripts survived the medieval period. The critical moment came in January 1417, when the humanist Poggio Bracciolini, visiting a monastery in Germany -- probably Fulda -- found a manuscript of the DRN in the library and had it copied. This is one of the great intellectual rescues of the Renaissance: a text that was a few copying decisions away from complete disappearance found its way back into circulation. Stephen Greenblatt's popular history *The Swerve* (2011) dramatizes the event, though it somewhat overstates the poem's subsequent impact on Renaissance thought. The core story is accurate.\n\nAfter Poggio's copy began to circulate, Lucretius became increasingly available and increasingly read. Montaigne read him closely and the materialism marked him. Enlightenment thinkers absorbed him; Thomas Jefferson owned several copies of the DRN and described himself in correspondence as an Epicurean, meaning substantially what Lucretius taught: material world, no afterlife, happiness through moderation and the removal of irrational fear.\n\nWithin Latin literary history, Lucretius's most important legacy is Virgil. The Georgics are the most Lucretian text after the DRN itself -- same meter, same didactic ambition, same philosophical register, same image sequences doing philosophical work. Virgil's debt was recognized in antiquity and is unmistakable on the page. He took Lucretius's hexameter and made it smoother; he took his philosophical reach and domesticated it within Roman civic ideology; he turned the Epicurean project of liberation from fear into a celebration of Italian land and labor. The relationship is not influence but transformation. Reading Lucretius after Virgil, you understand what Virgil was working with and working against simultaneously.\n\n---\n\n## Proposed Tier\n\n**Tier: A (Very Difficult)**\n\nLucretius sits solidly in the A tier -- and firmly at the higher end of it.\n\n**Medium:** Dactylic hexameter is harder than prose on principle, and Lucretius's hexameter is no exception. The verse imposes word order constraints that produce persistent hyperbaton, and the enjambment across lines means that sentence structure frequently does not coincide with line structure. Students trained on prose must recalibrate entirely.\n\n**Lexicon:** The vocabulary burden is exceptional and operates on three levels simultaneously. First, archaic forms (*aulai*, *materiai*, *Iphianassai*, *Triviai*) that are not in standard dictionaries or, when they are, are marked as rare. Second, the standard Epicurean technical vocabulary (*primordia*, *inane*, *genitalia corpora*, *clinamen*) which must be learned as a system rather than word by word. Third, the image-sequence vocabulary, which is often concrete, visual, and from registers -- farming, navigation, metalwork -- not typically covered in introductory Latin courses.\n\n**Syntactic complexity:** The hexameter line drives heavy use of hyperbaton (separation of words that belong together), which in prose would simply appear next to each other but in verse are spread across the line or across enjambment. Add to this that Lucretius's arguments are genuinely complex multi-step logical sequences that require the reader to track the thread of reasoning while simultaneously parsing difficult syntax.\n\n**Allusion density:** Lucretius alludes to Ennius constantly, to Empedocles (his great philosophical predecessor-rival), to Homer, and to the entire Greek scientific tradition. Understanding the allusions requires either substantial background or a very good commentary.\n\n**Why not S?** The S tier is for texts that combine maximum difficulty across all criteria simultaneously, including the kind of deliberate obscurity or compression that Tacitus, late Virgil, or Horace at his most Pindaric can produce. Lucretius is difficult but not obscure: his arguments, once the vocabulary is learned and the verse is parsed, move forward with reasonable clarity. He wants to be understood. He is not hiding. He is putting barriers of form and vocabulary between Memmius and the ideas, but the ideas themselves, once reached, are not designed to elude. For the reader who has done the work, Lucretius opens. For Tacitus or Persius, having done the work is no guarantee.\n\n---\n\n# GAIUS SALLUSTIUS CRISPUS -- SALLUST (86--35 BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nThe greatest irony in Latin literary history is that the man who wrote most incisively about Roman corruption was himself one of the more spectacularly corrupt public figures of his generation. Gaius Sallustius Crispus was born in 86 BC in Amiternum, a small Sabine town, to an equestrian family -- good enough to open doors in Rome, not quite good enough to open all of them automatically. He entered political life as a populist tribune of the plebs in 52 BC, the year of Clodius's murder and the subsequent political chaos, and used the position with characteristic energy to attack Cicero and Milo. Then, in 50 BC, the censors expelled him from the Senate on charges of immorality -- the specific allegations are disputed, but the phrasing suggests something beyond political convenience: *probri gratia*, \"for the sake of disgrace.\"\n\nEnter Gaius Julius Caesar. Sallust attached himself to Caesar with the speed of a man who understood which way the political wind was blowing, served as a military tribune and later quaestor (which automatically restored his senatorial rank), and participated in Caesar's African campaigns with sufficient competence to be rewarded. In 46 BC, following the defeat of the Pompeians at Thapsus, Sallust was appointed governor of the new province of Africa Nova -- what had been Numidia, the kingdom whose history he would later write.\n\nHis governorship of Numidia was distinguished by the thoroughness of his plunder. The provincials accused him of extortion so systematic and brazen that charges were brought before the courts. The charges were, again, dropped -- Caesar saw to it -- but the scale of what Sallust had extracted from the province became something of an open scandal in Rome. He retired from public life entirely after Caesar's assassination in 44 BC, never seeking office again, and spent his remaining years -- he died around 35 BC -- in learned leisure funded by the proceeds of his time in Africa.\n\nThe monument to those proceeds is the *Horti Sallustiani*, the Gardens of Sallust, one of the most extravagant private landscape projects in the history of Rome. Sallust laid out his gardens on the slope between the Quirinal and Pincian hills, with artificial terraces, elaborate waterworks, sculptures, a triumphal arch of his own construction, and grounds that rivaled anything the old aristocracy could show. They were so magnificent that Augustus subsequently purchased them to use as imperial property. The man who wrote that avarice \"subverts faith, honesty, and all other good qualities\" and that wealth had corrupted the Roman character built himself the most ostentatious monument to wealth in the city.\n\nNow open *De Coniuratione Catilinae*, chapters 1-4. Sallust explains why he has turned to writing history: in a republic so sunk in corruption and faction, honest political participation is impossible. Virtuous men are edged out by the unscrupulous. The only genuine service left to offer is historiography -- recording what happened, with surgical clarity, so that posterity may understand. He presents himself as a man who tried politics, found it intolerable, and withdrew to the higher calling of truth-telling.\n\nEvery sentence is technically accurate. It is also one of the most magnificent pieces of self-reinvention in Latin prose. The man who was expelled from the Senate for immorality, who enriched himself through provincial extortion, who survived three political crises by attaching himself to the right powerful patron, presents himself as a disappointed idealist. And then he proceeds to write some of the most penetrating moral analysis in Latin literature. The contradiction is not a bug. It may be the engine.\n\n---\n\n## Most Important Works\n\nSallust produced three historical works, of which two survive complete and one in fragments.\n\nThe *De Coniuratione Catilinae* (also called the *Bellum Catilinae*) covers the conspiracy of 63 BC, in which Lucius Sergius Catilina attempted to seize control of the Roman state through a coordinated armed uprising, only to be crushed by Cicero's consulship and the senatorial decree that led to the execution of his co-conspirators. At roughly 61 chapters, it is a short, tightly compressed monograph -- not an annalistic chronicle but a focused analytical narrative organized around a single crisis and the moral questions it raises. The characters (Catiline, Cicero, Caesar, Cato) are drawn with economic precision, the political analysis is unflinching, and the structure loops back constantly to the opening moral diagnosis: the conspiracy is a symptom, not a disease.\n\nThe *Bellum Iugurthinum* covers the Jugurthine War of 111-104 BC, in which the Numidian prince Jugurtha bribed, manipulated, and murdered his way to power while the Roman Senate -- corrupted by his gold -- repeatedly failed to act. At 113 chapters it is longer and more complex than the *Catilinae*, with a wider cast and a more elaborate narrative structure. It introduces one of Sallust's great counter-characters: Gaius Marius, the *novus homo* (new man, i.e. first of his family to reach the consulship) who ultimately defeats Jugurtha through competence and energy rather than aristocratic connection. The war is the diagnosis; Marius is the treatment; the treatment, as Sallust implicitly knows, only delays the disease.\n\nThe *Historiae*, covering Roman history from 78 BC onward, survives only in fragments -- speeches and letters extracted by later grammarians, plus scattered quotations. It is a major loss. What survives suggests it was Sallust's most ambitious work: broader in scope, more syntactically dense, and operating at a register of compression that even the *Catilinae* and *Iugurthinum* do not always reach.\n\nWhy history? Sallust explains this directly in the *Catilinae* preface: the *animus* -- the mind, the spirit -- must be put to its highest use, and in a republic corroded by faction and greed, the highest use of an honest mind is to record, analyze, and understand. Writing history is not a consolation prize for a failed political career. It is, Sallust argues, the only form of public service that the current state of Roman politics leaves open to a man who refuses to participate in its corruption. This argument is, as noted, somewhat awkward in light of his biography. It is also, within its own logic, completely coherent. Sallust genuinely believed that historiography mattered -- that the surgical analysis of how republics decay was itself a political act.\n\n---\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\n### Brevitas: the politics of compression\n\nSallust's defining stylistic feature is *brevitas* -- concision taken to the point of aggression. Where Cicero constructs a periodic sentence that builds through multiple subordinate clauses toward a weighted close, Sallust stops before you expect him to. The sentence ends. A new one begins. Something essential has been left unsaid, and you are expected to supply it.\n\nThis is not laziness or carelessness. Sallust is a highly deliberate stylist who has read Thucydides closely enough to understand that compression carries moral weight. A long sentence performs persuasion; a short one performs diagnosis. Sallust is not trying to convince you. He is telling you what happened. The brevity is the tone.\n\nThe practical consequence for the reader is that Sallust's Latin frequently omits words, connectives, and transitions that classical Latin convention would ordinarily supply. Subject pronouns are dropped. Verbs are omitted and must be understood from context. The connective tissue between clauses -- the *enim*, the *autem*, the *igitur* -- is either absent or replaced with a blunt juxtaposition. You move from one statement to the next without being told how they relate. Working out the relationship is your job.\n\n### Inconcinnitas: deliberate asymmetry\n\nIf brevitas is Sallust's rhythm, inconcinnitas is his key signature. Where classical Latin prose -- Cicero above all, but also Caesar -- builds on parallelism and structural symmetry (*et... et*, *aut... aut*, balanced ablative absolutes, paired accusative-infinitive constructions), Sallust systematically breaks the expected pattern. A clause with three elements is followed by a parallel clause with two. A phrase with an adjective is mirrored by one without. The list that should have four members has three; the antithesis that should be exact is slightly off.\n\nThis is not carelessness. It is one of the most sophisticated stylistic choices in Latin literature, and its effect is precisely calibrated: symmetry creates comfort, closure, the sense that the world can be organized into balanced categories. Sallust's asymmetry creates unease. It mimics the actual texture of political reality, where nothing quite balances, where the expected resolution never quite arrives. Reading Sallust, you are always slightly off-footing, always waiting for a structural resolution that is not coming.\n\nThe practical difficulty this creates is severe. A reader trained on Cicero or Caesar learns to use syntactic structure as a parsing guide: you know where you are in the sentence because you know what shape the sentence is supposed to have. Sallust removes that guide. The shape changes. You must parse from first principles, word by word, without the structural scaffolding that more symmetrical Latin provides.\n\n### Archaisms and political vocabulary\n\nSallust writes in an ostentatiously archaic register -- not as archaic as Lucretius or Ennius, but deliberately older than his contemporaries. He uses *lubido* for *libido*, *voltus* for *vultus*, *maxumus* for *maximus*, *optumus* for *optimus*. He favors old political and legal vocabulary: *stipendium*, *imperium*, *magistratus*, *clientelae*, the whole apparatus of republican public life in its formal archaic dress. This is a political choice as much as a stylistic one: the archaic register invokes the authority of the old republic, the mos maiorum, the standards by which the present is being measured and found disastrously wanting.\n\nFor students, the archaisms compound the difficulty in a specific way: many of these forms appear in intermediate-level dictionaries only under their classical spellings, and recognizing *lubido* as *libido* requires either prior exposure or alert attention.\n\n### Ellipsis and missing words\n\nSallust's ellipsis goes beyond the ordinary Latin tolerance for implicit subjects and objects. He omits verbs that must be understood from earlier in the passage; he drops the *esse* that would complete an indirect statement; he writes noun phrases that float grammatically until you work backward to find where they connect. This is not random -- every ellipsis is reconstructable, and working out what is missing is part of reading Sallust correctly. But it demands a higher level of grammatical attention than almost any other prose author in the canon.\n\n### The speeches\n\nSallust embeds formal speeches throughout both works -- the speeches of Catiline, of Caesar and Cato at the senatorial debate over the conspirators' fate (the dramatic heart of the *Catilinae*), of Marius and Adherbal in the *Iugurthinum*. These speeches are not transcriptions. They are Sallustian compositions in which historical figures speak in Sallust's style, making the arguments Sallust wants them to make. The speech of Marius in BJ 85, in which a *novus homo* from the Italian countryside mocks the aristocrats who sneer at his lack of family portraits while being unable to match his record of service, is one of the most brilliant exercises in ironic ventriloquism in Latin prose -- written by a man who was himself a *novus homo*, who had enriched himself through the same provincial system he had Marius implicitly criticize, and who put these words in a general's mouth approximately forty years after the speech was supposedly delivered.\n\nThe Thucydidean model is never far away. Sallust's speeches have the same function as Thucydides's: not to record what was said but to illuminate what was at stake. Understanding this requires knowing Thucydides -- or at minimum understanding that the speeches are analytical instruments, not reportage.\n\n---\n\n## The Moralist and His Mirror: On Sallust's Anger\n\nBefore the excerpts, a word about what Sallust is actually doing when he describes Catiline.\n\nThe four opening chapters of the *De Coniuratione Catilinae* lay out Sallust's thesis: Rome has decayed from the virtuous republic of the ancestors into a state of moral catastrophe. The immediate cause is the destruction of Carthage in 146 BC, which eliminated Rome's external rival and with it the discipline that fear of external danger had imposed. Without an enemy to keep them honest, the Romans became corrupt. Corruption produced men like Catiline.\n\nThis is the analytical frame. But the portrait of Catiline himself (chapters 5 and 15) is something more complex than analytical framing. There is anger in it, and there is something that looks uncomfortably like fascination -- or recognition. Sallust describes Catiline's physical gifts with something close to admiration (*magna vi et animi et corporis*), acknowledges his endurance and his energy, notes his intelligence, and then pivots: *sed ingenio malo pravoque* -- \"but with a character that was evil and perverse.\" The \"but\" carries enormous weight. Sallust is not describing a weakling or a fool. He is describing a genuinely formidable man whose gifts were entirely in the service of destruction.\n\nThe anger underneath the portrait is not the righteous indignation of a shocked moralist. It is the anger of a man who has looked at Rome's political class closely enough to understand how it worked, who has participated in it himself, and who is writing about its failure from inside the very failure he is describing. When Sallust writes about corruption, he is not writing from outside. He is writing from a position of thorough, personal familiarity. The surgical precision of his analysis is the precision of someone who knows where the organs are because he has operated on them himself.\n\nThis is what makes the hidden emotion in the Catiline portrait so hard to pin down: it is not simple disgust, not simple admiration, not simple moral condemnation. It is the portrait of a man who sees in Catiline a kind of dark perfection -- the ultimate product of the system Sallust is diagnosing -- and who cannot quite keep the recognition out of the prose.\n\n---\n\n## Latin Excerpts\n\n### Excerpt 1: De Coniuratione Catilinae 5 -- The Portrait of Catiline\n\n**Source:** Sallust, *De Coniuratione Catilinae*, chapter 5, from Lacus Curtius / penelope.uchicago.edu\n\n**Context:** Chapter 5 is Sallust's formal *characterisatio* of Catiline -- the compressed moral and physical portrait that sets the reader's frame for everything that follows. It arrives immediately after the moral digression on Rome's decay, so the reader already knows what a corrupted republic produces before Sallust names his protagonist. The portrait does not disappoint: five sections, no wasted word, and a closing image that makes Catiline sound less like a conspirator and more like a force of nature gone badly wrong.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> L. Catilina, nobili genere natus, fuit magna vi et animi et corporis, sed ingenio malo pravoque. Huic ab adulescentia bella intestina, caedes, rapinae, discordia civilis grata fuere, ibique iuventutem suam exercuit. Corpus patiens inediae, algoris, vigiliae supra quam cuiquam credibile est. Animus audax, subdolus, varius, cuius rei lubet simulator ac dissimulator, alieni appetens, sui profusus, ardens in cupiditatibus; satis eloquentiae, sapientiae parum. Vastus animus immoderata, incredibilia, nimis alta semper cupiebat.\n>\n> Hunc post dominationem L. Sullae lubido maxuma invaserat rei publicae capiundae; neque id quibus modis adsequeretur, dum sibi regnum pararet, quicquam pensi habebat. Agitabatur magis magisque in dies animus ferox inopia rei familiaris et conscientia scelerum, quae utraque iis artibus auxerat, quas supra memoravi. Incitabant praeterea corrupti civitatis mores, quos pessuma ac divorsa inter se mala, luxuria atque avaritia, vexabant.\n\n*(DCC 5.1-8)*\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nLucius Catiline, born of a noble family, possessed great force of both mind and body, but a character that was evil and perverse. From youth, civil war, murder, plunder, and political strife were welcome to him, and it was in these that he exercised his young years. His body could endure hunger, cold, and sleeplessness to a degree beyond what anyone would believe. His mind was bold, devious, and changeable -- capable of pretending to be anything or dissembling anything as occasion required. Greedy for others' property, wasteful of his own, burning in his desires; sufficiently eloquent, with very little wisdom. His vast spirit always craved things immoderate, unbelievable, impossibly high.\n\nAfter the domination of Sulla, an overwhelming desire had seized him to seize control of the republic; and he had no scruple about what methods he used, provided he was securing power for himself. His fierce spirit was driven on more and more each day by poverty of private means and by awareness of his crimes -- both of which he had increased through the same practices I described above. He was further incited by the corrupted morals of the state, which were being torn apart by two equally ruinous but opposite vices: extravagance and greed.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe opening clause announces the structure of the entire portrait: *nobili genere natus* (noble birth), *magna vi et animi et corporis* (great force of mind and body), then the pivot: *sed ingenio malo pravoque*. Everything Sallust will say about Catiline flows from this \"but.\" He is not a deficient man. He is a man whose genuine force has been put entirely in the service of the wrong ends.\n\nWhat follows is a masterclass in Sallustian brevitas and inconcinnitas. *Corpus patiens inediae, algoris, vigiliae supra quam cuiquam credibile est.* Three nouns (hunger, cold, sleeplessness) in asyndeton, followed by a comparative clause that does not resolve into a main verb -- just the bare statement of endurance beyond belief, hanging in the air. Then: *Animus audax, subdolus, varius* -- three adjectives -- then a relative clause, then four participial phrases: *cuius rei lubet simulator ac dissimulator, alieni appetens, sui profusus, ardens in cupiditatibus.* The structural parallel with the *corpus* sentence never fully materializes -- the *corpus* sentence has three nouns and a clause; the *animus* sentence has three adjectives, a relative, and four participials. You keep waiting for the shape to balance. It does not.\n\n*Satis eloquentiae, sapientiae parum.* Two nouns in the genitive of quality, no verb. \"Enough eloquence, too little wisdom.\" This is not neutral reporting. It is contempt delivered in six words. And then *Vastus animus immoderata, incredibilia, nimis alta semper cupiebat.* Note the word *vastus*: in Latin it means \"vast\" but also \"desolate,\" \"ravaged,\" \"barren\" -- the adjective for deserts and ruins. A *vastus animus* is not just an ambitious soul. It is an empty one, hollowed out by its own limitless wanting.\n\nSections 6-8 shift the portrait from static description to dynamic causation -- Sallust moves from what Catiline is to why he acts when he does. Section 6 locates the decisive moment: *post dominationem L. Sullae.* Sulla's dictatorship (82-79 BC) is the hinge. It proved that one man could seize the Roman state by force, and Catiline took the lesson. *Lubido* (the archaic form of *libido*) -- desire, appetite, drive -- is the word Sallust gives to this ambition, and it is not a neutral word: throughout the *Catilinae* and *Iugurthinum*, *lubido* marks corrupted motivation, the irrational want that overrides reason and law. *Neque id quibus modis adsequeretur... quicquam pensi habebat*: he had \"no scruple\" about the means -- the phrase *quicquam pensi habere* (literally \"to have anything weighed,\" hence \"to care about anything\") is a colloquial legal expression for moral accountability. He had none.\n\nSection 7 names the two internal drivers with characteristic compression: *inopia rei familiaris* (poverty of private means) and *conscientia scelerum* (awareness of crimes). Neither is explained; neither needs to be. The *conscientia scelerum* is especially devastating: it refers back to crimes already committed (the ones the reader will not learn about until chapter 15 and beyond) and presents them not as a confession but as a structural force. The crimes drive the ambition further. The worse Catiline has become, the more he needs to win.\n\nSection 8 broadens the frame to Rome itself: *corrupti civitatis mores* torn by *luxuria atque avaritia* -- two words paired as opposites (*divorsa inter se mala*). Extravagance spends; greed hoards. They seem antithetical, but Sallust argues they are equally destructive manifestations of the same underlying disease: the subordination of civic virtue to private appetite. Catiline is, in this reading, not the cause of Rome's crisis. He is its most perfect symptom.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 2: Bellum Iugurthinum 85 (selected) -- Marius Addresses the Roman People\n\n**Source:** Sallust, *Bellum Iugurthinum*, chapter 85 (selected sections), from Splash Latino (splashlatino.it)\n\n**Context:** It is 107 BC. Gaius Marius, newly elected consul despite having no family connections among the Roman nobility, is about to take command of the war in Numidia that the aristocratic commanders have been managing incompetently for years. Before leaving, he addresses the Roman people in a contio -- a public meeting -- justifying his election and attacking the senatorial class that resents it.\n\nThe speech is not a historical transcript. It is a Sallustian composition: Marius speaks in Sallust's style, makes Sallust's arguments, and serves Sallust's analytical purpose, which is to show that the Roman nobility's contempt for talent over lineage is itself a form of the corruption that is destroying the republic. The irony runs deep: Sallust, a *novus homo* from Amiternum who survived politics by attaching himself to powerful patrons and enriched himself through a province, is writing a *novus homo*'s attack on aristocratic privilege.\n\n**Latin Text (selected):**\n\n> Scio ego, Quirites, plerosque non isdem artibus imperium a vobis petere et, postquam adepti sunt, gerere: primo industrios supplices modicos esse, dein per ignaviam et superbiam aetatem agere. Sed mihi contra ea videtur.\n\n> [...]\n\n> Ad hoc, alii si deliquere, vetus nobilitas, maiorum fortia facta, cognatorum et affinium opes, multae clientelae, omnia haec praesidio assunt; mihi spes omnes in memet sitae, quas necesse est virtute et innocentia tutari; nam alia infirma sunt.\n\n> Bellum me gerere cum Iugurtha iussistis, quam rem nobilitas aegerrime tulit. Quaeso, reputate cum animis vestris, num id mutare melius sit, si quem ex illo globo nobilitatis ad hoc aut aliud tale negotium mittatis, hominem veteris prosapiae ac multarum imaginum et nullius stipendi: scilicet ut in tanta re ignarus omnium trepidet, festinet, sumat aliquem ex populo monitorem offici sui. Ita plerumque evenit, ut, quem vos imperare iussistis, is sibi imperatorem alium quaerat.\n\n> Atque ego scio, Quirites, qui, postquam consules facti sunt, et acta maiorum et Graecorum militaria praecepta legere coeperint: praeposteri homines, nam gerere quam fieri tempore posterius, re atque usu prius est.\n\n> Comparate nunc, Quirites, cum illorum superbia me hominem novum. Quae illi audire aut legere solent, eorum partem vidi, alia egomet gessi: quae illi litteris, ea ego militando didici.\n\n*(BJ 85, selected)*\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nI know, Romans, that most men use one set of qualities to seek power from you and another to wield it once they have it: at first they are industrious, deferential, restrained; then they spend their time in laziness and arrogance. But to me the opposite seems right.\n\n[...]\n\nOn top of this, when others commit mistakes, they have ancient noble lineage, the brave deeds of their ancestors, the resources of their relatives and in-laws, many networks of clients -- all of these stand ready as protection. For me, all my hopes rest in myself alone, and I must defend them through virtue and integrity; for I have no other resources.\n\nYou ordered me to wage war against Jugurtha -- a commission the nobility took very badly. I ask you to consider in your own minds whether it would be better to change this, and send instead one of that cluster of the nobility: a man of ancient lineage and many ancestral portrait masks, and of zero military service. So that in so great a matter, ignorant of everything, he stumbles, rushes about, and takes some man from among the people as a tutor in his own duties. It usually turns out this way: the man you ordered to command goes looking for another commander for himself.\n\nAnd I am fully aware, Romans, of those who, after being elected consuls, have begun reading the records of their ancestors and the military manuals of the Greeks. Men who do things in the wrong order -- for commanding comes later in time than being made a commander, but earlier in the reality and practice of the job.\n\nCompare me now, Romans -- a new man -- with their arrogance. What they are accustomed to hear about or read, some of it I have seen, and the rest I have carried out myself. What they know from books, I have learned in the field.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe opening is a textbook demonstration of Sallust's inconcinnitas. The first sentence establishes a contrast: *non isdem artibus... petere et... gerere* -- \"not with the same qualities to seek and to wield.\" The secondary clause -- *primo industrios supplices modicos esse, dein per ignaviam et superbiam aetatem agere* -- lists three adjectives in the first half (*industrios, supplices, modicos*) and two nouns in the second (*per ignaviam et superbiam*). Three vs. two, adjectives vs. abstract nouns, parallel but not equal. Then: *Sed mihi contra ea videtur.* Six words. No elaboration. The contrast is announced; the proof is the rest of the speech.\n\nThe passage on the nobility's safety net vs. Marius's total exposure is the analytical heart. The list of the nobility's protections -- *vetus nobilitas, maiorum fortia facta, cognatorum et affinium opes, multae clientelae* -- accumulates through four noun phrases in asyndeton, then lands on *omnia haec praesidio assunt*. The parallel for Marius: *mihi spes omnes in memet sitae.* One clause. One thing. Himself. Then: *quas necesse est virtute et innocentia tutari.* And the close: *nam alia infirma sunt.* Three words. This is inconcinnitas as argument: the structural imbalance between the two halves of the comparison is the argument.\n\nThe two central paragraphs move from defense to attack. *Bellum me gerere cum Iugurtha iussistis, quam rem nobilitas aegerrime tulit* -- the pivot from justifying himself to going after the nobility directly. The description that follows is devastating satire: a noble commander who is *ignarus omnium*, who *trepidat*, who *festinat*, and who has to hire a commoner to explain his own job to him. *Ita plerumque evenit, ut quem vos imperare iussistis, is sibi imperatorem alium quaerat* -- \"the man you ordered to command goes looking for another commander.\" The sentence turns on itself with Sallustian elegance: *imperare iussistis* (you ordered him to command) against *imperatorem alium quaerat* (he looks for another to command him). Same root, inverted roles.\n\nThe *praeposteri homines* paragraph is a compressed gem. *Praeposteri* means \"doing things in the wrong order\" (literally \"with the later part in front\"), which is exactly the charge: they read the military manuals *after* getting the consulship. *Nam gerere quam fieri tempore posterius, re atque usu prius est* -- \"commanding comes later in time than being made a commander, but earlier in the reality and practice.\" The chiasmus (*tempore posterius / re atque usu prius*) is one of those rare moments when Sallust allows himself a symmetrical structure -- but he loads it with *re atque usu* (two nouns) against the single *tempore*, so even here the balance is slightly off.\n\nThe finale (*Comparate nunc*) closes the argument. *Quae illi audire aut legere solent, eorum partem vidi, alia egomet gessi*: they passively receive information (hear or read); he has witnessed and he has acted -- two verbs against one gerund phrase, experience against text. Then the one-liner: *quae illi litteris, ea ego militando didici.* The verb (*didicerunt*) is dropped from the first clause; you supply it from *didici* at the end. Ten words. The entire argument in summary. Sallust at his most concentrated.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nSallust's immediate successors knew exactly what he had done. Livy, writing a generation later, engaged with him as a predecessor worth measuring against -- and occasionally disagreeing with on matters of fact. Quintilian, a century after that, placed him as the Latin equivalent of Thucydides, the highest compliment ancient literary criticism offered to a historical prose writer. The most consequential inheritor was Tacitus. In the late first and early second century AD, Tacitus took Sallust's methods and drove them to their logical extreme: Sallust's *brevitas* became Tacitean compression; Sallust's inconcinnitas became the unnerving asymmetry of the Annals; Sallust's moralizing digressions became the irony woven into every sentence of the Histories. Ancient critics noticed the connection explicitly. The phrase \"Sallustianus Crispus\" appears in ancient sources as shorthand for the compressed, adversarial, moralizing historical mode. Tacitus is its fullest realization, but Sallust built the template.\n\nThe *Horti Sallustiani*, the monument to his provincial extortion, outlasted him by centuries. Augustus purchased them and they became imperial property. They appear in ancient sources through the late empire and were probably destroyed in the Visigothic sack of Rome in 410 AD -- which would have given Sallust, had he been around, considerable material about the wages of *avaritia*.\n\nHis texts survived the Middle Ages in good shape, which is not a given. Medieval readers valued the DCC in particular as a source on late republican history and as a model of compressed Latin. Renaissance humanists found in him a compatible sensibility: sharp analytical vision, contempt for institutional corruption, willingness to write about power without flattering it. Machiavelli read him; the influence is not quotable in the direct sense, but the shared analytical method -- history as the study of how power is acquired, maintained, and lost through moral failure -- is unmistakable.\n\nIn Latin education, the DCC remains one of the most widely taught texts in the advanced curriculum, typically introduced after Caesar and Cicero when students are ready for something that will genuinely challenge them. Nothing in those earlier authors prepares a student for the first sentence of chapter 5, and the recalibration it forces -- parse without structural prediction, follow without symmetry, supply what is missing -- is itself a form of education. Students who have read Sallust know something about Latin that students who have not read Sallust do not know. The lesson is not comfortable. That is the point.\n\n---\n\n## Proposed Tier\n\n**Tier: S (God Please Help)**\n\nThis decision requires explanation, because the case for high A is real and the user has framed it that way. Here is why S is correct.\n\nThe S tier is reserved for texts that combine maximum difficulty across multiple criteria simultaneously. Sallust qualifies on every axis -- and the axes compound each other in a way that is specific to his style.\n\n**Lexicon:** Archaic forms (*lubido*, *voltus*, *maxumus*, *optumus*), specialized republican political vocabulary (*stipendium*, *clientelae*, *mos maiorum*), and Thucydidean calques (deliberate Latin translations of Greek analytical vocabulary). None of these are uniquely extreme, but they add up.\n\n**Syntactic complexity -- inconcinnitas and brevitas:** This is where Sallust earns S. The combination of deliberate asymmetry with deliberate compression means that the two standard tools students use to parse Latin prose -- (1) predict the structure from its opening, (2) use symmetry to align parallel elements -- are both actively undermined. You cannot predict the structure because Sallust changes it. You cannot use symmetry to align elements because the elements are deliberately misaligned. Every sentence must be parsed from scratch, without scaffolding. This is syntactic difficulty of a qualitatively different kind from what authors like Caesar or even Cicero present.\n\n**Stylistic figures:** Sallust uses ellipsis systematically, asyndeton relentlessly, and chiasmus only to break it. The effect is a prose texture that requires the reader to be both very fast (following the rhythm) and very slow (recovering the omitted elements). Doing both simultaneously is hard.\n\n**Allusion density and political subtext:** Understanding Sallust fully requires knowing Thucydides (whose compressed, asymmetric Greek Sallust is consciously adapting), Cato the Elder (whose archaisms Sallust selectively imports), and the entire political landscape of the late republic (without which the ironies in the speeches and moral digressions are simply invisible). This is not ordinary allusion -- it is a sustained stylistic and intellectual conversation with specific predecessors that most students will not be equipped to follow.\n\n**Why not just high A?** High A (the Lucretius position) is appropriate for texts that are very difficult on multiple criteria but that ultimately want to be understood -- that clear paths through their difficulty for the reader who has done the work. Sallust does not do this. His style is adversarial. The inconcinnitas is not just an obstacle; it is a message: reality is not symmetrical, and the reader who comes to Sallust expecting Ciceronian comfort will not get it. There is no reward at the end of the parsing process equivalent to Lucretius's beautiful arguments or Caesar's elegant clarity. There is just more Sallust.\n\nTacitus, who studied Sallust with enough devotion that later critics called him a student of his master, takes these techniques further -- more compression, more archaism, more brutal ellipsis. The Annals are S tier with no debate. Sallust in his extant works does not quite reach Tacitean density. But he operates in the same register, and the difficulty is the same in kind if slightly less extreme in degree. S is correct.\n\n---\n\n# GAIUS VALERIUS CATULLUS (c. 84--54 BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nGaius Valerius Catullus was born around 84 BC in Verona, in Cisalpine Gaul -- technically not yet Roman Italy, but wealthy and connected enough that his father moved comfortably in Roman circles. Connected enough, in fact, to host Julius Caesar when the general passed through the region on official business. This must have made for interesting dinners, given what Catullus would later write about Caesar -- which was not, to put it diplomatically, a glowing endorsement.\n\nAt some point in his twenties Catullus moved to Rome, fell into its literary scene, and fell -- with the force of someone pushed off a cliff -- in love with a woman he called \"Lesbia\" in his poems. The pseudonym was not subtle: Lesbia = Sappho's island = the greatest lyric poet the ancient world had known. The identification behind the mask is almost certain: Clodia Metelli, wife of the consul Quintus Caecilius Metellus Celer, sister of the demagogue Publius Clodius Pulcher, and -- in Cicero's immortal description in the *Pro Caelio* -- \"a Palatine Medea\" and \"a two-penny Clytemnestra.\" She was older, politically connected, brilliant, and apparently devastating. Catullus was finished from the start.\n\nThe relationship produced poetry the way a fire produces heat -- as a natural byproduct of something that could not help burning. Over what seems to have been several years, Catullus wrote about Lesbia in every emotional register: adoration (*da mi basia mille* -- give me a thousand kisses), jealousy, wounded pride, contempt, and the terrible lucidity of *odi et amo* (\"I hate and I love\") -- a whole philosophy of feeling compressed into three words and one pentameter.\n\nWhat turned the affair into poetry rather than gossip is the way Catullus thought about it. He did not describe Lesbia only as a lover. He described what they had as a *foedus*, a sworn pact, using the words Romans kept for treaties between states and for the trust between friends and partners: *fides*, good faith; *pietas*, dutiful loyalty; *amicitia*, friendship. So when she took other lovers, he wrote about it as a broken contract as well as a broken heart, and he kept score: he had kept faith, she had not. That borrowed vocabulary of Roman obligation is the key to his love poems, and the section on his lexicon below explains how it works.\n\nA note on his temperament: Catullus is that friend who, if a mosquito brushes against him, does not swat it. He waits, picks up a stylus, and within the hour has set the mosquito on fire in eleven-syllable lines -- with mythological references, a formal dedication, and a curse on the mosquito's entire lineage. Carmina 16, 36, 37, 39, 40, 54 -- the list of people and things he publicly immolated in verse is long, and the quality of the invective is, despite (or because of) the occasions, often extraordinary.\n\nThere was grief alongside the fire. His brother died young, far from home, buried near the Troad in the shadow of Troy. This loss surfaces throughout the poems -- at the end of an elegy about Lesbia, in the middle of a learned mythological poem -- like a bruise you keep finding. In 57 BC, Catullus traveled to Bithynia (modern Turkey) on the staff of the governor Gaius Memmius -- yes, the same Memmius to whom Lucretius had recently dedicated the *De Rerum Natura*. Catullus went hoping to make money; Memmius gave him essentially nothing. He did visit his brother's grave and produced what may be the finest short elegy in Latin.\n\nHe died around 54 BC, probably before thirty. He left one book.\n\n---\n\n## Most Important Works\n\nThe *Liber Catulli* -- the Book of Catullus -- is a collection of 116 carmina organized in three broad groups, probably assembled by an editor working from Catullus's papers after his death.\n\n**Carmina 1-60, the polymetric poems:** Short lyrics in varied meters, dominated by the Phalecian hendecasyllable. The range within this group is remarkable: from playful trifles (dinner invitations, affectionate jibes at friends) to savage invective to some of the most luminous love poetry in Latin. This is where most readers meet Catullus first, and rightly so.\n\n**Carmina 61-68, the carmina docta (learned poems):** Longer, formally ambitious, metrically diverse, packed with literary allusion. Two epithalamia (wedding hymns), the great *epyllion* of carme 64 (the wedding of Peleus and Thetis, with its famous digression on Ariadne abandoned on Naxos), and the complex elegies of 65-68. This is the neoteric program operating at full scale.\n\n**Carmina 69-116, the epigrammatic poems:** Elegiac couplets, mostly short, ranging from love poems to pointed social observation to outright abuse. The tone shifts markedly here toward the sharper, more compressed mode.\n\nRecurring themes across all three groups: love (Lesbia above all), friendship (the beautiful carme 50 to Calvus), grief (the brother poems), literary rivalry, invective, wit, and the persistent project of giving small things the full weight they deserve.\n\n---\n\n## The Neoteroi: Poetry About Everything\n\nThe word *neoteroi* (νεώτεροι, \"the newer ones\") comes from Cicero, who applied it to a cohort of poets turning their backs on Roman epic ambitions and writing polished, personal, Alexandrian-influenced verse. Cicero did not mean it as a compliment. Catullus wore it as a badge.\n\nThe intellectual foundation was Callimachus, the great Hellenistic poet and chief librarian of Alexandria (c. 310-240 BC), who famously held that \"a big book is a big evil\" (μέγα βιβλίον μέγα κακόν). Against the vast gravity-laden epic in the Ennian tradition, Callimachus championed the short, refined, densely allusive poem -- technically perfect, personally voiced. The neoteroi adopted this program wholesale: brevity over grandeur, polish over volume, the individual over the civic.\n\nIn practice, this meant stepping away from political and celebratory poetry. Catullus was not interested in Caesar's Gallic campaigns (though he mentions Caesar -- dismissively). He was interested in a sparrow's death, in the pleasure of a dinner, in his brother's grave, in the exact texture of falling out of love.\n\nCatullus makes his position explicit. In carme 36 he consigns Volusius's *Annales* to the flames, calling them *cacata carta* -- a phrase that translates roughly as \"toilet-paper verse.\" In carme 95 he praises Cinna's *Zmyrna* (nine years in the making, Callimachean to the core) over Volusius's *Annales*, which will \"serve to wrap mackerel.\" The joke is pointed: epic scope is not the measure of a poem's value.\n\nBut the deeper argument -- the one that makes the neoteroi a movement rather than a fashion -- is about dignity. When Catullus writes an epic-scale lament for a sparrow, he is not being playfully ironic. He is making an argument: love's objects deserve love's full attention, and a poet who brings the complete weight of his craft to a small subject is doing more serious work than one who applies lazy grandeur to a large one. The dignity of a poem is not in its subject; it is in what the poet does with it. Every subject is deserving of poetry. Poetry about everyday life deserves the same dignity as an epic about Troy.\n\n---\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nReading Catullus is not one experience but three.\n\nThe **polymetric poems** (carmina 1-60) are among the most accessible entry points in all of classical Latin. The Phalecian hendecasyllable -- eleven syllables, with a predictable rhythmic pattern once internalized -- is one of the friendlier lyric meters to work with. Syntax is direct, vocabulary is mostly standard literary Latin leavened with colloquialisms, and the emotional content is immediate. A student who has finished Caesar can, with modest effort, read a large portion of carmina 1-60. This is not a slight against Catullus; it is an achievement. He makes the difficult look effortless.\n\nThe **epigrammatic poems** (69-116) are similarly navigable. Elegiac couplets (hexameter + pentameter) structure the reading, and the compression here comes from irony and implication rather than syntactic obscurity. The challenge is tonal -- Catullus can shift from bitter to tender to obscene within a single poem -- rather than grammatical.\n\nThe **carmina docta** (61-68) are a different matter. Carme 64 in particular -- 408 lines of dactylic hexameter -- is dense with Alexandrian allusion, extended periodic syntax, rare vocabulary, and a narrative structure that deliberately interrupts itself and resumes from unexpected angles. The difficulty here approaches the lower end of B tier: not because the Latin is hostile (it does not have Tacitus's venom or Horace's compression) but because navigating it fully requires both grammatical competence and a working knowledge of Hellenistic mythology and literary tradition.\n\nThroughout the collection, Catullus's language is one of its most distinctive features. He ranges from elevated poetic diction to the warm softening of diminutives (*ocelli* for eyes, *miselle* for \"poor little,\" *turgiduli* for \"slightly swollen,\" the onomatopoeic *pipiabat* for a sparrow's chirp) to Greek-derived coinages (*basiationes*, \"little kisses\"; *venustiorum*, \"of more refined persons\") to outright obscenity. Carme 16 opens with a line so explicit it was expurgated from most printed editions until the mid-twentieth century -- and is simultaneously a poetic manifesto. Catullus argues that the poet himself must be decent, but his poems have no such obligation. This is not shock for its own sake; it is a defense of the right to write about everything, at any register, with full artistic force. The sparrow and the obscenity sit on the same spectrum: both assert that whatever the poet takes seriously deserves to be taken seriously by the poem.\n\n### The Lexicon of Love: fides, foedus, amare, bene velle\n\nThe words in Catullus look easy, and that is the trap. Almost every one of them is in a first-year dictionary: *amare*, *fides*, *foedus*, *pietas*, *bene velle*. But Catullus takes the ordinary vocabulary of Roman life, above all the vocabulary of its public duties, and gives it a private meaning. A reader who translates the dictionary sense will understand every word and miss the poem.\n\nThe key move is to describe love as a contract. In Roman society *fides* was good faith, the trust that held together patron and client, allies, and partners in business. A *foedus* was a treaty sworn between states, sacred and binding. *Pietas* was the dutiful loyalty owed to the gods, to family and to country. *Officium* and *gratia* were the services friends did for each other and the gratitude those services earned, and *iniuria* was a wrong done against what was owed. No Roman poet before Catullus used this language for an affair with a married woman. He does: he calls his love for Lesbia *aeternum hoc sanctae foedus amicitiae*, this everlasting pact of holy friendship (Carmen 109); he boasts that no one ever kept *fides* in a *foedus* as he did (Carmen 87); and when it ends, he claims *pietas* for himself and asks the gods to repay it (Carmen 76). Her betrayal is not only heartbreak but a breach of contract, and the poems put it in exactly those terms.\n\nThe second key is the pair *amare* and *bene velle*. In ordinary Latin both can mean \"to love\". Catullus pulls them apart. *Amare* becomes desire, passion, the physical side of love; *bene velle*, to wish someone well, becomes affection and esteem, the loyal love of a friend, or of a father for his sons and sons-in-law, which is the comparison Carmen 72 makes. The bitter discovery of the epigrams is that betrayal increases the first and destroys the second: it forces a lover *amare magis, sed bene velle minus*, to love more but to wish well less (Carmina 72 and 75). *Odi et amo*, I hate and I love (Carmen 85), is the same idea in two verbs.\n\nThe third group is the vocabulary of style and charm: *Venus* and its adjective *venustus*, *lepos* and *lepidus*, *sal* (wit, literally salt), *urbanus*, *elegans*, and their opposites *invenustus*, *illepidus*, *insulsus*. They measure a poem, a joke and a person on a single scale, and Catullus uses them as badges of his circle (Carmen 10 judges a girl with them, Carmen 13 invites a friend with them). Add *nugae*, trifles, his own name for his poems; *otium*, free time, the space in which he writes and loves, which he warns himself has destroyed kings (Carmen 51); and the soft diminutives, *ocelli*, *labella*, *misellus*, *solaciolum*, which mark either tenderness or mockery. That is most of the private dictionary. None of it is hard the way Lucretius's technical vocabulary is hard. It is hard because the words mean more than they say, and because a student has to unlearn the dictionary sense to hear them. That is why the Lexicon bar for Catullus sits at Challenging even though his syntax, in most of the book, is easy.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 1: Carmina 3 -- The Death of Lesbia's Sparrow\n\n**Source:** The Latin Library (thelatinlibrary.com/catullus.shtml)\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Lugete, o Veneres Cupidinesque,\n> et quantum est hominum venustiorum:\n> passer mortuus est meae puellae,\n> passer, deliciae meae puellae,\n> quem plus illa oculis suis amabat.\n> nam mellitus erat suamque norat\n> ipsam tam bene quam puella matrem,\n> nec sese a gremio illius movebat,\n> sed circumsiliens modo huc modo illuc\n> ad solam dominam usque pipiabat.\n> qui nunc it per iter tenebricosum\n> illuc, unde negant redire quemquam.\n> at vobis male sit, malae tenebrae\n> Orci, quae omnia bella devoratis:\n> tam bellum mihi passerem abstulistis.\n> o factum male! o miselle passer!\n> tua nunc opera meae puellae\n> flendo turgiduli rubent ocelli.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nMourn, all you Venuses and Cupids,\nand everyone of any refinement:\nthe sparrow of my girl is dead --\nthe sparrow, darling of my girl,\nwhom she loved more than her own eyes.\nHe was sweet as honey and knew his mistress\nas well as a girl knows her own mother;\nhe never stirred from her lap,\nbut hopping about now here, now there,\nhe chirped always to his mistress alone.\nNow he travels the dark road\nto where, they say, none return.\nCurses on you, wicked shadows\nof Orcus, who devour all lovely things:\nyou have taken from me so lovely a sparrow.\nO dreadful deed! O poor little sparrow!\nBecause of you my girl's\nlittle eyes are red and swollen from weeping.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe poem opens like an epic invocation: \"Lugete, o Veneres Cupidinesque\" -- mourn, divine powers of love. The same register in which a Roman poet might summon the Muses before a battle, Catullus deploys to summon the Venuses and Cupids before a bird's death. This is the neoteric manifesto in miniature: the scale of treatment is dictated not by the subject's importance in the cosmic order, but by its importance to the lover.\n\nThe first ten lines are almost playful in their tenderness. The sparrow \"knew its mistress as well as a girl knows her own mother\" (*norat ipsam tam bene quam puella matrem*); it hopped \"now here, now there\" (*modo huc modo illuc* -- the repeated *modo* mimicking quick darting motion); it \"chirped always to its mistress alone\" (*ad solam dominam usque pipiabat* -- the onomatopoeic *pipiabat* being one of the most charming verbs in Latin). Diminutives pile up and soften: *deliciae* (darling), *mellitus* (honey-sweet), and at the end *miselle* (poor little), *turgiduli* (slightly swollen), *ocelli* (little eyes). The language is deliberately small-scaled, tender, almost childlike.\n\nThen line 11 turns: *qui nunc it per iter tenebricosum* -- \"who now travels the dark road.\" The imagery is straight from epic death poetry; the underworld here is the same place from which no hero returns, described with the gravity appropriate to fallen warriors. The joke is not that this is funny. It is that it works. The sparrow's death earns the language, because the grief it causes is genuine.\n\nThe final two lines are the whole point: *flendo turgiduli rubent ocelli* -- \"from weeping, her little eyes are swollen and red.\" Catullus is not grieving the sparrow. He is grieving because Lesbia is grieving. The sparrow matters because Lesbia matters. And Lesbia's tears are, to Catullus, a catastrophe of the first order. The epic machinery was never about the bird; it was always about her.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 2: Carmina 101 -- A Brother's Grave\n\n**Source:** The Latin Library (thelatinlibrary.com/catullus.shtml)\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Multas per gentes et multa per aequora vectus\n> advenio has miseras, frater, ad inferias,\n> ut te postremo donarem munere mortis\n> et mutam nequiquam alloquerer cinerem,\n> quandoquidem fortuna mihi tete abstulit ipsum,\n> heu miser indigne frater adempte mihi,\n> nunc tamen interea haec, prout ea consuetudo,\n> tradita sunt tristi munere ad inferias,\n> accipe fraterno multum manantia fletu,\n> atque in perpetuum, frater, ave atque vale.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nCarried through many peoples and many seas,\nI arrive here, brother, at these wretched rites,\nto give you the last gift that death allows\nand speak in vain to your silent ashes.\nSince fortune has taken you, yourself, from me --\nO poor brother, torn from me undeservedly --\nnow at least these offerings, as old custom has it,\nare given as a sad tribute at funeral rites.\nReceive them, flowing with a brother's many tears,\nand forever, brother: hail and farewell.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThis is an entirely different Catullus -- no wit, no sparrows, no diminutives, no fire. The poem opens in the register of epic: *multas per gentes et multa per aequora vectus* scans almost as a dactylic hexameter, and the word *vectus* (\"carried\") immediately sets a passive, grief-laden tone. Catullus has been carried by circumstances, by fate, by the fact of his brother's death, to this distant grave. The word order places \"many peoples\" and \"many seas\" before the speaker arrives; the journey absorbs the first line before the act of arriving occupies the second.\n\nThe formal structure is ten lines of elegiac couplets -- entirely controlled, the emotion contained within strict form as grief tends to be in Roman poetry. The contrast between the scale of the journey (many peoples, many seas) and the smallness of the arrival (*has miseras inferias* -- \"these wretched funeral offerings\") is the poem's structural spine. He has come so far to give so little. The ashes cannot speak back: *mutam nequiquam alloquerer cinerem* -- the ashes are *mutam* (silent, feminine singular modifying *cinerem*), the speaking *nequiquam* (in vain). The whole poem is a necessary futility.\n\nLine 5 has the emphatic *tete* -- the doubled form of *te*, used for stress -- \"fortune has taken *you yourself* from me,\" underlining the irreplaceability of the loss. Line 6 adds *indigne*: not \"my brother has died\" but \"my brother has been wronged by death,\" the passive participle *adempte* (\"taken away\") casting death as a theft.\n\nThe final line is the whole poem in ten syllables: *atque in perpetuum, frater, ave atque vale.* The Roman greeting (*ave*, said on meeting) and the Roman farewell (*vale*, said on parting) in the same breath -- because there will be no more meetings after this. This one farewell must stand for all time. The repetition of *frater* (brother) across the poem -- three times in ten lines -- is insistent, a word summoned against silence.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 3: Carmina 64.1--7 -- The Opening of the Peleus and Thetis Epyllion\n\n**Source:** The Latin Library (thelatinlibrary.com/catullus.shtml)\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Peliaco quondam prognatae vertice pinus\n> dicuntur liquidas Neptuni nasse per undas\n> Phasidos ad fluctus et fines Aeetaeos,\n> cum lecti iuvenes, Argiuae robora pubis,\n> auratam optantes Colchis avertere pellem\n> ausi sunt vada salsa cita decurrere puppi,\n> caerula verrentes abiegnis aequora palmis.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nThe pines once born on the summit of Pelion\nare said to have swum through Neptune's clear waters\nto the streams of Phasis and the Aeetaean bounds,\nwhen the chosen young men, the strength of Argive youth,\nhoping to carry off the golden fleece from Colchis,\ndared to race across the salt shallows in their swift ship,\nsweeping the blue waters with oars of silver fir.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nSeven lines, one main verb. *Ausi sunt... decurrere* (\"they dared to race\") does not arrive until line 6; everything before it is subordination -- relative clause, indirect statement, purpose participle, temporal clause -- piling context on context before the act itself. This is the Alexandrian mode at its most characteristic: the interest is not in the event but in the texture and weight of the event. Every element of the Argo's launch matters: the timber (pines from Pelion's summit), the sea (Neptune's clear waters), the destination (Phasis, and \"the Aeetaean bounds\" -- Aeetes, king of Colchis, father of Medea, named obliquely and without explanation), the crew (chosen young men, the *robora* -- the oaken strength -- of Argive youth), the goal (the golden fleece), and finally the image of the oars themselves: *caerula verrentes abiegnis aequora palmis* -- \"sweeping the blue waters with oars of silver fir,\" where *palmis* (literally \"palms of the hand,\" hence oar-blades) is precisely the kind of precise, unexpected image that marks a Callimachean poet.\n\nCompared to the short poems, the differences are immediate. The periodic syntax requires holding an unresolved sentence in mind for six lines before it closes. The mythological references require background (who is Aeetes? what is the Phasian shore?). The diction is elevated throughout, with none of the colloquial warmth of the polymetrics. This is why the carmina docta push toward B tier even as the bulk of Catullus sits comfortably at C: here he is writing in a different mode entirely, for a reader who has done the preparation.\n\n---\n\n## The Neoteric Circle: Brief Notes\n\nThe poets in Catullus's circle shared his Callimachean program but survive today largely as names and fragments -- which is precisely why they produce only one answer in a difficulty ranking.\n\n**Gaius Licinius Calvus** (c. 82-47 BC): Catullus's closest friend and poetic alter ego. A respected orator (Cicero rated him highly as a rival at the bar) who also wrote personal verse, including love poems to a woman named Quintilia. Catullus writes warmly and admiringly to him in carme 14, 50, and 96. Almost nothing of his poetry survives -- a handful of fragments quoted by later grammarians for linguistic interest, enough to confirm the style but not to assess it. **Tier: NC.**\n\n**Marcus Furius Bibaculus** (c. 103 -- after 27 BC): The senior figure in the neoteric set, known for satirical epigrams that apparently included pointed material about Julius Caesar. A few fragments survive, preserved mainly for their linguistic curiosity rather than literary completeness. **Tier: NC.**\n\n**Helvius Cinna** (fl. 60s-44 BC): Author of the *Zmyrna*, the Callimachean ideal made flesh -- a brief, densely learned *epyllion* that took nine years to write and was praised extravagantly by Catullus in carme 95. Helvius Cinna was killed in the chaos following Caesar's assassination, mistaken by a mob for the conspirator Cinna the praetor. Shakespeare preserves the episode: *I am Cinna the poet, I am Cinna the poet -- yet they tear me for my bad verses.* Almost nothing of the *Zmyrna* survives. **Tier: NC.**\n\n**Cornificius** (dates uncertain, probably died c. 41 BC): Addressed affectionately by Catullus (carme 38) and later a correspondent of Cicero. Wrote poetry, now essentially lost. **Tier: NC.**\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nCatullus's most direct heirs are the Latin elegists of the next generation. Tibullus, Propertius, and Ovid all absorbed his emotional register, his first-person love poetry, his Greek models, and his conviction that the personal is a legitimate subject for high art. Propertius opens his first book with an unmistakable nod: *Cynthia prima suis miserum me cepit ocellis* -- even the diminutive *ocellis* is Catullan. Ovid in the *Amores* and *Ars Amatoria* could not exist without Catullus's example. The entire tradition of Latin love elegy is, in a real sense, Catullus's aftermath.\n\nThe collection survived antiquity in a single manuscript, the *Codex Veronensis*, associated with Verona. This manuscript was apparently rediscovered around 1300, when it briefly resurfaced before being lost or dispersed again; all surviving copies of Catullus descend from copies made of it during that narrow window. The entire textual tradition hangs by one thread.\n\nAround 1400, the humanist Guarino of Verona obtained a copy and helped circulate the text among scholars. But the most significant pre-Guarino reader was Francesco Petrarch, who encountered the Veronensis manuscript in the fourteenth century and studied it closely. The influence on the *Canzoniere* -- Petrarch's sequence of 366 poems addressed to Laura -- is not coincidental: the sustained lyric sequence organized around a single, idealized, largely unattainable beloved woman is Catullan to its core. Petrarch elevated the Catullan emotional architecture into the defining model of European love poetry for the next three centuries. Every Elizabethan sonnet sequence, every Renaissance canzoniere, every poet who ever wrote about unrequited love for a woman named after a laurel tree is, at some remove, downstream of Catullus's Lesbia.\n\nCatullus quickly became central to Renaissance humanism more broadly: his combination of classical precision, personal directness, and emotional range made him immediately recognizable. The explicitness of several poems (carme 16 above all) caused predictable difficulties for early printers; expurgated editions were common until the mid-twentieth century, and some left explicit gaps where the offensive lines would have been. The modern scholarly consensus is that the explicit poems are among the most important in the corpus -- not despite their obscenity but because of what the obscenity argues: that a poet's full voice, including its least polite registers, is inseparable from his art.\n\n---\n\n## Proposed Tier\n\n**Tier: C (Manageable)**\n\nCatullus sits at C tier across the collection, with the carmina docta touching the lower edge of B.\n\n**Medium and meter:** The polymetric poems use the Phalecian hendecasyllable, one of the more accessible lyric meters; the epigrammatic poems use elegiac couplets. The carmina docta add dactylic hexameter and rarer lyric meters. Averaged across the whole corpus, the metrical burden settles at C.\n\n**Lexicon:** On the surface, standard literary Latin in the short poems, supplemented by colloquialisms, diminutives, Greek coinages, and occasional obscenity; the carmina docta add rarer vocabulary and denser allusion to Hellenistic sources. The real lexical burden is hidden, though. The common words of Roman obligation (*fides*, *foedus*, *pietas*, *amare* against *bene velle*) and of style (*venustas*, *lepos*, *sal*, *nugae*) carry a private, almost technical sense that no dictionary gives, and the love poems cannot be understood without it. This is the one criterion where Catullus reaches Challenging. Averaged: low B.\n\n**Syntactic complexity:** The short poems are syntactically clear -- Catullus at his most direct is almost conversational. The carmina docta use extended periodic structures (see the carme 64 opening above). Averaged: C.\n\n**Allusion density:** Low in the polymetrics, high in the carmina docta (dense Alexandrian mythological apparatus requiring familiarity with Hellenistic models). Averaged: C.\n\n**Why not B?** B tier requires sustained difficulty across multiple criteria. Catullus delivers that only in the carmina docta, a portion of the whole. A student reading primarily the polymetrics or epigrams will not encounter B-tier resistance. The entry point is too generous. The lexicon does not change this on its own: a word with a hidden meaning makes a poem harder to interpret, but the sentence it sits in is still easy to construe, and one Challenging criterion beside three Manageable ones is a C profile.\n\n**Why not D?** Because the carmina docta exist and cannot be ignored in any honest assessment. Carme 64 is not D-tier reading. And even the short poems reward close attention in ways that D-tier texts do not.\n\nC tier is the right call. Come for carme 3. Stay for carme 64.\n";
window.__AUGUSTAN_MD__="# AUGUSTAN ERA (44 BC -- 14 AD)\n\nThe men who stabbed Caesar in 44 BC believed they were saving the Republic. Instead they handed Rome to a sickly nineteen-year-old nobody had taken seriously. Octavian, Caesar's adopted son, avenged him at Philippi, shared the world with Mark Antony for a decade, and beat Antony and Cleopatra at Actium in 31 BC. In 27 BC he announced, with a perfectly straight face, that he was giving the Republic back; the grateful Senate gave him the name *Augustus* and, in practice, everything else. He ruled as *princeps*, \"first citizen\", for forty-one years, offering an exhausted Rome peace and a program to go with it: old gods, old morals, laws against adultery, and a city he claimed to have found in brick and left in marble. On his deathbed, says Suetonius, he asked his friends whether he had played his part in the comedy of life well, and if so, would they kindly applaud.\n\nThis is the **Golden Age** of Latin literature, the pinnacle of stylistic perfection. The neoterics had taught Roman poets to polish every line; the Augustans kept the polish and aimed it at the greatest Greek models. Virgil takes on Homer, Horace takes on Alcaeus, Sappho and Pindar. Rome no longer wants to translate Greece. It wants to equal it, and for once it arguably does.\n\nThe price is that the greatest poets of the era are **poets at the service of the state**. Maecenas, Augustus's friend and unofficial minister of culture, gathered Virgil, Horace and Propertius into his circle and gave them money and land. In return the regime got the *Aeneid*, an epic whose whole plot points towards Augustus, and Horace's *Carmen Saeculare*, the official hymn of the festival of 17 BC. Propaganda, in some sense, although nobody handed out scripts: the poets were friends of the regime and knew who was paying for the farm. When they did not want to write something, they used the *recusatio*, explaining in exquisite verse that they would love to sing Augustus's wars but their talent was, sadly, far too small. It is the most elegant way ever invented of saying no to an emperor.\n\nNot everyone sang in the official key. The era's own Roman genre, **love elegy** (Gallus, Tibullus, Propertius, Ovid), was its counter-culture: the elegists refuse careers, war and marriage, and live as \"slaves\" to a mistress usually married to someone else. In a city passing laws against adultery, this was not a harmless hobby.\n\nAnd that is the darker side of the Golden Age: Rome discovered **censorship**. In AD 8 Ovid, the most fashionable poet in the city, was banished to the Black Sea for *carmen et error*, \"a poem and a mistake\". The poem was the *Ars Amatoria*, a manual of seduction, and his books were removed from Rome's public libraries; he spent his last ten years begging to come home and never did. The historian Titus Labienus, so savage that people called him *Rabienus*, \"the rabid one\", had his books burned by order of the Senate, the first time Rome punished literature that way; he shut himself in his family tomb rather than outlive them. To be fair, Augustus censored himself too: he wrote a tragedy on Ajax, judged it bad and wiped it out with a sponge, joking that his Ajax had fallen on his sponge instead of his sword.\n\nFor a translator, the battlefield changes. In Caesar's era the enemy was Cicero's periodic sentence and Sallust's archaism; here it is mostly **verse**: the hexameter (Virgil, Ovid's *Metamorphoses*), the elegiac couplet (Tibullus, Sulpicia, Propertius) and the Greek lyric metres Horace brought to Rome. The archaisms are gone; in their place come free word order, poetic plurals and unexplained mythology. In Horace's *Quis multa gracilis te puer in rosa*, *multa* waits four words for *rosa*, and *te*, the girl, sits right between *gracilis* and *puer*, so the boy is literally wrapped around her. The result is a sharp spike in difficulty compared with Caesar's era: with so much of the literature written in verse, many of these authors are hard going, and Virgil and Horace above all. The prose is gentler, from Augustus's plain *Res Gestae* and Vitruvius's honest technical Latin to Livy's long, flowing periods.\n\nThe corpus is in excellent shape where it matters most. Virgil, Horace and Ovid survive complete, and Virgil only because Augustus refused his dying wish to burn the unfinished *Aeneid*. Livy survives in 35 of 142 books, the *Res Gestae* in a copy carved on a temple wall in Ankara. At the other extreme, Gallus, the founder of elegy, is down to about ten lines on a papyrus scrap found in Egypt in 1978.\n\n---\n\n# AUGUSTUS (63 BC -- 14 AD)\n\n## Biography\n\nGaius Octavius was born in Rome on 23 September 63 BC, the year of Cicero's consulship and the Catilinarian conspiracy, into a respectable but unremarkable equestrian family from Velitrae, in the hills south of Rome. His father died when he was four. What made him remarkable was his mother, Atia, who was the niece of Julius Caesar. Caesar had no legitimate son, took an interest in the clever, sickly boy, and brought him along on campaign in Spain. In March 44 BC the eighteen-year-old Octavius was studying at Apollonia, across the Adriatic, when the news arrived: Caesar had been murdered, and his will adopted Octavius as his son and heir. Against the advice of his family, he went to Rome to claim the inheritance and, above all, the name. From now on he was Gaius Julius Caesar, and that name was worth more than any army.\n\n**The boy who owed everything to his name.** Nobody took him seriously at first. Mark Antony, Caesar's right-hand man, called him \"a boy who owes everything to his name\". Cicero thought he could use him against Antony, and summed up his plan in a pun that somebody, inevitably, repeated to Octavian: the young man was to be *laudandum, ornandum, tollendum*, \"praised, honoured, and lifted up\", where *tollere* also means \"got rid of\". Octavian raised a private army out of Caesar's veterans, fought for the Senate against Antony at Mutina in 43 BC, and watched both consuls of the year, Hirtius and Pansa, die in the campaign (the same Hirtius who finished Caesar's *Gallic War*; ancient gossip, needless to say, blamed Octavian). Then he marched on Rome, had himself made consul at nineteen, and changed sides: he formed the Second Triumvirate with Antony and Lepidus, and the three drew up the proscription lists. Cicero's name was on them. So much for *tollendum*.\n\n**Philippi, Actium, and a broken nose.** In 42 BC the triumvirs destroyed Brutus and Cassius at Philippi. Antony did the fighting; Octavian, who was ill, spent part of the battle hiding in a marsh for three days. For the next decade the Roman world was split: Antony took the East, and Cleopatra; Octavian took Italy and the West, the unpopular confiscations of land for veterans, and the war against Sextus Pompeius, which was won for him in 36 BC by his best friend and admiral, Marcus Agrippa. Lepidus was quietly pushed aside. The final showdown came at Actium on 2 September 31 BC, and again it was Agrippa who won it. The following year, in Alexandria, Antony and Cleopatra killed themselves. Octavian went to see the embalmed body of Alexander the Great, touched it, and (says Cassius Dio) accidentally broke off a piece of the nose. Asked if he would also like to see the tombs of the Ptolemies, he said he had come to see a king, not a row of corpses.\n\n**The restored Republic.** At thirty-three he was master of the world, and he had learned from Caesar's mistakes: Caesar had ruled openly as dictator and had been stabbed for it. So in January 27 BC Octavian solemnly gave the Republic back to the Senate and the People, and the Senate, which had no illusions about who was in charge, gave him the new name *Augustus*, \"the revered one\". Over the following decades he collected the powers of the old magistrates one by one (the powers of a tribune from 23 BC, the office of *pontifex maximus* in 12 BC, the title of *pater patriae* in 2 BC) without ever holding a title that sounded like \"king\". He called himself *princeps*, first among citizens, and the system he built, the Principate, lasted three centuries.\n\n**The man.** Suetonius, who had access to the imperial archives, gives us a portrait that is wonderfully unheroic. Augustus was short and wore shoes with thick soles to look taller. His teeth were small, few and decaying. He was so afraid of thunderstorms that he carried a piece of sealskin as a lightning charm, and hid in an underground room when a storm got bad. He loved gambling with dice and wrote cheerful letters about losing. He was also capable of real despair: in AD 9, when Varus lost three legions in the forests of Germany, Augustus went for months without cutting his hair or beard, beating his head against the doors and shouting *Quintili Vare, legiones redde!*, \"Quintilius Varus, give me back my legions!\"\n\n**The succession problem.** The one thing he could not arrange was an heir. He had a single child, Julia, and a long and genuinely devoted marriage to Livia, whom he had taken from her first husband while she was pregnant. One by one, every successor he chose died before him: his nephew Marcellus in 23 BC, Agrippa in 12 BC, his grandsons Lucius and Gaius in AD 2 and AD 4. He ended up adopting Livia's son Tiberius, whom he never much liked. Julia was exiled for adultery in 2 BC and her daughter, also Julia, in AD 8; Augustus used to call his daughter, his granddaughter and his last grandson \"my three boils\", and quoted Homer: \"I wish I had never married, and died without children.\"\n\n**The death, and the figs.** He died at Nola on 19 August AD 14, in the month that had already been renamed after him, a few weeks short of his seventy-sixth birthday. Suetonius gives him a perfect theatrical exit: he asked for a mirror, had his hair combed, asked his friends whether he had played his part in the comedy of life well, and quoted the Greek line that ends a play, \"if the show has pleased you, give it your applause\". His last words were to Livia: \"Livia, live mindful of our marriage, and farewell.\" Cassius Dio reports a darker version. Augustus, who was careful about what he ate, liked to pick figs with his own hands straight from the trees in his garden. Livia, so the rumour went, smeared poison on the figs still hanging there, ate the clean ones herself, and let her husband eat the rest, to make sure Tiberius succeeded before Augustus could change his mind. Nobody can prove it, and Dio himself only reports it as something \"some suspected\", but it has been too good a story to die. The Senate made him a god, helped by a former praetor who swore he had seen Augustus's spirit rising from the funeral pyre into the sky; Dio adds that Livia rewarded him with a million sesterces for his excellent eyesight.\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n- **Res Gestae Divi Augusti** (*The Achievements of the Divine Augustus*, finished AD 13-14) -- Augustus's own account of his career in thirty-five short chapters, written in the first person in his seventy-sixth year and left with his will, with orders to have it inscribed on two bronze pillars in front of his mausoleum. Modern editors divide it into three parts: the honours he received (chapters 1-14), the money he spent on the Roman people (15-24), and his deeds in war and peace (25-35), with a short appendix added after his death. The bronze originals are lost; the text survives because copies were carved on temples in the province of Galatia, above all the Temple of Rome and Augustus at Ankara. [Complete]\n- **De Vita Sua** (*On His Own Life*) -- an autobiography in thirteen books, taking the story up to the Cantabrian war in Spain (around 25 BC). [Lost]\n- **Letters** -- Augustus was a lively and prolific letter-writer, and Suetonius and Aulus Gellius quote him often, which is how we know about his spelling habits and his dice games. [Fragmentary]\n- **Literary experiments** -- a hexameter poem called *Sicilia*, a book of epigrams written mostly in the bath, a reply to Brutus's praise of Cato, and a tragedy on Ajax, which he destroyed himself: when asked how his Ajax was doing, he said it had \"fallen on its sponge\". [Lost]\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nAugustus had firm opinions about Latin style, and Suetonius records them gleefully. He wanted above all to be clear. He avoided what he called \"the stench of far-fetched words\", mocked Maecenas for his perfumed, curling prose, mocked Tiberius for digging up obsolete words, and mocked Antony for writing in a style that people admired rather than understood. He repeated prepositions rather than leave any doubt about the construction. His everyday speech was full of homely expressions: someone who would never pay would pay \"on the Greek Kalends\", a quick job was done \"faster than asparagus cooks\".\n\nThe *Res Gestae* is that taste for clarity carved in stone. It is official, inscriptional prose: short sentences, almost always in the first person, almost always ending on a perfect-tense verb (*comparavi*, *vindicavi*, *feci*, *refeci*, *dedi*). The syntax is the gentlest of any author so far after Nepos. The main constructions are the ablative absolute, used constantly for dates (*C. Pansa et A. Hirtio consulibus*, \"when Pansa and Hirtius were consuls\"), relative clauses, a few subordinate clauses with *cum* and *ut*, and lists, lists, lists. **Syntactic complexity: LOW; Stylistic figures: LOW.**\n\nThe real difficulty is elsewhere. The first is **lexicon**: the text is full of the technical vocabulary of Roman institutions (*tribunicia potestas*, *imperium*, *ovatio*, *supplicatio*, *lustrum*, *congiarium*, *aurum coronarium*) and of Roman numbers, written the Roman way: distributives like *trecenos*, \"three hundred each\", multiplicative adverbs like *quinquiens* and *viciens et semel*, and sums in sesterces such as *HS milliens*, which means a hundred million. A student who has never met these will spend more time with the dictionary than with the grammar. **Lexicon: MEDIUM.** The second is **context**. The *Res Gestae* is propaganda by omission, and it is a masterpiece of the genre. Antony, Brutus and Cassius, Lepidus and Cleopatra are never named; the proscriptions are never mentioned; civil wars become \"wars against the enemies of the Republic\", and Sextus Pompeius becomes a pirate. To translate it is easy. To understand what it is not saying, you need to know the history of the previous thirty years. **Allusion density: MEDIUM to HIGH, but historical rather than literary.**\n\nThis is exactly why the *Res Gestae* is the best possible way into the Augustan Era. In thirty-five short chapters it gives you the whole political frame that the poets of the period take for granted: the civil wars, the victory, the \"restored Republic\", the buildings, the games, the conquests, the peace, and the man at the centre of it all. Virgil, Horace, Livy and Ovid are much easier to understand once you have read the official version they are writing with, around or against. It is also an ideal text for brushing up on prose before the era's poetry hits: clean classical Latin, regular word order, standard constructions, and a sense of moving quickly through a real text. Read it first.\n\n## Latin Excerpts\n\n### Excerpt 1: Res Gestae 1-3 -- Part I: the Rise to Power\n\n**Source:** Augustus, *Res Gestae Divi Augusti*, chapters 1-3, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/augustus/resgestae.html)\n\n**Context:** The opening of the *Res Gestae* and of its first part, on the honours Augustus received. In three chapters he covers the years from 44 to about 30 BC: the private army he raised after Caesar's murder, the war of Mutina, his first consulship, the triumvirate, Philippi and the civil wars. That is, the most violent fourteen years of his life. Read it with the biography above open beside it, and count how many of those events are actually named.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n[1] Annos undeviginti natus exercitum privato consilio et privata impensa comparavi, per quem rem publicam a dominatione factionis oppressam in libertatem vindicavi. [Ob quae] senatus decretis honorificis in ordinem suum me adlegit, C. Pansa et A. Hirtio consulibus, consularem locum sententiae dicendae tribuens, et imperium mihi dedit. Res publica ne quid detrimenti caperet, me propraetore simul cum consulibus providere iussit. Populus autem eodem anno me consulem, cum cos. uterque bello cecidisset, et triumvirum rei publicae constituendae creavit.\n\n[2] Qui parentem meum trucidaverunt, eos in exilium expuli iudiciis legitimis ultus eorum facinus, et postea bellum inferentis rei publicae vici bis acie.\n\n[3] Bella terra et mari civilia externaque toto in orbe terrarum saepe gessi, victorque omnibus veniam petentibus civibus peperci. Externas gentes, quibus tuto ignosci potuit, conservare quam excidere malui. Millia civium Romanorum sub sacramento meo fuerunt circiter quingenta. Ex quibus deduxi in colonias aut remisi in municipia sua stipendis emeritis millia aliquanto plura quam trecenta, et iis omnibus agros adsignavi aut pecuniam pro praemiis militiae dedi. Naves cepi sescentas praeter eas, si quae minores quam triremes fuerunt.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\n[1] At the age of nineteen, on my own initiative and at my own expense, I raised an army, with which I set free the Republic, oppressed by the tyranny of a faction. For this the Senate, with honorific decrees, enrolled me in its order in the consulship of Gaius Pansa and Aulus Hirtius, giving me the right to speak among the former consuls, and it granted me military command. It ordered me, as propraetor, to see to it together with the consuls that the Republic should suffer no harm. And in the same year, since both consuls had fallen in war, the people made me consul and triumvir for the restoration of the Republic.\n\n[2] Those who butchered my father I drove into exile, avenging their crime through lawful courts; and afterwards, when they made war on the Republic, I defeated them twice in battle.\n\n[3] I waged wars, civil and foreign, by land and sea, all over the world, many times, and as victor I spared all citizens who asked for mercy. Foreign peoples who could safely be pardoned I preferred to preserve rather than destroy. About five hundred thousand Roman citizens were bound to me by the military oath. Of these I settled in colonies, or sent back to their own towns when their service was over, somewhat more than three hundred thousand, and to all of them I assigned land or gave money as a reward for their service. I captured six hundred ships, not counting any that were smaller than triremes.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\n*Annos undeviginti natus*, \"at the age of nineteen\": the first words of the text are not a name or a title but an age, and they set the tone of everything that follows. The boy that Antony and Cicero underestimated becomes the young saviour who acted when nobody else would. Look at how he presents the facts. A nineteen-year-old raising an army of his own, *privato consilio et privata impensa*, was flatly illegal: no magistrate had authorised it. Augustus turns that illegality into a virtue, repeating *privato... privata* so that it sounds like personal generosity. The \"faction\" whose tyranny oppressed the Republic is Antony's, but Antony is never named, here or anywhere else in the text. Nor are the consuls' deaths explained (*cum cos. uterque bello cecidisset*, a simple fact of war), nor what the *triumvir rei publicae constituendae* actually did in his first months in office: the proscriptions, and the murder of Cicero, simply do not exist.\n\nThe phrase *Res publica ne quid detrimenti caperet*, \"that the Republic should suffer no harm\", is a legal formula: the wording of the Senate's emergency decree, the *senatus consultum ultimum*. It is the same decree that Caesar complains about in *Bellum Civile* I.7, used this time in favour of a Caesar rather than against one. Augustus quotes it because it makes his private army the Senate's army.\n\nChapter 2 is a small masterpiece of silence. *Qui parentem meum trucidaverunt*, \"those who butchered my father\": Brutus and Cassius go unnamed, and the verb *trucidare*, a violent, butcher's word, is the only emotional word in the passage. *Iudiciis legitimis* refers to the special court set up in 43 BC that condemned the assassins in their absence; *bis acie* is the two battles of Philippi, one of which Augustus spent in a marsh.\n\nChapter 3 introduces the great theme of the reign, *clementia*: he spared the citizens who asked, and preferred to preserve foreign peoples \"who could safely be pardoned\", a very large qualification hidden in a relative clause. Then come the numbers, which are the real challenge for a translator: *millia... circiter quingenta*, where *millia* is separated from its numeral by four words, and *millia aliquanto plura quam trecenta*, \"somewhat more than three hundred thousand\".\n\nGrammatically, the passage is a good revision test. Note the long line of first-person perfects (*comparavi, vindicavi, expuli, vici, gessi, peperci, malui, deduxi, remisi, adsignavi, dedi, cepi*); the ablatives absolute (*C. Pansa et A. Hirtio consulibus*, *stipendis emeritis*); the deponent participle *ultus*, \"having avenged\"; the old accusative plural *inferentis* for *inferentes*; *si quae* for *si aliquae*; and above all *quibus tuto ignosci potuit*. Since *ignosco* takes the dative, its passive is impersonal: literally \"to whom it was possible to be forgiven safely\", that is, \"who could safely be pardoned\". That one clause will catch more students than the rest of the passage put together.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 2: Res Gestae 20-21 -- Part II: the Builder of Rome\n\n**Source:** Augustus, *Res Gestae Divi Augusti*, chapters 20-21, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/augustus/resgestae.html)\n\n**Context:** From the second part of the *Res Gestae*, the *impensae*: the money Augustus spent on the Roman people out of his own pocket. Chapters 15-18 list cash handouts and grain, chapter 19 lists new buildings, and chapters 20-21 cover restorations and the great monuments paid for out of war booty. This is the Augustus who boasted, according to Suetonius, that he had found Rome a city of brick and left it a city of marble, and many of the buildings named here can still be visited in Rome today.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n[20] Capitolium et Pompeium theatrum utrumque opus impensa grandi refeci sine ulla inscriptione nominis mei. Rivos aquarum compluribus locis vetustate labentes refeci, et aquam quae Marcia appellatur duplicavi fonte novo in rivum eius inmisso. Forum Iulium et basilicam quae fuit inter aedem Castoris et aedem Saturni, coepta profligataque opera a patre meo, perfeci et eandem basilicam consumptam incendio, ampliato eius solo, sub titulo nominis filiorum meorum incohavi, et, si vivus non perfecissem, perfici ab heredibus meis iussi. Duo et octoginta templa deum in urbe consul sextum ex auctoritate senatus refeci nullo praetermisso quod eo tempore refici debebat. Consul septimum viam Flaminiam ab urbe Ariminum refeci pontesque omnes praeter Mulvium et Minucium.\n\n[21] In privato solo Martis Ultoris templum forumque Augustum ex manibiis feci. Theatrum ad aedem Apollinis in solo magna ex parte a privatis empto feci, quod sub nomine M. Marcelli generi mei esset. Dona ex manibiis in Capitolio et in aede divi Iuli et in aede Apollinis et in aede Vestae et in templo Martis Ultoris consacravi, quae mihi constiterunt HS circiter milliens. Auri coronari pondo triginta et quinque millia municipiis et colonis Italiae conferentibus ad triumphos meos quintum consul remisi, et postea, quotienscumque imperator appellatus sum, aurum coronarium non accepi decernentibus municipiis et colonis aeque benigne adque antea decreverant.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\n[20] The Capitol and the theatre of Pompey, both of them, I restored at great expense without any inscription of my own name. I restored the channels of the aqueducts, which in many places were collapsing with age, and I doubled the aqueduct called the Marcia by bringing a new spring into its channel. The Forum of Julius and the basilica that stood between the temple of Castor and the temple of Saturn, works begun and nearly finished by my father, I completed; and when that same basilica was destroyed by fire, I began to rebuild it on an enlarged site, under the name of my sons, and gave orders that, if I did not finish it in my lifetime, it should be finished by my heirs. In my sixth consulship, on the authority of the Senate, I restored eighty-two temples of the gods in the city, leaving out none that needed restoring at the time. In my seventh consulship I restored the Via Flaminia from Rome to Ariminum, and all its bridges except the Mulvian and the Minucian.\n\n[21] On land I owned privately, and with the spoils of war, I built the temple of Mars the Avenger and the Forum of Augustus. Next to the temple of Apollo, on ground bought for the most part from private owners, I built a theatre, to bear the name of my son-in-law Marcus Marcellus. With the spoils of war I dedicated gifts on the Capitol and in the temple of the Deified Julius, in the temple of Apollo, in the temple of Vesta and in the temple of Mars the Avenger, which cost me about a hundred million sesterces. Thirty-five thousand pounds of crown gold, which the towns and colonies of Italy were contributing for my triumphs, I remitted in my fifth consulship; and afterwards, whenever I was hailed as *imperator*, I did not accept the crown gold, although the towns and colonies voted it just as generously as they had before.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe first sentence is the best joke in the *Res Gestae*, although Augustus did not intend it as one. He restored the Capitol and Pompey's theatre \"without any inscription of my own name\", and he tells us so in an inscription of his own name, set up in front of his own mausoleum. Modesty, in Augustan Rome, was something you advertised. The choice of buildings is not innocent either: the Capitol was the religious heart of the state, and the theatre of Pompey had been built by the man his adoptive father defeated. Restoring it without his name was a public gesture of reconciliation.\n\nThe passage works like a rhythmic inventory. Almost every clause ends on the same verb or one of its family, *refeci... refeci... perfeci... incohavi... refeci... refeci... feci... feci*, so that the city seems to rise again sentence by sentence. Behind the inventory there is a family history. *A patre meo* is Caesar again, whose unfinished projects Augustus completes as a son should. *Sub titulo nominis filiorum meorum*, \"under the name of my sons\", refers to Gaius and Lucius, his grandsons and adopted sons, both dead by the time he wrote this. The theatre in the name of Marcellus, the nephew and son-in-law who died at nineteen in 23 BC, is the same young man Virgil mourns at the end of *Aeneid* VI. And the temple of *Mars Ultor*, Mars the Avenger, was vowed at Philippi: it is chapter 2's revenge for the \"butchered father\", turned into marble.\n\nThe last sentence is about refusing money, which is the most Augustan kind of boast. *Aurum coronarium*, \"crown gold\", was the gold that towns traditionally sent a victorious general to make his triumphal crowns. Augustus refused thirty-five thousand pounds of it, and the ablative absolute at the end, *decernentibus municipiis et colonis aeque benigne adque antea decreverant*, makes sure we know that the towns kept offering it.\n\nFor the translator, the passage is a lesson in Latin word order and in compact constructions rather than in hard grammar. Watch for the objects placed first, before the verb they depend on (*Capitolium et Pompeium theatrum... refeci*, *Forum Iulium et basilicam... perfeci*), with *utrumque opus* in apposition; the participles *coepta profligataque*, \"begun and nearly finished\" (*profligare* here means \"to bring almost to completion\", a sense the dictionary gives second); the passive infinitive after *iussi*, *perfici ab heredibus meis iussi*, \"I ordered it to be finished by my heirs\"; the pluperfect subjunctive *si vivus non perfecissem*, a condition in reported form; the relative clause of purpose *quod sub nomine M. Marcelli... esset*; *Ariminum* without a preposition, because names of towns take a plain accusative of motion; the adverbial numerals *consul sextum*, *consul septimum*, *quintum consul*; and the old spelling *adque* for *atque*. Then the numbers again: *HS circiter milliens* is about a hundred million sesterces, and *pondo triginta et quinque millia* is thirty-five thousand pounds by weight.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nAugustus's name is still on every calendar: in 8 BC the Senate renamed the month of Sextilis after him, and it has been August ever since. His title became a rank. Every Roman emperor after him was an *Augustus*, just as every one of them was a *Caesar*, and the model of power he invented, a monarchy that insists it is not one, has been imitated by rulers ever since.\n\nThe *Res Gestae* itself had an unusual afterlife. The bronze pillars in Rome disappeared, and the text survived only because the province of Galatia, in modern Turkey, had copies carved on its temples. The most complete, in Latin with a Greek translation, is on the walls of the Temple of Rome and Augustus at Ankara, the *Monumentum Ancyranum*, which was copied by a Western embassy to the Ottoman court in 1555. Two other copies, at Antioch in Pisidia and Apollonia, have filled most of the gaps. The great German historian Theodor Mommsen called it the *regina inscriptionum*, the queen of Latin inscriptions.\n\nIn 1938, for the two-thousandth anniversary of Augustus's birth, Mussolini had the whole text carved on the wall of the pavilion built to house the reconstructed Ara Pacis, beside the Mausoleum of Augustus in Rome. It is still there, on the side of the modern museum facing Via di Ripetta: the propaganda of one regime, reused by another. Few texts have been used so successfully, twice, for exactly the purpose they were written for.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: D (Good Exercise)**\n\nThe *Res Gestae* is short, complete, and written in clean, regular classical prose: first-person statements, standard constructions, almost no subordination, almost no figures of speech. Its only real obstacles are institutional vocabulary and Roman numerals, both solved with a good dictionary and a little patience, and its political silences, which make it harder to *understand* than to translate. It is the right text to open the Augustan Era with: it gives the reader the historical background that every other author of the period assumes, and it is a comfortable prose warm-up before the era's poetry, which is a very different matter. A clear D, and one of the most useful texts in the whole project.\n\n---\n\n# MARCUS VITRUVIUS POLLIO -- VITRUVIUS (c. 80 -- c. 15 BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nFor the man who wrote the most influential book on architecture in Western history, we know remarkably little about Vitruvius, and almost all of it comes from his own prefaces. Even his name is uncertain: *Vitruvius* is secure, but *Marcus* rests on weak evidence and *Pollio* comes from a later writer who summarised him. He was probably born in the 80s or 70s BC, possibly in Campania or in central Italy, and he tells us that his parents gave him a broad education in the liberal arts, for which he is still grateful.\n\n**Caesar's artillery man.** As a young man he served Julius Caesar as a military engineer. Together with three colleagues, whom he names, he was in charge of building and repairing *ballistae*, *scorpiones* and the other siege engines of the Roman army, which makes him the Roman equivalent of an artillery officer. Where exactly he served we do not know, though Gaul and Africa are the usual guesses. A German scholar once tried to identify him with Mamurra, Caesar's chief engineer, whom Catullus mocks in some of his crudest poems as a corrupt, bankrupt playboy. Almost nobody accepts the theory, which is a pity, because it would be the best-documented career change in Latin literature.\n\n**A pension from Octavia.** After Caesar's death he passed into the service of Octavian, and here comes the most charming detail of his life. In the dedication of his book he thanks the young Caesar for a pension that allowed him to work without worrying about poverty in his old age, and he adds that the pension was kept up \"on the recommendation of your sister\", Octavia. The great theorist of Roman architecture, in other words, owed his comfortable retirement to a reference letter from the emperor's sister.\n\n**The ugly architect.** Vitruvius is also the only Roman author who opens a book by apologising for his face. In the preface to Book II he tells the story of Dinocrates, a tall, handsome Greek architect who, unable to get an audience with Alexander the Great, oiled himself, put on a lion skin, crowned himself with poplar leaves, took a club and walked into the king's assembly dressed as Hercules. It worked: Alexander noticed him, and Dinocrates went on to plan the city of Alexandria (after first proposing to carve Mount Athos into a giant man holding a city in his hand, which Alexander politely declined). Vitruvius then turns to Augustus and admits: \"nature has not given me height, age has disfigured my face, and illness has taken away my strength\". Since he cannot win the emperor over with his looks, he will have to do it with his knowledge. It is the most endearing self-portrait in Latin prose.\n\n**One building.** For all his theory, Vitruvius mentions only one building of his own: a basilica at Fanum Fortunae, modern Fano on the Adriatic coast, which he describes in detail in Book V. For centuries architects tried to reconstruct it from his description, and nobody knew where it stood. In recent years excavations in the centre of Fano have uncovered remains that some archaeologists believe may finally be his basilica, although the identification is still debated. He wrote the *De Architectura* in the 20s BC, as an old man, and dedicated it to Augustus. After that he disappears, and we do not know when he died.\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n- **De Architectura** (*On Architecture*, c. 30-20 BC) -- ten books, dedicated to Augustus, covering everything a Roman architect was expected to know. Book I deals with the education of the architect and the planning of cities; Book II with building materials (and the origins of human housing); Books III and IV with temples and the architectural orders; Book V with public buildings, including the acoustics of theatres; Book VI with private houses; Book VII with plaster, stucco, floors and pigments; Book VIII with water and aqueducts (where he warns that lead pipes are bad for your health, two thousand years ahead of schedule); Book IX with astronomy and sundials; and Book X with machines, from water wheels to siege engines. The text survives complete, but the illustrations he refers to throughout (*subscripta forma*, \"a diagram is drawn below\") were lost, which has kept readers guessing for two millennia. [Complete]\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nVitruvius knew perfectly well that he was not a great writer, and he said so. At the end of the first chapter of Book I he asks Caesar and his readers to forgive him if anything is \"not quite according to the rules of grammar\", because he has written \"not as a great philosopher, nor as an eloquent rhetorician, nor as a grammarian trained in the highest principles of the art, but as an architect who has had a taste of letters\". This is not false modesty. His Latin is the Latin of a practical man with a technical subject and no literary training: honest, functional and frequently clumsy.\n\nThe first difficulty is the **lexicon**, which is enormous and highly technical. Vitruvius needs words for column bases, capitals, cornices, mouldings, types of brick and sand, kinds of lime, the parts of catapults and water organs, and Latin does not have most of them. So he borrows from Greek wholesale, sometimes translating, sometimes not: *symmetria*, *analogia*, *eurythmia*, *taxis*, *gnomonice*, *teleon*. A student meets a new term in almost every line, and many of them are not in a school dictionary. **Lexicon: HIGH.**\n\nThe second is **syntax**, which is difficult in a different way from Cicero's or Sallust's. Their sentences are hard because they are built with great art; Vitruvius's sentences are hard because they are not quite built at all. They run on, pile up clauses, change construction halfway through, and occasionally lose track of their subject. The great Renaissance architect Leon Battista Alberti, who owed him almost everything, still complained that Vitruvius wrote in such a way that the Latins thought he wanted to sound Greek and the Greeks thought he was speaking Latin. Add the lost diagrams, which leave several descriptions of machines nearly impossible to visualise, and you get a text that is easy to parse sentence by sentence and surprisingly hard to understand paragraph by paragraph. **Syntactic complexity: MEDIUM.**\n\nIn everything else he is gentle. He uses almost no rhetorical figures, his allusions are to Greek scientists and mathematicians rather than to myth or poetry, and he likes to break the technical material with good stories: Dinocrates dressed as Hercules, Archimedes running naked through Syracuse, the origins of the Corinthian capital in a basket left on a young girl's grave, around which an acanthus plant grew. **Stylistic figures: LOW; Allusion density: LOW to MEDIUM.** And he writes prose, which in the Augustan Era is already a gift.\n\n## Latin Excerpts\n\n### Excerpt 1: De Architectura I.3.1-2 -- Firmitas, Utilitas, Venustas\n\n**Source:** Vitruvius, *De Architectura*, Book I, chapter 3, sections 1-2, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/vitruvius/vitruvius1.html)\n\n**Context:** After two chapters on what an architect must study and on the basic concepts of the discipline, Vitruvius divides architecture into its parts, and then sets out the three requirements that every building must meet. Those three words, *firmitas, utilitas, venustas*, are the most famous sentence ever written about architecture. In the seventeenth century the English diplomat Sir Henry Wotton translated them as \"commodity, firmness and delight\", and they are still taught in every school of architecture today.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n[1] Partes ipsius architecturae sunt tres: aedificatio, gnomonice, machinatio. Aedificatio autem divisa est bipertito, e quibus una est moenium et communium operum in publicis locis conlocatio, altera est privatorum aedificiorum explicatio. Publicorum autem distributiones sunt tres, e quibus est una defensionis, altera religionis, tertia opportunitatis. Defensionis est murorum turriumque et portarum ratio ad hostium impetus perpetuo repellendos excogitata, religionis deorum immortalium fanorum aediumque sacrarum conlocatio, opportunitatis communium locorum ad usum publicum dispositio, uti portus, fora, porticus, balinea, theatra, inambulationes ceteraque, quae isdem rationibus in publicis locis designantur.\n\n[2] Haec autem ita fieri debent, ut habeatur ratio firmitatis, utilitatis, venustatis. Firmitatis erit habita ratio, cum fuerit fundamentorum ad solidum depressio, quaque e materia, copiarum sine avaritia diligens electio; utilitatis autem, [cum fuerit] emendata et sine inpeditione usus locorum dispositio et ad regiones sui cuiusque generis apta et conmoda distributio; venustatis vero, cum fuerit operis species grata et elegans membrorumque commensus iustas habeat symmetriarum ratiocinationes.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\n[1] Architecture itself has three parts: building, gnomonics (the making of sundials) and mechanics. Building in turn is divided into two: one part is the placing of city walls and public works in public spaces, the other is the construction of private buildings. Public buildings are of three kinds: one for defence, another for religion, the third for convenience. Defence means the planning of walls, towers and gates, designed to drive back enemy attacks for all time; religion, the siting of the shrines and sacred temples of the immortal gods; convenience, the layout of common spaces for public use, such as harbours, forums, porticoes, baths, theatres, promenades and all the rest that are designed on the same principles in public places.\n\n[2] All of these must be built in such a way that account is taken of strength, utility and beauty. Account will have been taken of strength when the foundations have been dug down to solid ground and the materials, whatever they are, have been chosen with care and without stinginess; of utility, when the arrangement of the spaces is faultless and allows them to be used without obstruction, and each type of building is placed suitably and conveniently with respect to its orientation; and of beauty, when the appearance of the work is pleasing and elegant, and the proportions of its parts follow the correct calculations of symmetry.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThis is Vitruvius at his most organised, and it shows how his mind works: by division. Architecture has three parts; building has two; public buildings have three kinds; each kind gets a definition in the genitive (*defensionis est... religionis... opportunitatis...*), with the verb *est* understood after the first. It reads less like an essay than like a table of contents spoken aloud, which is exactly what it is.\n\nThen comes the famous sentence: *ut habeatur ratio firmitatis, utilitatis, venustatis*. Three abstract nouns in *-tas*, in asyndeton, all depending on *ratio*, \"account, consideration\". Notice that Vitruvius does not say that a building must *be* strong, useful and beautiful, but that the builder must *take account* of strength, utility and beauty. Architecture, for him, is a matter of method. Each quality is then defined with a future perfect (*erit habita ratio, cum fuerit...*), \"account will have been taken when there will have been...\", the tense of the instruction manual: when you have done this, you will have achieved that. The definition of *venustas* is the one that matters most for the rest of the book: beauty is not taste, it is proportion, *symmetriarum ratiocinationes*, a matter of calculation. That idea leads straight to Excerpt 2.\n\nThis passage also shows why Vitruvius needs a careful translator. *Gnomonice* is a Greek word for the science of sundials; *machinatio* covers everything from cranes to catapults; *ad regiones* refers to the orientation of a building towards the points of the compass and the winds, a subject he has just discussed in chapter 2. And *quaque e materia* (\"from whatever material\") is dropped into the middle of the clause with no grammatical connection, a typical Vitruvian shortcut.\n\nA note on the text: The Latin Library prints *turrimque* for *turriumque*, *distrtbutio* for *distributio*, and leaves out two pieces of punctuation (the comma after *aedificatio* and the break before *venustatis*). All four are plain printing errors and have been corrected here, checked against Krohn's edition on LacusCurtius. The square brackets around *cum fuerit* are the editor's: the words are not in the manuscripts but are needed for the sense.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 2: De Architectura III.1.2-3 -- The Vitruvian Man\n\n**Source:** Vitruvius, *De Architectura*, Book III, chapter 1, sections 2-3, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/vitruvius/vitruvius3.html)\n\n**Context:** Book III is about temples, and Vitruvius opens it by explaining that a temple must be built with *symmetria*, the correct proportion of every part to the whole. To show what he means, he turns to the most perfectly proportioned thing in nature: the human body. Around 1490 Leonardo da Vinci read this passage, took a pen, and drew a man inside a circle and a square, with a note in his mirror-writing that opens by naming \"Vetruvio architecto\" as his source. The drawing, now in Venice, is probably the most reproduced image in the history of art, and it is on the Italian one-euro coin.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n[2] Corpus enim hominis ita natura composuit, uti os capitis a mento ad frontem summam et radices imas capilli esset decimae partis, item manus palma ab articulo ad extremum medium digitum tantundem, caput a mento ad summum verticem octavae, cum cervicibus imis ab summo pectore ad imas radices capillorum sextae, <a medio pectore> ad summum verticem quartae. Ipsius autem oris altitudinis tertia est pars ab imo mento ad imas nares, nasum ab imis naribus ad finem medium superciliorum tantundem, ab ea fine ad imas radices capilli frons efficitur item tertiae partis. Pes vero altitudinis corporis sextae, cubitum quartae, pectus item quartae. Reliqua quoque membra suas habent commensus proportiones, quibus etiam antiqui pictores et statuarii nobiles usi magnas et infinitas laudes sunt adsecuti.\n\n[3] Similiter vero sacrarum aedium membra ad universam totius magnitudinis summam ex partibus singulis convenientissimum debent habere commensus responsum. Item corporis centrum medium naturaliter est umbilicus. Namque si homo conlocatus fuerit supinus manibus et pedibus pansis circinique conlocatum centrum in umbilico eius, circumagendo rotundationem utrarumque manuum et pedum digiti linea tangentur. Non minus quemadmodum schema rotundationis in corpore efficitur, item quadrata designatio in eo invenietur. Nam si a pedibus imis ad summum caput mensum erit eaque mensura relata fuerit ad manus pansas, invenietur eadem latitudo uti altitudo, quemadmodum areae quae ad normam sunt quadratae.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\n[2] For nature has composed the human body in such a way that the face, from the chin to the top of the forehead and the lowest roots of the hair, is a tenth of the whole height; the palm of the hand, from the wrist to the tip of the middle finger, the same; the head, from the chin to the crown, an eighth; from the top of the chest, including the base of the neck, to the lowest roots of the hair, a sixth; from the middle of the chest to the crown, a quarter. Of the height of the face itself, one third runs from the bottom of the chin to the bottom of the nostrils; the nose, from the bottom of the nostrils to the point between the eyebrows, is the same; and from that point to the lowest roots of the hair, the forehead also makes up a third. The foot is a sixth of the height of the body, the forearm a quarter, the chest also a quarter. The other limbs, too, have their own proportions, and it was by using them that the famous painters and sculptors of antiquity won great and boundless praise.\n\n[3] In the same way, the parts of sacred temples must have, from each single part up to the total size of the whole, the most harmonious correspondence of measurements. Likewise, the natural centre of the body is the navel. For if a man is laid on his back with his hands and feet spread out, and the point of a compass is placed on his navel, then by drawing a circle around him the fingers of both his hands and the toes of both his feet will touch the line. And just as a circle can be traced on the body, a square will also be found in it. For if you measure from the soles of the feet to the top of the head, and set that measure against the outstretched hands, the width will be found to be the same as the height, just as in surfaces squared off with a set square.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe first section is a list of fractions, and it is a fine exercise in the genitive of quality and measure: *esset decimae partis*, \"was of a tenth part\", then *octavae*, *sextae*, *quartae*, with *partis* understood each time and the verb *esset* carried silently through the whole series. Vitruvius never repeats what he can leave out, which is the main reason the passage looks harder than it is. Read *est* or *esset* and *partis* into every clause and it falls into place.\n\nThe second section contains the image that made the passage immortal. A man lying on his back, arms and legs spread out, a compass on his navel: the circle touches his fingers and toes. Then, measured from head to foot and from fingertip to fingertip, the same body fits into a square. The circle and the square were, for ancient and Renaissance thinkers alike, the two perfect geometrical figures, so the human body turns out to be the meeting point of geometry and nature. And since temples must be proportioned like bodies, a well-built temple is a kind of portrait of man. Leonardo's famous drawing is, quite literally, an illustration of these two sentences, and a correction of them: Leonardo noticed that if the man is to fit both figures, the circle and the square cannot share a centre, so he moved the square down and put its centre at the groin.\n\nGrammatically, watch the future perfects again (*conlocatus fuerit*, *mensum erit*, *relata fuerit*), the tense of instructions; the gerund *circumagendo*, \"by turning (the compass) round\", which has no expressed object; *pansis*, from *pando*, a rarer participle form than *passis*; and the impersonal *mensum erit*, \"it will have been measured\", which Italian and English both turn into \"if you measure\".\n\nA note on the text: The Latin Library has three misprints here: *ReIiqua* (with a capital I) for *Reliqua*, *vertices* for *verticem* in the first sentence, and *conlocantum*, which is not a word, for *conlocatum*. All three are corrected, as confirmed by Krohn's edition on LacusCurtius. The angle brackets around *a medio pectore* are the editor's: the words are restored to fill a gap in the manuscripts.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 3: De Architectura IX, Praefatio 9-10 -- Eureka!\n\n**Source:** Vitruvius, *De Architectura*, Book IX, preface, sections 9-10, from *LacusCurtius* (penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/L/Roman/Texts/Vitruvius/9*.html), which reproduces F. Krohn's Teubner edition (1912). The Latin Library's text of this passage is unusually corrupt (it prints *Nimium* for *nimirum* and *verius* for *versus*, and omits Archimedes' Greek words altogether), so LacusCurtius is used instead. Sentence-initial capitals have been added, since Krohn prints the text in lower case throughout.\n\n**Context:** Each book of the *De Architectura* has a preface, and Vitruvius uses them for his best stories. Book IX, on astronomy and sundials, opens with a reflection on how athletes are showered with honours while the thinkers who truly benefit humanity are forgotten. To prove the point, he tells stories about great thinkers. The first two are about Plato and Pythagoras; the third is this one, the oldest surviving version of the most famous story in the history of science.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n[9] Archimedis vero cum multa miranda inventa et varia fuerint, ex omnibus etiam infinita sollertia id quod exponam videtur esse expressum. Nimirum Hiero Syracusis auctus regia potestate, rebus bene gestis cum auream coronam votivam diis inmortalibus in quodam fano constituisset ponendam, manupretio locavit faciendam et aurum ad sacoma adpendit redemptori. Is ad tempus opus manu factum subtiliter regi adprobavit et ad sacoma pondus coronae visus est praestitisse.\n\n[10] Posteaquam indicium est factum dempto auro tantundem argenti in id coronarium opus admixtum esse, indignatus Hiero se contemptum esse neque inveniens qua ratione id furtum deprehenderet, rogavit Archimeden uti insumeret sibi de eo cogitationem. Tunc is cum haberet eius rei curam, casu venit in balineum ibique cum in solium descenderet, animadvertit quantum corporis sui in eo insideret tantum aquae extra solium effluere. Idque cum eius rei rationem explicationis ostendisset, non est moratus sed exsiluit gaudio motus de solio et nudus vadens domum versus significabat clara voce invenisse quod quaereret. Nam currens identidem graece clamabat ευρηκα ευρηκα.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\n[9] Of the many and varied marvellous discoveries of Archimedes, the one I am about to describe seems to show, above all the others, an ingenuity without limits. Hiero, you see, having risen to royal power in Syracuse, decided after his successes to place a golden crown in a certain temple as a votive offering to the immortal gods. He contracted out the work for a fee, and weighed out the gold for the contractor on the scales. The contractor delivered the work on time, finely crafted, to the king's satisfaction, and on the scales the crown seemed to match the weight of the gold.\n\n[10] Afterwards, word came that some of the gold had been taken out and the same weight of silver mixed into the crown. Hiero was furious at having been cheated, but since he could find no way to prove the theft, he asked Archimedes to take the problem on. While Archimedes had the matter on his mind, he happened to go to the baths, and as he got down into the tub he noticed that the more of his body sank into it, the more water overflowed from the tub. Since this showed him how the problem could be explained, he did not wait a moment: overjoyed, he leapt out of the tub and ran home naked, announcing at the top of his voice that he had found what he was looking for. For as he ran he kept shouting in Greek: *heureka, heureka*, \"I have found it, I have found it!\"\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThis is Vitruvius in storytelling mode, and his Latin relaxes accordingly. The construction is mostly the narrative staple of every Latin textbook: *cum* with the subjunctive (*cum... constituisset*, *cum haberet*, *cum... descenderet*, *cum... ostendisset*), the ablative absolute (*rebus bene gestis*, *dempto auro*), indirect statement after verbs of perceiving and saying (*animadvertit... effluere*, *significabat... invenisse*), and an indirect question (*quod quaereret*). A student who has read Caesar will feel at home.\n\nThe technical touches are where the care is needed. *Manupretio locavit faciendam* is legal-commercial language: *locare* with a gerundive means \"to put out to contract\", and *manupretium* is the price of the labour, so Hiero hired a goldsmith for a fee. *Ad sacoma* is a Greek loan-word for the counterweight on a scale. *Redemptor* is not a \"redeemer\" but the contractor who has undertaken the job. And the central observation, *quantum corporis sui in eo insideret tantum aquae extra solium effluere*, is a correlative pair, *quantum... tantum*, \"as much... so much\", which sets out in one line what we now call the displacement of water. Notice that Vitruvius does not mention a law of buoyancy, or anything about the weight of displaced water: that is Archimedes' later work *On Floating Bodies*. What he describes is volume. Gold is denser than silver, so a crown with silver in it takes up more space than the same weight of pure gold, and pushes out more water. The rest of the preface (sections 11-12) describes the experiment, which proves the theft.\n\nAnd then the punchline: *nudus vadens domum versus*. The greatest mathematician of antiquity running naked through the streets of Syracuse. Vitruvius ends the section on Archimedes' words in Greek, *heureka*, which every modern language has borrowed. For an author who apologised for his writing, it is a perfectly told anecdote.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nThe *De Architectura* is the only treatise on architecture to survive from the ancient world. Greek architects wrote many books on their buildings, and Vitruvius quotes them; Romans after him wrote more; every one of them is lost. Everything we know at first hand about how a Roman architect was trained, how he thought about proportion, how he chose his sand or sited his city, comes from this one book. That alone would make him important. What made him decisive was the Renaissance.\n\nThe text never quite disappeared: it was copied in Carolingian monasteries, and dozens of medieval manuscripts survive. But it was the humanists who turned it into a sacred text. In 1416 Poggio Bracciolini, the great book-hunter of the age, found a copy at the monastery of St Gall and helped bring it back into circulation. From then on, anyone in Italy who wanted to build *all'antica*, in the manner of the ancients, read Vitruvius. When humanist princes and popes rebuilt their cities and Renaissance architects designed their churches, palaces and squares, Vitruvius was the manual open on the table, and the ruins of Rome were the examples they measured him against. Brunelleschi studied the ruins; Alberti studied Vitruvius; Bramante, Raphael and Michelangelo all worked in a world shaped by him. In the 1540s a group of humanists in Rome even founded an *Accademia della Virtù*, nicknamed the Vitruvian Academy, with the aim of producing a complete edition and commentary of the text.\n\nHis influence was just as great on the books that came after him, because every major architectural treatise of the Renaissance was built on Vitruvius, whether it followed him, corrected him or argued with him. Leon Battista Alberti's *De re aedificatoria* (1452), the first great architectural treatise of the modern age, is written in Latin, deliberately divided into ten books like Vitruvius's, and built on his ideas from start to finish, even where it corrects him. It was followed by the first printed edition of Vitruvius (Rome, c. 1486), the first illustrated edition by Fra Giocondo (Venice, 1511), the first Italian translation by Cesare Cesariano (Como, 1521), and the great annotated edition of Daniele Barbaro (Venice, 1556), with illustrations by Andrea Palladio. Then came the manuals that taught architecture to the whole of Europe: Sebastiano Serlio's books on architecture, Vignola's *Regola delli cinque ordini* (1562), and Palladio's *I quattro libri dell'architettura* (1570), whose villas and churches were copied from England to Virginia. The five classical orders of columns, the idea that a building should be proportioned like a human body, and the triad of *firmitas, utilitas, venustas* all reached the modern world through these books, and all of them go back to Vitruvius. When Thomas Jefferson designed his house at Monticello and the University of Virginia, he worked from Palladio, which means that, at second hand, he was working from a retired Roman artillery engineer with a pension from Octavia.\n\nAnd then, of course, there is Leonardo's *Vitruvian Man*, which made Vitruvius's name famous even among people who have never opened his book.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: C (Manageable)**\n\nVitruvius is prose, and in the Augustan Era that already puts him on the easier side. His grammar is standard, his stories are told in plain narrative Latin, and he uses almost no rhetorical figures or literary allusion. What lifts him above D is his lexicon: a dense, technical vocabulary full of Greek loan-words, many of them missing from school dictionaries, together with a loose and sometimes awkward sentence structure that makes his descriptions harder to follow than to parse, especially without the lost diagrams. The narrative prefaces, like the Archimedes story, sit close to D; the technical chapters on temples or machines can touch B. On balance, a solid C: approachable for any student who has read Caesar and Cicero, provided they keep a good dictionary and a lot of patience within reach.\n\n---\n\n# GAIUS CORNELIUS GALLUS (70/69 -- 26 BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nGaius Cornelius Gallus was the most dangerous kind of man to be in Augustan Rome: brilliant at two things at once. He invented a whole genre of Latin poetry, and he governed Egypt. The second of these killed him, and in killing him it took most of the first one with it.\n\n**A provincial from Fréjus.** He was born in 70 or 69 BC at Forum Iulii, modern Fréjus on the coast of Provence, in the province of Gallia Narbonensis, probably into a family of no particular distinction; his name may even suggest Gallic origins. He was almost exactly the same age as Virgil, and like Virgil he came to Rome from the provinces with nothing but talent. The first time he appears in history is in a letter of 43 BC from Asinius Pollio to Cicero, where he is mentioned in passing as \"my friend Cornelius Gallus\". Within a few years he was one of the most admired young poets in the city.\n\n**Virgil's friend.** Gallus and Virgil were close friends, and the friendship is written into the *Eclogues*. According to the ancient commentators, when Virgil's family farm near Mantua was confiscated for veterans after Philippi, Gallus was one of the influential men who helped him. Virgil paid him back in the only currency a poet has. In *Eclogue* 6 he imagines Gallus being led up Mount Helicon by one of the Muses, where the whole chorus of Apollo rises to greet him and he is handed the pipes of Hesiod: a solemn consecration as a poet. And the whole of *Eclogue* 10, the last poem of the book, is dedicated to him: *pauca meo Gallo*, \"a few verses for my Gallus\". In it Gallus is lying under a rock in Arcadia, dying of love, while shepherds, gods and even the sheep gather round to ask what is wrong. It is Gallus who speaks the most famous line of the poem, *omnia vincit Amor: et nos cedamus Amori*, \"Love conquers all: let us too give in to Love\". Ancient commentators believed that some of the lines Virgil gives him there were adapted from Gallus's own poems, which would make *Eclogue* 10 the best surviving witness to what they sounded like.\n\n**Lycoris.** The woman Gallus suffers for in *Eclogue* 10, and the heroine of his four books of love elegies, is called Lycoris. Her real name, according to the commentators, was Volumnia Cytheris, and she was the most famous actress in Rome: a freedwoman who performed in mime, the scandalous, semi-improvised popular theatre of the day, and who had previously been the mistress of Mark Antony, with whom she toured Italy in a litter, to the horror of respectable society. Cicero once found himself at a dinner party next to her and wrote to a friend, plainly embarrassed, that he had had no idea she would be there. In *Eclogue* 10 Lycoris has left Gallus to follow a soldier to the snows of the Alps and the Rhine. The pattern of Roman love elegy, the poet devoted to a beautiful, unfaithful woman under a Greek pseudonym, begins here.\n\n**The general.** Unlike almost every other Roman poet, Gallus was also a successful soldier. In the final campaign against Antony and Cleopatra in 30 BC he commanded Octavian's legions in the west of Egypt, captured the town of Paraetonium and drove back Antony's attack on it. He then played a part in the capture of Cleopatra: according to Plutarch, Gallus kept the queen talking at the barred door of her mausoleum while his colleague Proculeius climbed in through a window behind her. Octavian rewarded him with the greatest prize available to a man of his rank: in 30 BC Gallus became the first Roman prefect of Egypt.\n\n**Too much glory.** Egypt was not an ordinary province. It was the richest territory in the empire and the granary of Rome, and Augustus governed it as his personal possession, through a man of equestrian rank, not a senator, so that nobody powerful enough to rebel could ever control it. Gallus did his job extremely well. He crushed a revolt in Upper Egypt, took five cities in fifteen days, pushed beyond the First Cataract of the Nile, and received ambassadors from the king of Ethiopia. Then he made the fatal mistake of telling everybody. In April 29 BC he set up at Philae a stone inscription in three languages (Latin, Greek and Egyptian hieroglyphs) in which he boasted that he had led an army \"where neither the Roman people nor the kings of Egypt had ever carried arms\", and mentioned Octavian only briefly. According to Cassius Dio, he also put up statues of himself all over Egypt, had his achievements carved on the pyramids, and, worst of all, said disrespectful things about Augustus, probably after a few drinks.\n\n**The fall.** In 27 or 26 BC he was recalled and denounced by a former friend. Augustus formally renounced his friendship (*renuntiatio amicitiae*) and banned him from his house and his provinces, which in Augustan Rome was the end of a career and very nearly the end of a life. The Senate, eager to show its loyalty, piled on: it voted to have him tried, exiled, and his property handed over to Augustus. Gallus did not wait for the verdict. He killed himself in 26 BC, at the age of forty-three. Suetonius says that Augustus wept when he heard the news, and complained that he alone was not allowed to be angry with his friends only as much as he wanted. Ovid, who knew something about displeasing Augustus, later summed up the case in two lines: it was not writing about Lycoris that ruined Gallus, but not holding his tongue when he had had too much wine.\n\n**Erased.** What followed is often called a *damnatio memoriae*, and although the term is modern and there is no record of a formal decree under that name, the effect was very much the same: Gallus was removed from the record. The Philae inscription was smashed and its pieces used as building material for a later temple, where archaeologists found it in 1896. In Alexandria he had built a new forum for Octavian and dedicated an obelisk there in his own name; his inscription was removed and replaced. That obelisk was later brought to Rome by Caligula and now stands in the middle of St Peter's Square, and in the 1960s the archaeologist Filippo Magi realised that the rows of small holes on its base were the fixing holes of bronze letters from the original dedication. When he joined the dots, they spelled out the name of Gallus. Every pilgrim who has ever crossed St Peter's Square has walked past the one monument in Rome that still, invisibly, carries his name. The fourth-century commentator Servius adds a more famous and more doubtful story: that the fourth book of Virgil's *Georgics* originally ended with a long praise of Gallus, and that after his fall Augustus ordered Virgil to replace it, which is how we got the story of Aristaeus and Orpheus instead. Most modern scholars think this is a legend, but it says a great deal that ancient readers found it believable.\n\nHis poetry did not vanish immediately. Ovid could still promise that \"Gallus will be known in the West, Gallus in the East, and his Lycoris with him\", and Quintilian read and ranked him at the end of the first century AD. But no manuscript of his four books survived the end of antiquity. Until 1978, the founder of Latin love elegy was represented by exactly one line.\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n- **Amores** (*Love Poems*, probably in the 40s and 30s BC) -- four books of love elegies addressed to Lycoris, the work that founded Roman love elegy as a genre. Before 1978 only one pentameter survived, quoted by a late geographer for its description of a river: *uno tellures dividit amne duas*, \"with a single stream it divides two lands\". In 1978 a papyrus found at Qasr Ibrim in Egyptian Nubia gave us about ten more lines (see the excerpt below). [Fragmentary]\n- **Translations or adaptations of Euphorion** -- Virgil's *Eclogues* hint that Gallus adapted the obscure, learned Greek poet Euphorion of Chalcis, the kind of Alexandrian model the neoterics loved, possibly including a poem on the origin of the Grynean grove of Apollo. [Lost]\n- **The Philae inscription** (29 BC) -- not literature, but the only surviving text certainly written in Gallus's name: his official account of his victories in Egypt, in Latin and Greek, with an Egyptian version in hieroglyphs. It is now in the Egyptian Museum in Cairo. [Survives, damaged]\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nWith about ten lines of verse, there is very little to say about Gallus's style from his own words, and anything we say rests largely on what others said about him. Quintilian, ranking the Latin elegists, called Tibullus the most polished and elegant, said some preferred Propertius, found Ovid too playful, and described Gallus as *durior*, \"rather harsh\". That fits what the papyrus shows. His couplets are self-contained, each making a single point and stopping. He ends a pentameter on a five-syllable word, *historiae*, which Ovid, a generation later, would almost never do. And his spelling is old-fashioned: *quom* for *cum*, *spolieis* and *deiuitiora* with *ei* for long *i*, *Kato* with a K. These may be the habits of the copyist rather than of the poet, but they give the lines an archaic look that fits an author writing at the very beginning of the Augustan age, closer to Catullus than to Ovid.\n\nThe papyrus also confirms, at least in miniature, what the ancients said of his range: in nine lines he moves from his mistress, to Caesar's glory, to the Muses, to the literary critics whose judgement he fears. Love, politics and poetry in one breath. That combination, the love poet who also writes for the regime and keeps one eye on his critics, is exactly the tension that runs through the whole of Augustan elegy.\n\n## Latin Excerpt\n\n### Excerpt: Gallus fr. 2 (the Qasr Ibrim papyrus)\n\n**Source:** Gallus, fragment 2 Courtney, from *Living Poets*, Durham University (livingpoets.awh.durham.ac.uk/w/index.php/Gallus_fr._2), following the text first published by R. D. Anderson, P. J. Parsons and R. G. M. Nisbet in the *Journal of Roman Studies* (1979). The Latin Library and Splash Latino do not carry Gallus. Square brackets mark letters lost on the papyrus and restored by editors; dots mark letters that are lost or illegible.\n\n**Context:** In 1978 an expedition of the Egypt Exploration Society was excavating Qasr Ibrim, a fortress on a cliff above the Nile in Nubia, far to the south of Egypt, where a Roman garrison was stationed around 25-21 BC. In a heap of rubbish from the Roman occupation they found a scrap of papyrus with Latin elegiac couplets written on it, with the name *Lycoris* in the first surviving line. Since Lycoris was Gallus's mistress, and since no other poet is known to have used the name, the lines were identified as his. The papyrus was probably copied within a few decades of the poems being written, possibly in the poet's own lifetime, which makes it the oldest surviving manuscript of Latin poetry. That Gallus's poems should reappear in Egypt, the province that ruined him, carried there perhaps by one of the soldiers who served under his successor, is an irony he would have appreciated.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> ] tristia nequit[ia fact]a, Lycori, tua.\n>\n> fata mihi, Caesar, tum erunt mea dulcia quom tu\n> maxima Romanae pars eris historiae,\n> postque tuum reditum multorum templa deorum\n> fixa legam spolieis deiuitiora tueis.\n>\n> . . . . . ] . . . . . tandem fecerunt c[ar]mina Musae\n> quae possem domina deicere digna mea.\n> . . . . . . . . . . . ] . atur idem tibi, non ego, Visce,\n> . . ] . . . . . . . . l . Kato, iudice te uereor.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\n> ... made sad, Lycoris, by your wickedness.\n>\n> My fate will be sweet to me, Caesar, at the moment when you\n> become the greatest part of Roman history,\n> and when, after your return, I read that the temples of many gods\n> are the richer for your spoils hung up in them.\n>\n> ... at last the Muses have made poems\n> that I could recite, worthy of my mistress.\n> ... the same to you, I do not, Viscus,\n> ... Cato, with you as my judge, fear.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThese nine lines, some of them broken, are almost everything we have of the man who invented Latin love elegy, so every word has been argued over. The first line, of which only the end survives, is a pentameter addressed to Lycoris, accusing her of *nequitia*, a word that means anything from naughtiness to sheer wickedness and will become a key word of later elegy, where the lover's *nequitia* and the mistress's are both on display. That single half-line already contains the whole elegiac situation: the poet, the woman, and the hurt.\n\nThe next four lines are a complete epigram of a completely different kind: a political compliment. \"My fate will be sweet to me, Caesar, when you become the greatest part of Roman history.\" Which Caesar is meant is debated. Many scholars think it is Julius Caesar, about to leave for his planned war against Parthia in 44 BC, which would date the poem before the Ides of March; others think it is Octavian. Either way, the lines show that the founder of love elegy was also, already, writing praise of the ruler, the same mix that Propertius and Ovid would handle so uneasily. The grammar is clean: a future *erunt* answered by *quom tu... eris* (with *quom*, the old spelling of *cum*), then *legam*, \"I shall read\", governing an indirect statement with *esse* understood, *templa... deiuitiora*, \"that the temples are richer\". *Fixa... spolieis... tueis* refers to the Roman custom of nailing captured arms and trophies to the walls of temples.\n\nThe last four lines are literary: at last the Muses have produced poems \"worthy of my mistress\", and the poet wonders how they will be received by two critics, Viscus and Cato. Viscus is probably one of the Visci brothers whom Horace lists among the readers whose approval he wants; Cato is almost certainly Valerius Cato, the grammarian and poet who was the great authority of the neoteric generation. So in nine lines Gallus places himself exactly: between his mistress, the most powerful man in Rome, and the literary establishment.\n\nFormally, each couplet stands alone, and the pentameters end on a two-syllable word (*tua*, *mea*) or, unusually, on a longer one (*historiae*, *uereor*). Later elegists, above all Ovid, made the two-syllable ending almost compulsory, so the papyrus gives us a glimpse of the genre before its rules were fixed. Notice also the old-fashioned spellings, *spolieis*, *deiuitiora*, *deicere* (for *dicere*), *quom*, *Kato*, and the use of *u* for consonantal *v* (*deiuitiora*, *uereor*), which is how the Romans themselves wrote it.\n\nA word of caution: a minority of scholars have doubted that these lines are by Gallus at all, or have found them disappointingly flat for such a famous poet. Most accept them. Whether they are his best lines is another question, and an unfair one: they are simply the lines that happened to end up on a rubbish heap in Nubia.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nGallus is the clearest case in the whole project of an author whose importance has nothing to do with how much of him survives. He is the founder of Latin love elegy as a genre. Catullus had written love poems in elegiac couplets, but Gallus seems to have been the first to make an entire book, and then four, out of elegies about a single mistress under a Greek pseudonym, and in doing so he fixed the shape of the genre that Tibullus, Propertius and Ovid would bring to its peak. The ancients were in no doubt about this. When Ovid lists the line of Roman elegists, Gallus comes first; when Quintilian claims that in elegy \"we challenge the Greeks\", Gallus is one of the four names he gives; and Virgil's *Eclogue* 10 turns him into the very image of the elegiac lover, suffering in Arcadia, defeated by Love.\n\nMany of the conventions of Roman elegy probably go back to him: the poet as the slave of his mistress (*servitium amoris*), love as a kind of military service (*militia amoris*), the beloved under a pseudonym that hides a real woman, the poet who refuses war and career in favour of love and then writes about Caesar's victories anyway. We cannot prove most of this from his own lines, because the lines are gone. But the genre he started is one of the great Roman contributions to European literature, and it runs from the Augustan elegists to the troubadours, Petrarch, and every love poet who has ever complained in verse about a cruel beloved.\n\nHis other legacy is a warning. Gallus was the first great writer of the Augustan age to discover that the regime gave a great deal and could take it all back. His fall in 26 BC came only a year after the \"restored Republic\" of 27 BC, and it showed everyone, poets included, where the limits were. Ovid would learn the same lesson thirty-four years later.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: NC (Not Comparable)**\n\nAbout ten lines of verse, several of them broken, cannot support a reliable rating. What survives is not especially hard: the syntax of the papyrus is clear, the vocabulary standard, and the main obstacles are the archaic spelling, the gaps in the text and the elegiac metre itself. But nine lines cannot tell us how Gallus handled the long, dense, allusive elegies his contemporaries admired, and Quintilian's *durior* suggests that he was not an easy read. So NC, as with Livius Andronicus. What NC does not measure is importance, and here Gallus ranks with anyone in the era: without him there would be no Tibullus, no Propertius, and no Ovid as we know them.\n\n---\n\n# PUBLIUS VERGILIUS MARO -- VIRGIL (70 -- 19 BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nVirgil is the most famous Latin poet who ever lived, and possibly the most famous Latin author of any kind. He is also, by every ancient account, one of the shyest, most awkward and most anxious men ever to become a celebrity. Most of what we know about his life comes from a biography by Suetonius that survives in a version by the fourth-century grammarian Donatus, and from the commentary of Servius. Some of it is legend. All of it is worth telling. And set him beside his friend Horace, and the difference between them is clear at once: Virgil is always aiming at the stars and beyond, at fate, empire, the gods and the meaning of history, while Horace is content to remind us of our humanity. Both are reasons to enjoy them fully.\n\n**A laurel in a ditch.** Publius Vergilius Maro was born on 15 October 70 BC in the village of Andes, near Mantua, in what was then Cisalpine Gaul. His father was a man of modest origins: according to Donatus, either a potter or the hired hand of a minor official called Magius, who did well enough to marry his employer's daughter, Magia Polla, and to buy land and bees of his own. Before Virgil was born, his mother dreamed that she gave birth to a laurel branch, which took root and grew at once into a fully grown tree covered in fruit and flowers. The next day she gave birth in a ditch by the side of the road, on the way to the country. The baby, we are told, did not cry, and had such a gentle expression that everyone took it as a sign of good fortune. A poplar planted on the spot grew so fast that local women started praying to it. Every great Roman got a few omens; Virgil got a botanical garden.\n\n**The maiden.** He was educated at Cremona and Milan, then in Rome, where he studied rhetoric to become a lawyer. It did not go well. Donatus says he pleaded exactly one case in court, and spoke so slowly that he seemed almost uneducated. He then turned to medicine and, above all, mathematics, before ending up at Naples in the Epicurean school of Siro. He was tall, dark-skinned, with the face of a countryman, and in poor health all his life: he suffered from his stomach, his throat and headaches, and often coughed up blood. In Naples people called him *Parthenias*, \"the maiden\", because of the modesty of his life. When he did become famous, on the rare occasions he visited Rome and people recognised him in the street and pointed him out, he would duck into the nearest house to escape them.\n\n**The farm.** In 41 BC, after Philippi, the triumvirs confiscated land all over northern Italy to settle their veterans, and the Virgil family farm was among them. According to the ancient commentators, Virgil was nearly killed by the veteran who took it, and only got it back (if he ever did) thanks to influential friends, among them Asinius Pollio and Cornelius Gallus. The experience went straight into his first book. The opening poem of the *Eclogues* is a dialogue between a shepherd who has kept his land and one who has been thrown off it, and the dispossessed shepherd's question to the new soldier-owner, *impius haec tam culta novalia miles habebit?*, \"is some godless soldier going to have these well-tended fields?\", is the civil wars seen from the farmyard.\n\n**Maecenas, and a bear licking her cubs.** The *Eclogues* made him famous almost overnight: they were even performed on stage by actors, to wild applause. They also brought him into the circle of Maecenas, who became his patron and friend, and who asked him for a poem on farming. Virgil spent seven years on the four books of the *Georgics*, about 2,200 lines, which works out at less than a line a day. His method, as Donatus describes it, explains why. Every morning he dictated a large number of verses; then he spent the rest of the day reworking them and cutting them down to a very few, saying that he produced his poems the way a she-bear produces her cubs: by licking them into shape. In 29 BC he read the finished *Georgics* to Octavian, just back from Actium, at Atella in Campania. The reading took four days, and whenever Virgil's voice gave out, Maecenas took over.\n\n**The Aeneid, and an emperor who would not wait.** For the last eleven years of his life he worked on the *Aeneid*. He first wrote the whole story out in prose and divided it into twelve books, then turned it into verse piece by piece, in no particular order, as the mood took him. Where he could not yet find the right line, he left a half-line and moved on, calling these unfinished verses \"props\" (*tibicines*), put in to hold up the building until the real columns were ready. Fifty-eight of those half-lines are still in the poem, and one of them appears in the excerpt below. Augustus could hardly bear the wait. While fighting in Spain in 26-25 BC he wrote to Virgil begging, half jokingly and half threateningly, for \"even a first sketch or any section at all\" of the poem. Virgil replied that he had nothing worthy to send, and that he had taken on something so enormous that he sometimes thought he must have been out of his mind to start it.\n\n**Octavia faints.** Eventually he gave in, and read three books to Augustus and his family: Books II, IV and VI. Book VI ends with Aeneas in the underworld, where his father Anchises shows him the souls of the great Romans of the future waiting to be born. Last of all comes a young man of extraordinary promise, surrounded by mourning, and Anchises, weeping, reveals who he is: *tu Marcellus eris*, \"you will be Marcellus\". This was Augustus's nephew, son-in-law and intended heir, who had died in 23 BC at the age of nineteen, and his mother, Augustus's sister Octavia, was in the room. Donatus says that when Virgil reached those lines she fainted, and was revived only with difficulty; later tradition adds that Augustus and Octavia both wept, and that Octavia rewarded the poet with ten thousand sesterces for every line. It is the best evidence we have of what Virgil's poetry could do to an audience. These were not readers studying a set text. They were a grieving family hearing their dead boy turned into the last and saddest of Rome's heroes, in lines so beautiful that they could not bear them.\n\n**Best or bust.** Virgil was, in the most literal sense, a perfectionist: he would rather have no poem than an imperfect one. In 19 BC, with the *Aeneid* complete in draft, he set out for Greece and Asia, planning to spend three more years revising it and then devote the rest of his life to philosophy. In Athens he met Augustus, who persuaded him to come home. On a visit to Megara in the burning sun he fell ill; the illness worsened on the voyage, and he died at Brundisium on 21 September 19 BC, a few weeks short of his fifty-first birthday. Before leaving Italy he had asked his friend Varius to burn the *Aeneid* if anything happened to him, and on his deathbed he called for the manuscript boxes so that he could burn it himself. Nobody brought them. In his will he left the manuscript to Varius and Plotius Tucca, on condition that they publish nothing he had not already published. Augustus overruled the will, and ordered them to publish the poem exactly as it was, adding nothing and correcting only what was obviously wrong: which is why the half-lines are still there. We have the most important poem in Latin literature because the emperor refused to respect a dead man's wishes. It is very nearly a matter of luck.\n\n**Fame, fortune and thieves.** He died rich, with a fortune of almost ten million sesterces from the generosity of his friends, and a house on the Esquiline next to the gardens of Maecenas, which he hardly used. He was buried at Naples, under an epitaph that sums up his whole career in two lines: *Mantua me genuit, Calabri rapuere, tenet nunc / Parthenope; cecini pascua, rura, duces*, \"Mantua bore me, Calabria took me, now Naples holds me; I sang of pastures, fields and leaders\". He also had his critics, who accused him of stealing whole lines from Homer. His answer, according to Donatus, was the best literary comeback in antiquity: \"Why don't they try the same thefts themselves? They will find it is easier to steal the club from Hercules than a line from Homer.\"\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n- **Bucolica** or **Eclogae** (*Eclogues*, c. 42-38 BC) -- ten short pastoral poems, modelled on the Greek idylls of Theocritus, set in an imaginary countryside of singing shepherds that later centuries would call Arcadia. The themes alternate between love (Eclogues 2, 8 and 10, the last one for Gallus), singing contests and poetry (3, 5, 7), the land confiscations and the intrusion of history into the countryside (1 and 9), and grand visions of the cosmos and the future (4 and 6). The fourth, which announces the birth of a child who will bring back the Golden Age, was later read by Christians as a prophecy of Christ. [Complete]\n- **Georgica** (*Georgics*, c. 37-29 BC) -- a didactic poem on farming in four books, dedicated to Maecenas. Book I is about crops and the weather, Book II about trees and vines (with the famous praise of Italy), Book III about cattle, horses and sheep (ending in a terrible plague), Book IV about bees. On the surface it is a manual; underneath, it is a poem about work (*labor omnia vicit / improbus*, \"relentless work conquered everything\"), about Italy, and about how order can be rebuilt after the chaos of civil war. It ends with the myth of Aristaeus, who loses his bees and gets them back, and of Orpheus, who loses Eurydice and does not. [Complete]\n- **Aeneis** (*Aeneid*, c. 29-19 BC) -- the national epic of Rome, in twelve books, unfinished at the poet's death. The first six books, the wanderings of Aeneas from burning Troy to Italy, are Virgil's *Odyssey*; the last six, the war in Latium, are his *Iliad*. Its hero is defined not by anger, like Achilles, or cunning, like Ulysses, but by *pietas*, duty to the gods, to his family and to his mission. Its great theme is the price of that mission: Rome is founded, but at the cost of Troy, of Dido, of Turnus and of countless young men, and the poem ends abruptly, with Aeneas killing Turnus in a moment of rage. It is a celebration of Augustan Rome and, at the same time, one of the saddest poems ever written about what empires cost. [Complete, but unrevised]\n- **Appendix Vergiliana** -- a collection of minor poems attributed to Virgil in antiquity (the *Culex*, the *Ciris*, the *Moretum* and others), almost all of them written by other people and later attached to his name, which is what happens when you become the most famous poet in the world. [Mostly spurious]\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nVirgil is the point at which Latin poetry reaches stylistic perfection, and he made the dactylic hexameter what it would be for the rest of antiquity. Ennius had brought the hexameter into Latin two centuries earlier, and Lucretius and Catullus had refined it; but it was Virgil who fixed its rules, its rhythms, its pauses and its endings, so completely that after him every Latin epic poet wrote Virgil's hexameter, and every poet who wrote in hexameters in the genres he touched, pastoral and didactic as well as epic, wrote in his shadow. Every line is weighed. The sound imitates the sense: slow, heavy spondees for effort, grief or great size, quick dactyls for speed. Words are placed for emphasis at the start of the line or at its main pause. And there is not a word too many. For a translator, this perfection is precisely the problem.\n\nThe difficulties come in layers. The first is **poetic word order**. Adjectives and nouns are separated across the line, often framing it, and the most famous device of all is the transferred epithet (*hypallage*), where an adjective is attached grammatically to one noun while it really describes another, for the sake of the meaning and of the metre. The seventh line of the *Aeneid* is the classic example: *altae moenia Romae*, literally \"the walls of high Rome\", where we would expect \"the high walls of Rome\". Then come **poetic plurals** (*colla* for a single neck, *silentia* for silence), Greek forms and case endings for Greek names, archaisms (*olli* for *illi*, *aulai* for *aulae*), and **elision**, where a final vowel or *-m* before another vowel disappears in pronunciation, sometimes several times in one line, so that words have to be mentally pulled apart before they can be read. **Lexicon: MEDIUM to HIGH; Syntactic complexity: HIGH; Stylistic figures: HIGH.**\n\nThe second layer is **allusion**. Virgil writes with the whole of Greek and Latin literature open on the desk: Homer above all, but also Hesiod, Theocritus, Apollonius of Rhodes, Ennius, Lucretius and Catullus. Myths are referred to, not told; a single epithet can stand for a whole story. And compared with Horace, the Latin is honest: there is none of Horace's wilful game-playing with word order. But the density of everything at once makes Virgil hard all the time. **Allusion density: HIGH.**\n\nDifficulty also varies a great deal across his works, and here a widespread school habit is simply wrong. Students are very often started on the *Aeneid*, as if Virgil's most famous poem were the natural way in. It is not. The **Eclogues** are the right place to begin: they are short, the vocabulary is limited, the situations are simple, and a student can translate a whole poem from beginning to end and enjoy the rare satisfaction of having actually finished a work of Virgil. They range from B to low A. The **Aeneid** is a step up: the narrative helps, but the speeches, the similes and the sheer density of the verse put it in the middle to upper range of A. The **Georgics** are the hardest of the three, and probably the hardest Latin poetry of the whole era outside Horace's lyrics: technical farming vocabulary, rare plant and animal names, Greek words, mythological allusions and a compressed didactic syntax all at once. At their most difficult they push into S territory.\n\n## Latin Excerpts\n\n### Excerpt 1: Bucolica (Eclogue) 2.6-18 -- Corydon's Complaint\n\n**Source:** Virgil, *Bucolica*, Eclogue 2, lines 6-18, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/vergil/ec2.shtml)\n\n**Context:** Eclogue 2 is a love complaint. The shepherd Corydon is in love with Alexis, a beautiful young slave who is his master's favourite, and who does not care for him at all. The first five lines, spoken by the narrator, tell us that Corydon keeps coming alone to a grove of beech trees to pour out his \"unpolished\" song to the mountains and the woods, in vain. Then Corydon begins to speak, and these are the first thirteen lines of his monologue. Virgil's model is the eleventh *Idyll* of Theocritus, in which the Cyclops Polyphemus sings to the sea nymph Galatea, who will not have him either. Keep that Cyclops in mind: he comes back in Excerpt 3. (The ancient biographers, inevitably, identified Alexis with a real slave boy given to Virgil by Pollio; there is no way of knowing, and in Greek pastoral the love of a shepherd for a boy was a standard literary situation.)\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> 'O crudelis Alexi, nihil mea carmina curas?\n> nil nostri miserere? mori me denique cogis?\n> nunc etiam pecudes umbras et frigora captant,\n> nunc virides etiam occultant spineta lacertos,\n> Thestylis et rapido fessis messoribus aestu               10\n> alia serpyllumque herbas contundit olentis.\n> at mecum raucis, tua dum vestigia lustro,\n> sole sub ardenti resonant arbusta cicadis.\n> nonne fuit satius tristis Amaryllidos iras\n> atque superba pati fastidia? nonne Menalcan,               15\n> quamvis ille niger, quamvis tu candidus esses?\n> o formose puer, nimium ne crede colori;\n> alba ligustra cadunt, vaccinia nigra leguntur.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\n\"O cruel Alexis, do you care nothing for my songs?\nHave you no pity for me? Will you drive me to my death at last?\nNow even the flocks are looking for shade and cool air,\nnow even the thornbushes hide the green lizards,\nand Thestylis, for the reapers worn out by the scorching heat,\nis pounding garlic and thyme, the fragrant herbs.\nBut with me, as I follow your footsteps,\nunder the blazing sun, the orchards ring with hoarse cicadas.\nWould it not have been better to put up with Amaryllis's\nbitter rages and her proud disdain? Or with Menalcas,\nhowever dark he was, however fair you are?\nO beautiful boy, do not trust your colouring too much:\nthe white privet flowers fall, the dark bilberries are picked.\"\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThis is the kind of passage that makes the Eclogues the right place to start Virgil. The situation is simple, the sentences are short, and almost every line is a complete thought. The difficulties are real but manageable, and they are exactly the ones you will meet everywhere else in Virgil.\n\nStart with the traps. *Nil nostri miserere*: *misereor* takes the genitive, and *nostri* is a poetic plural for *mei*, \"of me\". *Alia* in line 11 is not \"other things\" but the neuter plural of *alium*, \"garlic\", one of the classic false friends of Latin poetry; *olentis* is the old accusative plural for *olentes*, agreeing with *herbas*, and *serpyllum* is a Greek plant name, wild thyme. *Satius* is the comparative of *satis*, \"better\", and *quamvis... esses* is a concessive subjunctive. Line 15 ends on *Menalcan*, a Greek accusative. And *vaccinia* in the last line has been argued over for centuries: it may be bilberries or a kind of dark hyacinth; either way, it is the dark flower or fruit that people pick, against the white flower that is left to fall.\n\nThen look at how the lines are built. *raucis... cicadis* frames lines 12 and 13 together, the hoarse cicadas surrounding the whole scene with their noise, while Corydon wanders in the noonday heat, the one time of day when every sensible creature, even the lizards, is in the shade. The repeated *nunc etiam... nunc... etiam* stresses that the whole world is resting except him. The last line is a perfect chiasmus: *alba ligustra cadunt, vaccinia nigra leguntur*, adjective-noun-verb, noun-adjective-verb, white against black, falling against being chosen. It is a proverb in miniature, telling a beautiful boy that beauty fades and fair skin is no guarantee of being wanted, and it is the first line of Virgil that many readers learn by heart.\n\nFinally, notice the tone. Corydon is pathetic and a little ridiculous: he is literally out in the midday sun, he lists the people he could have had instead, and in the very next lines (not in the excerpt) he starts boasting about how many sheep he owns. Virgil lets us smile at him and feel for him at the same time, which is the emotional key to all of Virgil.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 2: Georgica IV.1-24 -- A Home for the Bees\n\n**Source:** Virgil, *Georgica*, Book IV, lines 1-24, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/vergil/geo4.shtml). The dashes around *nam pabula venti / ferre domum prohibent* in the source have been printed as parentheses.\n\n**Context:** The opening of the last book of the *Georgics*. After crops, trees and livestock, Virgil turns to bees, and announces his subject to Maecenas in the first seven lines with a mixture of solemnity and irony: he is going to write about the bees as if they were a nation, with leaders, customs and wars. Then, with the word *Principio*, \"first of all\", the farming manual begins, and the first practical problem is where to put the hives. This is the book that ends with Aristaeus and Orpheus, and that Servius believed originally ended with the praise of Gallus.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Protinus aerii mellis caelestia dona\n> exsequar: hanc etiam, Maecenas, adspice partem.\n> Admiranda tibi levium spectacula rerum\n> magnanimosque duces totiusque ordine gentis\n> mores et studia et populos et proelia dicam.               5\n> In tenui labor; at tenuis non gloria, si quem\n> numina laeva sinunt auditque vocatus Apollo.\n> Principio sedes apibus statioque petenda,\n> quo neque sit ventis aditus (nam pabula venti\n> ferre domum prohibent) neque oves haedique petulci               10\n> floribus insultent aut errans bucula campo\n> decutiat rorem et surgentes atterat herbas.\n> Absint et picti squalentia terga lacerti\n> pinguibus a stabulis meropesque aliaeque volucres\n> et manibus Procne pectus signata cruentis;               15\n> omnia nam late vastant ipsasque volantes\n> ore ferunt dulcem nidis immitibus escam.\n> At liquidi fontes et stagna virentia musco\n> adsint et tenuis fugiens per gramina rivus,\n> palmaque vestibulum aut ingens oleaster inumbret,               20\n> ut, cum prima novi ducent examina reges\n> vere suo ludetque favis emissa iuventus,\n> vicina invitet decedere ripa calori,\n> obviaque hospitiis teneat frondentibus arbos.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nNext I shall go on to the heavenly gift of honey that comes from the air:\nlook on this part too, Maecenas.\nI shall tell you of a marvellous spectacle of tiny things:\ngreat-hearted leaders and, in due order, a whole nation's\ncustoms and pursuits, its peoples and its battles.\nThe labour is on a slight subject; but not slight the glory, if the unfriendly\npowers allow a poet, and Apollo hears when he is called.\nFirst of all, a home and a settled place must be found for the bees,\nwhere the winds can find no way in (for the winds stop them\nfrom carrying their food home), where no sheep and frisky kids\ntrample on the flowers, and no heifer, straying across the plain,\nshakes off the dew and wears down the growing grass.\nKeep away from the rich hives the painted lizard with its scaly back,\nand the bee-eaters and the other birds,\nand Procne, her breast marked by bloodstained hands;\nfor they lay everything waste far and wide, and snatch the bees\nthemselves in flight, in their beaks, a sweet morsel for their pitiless nests.\nBut let there be clear springs nearby, and pools green with moss,\nand a thin stream slipping away through the grass,\nand let a palm or a great wild olive shade the entrance,\nso that, when the new kings lead out the first swarms\nin their own springtime, and the young ones, let out of the combs, are at play,\na nearby bank may invite them to escape the heat,\nand a tree in their path may hold them in its leafy welcome.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe first seven lines are a small manifesto. *In tenui labor; at tenuis non gloria*: \"the labour is on something slight, but the glory is not slight\". The same adjective is used twice, first for the subject and then, negated, for the fame, a figure called polyptoton, and the line is a Callimachean declaration: small subjects, perfectly treated, can win great glory. At the same time Virgil plays the epic game in miniature: *magnanimos duces*, *populos*, *proelia* are the vocabulary of the *Aeneid*, applied to insects. The Romans thought the leader of the hive was a male, a king, which is why the bees have *reges* in line 21. Throughout Book IV the hive is a little model of the Roman state, hard-working, disciplined, and ready to die for its leader, and the reader is never quite sure how seriously to take the comparison.\n\nFrom line 8 the manual begins, and with it the Georgics' typical difficulties. The syntax is instruction in the subjunctive: a gerundive of obligation (*petenda*, \"must be found\"), a relative clause of characteristic (*quo neque sit... neque... insultent... aut... decutiat... atterat*), and a series of jussives, *absint*, *adsint*, *inumbret*, \"let them be absent\", \"let there be\", \"let it shade\". The vocabulary is technical and zoological: *meropes* are bee-eaters, *oleaster* is the wild olive, *bucula* is a young cow, *haedi petulci* are kids that butt and frisk. And then, in the middle of the birdwatching, a myth arrives without warning: *manibus Procne pectus signata cruentis*. Procne killed her son Itys in revenge on her husband and was turned into a swallow, and the red mark on the swallow's breast was said to be the blood from her hands. Virgil does not explain any of this; he expects you to know. Note also the Greek-style accusative of respect, *pectus signata*, \"marked as to the breast\", and the framing of the line by *manibus... cruentis*, which encloses Procne in her own bloodstained hands.\n\nThe last section turns from danger to welcome. Notice how the future tenses (*ducent*, *ludet*) and the purpose clause (*ut... invitet... teneat*) imagine the young swarm's first spring, and how the vocabulary becomes almost human: *iuventus* is \"the youth\" of a city, *ludet* is play, *hospitiis* is hospitality. *Decedere calori* is a dative after a verb of yielding: \"to give way to the heat\", that is, to get out of it. The hive becomes a city, with a shaded entrance and a riverside park. This is the Georgics at their best and their hardest: a farming instruction, a lesson in natural history, a myth and a political allegory, all in the same twenty-four lines.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 3: Aeneis III.655-665 -- Polyphemus\n\n**Source:** Virgil, *Aeneis*, Book III, lines 655-665, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/vergil/aen3.shtml)\n\n**Context:** In Book III Aeneas is telling Dido the story of his wanderings. Landing in Sicily, near Etna, the Trojans meet a ragged, starving Greek called Achaemenides, one of Ulysses' men, who was left behind when Ulysses escaped from the cave of the Cyclops. He has been hiding in the woods for three months, and he has just finished begging the Trojans to take him away, or kill him, when the Cyclops himself appears on the mountain. Virgil is rewriting *Odyssey* IX, but from the other side: we see Polyphemus after the blinding.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Vix ea fatus erat summo cum monte videmus               655\n> ipsum inter pecudes vasta se mole moventem\n> pastorem Polyphemum et litora nota petentem,\n> monstrum horrendum, informe, ingens, cui lumen ademptum.\n> trunca manum pinus regit et vestigia firmat;\n> lanigerae comitantur oves; ea sola voluptas               660\n> solamenque mali.\n> postquam altos tetigit fluctus et ad aequora venit,\n> luminis effossi fluidum lavit inde cruorem\n> dentibus infrendens gemitu, graditurque per aequor\n> iam medium, necdum fluctus latera ardua tinxit.                665\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nHe had scarcely spoken these words when, high on the mountain, we see\nthe shepherd Polyphemus himself, moving his vast bulk among his flocks\nand making for the shore he knew so well:\na monster, horrible, shapeless, huge, whose sight had been taken from him.\nA lopped pine trunk guides his hand and steadies his steps;\nhis woolly sheep go with him, his only pleasure\nand the comfort of his misery.\nWhen he reached the deep water and came to the sea,\nhe washed away the blood still oozing from his gouged-out eye,\ngnashing his teeth and groaning; and he walks through the sea,\nright out into the middle, and still the waves have not wet his towering sides.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nLine 658 is one of the most famous lines in Latin poetry, and it is famous for its sound: *monstrum horrendum, informe, ingens, cui lumen ademptum*. Read it aloud with the elisions, as a Roman would have heard it: *monstr'horrend'inform'ingens*. Three times in a row the final syllable of a word disappears into the next one (this fusion of vowels across words is called *synaloephe*), so that four words collapse into one long, shapeless mass of sound, exactly like the shapeless mass of the giant. The metre does the same: the first four feet of the line are all spondees, long syllable after long syllable, so the line moves as heavily and slowly as Polyphemus himself, lumbering down the mountain. Then the movement changes. After the pile of adjectives, the line ends with the ordinary, quiet cadence of the hexameter, *lumen ademptum*, and a bare, short relative clause: \"whose sight had been taken\". After the horror, the pity. That is Virgil's emotional method in a single verse: the sound makes us feel the monster's size, and the last words make us feel his loss.\n\nThe rest of the passage keeps the pity going. The giant leans on a pine trunk like an old man on a stick; his sheep, his only joy, walk beside him; he washes his wound in the sea, groaning. Notice the shift of tenses: the narration moves between historic presents (*videmus*, *regit*, *comitantur*, *graditur*) and perfects (*tetigit*, *venit*, *lavit*), which keeps the scene vivid and slightly unstable. Notice also the half-line, *solamenque mali.* (661): this is one of Virgil's unfinished \"props\", left in the poem when he died. Some manuscripts fill it out with *de collo fistula pendet*, \"a pipe hangs from his neck\", but that is a later addition, and modern editors leave the line as Virgil left it. And, if you have read Excerpt 1, notice the word *informe*. In Eclogue 2, a few lines after our excerpt ends, Corydon, the lovesick shepherd modelled on Theocritus's lovesick Cyclops, insists *nec sum adeo informis*, \"I am not so ugly\". Here, at last, is the Cyclops himself, and he is *informis* indeed. Between the Eclogues and the Aeneid, Virgil has turned a comic figure of pastoral into a figure of tragedy.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nVirgil's legacy is so vast that it is hard to know where it ends. Within a few years of his death his poems were being taught in Roman schools, and they remained the basic school text of Latin education for the whole of antiquity and beyond. People scratched lines of the *Aeneid* on the walls of Pompeii. Later Romans even used his poems as an oracle, opening the book at random and reading the first line they found as a prophecy of their future, a practice called the *sortes Vergilianae*.\n\nHe became, above all, the model of stylistic perfection: the poet against whom every later Latin poet measured himself. That was especially true for epic. Every Roman epic of the Early Imperial Era was written in his shadow and had to find a way to deal with him. Lucan's *Bellum Civile* (or *Pharsalia*) is in many ways an anti-*Aeneid*, a civil-war epic without gods, without a hero and without hope. Statius ends his *Thebaid* by telling his own poem not to rival \"the divine *Aeneid*\" but to follow it from a distance and always worship its footprints. Silius Italicus, the author of the *Punica*, owned Virgil's tomb at Naples, visited it as if it were a temple, and celebrated Virgil's birthday more devoutly than his own. The hexameter they all wrote was Virgil's hexameter.\n\nHis survival into the Middle Ages owes a great deal to an extraordinary stroke of luck. Eclogue 4, written around 40 BC, announces the birth of a child under whom a new Golden Age will begin. Virgil almost certainly meant the child of a Roman aristocrat, probably connected with the political settlement of that year. But to Christian readers, beginning with Lactantius and the emperor Constantine, the poem looked like a prophecy of the birth of Christ, written by a pagan who had somehow been allowed to glimpse the truth. Virgil was promoted from poet to prophet, and his works were copied, read and protected by the Church with a reverence given to almost no other pagan author. Medieval legend went further and turned him into a magician: in Naples it was said that the city stood safe as long as an egg he had hidden in the foundations of the Castel dell'Ovo remained unbroken.\n\nAll this made him so famous that his fame eventually outgrew his poetry, and shifted from his works to his figure. The decisive moment was Dante. In the *Divine Comedy*, Dante chooses Virgil as his guide through Hell and Purgatory, greets him as \"my master and my author\", and even has the poet Statius claim that Virgil's fourth Eclogue made him a Christian: \"through you I became a poet, through you a Christian\". From then on, for a great many people, Virgil is first of all \"Dante's guide\", or simply \"the man who wrote the *Aeneid*\", rather than the author of poems they have actually read. The process has gone all the way to video games: the *Devil May Cry* series has twin brothers called Dante and Vergil. It is a strange fate for the shy man from Mantua who hid in doorways to avoid being recognised: to become so famous that people know his name without knowing why.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: A (Very Difficult)**\n\nVirgil is hard in almost every criterion at once: poetic word order and transferred epithets, poetic plurals, Greek forms, archaisms, elision, technical vocabulary in the *Georgics*, and a density of literary and mythological allusion that rarely lets up. What keeps him from S is his honesty: compared with Horace there is no wilful game-playing with the reader, the narrative of the *Aeneid* carries you forward, and the Eclogues offer a genuinely approachable way in. Taken work by work: the **Eclogues** from B to low A, and the right place to start; the **Aeneid** in the middle to upper range of A; the **Georgics** the hardest of all, pushing into S. Overall, a firm A. Do not start with the *Aeneid*.\n\n---\n\n# QUINTUS HORATIUS FLACCUS -- HORACE (65 -- 8 BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nIf Virgil is the shy genius of the Augustan Age, Horace is its best company. He is the friend who never takes himself too seriously, who will tell you your vices to your face, and then pour you a cup of Falernian to help you drink over them. He laughs at the greedy, the ambitious, the bores and the social climbers, and he laughs just as hard at himself: short, fat, prematurely grey, quick to lose his temper and quick to calm down again, and, by his own description, \"a pig from Epicurus's herd\". And then, having made you laugh, he writes some of the most beautiful lyric poetry in Latin. He is the great lyric poet of the Augustan Age, and probably the most quotable poet Rome ever produced. Where Virgil is always aiming at the stars and beyond, Horace is content to remind us of our humanity: friends, wine, the turning seasons, the limits of a single life, and how to live well inside them. Both are reasons to enjoy them fully.\n\n**The freedman's son.** Quintus Horatius Flaccus was born on 8 December 65 BC at Venusia, a military colony on the border between Apulia and Lucania in southern Italy. His father was a freedman, a former slave, who worked as a *coactor*, a collector of payments at auctions: a modest job, but a profitable one. He made one decision that changed his son's life: instead of sending the boy to the local school, where the sons of centurions went with their satchels and their writing tablets, he took him to Rome to be educated alongside the children of senators and knights, and went with him to every lesson himself, as a guardian. Horace never forgot it. In one of his satires he writes that if he has any good qualities at all, he owes them to his father, and that he would not swap him for any noble parent in Rome. His schoolmaster in Rome, Orbilius, is remembered in literature for one thing only: Horace calls him *plagosus*, \"fond of the whip\", the earliest famous school beating in history.\n\n**The general who ran away.** Around 45 BC he went to Athens to finish his education in philosophy, which is where he was when Caesar was murdered. Brutus came to Athens recruiting young Romans for the cause of the Republic, and Horace, the son of a former slave, found himself made a *tribunus militum*, one of the senior officers of a legion: a sign either of his talent or of how desperate Brutus was. At Philippi in 42 BC the republican army was destroyed, and Horace, by his own cheerful admission, ran. In an ode written years later he reminds an old comrade of the day \"when I felt that swift flight with you, and left my little shield behind, not very gloriously\" (*relicta non bene parmula*), and adds that the god Mercury picked him up in a cloud and carried him out of the battle. The joke is a literary one too: the Greek lyric poets Archilochus, Alcaeus and Anacreon had all boasted of throwing away their shields and running for their lives, so Horace makes his own cowardice a qualification for joining the club.\n\n**Poverty made me a poet.** He came back to Italy under an amnesty to find his father dead and the family land confiscated. He bought himself a job as a clerk in the treasury, and, as he says, \"bold poverty drove me to write verses\". The verses were good enough to catch the attention of Virgil and Varius, who in about 38 BC introduced him to Maecenas.\n\n**Maecenas.** The first meeting, as Horace describes it, was a disaster of shyness. He stammered out a few words, \"for speechless shame stopped me from saying more\"; Maecenas answered briefly, as was his habit; Horace went away. Then, nine months later, Maecenas called him back and asked him to join his circle of friends. It was the beginning of one of the great friendships of antiquity. Maecenas gave him financial security and, around 33 BC, a farm in the Sabine hills northeast of Rome, the place Horace loved most in the world, which appears again and again in his poems as the symbol of the quiet, independent life. In return Horace gave him the dedications of his books, his loyalty, and a promise: in one ode he swears that he will not outlive his friend, and that when Maecenas goes, they will go together. He very nearly kept it. Maecenas died in 8 BC, leaving a will that asked Augustus to \"remember Horace as you would remember me\". Horace died less than two months later, and was buried next to him on the Esquiline.\n\n**The emperor's admirer.** Augustus liked him enormously, and wanted him close. He offered Horace the post of his private secretary, and Horace politely declined, pleading poor health; Augustus, to his credit, took no offence, and went on writing him teasing letters. In one he calls Horace \"a most charming little man\". In another, after receiving a slim book of his poems, he jokes that Horace seems to be afraid his books will be bigger than he is, since what Horace lacks in height he makes up for in girth. And in a third he complains, half seriously, that Horace never addresses any of his poems to him: \"Are you afraid that being seen as my friend will damage your reputation with posterity?\" Horace took the hint, and wrote him a long verse letter, the first in his second book of *Epistles*. In 17 BC Augustus chose him to write the official hymn for the Secular Games, the *Carmen Saeculare*: the freedman's son who had fought against Caesar's heir at Philippi had become the poet laureate of his regime.\n\n**The last years.** He spent his last years between Rome and his Sabine farm, writing his last book of odes and his literary letters, and he died on 27 November 8 BC, a few days before his fifty-seventh birthday, leaving Augustus as his heir. He had predicted his own fame at the end of his third book of odes, in one of the proudest boasts in literature: *exegi monumentum aere perennius*, \"I have built a monument more lasting than bronze\", and *non omnis moriar*, \"I shall not wholly die\". He was right.\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n- **Sermones** (*Satires*, Book I c. 35 BC, Book II c. 30 BC) -- two books of hexameter \"conversations\", eighteen poems in all, in the tradition of Lucilius but gentler and more self-ironic. Horace's motto is *ridentem dicere verum*, \"to tell the truth while laughing\". The targets are the vices of everyday Roman life: greed, ambition, adultery, gluttony, superstition and the restless discontent that makes everyone want someone else's life. Among the most famous: the journey to Brundisium (I.5), the bore who will not let Horace go on the Via Sacra (I.9), and the town mouse and the country mouse (II.6). [Complete]\n- **Epodi** or **Iambi** (*Epodes*, c. 30 BC) -- seventeen poems of iambic verse modelled on the Greek poet Archilochus, the founder of poetic invective. They include curses and insults (against the poet Mevius, against the witch Canidia), but also anguished poems on the civil wars, a celebration of the victory at Actium, and the famous praise of country life, *Beatus ille*, which turns out at the very end to have been spoken by a moneylender who promptly goes back to lending money. [Complete]\n- **Carmina** (*Odes*, Books I-III 23 BC, Book IV c. 13 BC) -- one hundred and three lyric poems in the metres of the Greek poets Alcaeus and Sappho, Horace's masterpiece and his claim to be the first to bring Greek lyric into Latin. The themes are friendship, love, wine, the seasons, the shortness of life and the need to enjoy the present wisely, moderation (the *aurea mediocritas*), Rome and its moral renewal (the six \"Roman Odes\" that open Book III), Augustus and Actium (*Nunc est bibendum*, on the death of Cleopatra), and the immortality of poetry. [Complete]\n- **Carmen Saeculare** (*Secular Hymn*, 17 BC) -- the official hymn for the Secular Games, the festival that marked the beginning of a new age for Rome, sung by a choir of twenty-seven boys and twenty-seven girls on the Palatine and the Capitol. A prayer to Apollo and Diana for the prosperity of Rome, its families and its harvests. The official record of the games, carved on stone and partly found in 1890, still names him: *carmen composuit Q. Horatius Flaccus*. [Complete]\n- **Epistulae** (*Epistles*, Book I c. 20 BC, Book II c. 19-12 BC) -- two books of verse letters to friends, more philosophical and more serene than the *Satires*. Their subject is how to live: independence, contentment, friendship, self-knowledge. They contain some of his most famous phrases: *sapere aude*, \"dare to be wise\", *nil admirari*, \"to be astonished by nothing\", and *Graecia capta ferum victorem cepit*, \"captive Greece took her savage conqueror captive\". [Complete]\n- **Ars Poetica** (*The Art of Poetry*, also called the *Epistula ad Pisones*) -- a long verse letter of 476 lines on how to write poetry, especially drama: unity, consistency of character, appropriateness of style, the need to start *in medias res*, to revise for years (*nonumque prematur in annum*, \"let it be kept back until the ninth year\"), and to aim at both instruction and pleasure. It is the most influential work of literary theory ever written in Latin. [Complete]\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nVirgil is difficult, but he is still readable. Horace is a different matter. Virgil makes you work; Horace plays games with you.\n\nThe first game is **hyperbaton**, and in Horace it reaches lengths found nowhere else. Words that belong together grammatically, an adjective and its noun, a preposition and its object, are pulled apart and placed at opposite ends of a line, or of a whole stanza, with other words packed in between. In the *Odes* this is not decoration but structure: every word sits in exactly the place where its sound, its metre and its meaning do the most work. Nietzsche described an ode of Horace as a mosaic of words in which every single word, by its sound, its position and its meaning, radiates its force to right and left and over the whole. For the translator, that mosaic has to be taken apart tile by tile before it can be put back together in Italian or English. Horace himself gave the technique a name in the *Ars Poetica*: the *callida iunctura*, the \"clever joining\", by which a familiar word, placed in an unexpected combination, becomes new. In practice, for the student, a great deal of *callida iunctura* is simply more hyperbaton.\n\nThe second is **vocabulary**. Horace drops Greek words and Greek constructions into Latin without warning, uses rare words and new formations (in the *Ars Poetica* he explicitly defends the poet's right to coin new words, as long as it is done sparingly), and alludes constantly to Greek lyric poems the reader is expected to recognise. **Lexicon: HIGH; Allusion density: HIGH.**\n\nThe third is the **sermo cotidianus** of the *Satires* and *Epistles*. Here the problem is the opposite of the *Odes*: not compression into a perfect mosaic, but conversation. Horace writes the way educated Romans talked, rapid-fire and disorienting: unannounced changes of speaker, imaginary objections, half-sentences, ellipses, colloquial idioms, jokes that depend on a tone of voice we cannot hear. The *Satires* are the recommended starting point for Horace, and rightly, because the word order is closer to prose and the situations are often funny. But they are still tricky. **Syntactic complexity: HIGH; Stylistic figures: HIGH.**\n\nAnd if you are trying to scan him, the *Odes* are a nightmare of their own. Virgil wrote in one metre. Horace uses around a dozen: the Sapphic stanza, the Alcaic stanza, several kinds of Asclepiad system, Archilochian and Pythiambic couplets, and more, each with its own rules, its own fixed and variable syllables and its own pauses, changing from one poem to the next.\n\nNone of this is a reason to avoid him. Horace is one of the most interesting authors in Latin to translate precisely because every line is a puzzle with a solution, and the solution is always worth finding.\n\n## Latin Excerpts\n\n### Excerpt 1: Carmina II.10 -- The Golden Mean\n\n**Source:** Horace, *Carmina* (*Odes*), Book II, poem 10, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/horace/carm2.shtml)\n\n**Context:** An ode addressed to a friend called Licinius, probably Licinius Murena, the brother-in-law of Maecenas, and a man with a famously impulsive temperament. The theme is the *aurea mediocritas*, the \"golden mean\": the Aristotelian idea that virtue lies between two extremes, applied here to fortune and ambition. The irony is painful. If this Licinius is Murena, he did not take the advice: around 23-22 BC, about the time this book of odes was published, he was implicated in a conspiracy against Augustus and executed. The metre is the Sapphic stanza: three long lines and a short fourth line, the *adonius*.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Rectius vives, Licini, neque altum\n> semper urgendo neque, dum procellas\n> cautus horrescis, nimium premendo\n> litus iniquum.\n>\n> Auream quisquis mediocritatem\n> diligit, tutus caret obsoleti\n> sordibus tecti, caret invidenda\n> sobrius aula.\n>\n> Saepius ventis agitatur ingens\n> pinus et celsae graviore casu\n> decidunt turres feriuntque summos\n> fulgura montis.\n>\n> Sperat infestis, metuit secundis\n> alteram sortem bene praeparatum\n> pectus. Informis hiemes reducit\n> Iuppiter, idem\n>\n> summovet. Non, si male nunc, et olim\n> sic erit: quondam cithara tacentem\n> suscitat Musam neque semper arcum\n> tendit Apollo.\n>\n> Rebus angustis animosus atque\n> fortis appare; sapienter idem\n> contrahes vento nimium secundo\n> turgida vela.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nYou will live more wisely, Licinius, by neither always\npressing out into the open sea nor, while in your caution\nyou shudder at the storms, hugging too closely\nthe treacherous shore.\n\nWhoever loves the golden mean\nis safe: he is free of the squalor\nof a crumbling house, and, temperate, he is free\nof a palace that breeds envy.\n\nThe giant pine is shaken more often\nby the winds, and lofty towers fall\nwith a heavier crash, and the lightning strikes\nthe highest peaks of the mountains.\n\nA heart well prepared hopes in bad times\nand fears in good times\nfor a change of fortune. Jupiter brings back\nthe ugly winters; he also\n\ntakes them away. If things are bad now, they will not\nalways be so: sometimes Apollo wakes\nthe silent Muse with his lyre, and does not\nalways draw his bow.\n\nIn hard times show yourself spirited\nand brave; and in the same way, wisely,\nwhen the wind is too favourable you will take in\nyour swelling sails.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nFirst, a warning about the famous phrase. *Aurea mediocritas* does not mean \"golden mediocrity\" in the modern sense of being average and unambitious. *Mediocritas* is the middle point between two extremes, the Aristotelian mean, and it is \"golden\" because it is precious. The poem's argument is not \"aim low\" but \"do not go to extremes, in either direction\": not too far out to sea, not too close to the rocks; not a slum, not a palace; not despair in bad times, not overconfidence in good ones.\n\nThe structure is a ring. The ode opens and closes with the same image, sailing: in the first stanza Licinius must neither push out too far nor hug the coast, in the last he must be brave when the wind is against him and take in sail when it is too favourable. In between, the third stanza is a tricolon of disasters that strike whatever stands highest (the tallest pine, the highest towers, the mountain peaks), and the fourth and fifth turn to the gods: Jupiter brings winters and takes them away, and Apollo does not always shoot his arrows. Fortune always changes; a wise man expects it.\n\nThis is a good place to watch Horace's word order at work. In the second stanza, *obsoleti* waits until the start of the next line for its noun, *tecti*; *invidenda* is separated from *aula* by *sobrius*. The sense also runs across the ends of stanzas: the fourth stanza ends on *idem*, and the verb, *summovet*, only arrives at the start of the fifth, so that Jupiter literally takes the winter away across the gap. The grammar is mostly straightforward once the words have been reunited: a future used as advice (*Rectius vives*, *contrahes*), gerunds in the ablative (*urgendo*, *premendo*, \"by pressing\"), datives with *sperat* and *metuit* (*infestis*, *secundis*, \"in adverse and favourable circumstances\"), and an imperative, *appare*. Note *montis* and *Informis*, old accusative plurals in *-is*. And if you have read the Virgil entry, notice *Informis hiemes*, \"ugly winters\": the same adjective Virgil uses for Polyphemus.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 2: Epodi 10 -- A Curse on Mevius\n\n**Source:** Horace, *Epodi*, poem 10 (complete), from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/horace/epodes.shtml)\n\n**Context:** A *propempticon* is a send-off poem, wishing a friend a safe voyage; Horace himself wrote a famous one for Virgil's ship (*Odes* I.3). Epode 10 is the exact opposite: an anti-*propempticon*, a poem wishing a ship every possible disaster, because Mevius is on board. Mevius was a bad poet, and Virgil had already mocked him in the *Eclogues* along with his partner in crime Bavius: \"whoever does not hate Bavius, let him love your poems, Mevius\". Nobody knows exactly what Mevius did to deserve this; it may simply be that he wrote badly. The model is Archilochus, the Greek inventor of poetic invective, one of whose curses on a former friend, wishing him shipwrecked naked on the coast of Thrace, survives on a papyrus. The metre is the epodic couplet: an iambic trimeter followed by a shorter iambic dimeter.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Mala soluta navis exit alite\n> ferens olentem Mevium.\n> ut horridis utrumque verberes latus,\n> Auster, memento fluctibus;\n> niger rudentis Eurus inverso mari\n> fractosque remos differat;\n> insurgat Aquilo, quantus altis montibus\n> frangit trementis ilices;\n> nec sidus atra nocte amicum adpareat,\n> qua tristis Orion cadit;\n> quietiore nec feratur aequore\n> quam Graia victorum manus,\n> cum Pallas usto vertit iram ab Ilio\n> in inpiam Aiacis ratem.\n> o quantus instat navitis sudor tuis\n> tibique pallor luteus\n> et illa non virilis heiulatio\n> preces et aversum ad Iovem,\n> Ionius udo cum remugiens sinus\n> Noto carinam ruperit.\n> opima quodsi praeda curvo litore\n> porrecta mergos iuverit,\n> libidinosus immolabitur caper\n> et agna Tempestatibus.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nUnder an evil omen the ship slips its moorings and sets out,\ncarrying stinking Mevius.\nRemember, South Wind, to lash both its sides\nwith savage waves;\nlet the black East Wind, turning the sea upside down,\nscatter its rigging and its broken oars;\nlet the North Wind rise, as strong as when on the high mountains\nit shatters the trembling holm-oaks;\nand let no friendly star appear in the black night\nwhere grim Orion sets;\nand may he be carried on no calmer sea\nthan the victorious army of the Greeks\nwhen Pallas turned her anger from burnt Troy\nonto the impious ship of Ajax.\nOh, what sweat is waiting for your sailors,\nand for you, a sickly yellow pallor,\nand that unmanly wailing,\nand prayers to a Jupiter who has turned away,\nwhen the Ionian gulf, bellowing under the rainy\nSouth Wind, has smashed your keel!\nAnd if you end up as a rich prize stretched out on the curving shore,\nand make a feast for the seabirds,\na lustful billy goat will be sacrificed,\nand a lamb, to the Storms.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe joke is in the form. A send-off poem should pray for calm seas, favourable winds and a friendly star; Horace runs through the same checklist in reverse. Every wind of the compass is called up in turn (Auster from the south, Eurus from the east, Aquilo from the north, then Notus, another name for the south wind), each with an instruction, in a series of jussive subjunctives (*differat*, *insurgat*, *adpareat*, *feratur*) and a future imperative with an *ut* clause (*ut... verberes... memento*, \"remember to lash\"). The last couplet completes the parody: a traveller who arrived safely would make a thank-offering to the gods of the sea; Horace promises one to the Storms if Mevius drowns.\n\nIn the middle, a myth arrives, as always in Horace, without explanation. After the fall of Troy, the lesser Ajax dragged Cassandra away from the altar of Pallas Athena, and the goddess took her revenge by wrecking his ship on the way home. The reader is supposed to know the story, and to enjoy the hyperbole: Mevius deserves the same storm that the gods sent against the worst sacrilege of the Trojan War.\n\nThe vocabulary is part of the fun. *Olentem*, \"smelly\", is the only thing we are told about Mevius. *Pallor luteus* is a yellowish, sickly pallor; *heiulatio* is a rare word for loud, undignified wailing, from the verb *heiulare*, and Horace adds *non virilis*, \"unmanly\", to make sure the insult lands. *Rudentes* are a ship's ropes and rigging; *mergi* are diving seabirds, cormorants or gulls. And look at the grammar of the climax: *cum... ruperit*, a future perfect, \"when the gulf has broken your keel\", followed by *quodsi... iuverit*, \"but if you have pleased the birds\", both imagining Mevius's death as already accomplished. *Rudentis*, *trementis*: old accusative plurals in *-is* again. *Libidinosus caper* may be a final dig: the goat was proverbially lustful, and a little too appropriate as an offering for Mevius.\n\nA note on the text: The Latin Library prints *ilics*, which is not a word, for *ilices* (\"holm-oaks\") in line 8, and leaves out the punctuation at the end of line 20, after *ruperit*. Both are corrected here. *Inpiam* is the source's spelling of *impiam*, and has been kept.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 3: Sermones I.5.1-26 -- The Journey to Brundisium\n\n**Source:** Horace, *Sermones* (*Satires*), Book I, poem 5, lines 1-26, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/horace/serm1.shtml)\n\n**Context:** In 37 BC (or possibly 38) Maecenas travelled from Rome to Brundisium on a delicate diplomatic mission, to patch up the relationship between Octavian and Antony, and he took Horace along, together with Virgil, Varius and other friends. Horace turned the journey into a satire, a travel diary in verse, modelled on the *Iter Siculum*, the \"Journey to Sicily\", of Lucilius (whose fragments are in the Archaic Era chapter). The joke of the poem is that Horace tells us almost nothing about the diplomacy, and everything about bad water, bad inns, mosquitoes, his sore eyes and the comedians they met along the way. These are the first twenty-six lines: from Rome to Aricia, then by canal-boat through the Pontine Marshes, to Anxur, modern Terracina.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Egressum magna me accepit Aricia Roma\n> hospitio modico; rhetor comes Heliodorus,\n> Graecorum longe doctissimus; inde Forum Appi\n> differtum nautis cauponibus atque malignis.\n> hoc iter ignavi divisimus, altius ac nos\n> praecinctis unum: minus est gravis Appia tardis.\n> hic ego propter aquam, quod erat deterrima, ventri\n> indico bellum, cenantis haud animo aequo\n> exspectans comites. iam nox inducere terris\n> umbras et caelo diffundere signa parabat:\n> tum pueri nautis, pueris convicia nautae\n> ingerere: 'huc adpelle'; 'trecentos inseris'; 'ohe,\n> iam satis est.' dum aes exigitur, dum mula ligatur,\n> tota abit hora. mali culices ranaeque palustres\n> avertunt somnos; absentem cantat amicam\n> multa prolutus vappa nauta atque viator\n> certatim; tandem fessus dormire viator\n> incipit ac missae pastum retinacula mulae\n> nauta piger saxo religat stertitque supinus.\n> iamque dies aderat, nil cum procedere lintrem\n> sentimus, donec cerebrosus prosilit unus\n> ac mulae nautaeque caput lumbosque saligno\n> fuste dolat: quarta vix demum exponimur hora.\n> ora manusque tua lavimus, Feronia, lympha.\n> milia tum pransi tria repimus atque subimus\n> inpositum saxis late candentibus Anxur.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nWhen I had left great Rome, Aricia welcomed me\nwith modest hospitality; my companion was the rhetorician Heliodorus,\nby far the most learned of the Greeks. From there to Forum Appii,\ncrammed with boatmen and crooked innkeepers.\nThis stretch we lazily split into two days, though travellers more briskly\ngirt than us do it in one: the Appian Way is less of a strain if you take it slowly.\nHere, because of the water, which was foul,\nI declare war on my stomach, and wait, in no good temper,\nwhile my companions have their dinner. Now night was getting ready\nto draw its shadows over the earth and scatter the stars across the sky.\nThen the slaves start hurling abuse at the boatmen, the boatmen at the slaves:\n\"Pull in here!\" \"You're cramming in three hundred! Hey,\nthat's enough!\" While the fares are collected and the mule is harnessed,\na whole hour goes by. Damned mosquitoes and marsh frogs\nkeep sleep away; a boatman, soaked in cheap wine,\nsings of the girlfriend he left behind, and a passenger\ncompetes with him: at last the passenger, worn out, starts to fall asleep,\nand the lazy boatman lets the mule out to graze, ties its rope\nto a stone, and lies on his back snoring.\nDay was already breaking when we realise that the boat\nis not moving at all, until one hothead jumps out\nand with a willow cudgel gives both mule and boatman\na beating on the head and back. At last, only at the fourth hour, we are put ashore.\nWe wash our faces and hands in your spring, Feronia.\nThen, after lunch, we crawl three miles and climb up\nto Anxur, perched on its far-gleaming white rocks.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThis is the *sermo cotidianus* in action, and it shows why the *Satires* are both the best way into Horace and still tricky. The narrative is simple, a day-by-day travel diary, and the word order is close to prose. But the texture changes every few lines. Lines 9-10 are a deliberate parody of epic: *iam nox inducere terris / umbras et caelo diffundere signa parabat*, \"now night was preparing to draw its shadows over the earth and scatter the stars across the sky\", a perfect Virgilian line about nightfall, followed immediately by the slaves and the boatmen screaming at each other. The shouting itself is pure spoken Latin, given without any indication of who is speaking: *huc adpelle* (\"pull in here!\"), *trecentos inseris* (\"you're cramming in three hundred\"), *ohe, iam satis est* (\"hey, that's enough!\"). The translator has to work out the speakers from the sense.\n\nWatch the grammar. *Ingerere* in line 12 is a historic infinitive, used instead of a finite verb to describe a lively scene (\"they started hurling\"); the narrative jumps constantly between perfects (*accepit*, *divisimus*, *lavimus*) and historic presents (*indico*, *avertunt*, *sentimus*, *exponimur*, *repimus*). *Ventri indico bellum*, \"I declare war on my stomach\", is mock-military language for refusing to eat, because the water is too dangerous to drink. *Altius praecinctis*, \"those girt higher than us\", refers to travellers who hitch their tunics up to walk faster. *Vappa* is wine that has gone flat. *Cerebrosus* is a \"hothead\", from *cerebrum*. And *dolat*, from *dolare*, normally means to hew wood with an axe: the passenger \"carpenters\" the mule and the boatman with his cudgel.\n\nThen there is the geography, which is real. Aricia is modern Ariccia in the Alban Hills, the first stop south of Rome on the Appian Way. Forum Appii was the start of a canal through the Pontine Marshes, travelled overnight in a barge towed by a mule along the bank, which is how the mule and the boatman both come to be asleep. The spring of the goddess Feronia and the town of Anxur, with the temple of Jupiter Anxur on its gleaming white cliffs, are at Terracina, where the canal ended. The diplomatic mission, the reason for the whole trip, is mentioned in a single line a little later. Everything else is mosquitoes.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nHorace survived antiquity thanks to two different kinds of readers. For the schools, he was immediately a classic: within a generation of his death he was being taught to Roman schoolboys, and Quintilian called him \"almost the only Roman lyric poet worth reading\". For Christian readers in late antiquity and the Middle Ages, it was above all the *Satires* and the *Epistles* that mattered. A poet who exposed greed, ambition, luxury and hypocrisy, who preached contentment and moderation, and who laughed at the vanity of human desires could be read as a moralist, and medieval readers called him *Horatius ethicus*, \"Horace the moralist\". He was copied in monasteries all over Europe, and around 850 manuscripts survive, more than for almost any other classical poet.\n\nThat reputation is still visible in Dante. In the fourth canto of the *Inferno*, among the great poets of antiquity in Limbo, Horace appears second only to Homer, and Dante calls him *Orazio satiro*, \"Horace the satirist\": for Dante, the author of the *Satires* came before the author of the *Odes*. Petrarch, who loved him, wrote him a letter in verse. In the Renaissance the *Odes* came back into their own and became the model of lyric poetry across Europe: Ronsard in France, Ben Jonson in England, and in Italy the poets who tried to reproduce his metres in Italian, culminating centuries later in Carducci's *Odi barbare*. The *Ars Poetica*, meanwhile, became the rulebook of classical literary theory, the direct ancestor of Boileau's *Art poétique* and Pope's *Essay on Criticism*. For centuries Horace was not just a poet but the master of style, the authority educated Europeans quoted on how to write, how to judge and how to live.\n\nAnd then the modern world forgot almost all of it and kept two words. *Carpe diem* comes from a short ode of eight lines, *Odes* I.11, addressed to a woman called Leuconoe who is trying to find out from astrologers how long she and the poet have to live. Horace tells her to stop asking, because nobody can know, and to be wise, strain the wine, cut back long hopes, and *carpe diem, quam minimum credula postero*: \"pluck the day, trusting as little as possible in tomorrow\". *Carpere* is a gardener's verb: you pluck a fruit when it is ripe, carefully, one at a time. The message is Epicurean, and it is about moderation: do not waste the present worrying about a future you cannot control. Modern culture, helped along by a famous film, has turned it into the opposite: \"YOLO\", live as if there were no tomorrow, party as hard as you can because you might die. It is hard to think of a worse misreading. The man who wrote *carpe diem* also wrote the ode on the golden mean, the warning to take in sail when the wind is too favourable, and two books of satires against people who cannot control their appetites.\n\nRead him properly, and Horace is far more than a slogan: a satirist who laughs at everyone including himself, a moralist who never preaches, a friend who is loyal to the end, and one of the greatest lyric poets who ever lived.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: S (GOD PLEASE HELP)**\n\nHorace scores at or near the top in almost every criterion. In the *Odes*, the hyperbaton is more extreme than in any other Latin poet, the vocabulary mixes Greek words, rare words and new formations, the allusions to Greek lyric are constant and unexplained, and the metres change from poem to poem. In the *Satires* and *Epistles*, the challenge shifts to rapid, colloquial conversation, with changes of speaker that are never signalled and jokes that depend on tone. He is leagues harder than Virgil, who is difficult but honest; Horace is difficult on purpose. The *Satires* are the right place to start, but even they are tricky; the *Odes* are where the real summit is. Mid to high S, and well deserved. And worth every minute.\n\n---\n\n# TITUS LIVIUS -- LIVY (64 or 59 BC -- 17 AD)\n\n## Biography\n\nLivy wrote the history of Rome from the arrival of Aeneas in Italy down to his own day, in one hundred and forty-two books. Let that number settle for a moment. Caesar's *Gallic War* is seven books. Sallust wrote two short monographs. Livy wrote one hundred and forty-two books, each of them long, over roughly forty years, which works out at three or four books a year, every year, for the whole of his adult life. Even the Romans thought this was remarkable, and Livy himself seems to have found it a little alarming.\n\n**The man from Padua.** Titus Livius was born at Patavium, modern Padua, in northern Italy: in 59 BC according to Jerome, though many scholars prefer 64. Patavium was one of the richest cities in Italy, and it had a reputation for old-fashioned morality and strict family life, which shows in every page of his history. It also gave him an accent. The historian and politician Asinius Pollio, who never missed a chance to be unpleasant, sneered that Livy's Latin had a touch of *Patavinitas*, \"Paduan-ness\": a provincial flavour that a true Roman ear could detect. Nobody since has been able to work out exactly what Pollio meant, which may be the best proof that he was simply being a snob.\n\n**A historian with no career.** What makes Livy unusual among Roman historians is what he did not do. Sallust had been a senator and a governor; Caesar had conquered Gaul; Tacitus would be a consul. Livy never held office, never commanded an army, never sat in the Senate, and as far as we know never even tried. He was a private man of letters who moved to Rome, probably in the 30s BC, and devoted his life to writing. Before the history he wrote philosophical dialogues, now lost, and a letter to his son on how to write well, in which he advised him to read Demosthenes and Cicero and to imitate whoever was most like them. Around 27 BC, as Augustus was \"restoring the Republic\", he began the *Ab Urbe Condita*. He would still be writing it forty years later.\n\n**\"My little Pompeian\".** Livy was on good terms with Augustus, but on his own terms. In his account of the civil wars he praised Pompey so warmly that Augustus, teasing him, used to call him a \"Pompeian\" (*Pompeianum eum Augustus appellaret*): a partisan of the losing side, the side his adoptive father Caesar had destroyed. And yet, as Tacitus records, \"this did not damage their friendship\". Livy also wrote of Brutus and Cassius, Caesar's assassins, not as bandits and parricides but as distinguished men, and Augustus let it pass. The story is preserved for a reason. Tacitus puts it in the mouth of the historian Cremutius Cordus, on trial under Tiberius in AD 25 for having praised Brutus and Cassius in his own history; Cordus cites Livy as proof that under Augustus a historian could say such things. It did not save him. Cordus starved himself to death, and the Senate ordered his books to be burned. The contrast says everything about how much freedom Livy had, and how quickly it disappeared after him.\n\n**The tutor of an emperor.** Livy's influence reached into the imperial family. Suetonius says that it was Livy who encouraged the young Claudius, the future emperor, awkward, stammering and generally regarded by his family as a joke, to write history. Claudius took the advice, and wrote dozens of books, including a history of the Etruscans and one of Carthage, all of them lost.\n\n**The most famous man in Rome.** By the end of his life Livy was a celebrity. Pliny the Younger tells the story of a man from Gades, modern Cadiz in Spain, at the far western edge of the known world, who was so overwhelmed by Livy's fame that he travelled all the way to Rome just to see him, looked at him, and went home again. Livy himself, according to Pliny the Elder, wrote in the preface to one of his later books that he had already won enough glory and could have stopped, if his restless mind had not fed on the work. He could not stop. He carried the story down to the death of Augustus's stepson Drusus in 9 BC, and then, it seems, simply ran out of life rather than material.\n\n**Death, and a skeleton in Padua.** He returned to Padua and died there in AD 17, the same year as Ovid, if Jerome is right. Padua never forgot him. In 1413, during building work in the city, a lead coffin was dug up near an old inscription mentioning a Titus Livius, and the Paduans, delirious with civic pride, declared that they had found the bones of the great historian. The remains were carried in triumph and eventually placed in the Palazzo della Ragione, where a monument to Livy still stands. The inscription almost certainly belonged to a different, entirely obscure Livius, a freedman. Padua did not let that spoil the celebration.\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n- **Ab Urbe Condita Libri** (*Books from the Foundation of the City*, c. 27 BC -- AD 17) -- the history of Rome in one hundred and forty-two books, from the arrival of Aeneas and the founding of the city down to 9 BC: more than seven hundred years of history. Livy organised it in groups of five and ten books (pentads and decades), each around a great period: the kings and the early Republic, the conquest of Italy, the wars with Carthage, the conquest of the Greek East, the civil wars. It is a history built on moral examples: Livy says in his preface that the great value of history is that it shows you, set out in a clear record, models to imitate and disgraces to avoid. Only thirty-five books survive: Books I-X (from the beginnings to 293 BC) and Books XXI-XLV (from Hannibal's war in 218 BC to 167 BC), the last five with gaps. For almost all the rest we have the *Periochae*, brief summaries written in late antiquity, plus a few fragments and quotations. [Fragmentary -- 35 of 142 books]\n- **Philosophical dialogues** and **a letter to his son** on style -- mentioned by Seneca and Quintilian. [Lost]\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nAfter the hell of Virgil and Horace, Livy is honestly a breath of fresh air. He writes prose, his vocabulary is the standard vocabulary of classical Latin, and his Latin is clear, correct and full-bodied. Quintilian, contrasting him with the abrupt, compressed Sallust, praised his *lactea ubertas*, his \"milky richness\": the prose of an author who never cuts a corner and never leaves you short of anything.\n\nThat richness is also the problem. Livy builds his narrative out of long periods, sentences in which the main clause is surrounded by participles, ablatives absolute, *cum* clauses and relative clauses, each adding a detail, a cause, a circumstance or a consequence, all held together until the main verb arrives at the end. He narrates every step of every event, every embassy, every debate in the Senate, every manoeuvre before a battle, and he does it painstakingly slowly. It is no wonder he needed one hundred and forty-two books to cover seven centuries. The hardest thing about Livy may simply be staying awake until you get to the interesting part. **Syntactic complexity: MEDIUM to HIGH.**\n\nThere are tricky points as well. The speeches, of which there are hundreds, are often given in indirect discourse, so that whole paragraphs run in the accusative and infinitive and the subjunctive, and the reader has to keep track of who is speaking and when. Livy is fond of the **historic infinitive**, an infinitive used instead of a finite verb to describe rapid, vivid or repeated action (\"they shouted, they ran, they begged\"), which regularly confuses students who are looking for a main verb that is not there. The first decade, on the legends of early Rome, has a poetic colouring, with words and turns of phrase that recall Virgil and Ennius. And Livy loves variety in construction (*variatio*), changing the form of a phrase just when you expect it to repeat. **Lexicon: MEDIUM; Stylistic figures: MEDIUM.** The allusions are mostly to Roman history and institutions rather than to literature, and they are usually explained by the narrative itself. **Allusion density: LOW to MEDIUM.**\n\n## Latin Excerpts\n\n### Excerpt 1: Ab Urbe Condita XXV.31.4-11 -- The Fall of Syracuse and the Death of Archimedes\n\n**Source:** Livy, *Ab Urbe Condita*, Book XXV, chapter 31, sections 4-11, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/livy/liv.25.shtml). The Latin Library's text of Books XXV and XXXIX writes consonantal *v* as *u* (*uoluerit*, *uiis*) and does not capitalise sentences; both are kept here as printed.\n\n**Context:** 212 BC, the Second Punic War. Syracuse, Rome's old ally under King Hiero, had gone over to Carthage after Hiero's death, and the Roman general Marcus Claudius Marcellus had been besieging it for more than two years, held off largely by the war machines of the city's most famous citizen, the mathematician Archimedes. Now the last quarter of the city, Achradina, has surrendered, and its envoys ask only for their lives. This passage gives Marcellus's reply, the sack of the city, and the death of Archimedes, the scientist of Vitruvius's *eureka* story.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\nMarcellus consilio aduocato et adhibitis etiam Syracusanis qui per seditiones pulsi ab domo intra praesidia Romana fuerant, respondit non plura per annos quinquaginta benefacta Hieronis quam paucis his annis maleficia eorum qui Syracusas tenuerint erga populum Romanum esse; sed pleraque eorum quo debuerint reccidisse foederumque ruptorum ipsos ab se grauiores multo quam populus Romanus uoluerit poenas exegisse. se quidem tertium annum circumsedere Syracusas, non ut populus Romanus seruam ciuitatem haberet sed ne transfugarum duces captam et oppressam tenerent. quid potuerint Syracusani facere, exemplo uel eos esse Syracusanorum qui intra praesidia Romana fuerint uel Hispanum ducem Moericum qui praesidium tradiderit uel ipsorum Syracusanorum postremo serum quidem, sed forte consilium. sibi omnium laborum periculorumque circa moenia Syracusana terra marique tam diu exhaustorum nequaquam tantum fructum esse quod capere <sibi contigerit, quantum si seruare> Syracusas potuisset. inde quaestor cum praesidio ad Nassum ad accipiendam pecuniam regiam custodiendamque missus. urbs diripienda militi data est custodibus diuisis per domos eorum qui intra praesidia Romana fuerant. cum multa irae, multa auaritiae foeda exempla ederentur, Archimeden memoriae proditum est in tanto tumultu, quantum captae urbis in <uiis> discursus diripientium militum ciere poterat, intentum formis quas in puluere descripserat ab ignaro milite quis esset interfectum; aegre id Marcellum tulisse sepulturaeque curam habitam, et propinquis etiam inquisitis honori praesidioque nomen ac memoriam eius fuisse. hoc maxime modo Syracusae captae; in quibus praedae tantum fuit, quantum uix capta Carthagine tum fuisset cum qua uiribus aequis certabatur.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nMarcellus called a council, to which he also invited those Syracusans who had been driven from their homes in the civil strife and had taken refuge inside the Roman lines, and replied that the good deeds of Hiero over fifty years were no more numerous than the crimes committed against the Roman people in these last few years by those who had held Syracuse; but that most of those crimes had come back down on the heads that deserved them, and that the breakers of the treaties had exacted from themselves a far heavier penalty than the Roman people would have wished. He himself, he said, had been besieging Syracuse for the third year now, not so that the Roman people might hold the city in slavery, but so that the leaders of the deserters should not keep it captive and oppressed. What the Syracusans could have done was shown by the example of those Syracusans who had been inside the Roman lines, or of the Spanish commander Moericus, who had handed over the garrison, or finally by the decision of the Syracusans themselves, late, it was true, but brave. For all the labours and dangers he had endured for so long by land and sea around the walls of Syracuse, the reward of having managed to capture it was nowhere near as great as it would have been if he had been able to save Syracuse. Then the quaestor was sent with a guard to the Island, to take over and protect the royal treasury. The city was handed over to the soldiers to plunder, after guards had been posted at the houses of those who had been inside the Roman lines. While many shameful examples of rage and of greed were being given, it is recorded that Archimedes, amid all the uproar that soldiers running through the streets of a captured city to plunder it could raise, was absorbed in figures he had drawn in the dust, and was killed by a soldier who did not know who he was; that Marcellus took this hard; that care was taken for his burial; and that his relatives were even searched out, and his name and memory brought them honour and protection. This, more or less, is how Syracuse was taken; and there was as much booty in it as there would hardly have been in Carthage, had Carthage been taken at that time, a city with which Rome was fighting on equal terms.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThis passage shows both sides of Livy's style. The first half is Marcellus's reply, and it is entirely in **indirect discourse**: one long chain of accusatives and infinitives (*non plura... benefacta... esse*, *pleraque... reccidisse*, *ipsos... poenas exegisse*, *se... circumsedere*) and subjunctives in the subordinate clauses (*tenuerint*, *debuerint*, *uoluerit*, *haberet*, *tenerent*, *potuerint*), all depending on the single verb *respondit*. Nobody is quoted directly, and the translator has to rebuild the speech in the first person while keeping track of who \"he\" and \"they\" are. The final sentence of the speech is a good test: *nequaquam tantum fructum esse quod capere sibi contigerit, quantum si seruare Syracusas potuisset*, \"the fruit of having succeeded in capturing it was nowhere near as great as if he had been able to save it\". A conqueror regretting that he had to conquer is a very Roman pose, and Livy gives it to Marcellus with conviction.\n\nThe second half is narrative, and here comes the death of Archimedes, told with a restraint that makes it more moving. Notice how Livy distances himself from the story: *memoriae proditum est*, \"it has been handed down to memory\", followed by another chain of infinitives (*Archimeden... interfectum*, *Marcellum tulisse*, *curam habitam*, *nomen... fuisse*). He is reporting a tradition, not vouching for it. And notice the contrast he builds: the whole city in uproar, soldiers running through the streets, *multa irae, multa auaritiae foeda exempla* (a fine example of anaphora and of Livy's moralising), and in the middle of it all one old man staring at geometrical figures in the sand, so absorbed that he does not notice the end of his own city. Later writers added that he told the soldier \"do not disturb my circles\"; Livy does not, and his version is better for it.\n\nGrammar to watch: the ablatives absolute at the start (*consilio aduocato et adhibitis... Syracusanis*) and later (*custodibus diuisis*, *propinquis... inquisitis*); the gerundives *ad accipiendam pecuniam... custodiendamque* and *urbs diripienda... data est*, \"the city was given to be plundered\"; the double dative *honori praesidioque... fuisse*, \"was an honour and a protection to them\"; and the last sentence, an unreal comparison in the past, *quantum uix capta Carthagine tum fuisset*, \"as much as there would hardly have been if Carthage had been captured then\". The angle brackets in *<sibi contigerit, quantum si seruare>* and *<uiis>* mark words supplied by modern editors where the manuscripts have a gap.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 2: Ab Urbe Condita XXXIX.8.3-8 -- The Bacchanalia Scandal\n\n**Source:** Livy, *Ab Urbe Condita*, Book XXXIX, chapter 8, sections 3-8, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/livy/liv.39.shtml)\n\n**Context:** 186 BC. Rome has just won its wars in the Greek East, and Greek culture is pouring into the city. That year the consuls were ordered to investigate \"secret conspiracies\", and what they uncovered was the worship of Bacchus (Dionysus), carried out in secret, at night, by thousands of men and women. Livy tells the story at length, almost like a novel, with an informer, a brave freedwoman and a dramatic investigation, and the Senate's reaction was one of the most violent religious crackdowns in Roman history: thousands were arrested, many were executed, and the cult was placed under strict state control. The Senate's actual decree survives, engraved on a bronze tablet found in southern Italy in 1640 and now in Vienna, so we can compare Livy's account with the original document. This passage is Livy's description of how the cult began and what it involved. It is a fascinating text, because it is also a portrait of Roman fear: of foreigners, of secrecy, of women, and of anything happening at night.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\nGraecus ignobilis in Etruriam primum uenit nulla cum arte earum, quas multas ad animorum corporumque cultum nobis eruditissima omnium gens inuexit, sacrificulus et uates; nec is qui aperta religione, propalam et quaestum et disciplinam profitendo, animos errore imbueret, sed occultorum et nocturnorum antistes sacrorum. initia erant, quae primo paucis tradita sunt, deinde uulgari coepta sunt per uiros mulieresque. additae uoluptates religioni uini et epularum, quo plurium animi illicerentur. cum uinum animos <incendissent>, et nox et mixti feminis mares, aetatis tenerae maioribus, discrimen omne pudoris exstinxissent, corruptelae primum omnis generis fieri coeptae, cum ad id quisque, quo natura pronioris libidinis esset, paratam uoluptatem haberet. nec unum genus noxae, stupra promiscua ingenuorum feminarumque erant, sed falsi testes, falsa signa testamentaque et indicia ex eadem officina exibant: uenena indidem intestinaeque caedes, ita ut ne corpora quidem interdum ad sepulturam exstarent. multa dolo, pleraque per uim audebantur. occulebat uim quod prae ululatibus tympanorumque et cymbalorum strepitu nulla uox quiritantium inter stupra et caedes exaudiri poterat.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nA Greek of low birth came first into Etruria, bringing none of the many arts that the most learned of all peoples has brought to us for the cultivation of mind and body: a petty sacrificer and soothsayer; and not one of those who fill people's minds with error through an open religion, professing both their trade and their teaching in public, but a priest of secret, nocturnal rites. There were initiations, which at first were passed on to a few, but then began to spread widely among men and women. To the religion were added the pleasures of wine and feasting, to lure in the minds of more people. When wine had inflamed their minds, and the night, and the mingling of men with women and of the young with their elders, had extinguished every sense of shame, corruption of every kind began to be practised, since each person had at hand the pleasure to which his nature was most inclined. Nor was there only one kind of wrongdoing, the promiscuous debauchery of free men and women: false witnesses, forged seals and wills, and false evidence also came out of the same workshop; from the same source, too, came poisonings and murders within families, so that sometimes not even the bodies could be found for burial. Much was dared by trickery, more by violence. The violence was hidden, because amid the howling and the din of drums and cymbals no voice of those crying for help could be heard among the debauchery and the murders.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe first sentence is a small masterpiece of Roman prejudice. The man is *Graecus* and *ignobilis*, Greek and of low birth; he brings none of the arts for which the Greeks, *eruditissima omnium gens*, \"the most learned of all peoples\", are admired; he is only a *sacrificulus*, a diminutive and contemptuous word for a dealer in sacrifices. Livy then distinguishes him from the ordinary religious charlatans who at least work in the open (*aperta religione, propalam*) with a relative clause of characteristic, *nec is qui... imbueret*, \"not the kind of man who would...\". The real danger is secrecy: *occultorum et nocturnorum antistes sacrorum*, with the noun *sacrorum* held back to the very end, after its two adjectives.\n\nFrom there the passage builds like a prosecution speech. The cult spreads from the few to the many; wine and food are added *quo plurium animi illicerentur*, a purpose clause with *quo* because it contains a comparative (*plurium*); then the great *cum* clause, where wine, night and the mixing of sexes and ages extinguish all shame. Then the list of crimes grows: from sexual misconduct to forgery, perjury, poisoning and murder, all from *eadem officina*, \"the same workshop\", a deliberately commercial image, as if crime were being mass-produced. The chiasmus *multa dolo, pleraque per uim* sums it up, and the last sentence closes on sound: *ululatibus tympanorumque et cymbalorum strepitu*, the howling and the drums drowning out the cries for help.\n\nHow much of this is true is another question. The surviving decree of the Senate is much more measured: it does not ban the worship of Bacchus but limits it, forbidding large gatherings, male priests and secret funds unless the Senate gives permission. Livy's account reads like the official justification of a panic, and it is a valuable reminder that Roman historians, too, wrote from a point of view. For the translator, the main traps are the vocabulary of religion and law (*initia*, \"initiation rites\"; *antistes*, \"priest\"; *signa*, here \"seals\", not \"signs\"; *indicia*, \"evidence\"; *quiritantium*, from *quiritare*, \"to cry out for help\", originally to call on the Quirites, the Roman citizens) and the length of the central *cum* clause. *Intestinae caedes* are murders inside the family, not in the intestines.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 3: Ab Urbe Condita CXX (fragments) -- The Death of Cicero, and Livy's Verdict\n\n**Source:** Livy, *Ab Urbe Condita*, Book CXX (lost), preserved by Seneca the Elder, *Suasoriae* 6.17 and 6.22, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/seneca.suasoriae.html). The round brackets mark words supplied by modern editors. Like the Livy pages above, the text does not capitalise sentences, and that is kept here.\n\n**Context:** Book CXX of Livy, which covered the year 43 BC, is lost, like every book after XLV. But two of its paragraphs survive because Seneca the Elder, the father of the philosopher, quoted them in a collection of rhetorical exercises. One of the set topics for practice speeches was \"Cicero deliberates whether to beg Antony for his life\", and Seneca, discussing it, quotes what the historians had written about Cicero's death. Livy's account of the murder and his final judgement on Cicero are the most substantial pieces of the lost books we have. They are especially valuable because Livy wrote them under Augustus, the man who had agreed to put Cicero's name on the proscription lists.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n[17] M. Cicero sub adventum triumvirorum urbe cesserat pro certo habens, id quod erat, non magis Antonio (se) eripi quam Caesari Cassium et Brutum posse. primo in Tusculanum fugerat, inde transversis itineribus in Formianum ut ab Caieta navem conscensurus proficiscitur. unde aliquotiens in altum provectum cum modo venti adversi rettulissent, modo ipse iactationem navis caeco volvente fluctu pati non posset, taedium tandem eum et fugae et vitae cepit regressusque ad superiorem villam, quae paulo plus mille passibus a mari abest, 'moriar' inquit 'in patria saepe servata'. satis constat servos fortiter fideliterque paratos fuisse ad dimicandum; ipsum deponi lecticam et quietos pati quod fors iniqua cogeret iussisse. prominenti ex lectica praebentique immotam cervicem caput praecisum est. nec satis stolidae crudelitati militum fuit: manus quoque scripsisse aliquid in Antonium exprobrantes praeciderunt. ita relatum caput ad Antonium iussuque eius inter duas manus in rostris positum, ubi ille consul, ubi saepe consularis, ubi eo ipso anno adversus Antonium quanta nulla umquam humana vox cum admiratione eloquentiae auditus fuerat. vix attollentes (madentes) lacrimis oculos homines intueri trucidati membra civis poterant.\n\n[22] Vixit tres et sexaginta annos, ut, si vis afuisset, ne immatura quidem mors videri possit. ingenium et operibus et praemiis operum felix, ipse fortunae diu prosperae; sed in longo tenore felicitatis magnis interim ictus vulneribus, exilio, ruina partium, pro quibus steterat, filiae amatae exitu tam tristi atque acerbo, omnium adversorum nihil, ut viro dignum erat, tulit praeter mortem, quae vere aestimanti minus indigna videri potuit, quod a victore inimico (nihil) crudelius passus erat quam quod eiusdem fortunae compos victo fecisset. si quis tamen virtutibus vitia pensarit, vir magnus ac memorabilis fuit, et in cuius laudes (ex)equendas Cicerone laudatore opus fuerit.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\n[17] Marcus Cicero had left the city as the triumvirs approached, regarding it as certain, as indeed it was, that he could no more be rescued from Antony than Cassius and Brutus could be from Caesar (that is, Octavian). He had first fled to his villa at Tusculum; from there he sets out by cross-country roads for his villa at Formiae, meaning to take ship from Caieta. Several times he put out to sea from there, but now contrary winds drove him back, now he himself could not bear the tossing of the ship as the dark swell rolled it; and at last weariness both of flight and of life took hold of him. Going back to his villa further inland, which is a little more than a mile from the sea, he said: \"I shall die in the country I have so often saved.\" It is well established that his slaves were ready to fight bravely and loyally, and that he ordered them to set down the litter and to bear calmly whatever unjust fate compelled. As he leaned out of the litter and offered his neck without flinching, his head was cut off. Nor was that enough for the brutish cruelty of the soldiers: they cut off his hands as well, taunting them for having written against Antony. So the head was brought back to Antony, and on his orders it was placed between the two hands on the Rostra, where he had been heard as consul, where he had been heard so often as a former consul, where that very year he had been heard speaking against Antony with such admiration for his eloquence as no human voice had ever received. People could hardly raise their eyes, wet with tears, to look upon the remains of their murdered fellow citizen.\n\n[22] He lived sixty-three years, so that, had there been no violence, his death could not even have seemed premature. His genius was fortunate both in its works and in the rewards of its works; he himself long enjoyed prosperous fortune; but in that long run of good fortune he was struck from time to time by great wounds: exile, the ruin of the party he had stood for, and the end, so sad and bitter, of his beloved daughter. Of all his misfortunes he bore none as a man should, except his death, which, to anyone who judges truly, may seem less undeserved, because he suffered nothing crueller at the hands of a victorious enemy than he would himself have done to the defeated, had he been master of the same fortune. Yet if one weighs his virtues against his faults, he was a great and memorable man, and one whose praises would need a Cicero to tell them in full.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThese two paragraphs are the closest we can get to the lost Livy, and they show him at his best. The narrative of the death is built in the classic Livian way: a sequence of pluperfects and perfects that move the story forward (*cesserat*, *fugerat*, *proficiscitur*, a historic present, *cepit*), a *cum* clause of circumstance with a balanced *modo... modo* (\"now... now\"), and then, at the moment of decision, Cicero's only words, direct speech, short and solemn: *moriar in patria saepe servata*. The death itself is told with brutal economy, a pair of present participles in the dative (*prominenti... praebentique*, \"to him as he leaned out and offered\") and a passive verb: *caput praecisum est*. Then the scene moves to the Rostra in Rome, and the triple anaphora *ubi... ubi... ubi* turns the place of the display into a summary of his whole career. It is one of the most powerful pieces of prose to survive from the age of Augustus.\n\nThe obituary is even more interesting, because it is a judgement, and an honest one. Seneca the Elder, who quotes it, explains that Thucydides had done this once or twice and Sallust for a very few people, but that Livy generously gave every great man such a closing summary, a kind of funeral speech; and he calls Livy \"by nature the fairest judge of all great talents\". The judgement is fair, but it is not flattering. Livy praises Cicero's genius and his long good fortune, lists the blows he suffered, and then says something sharp: of all his misfortunes, Cicero bore none of them *ut viro dignum erat*, \"as a man should\", except his death. Anyone who has read Cicero's letters from exile, full of despair and self-pity, will know what Livy means. Then, even more sharply: his death was less undeserved than it might seem, because Antony did nothing to him that Cicero would not have done to Antony if he had won. And then, finally, the balance: *si quis tamen virtutibus vitia pensarit, vir magnus ac memorabilis fuit*. The last clause is a graceful compliment: only Cicero himself would have been eloquent enough to praise Cicero properly.\n\nGrammar to watch: *non magis... quam*, \"no more... than\"; the future participle of intention *conscensurus*, \"intending to board\"; the perfect subjunctive *pensarit* (for *pensaverit*) in the condition; *compos* with the genitive, \"master of\"; and *opus fuerit* with the ablative, *Cicerone laudatore*, \"there would have been need of Cicero as eulogist\". One more note on the text. The Latin Library prints *filiae amatae exitu tam tristi atque acerbo*, which makes \"the end, so sad and bitter\" refer to Cicero's daughter, Tullia, who died in 45 BC after childbirth. Other editions read *filiae morte, exitu tam tristi atque acerbo*, in which \"an end so sad and bitter\" would be Cicero's own death, as the last of the blows. The translation follows the text as printed here.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nLivy's legacy is legendary. Within a generation of his death he had become *the* history of Rome: later Romans did not need to write the story of the Republic again, because Livy had done it, and almost every later account, Greek or Latin, depends on him. Quintilian ranked him with Herodotus; schoolboys read him; epitomes and summaries were made so that those who could not face one hundred and forty-two books could at least know what was in them. The poet Martial wrote a joke inscription for a pocket edition of \"huge Livy, whom my whole library cannot hold\", squeezed into a few small sheets of parchment.\n\nThat size is also why most of him is lost. In late antiquity and the Middle Ages, a work of one hundred and forty-two long books was simply too big to keep copying in full. It circulated in separate blocks of ten books, the decades, and some decades were copied while others were not, until, one by one, they disappeared. The monks and scholars who preserved classical literature saved what they could. We have about a quarter of the work: Books I-X and XXI-XLV, thirty-five books, which is still comfortably longer than all of Caesar, Sallust and Tacitus put together, and quite enough to understand his greatness. Every so often a scrap of the rest reappears: a page of Book XCI was found in a Vatican palimpsest in the eighteenth century. The dream of finding a complete Livy has haunted scholars for six centuries.\n\nThe Renaissance made him a hero. Dante, in the *Inferno*, calls him \"Livy, who does not err\". Petrarch wrote him a letter, complaining that he had been born in the wrong age, and personally assembled one of the most complete manuscripts of the surviving decades. And in the early 1500s Niccolò Machiavelli wrote his *Discourses on the First Ten Books of Titus Livy*, using the early Roman Republic as described by Livy to work out how free states are founded, how they decline and how they can be saved. Through Machiavelli, Livy's Republic became one of the models of modern political thought, read by the founders of the English and American republics. And his stories, Romulus and Remus, the rape of the Sabine women, Horatius at the bridge, Lucretia, Cincinnatus at his plough, Hannibal crossing the Alps, became the shared imagery of European painting, drama and opera for centuries.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: B (Difficult)**\n\nLivy is prose, with standard vocabulary and few literary allusions, so he is in a different league from Virgil and Horace. But he is not easy. His long periods, packed with participles, ablatives absolute and subordinate clauses, demand that the reader hold the whole structure in mind until the main verb arrives; his speeches in indirect discourse can run for paragraphs; the historic infinitive, the poetic colouring of the first decade and his love of *variatio* all catch out the unwary. And then there is the sheer patience required: Livy rewards the reader who keeps going, and punishes the one who loses concentration halfway through a sentence. He is clearly harder than Caesar and Nepos, and at the level of Cicero's speeches: demanding but approachable with preparation, which is exactly what a B means. The later decades, with their long military narratives, sit closer to C; the first decade and the big speeches touch the upper range of B.\n\n---\n\n# ALBIUS TIBULLUS (c. 55 -- 19 BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nTibullus is the least famous of the great Augustan poets today, and the one the Romans themselves considered the most elegant. He wrote love elegies, like Gallus before him and Propertius and Ovid after him, but his are unlike anyone else's: dreamy, gentle and melancholy, full of the countryside, of simple rustic gods, of peace, and of a constant, quiet awareness of death. If Propertius is the passionate one and Ovid the clever one, Tibullus is the daydreamer: the poet who, in the middle of a love poem, drifts off into a fantasy of living on a farm with his girlfriend, and then wakes up to find that she has left him for someone richer.\n\n**A handsome knight from the Latin countryside.** We know very little about his life, and his dates are approximate: he was born around 55 BC, probably on his family's estate at Pedum, in the hills between Tibur (Tivoli) and Praeneste (Palestrina), east of Rome. His family was of equestrian rank and had been rich, but lost much of its land, probably in the confiscations of the civil wars: in his first poem he calls himself the guardian of a field \"once prosperous, now poor\". A short ancient biography adds two details that tell us a lot: he was \"remarkable for his good looks and for the care he took of his appearance\". Unlike his friend Horace, who was short, fat and proud of it, Tibullus was the handsome one.\n\n**Messalla's man.** Instead of joining the circle of Maecenas, Tibullus attached himself to Marcus Valerius Messalla Corvinus, a great aristocrat, general and orator who had fought for Brutus at Philippi and later for Octavian at Actium, and who kept a literary circle of his own, more independent of Augustus than Maecenas's. It is through Messalla that we meet Tibullus's other surprise: the poet who wrote that he hated war actually went to war. He served on Messalla's staff in his campaign in Aquitania, in southwestern Gaul, around 28-27 BC, and won military decorations, which he mentions with some pride. A little earlier, around 30 BC, he had set out with Messalla for the East, and fell ill on the way, on the island of Corcyra (Corfu), the land of the Phaeacians in the *Odyssey*. Messalla sailed on without him. Tibullus, convinced he was dying, wrote one of his most famous elegies about it (it is the first excerpt below). He recovered, and lived to complain about it.\n\n**Horace's gloomy friend.** Horace wrote two poems to a friend called Albius, and almost everyone agrees that this is Tibullus. In one ode Horace tells him to stop grieving so much over \"cruel Glycera\", a girl who left him for a younger man, and to stop \"chanting pitiful elegies\": the most affectionate possible way of telling a friend that his poetry is a little repetitive. In a verse letter Horace imagines him wandering silently through the healthy woods of his estate at Pedum, thinking about what is worthy of a wise and good man, and reminds him that the gods have given him beauty, wealth and the art of enjoying them. And then he signs off with the line we already know: if you want a laugh, come and see me, \"fat and sleek, with well-cared-for skin, a pig from Epicurus's herd\". It is the perfect portrait of the two of them: the melancholy, handsome Tibullus, and Horace trying to cheer him up.\n\n**Delia, Marathus and Nemesis.** Tibullus's two books are built around three loves. In Book I the woman is **Delia**, a Greek pseudonym, like Gallus's Lycoris; according to Apuleius her real name was Plania, and *Delia* is a learned pun on it. She is apparently married, or at any rate guarded by someone, and in one poem Tibullus teaches her all the tricks for deceiving a husband or a doorkeeper, only to discover in a later poem that she is now using the same tricks against him. Ovid, defending his own *Ars Amatoria* from exile, would later point out that Tibullus had written exactly the same kind of advice and had never been punished for it. In Book I there is also **Marathus**, a beautiful boy whose love affairs Tibullus follows with jealousy, and on whose behalf the rustic god Priapus delivers a comic lecture on the art of courtship. In Book II the woman is **Nemesis**, whose name means \"Retribution\", and who lives up to it: she is greedy, and wants presents, and Tibullus, the poet of simple country poverty, ends up declaring that he would even sell his ancestral estate to keep her happy.\n\n**Death with Virgil.** He died young, in 19 BC, the same year as Virgil. A contemporary poet, Domitius Marsus, wrote an epitaph for the two of them: \"Unjust death sent you too, Tibullus, as a young man, to be Virgil's companion in the Elysian fields.\" Ovid, who was barely twenty and never had the chance to become his friend, wrote a long lament for him, in which both of his mistresses turn up at the funeral. Delia says that he was happier while he loved her; Nemesis answers that it was her hand he held as he died. Years later, Ovid remembered with regret that \"greedy fate did not give Tibullus time for my friendship\". Quintilian, a century after his death, still thought him the most polished and elegant of the Roman elegists.\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n- **Elegiae, Book I** (c. 27-26 BC) -- ten elegies, mostly about Delia and the boy Marathus, but also about Messalla's triumph, the countryside and death. The first poem sets out Tibullus's whole programme: let others pile up gold and fight wars; he wants a small farm, a quiet life, the old gods of the countryside, and Delia beside him at the end. [Complete]\n- **Elegiae, Book II** (c. 19 BC) -- six elegies, published shortly before or after his death: a country festival, a birthday poem, a celebration of Messalla's son, and the poems for Nemesis, the greedy mistress. [Complete]\n- **The Corpus Tibullianum, Book III** -- a collection of poems by other members of Messalla's circle, transmitted with Tibullus's two books and long attributed to him: the elegies of a poet calling himself Lygdamus, a long and rather clumsy *Panegyric of Messalla* in hexameters, a cycle of poems about the love of Sulpicia for Cerinthus, and Sulpicia's own six short elegies (which have their own entry in this chapter). Many editions split this material into Books III and IV. [Complete; mostly not by Tibullus]\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nTibullus is the purest, smoothest and least showy of the elegists. His vocabulary is clean and classical, with very few Greek words and very few rare ones. He almost never uses mythology, which sets him apart from Propertius and Ovid: where they compare their mistresses to half the heroines of Greek legend, Tibullus talks about vines, sheep, rustic altars, the household gods and the harvest. His sentences are mostly short and clear, and his elegiac couplets are regular and musical: Quintilian's *tersus atque elegans*, \"polished and elegant\", is exactly right. **Lexicon: LOW to MEDIUM; Allusion density: LOW.**\n\nThe difficulty of Tibullus lies elsewhere, in the way his poems move. They do not follow a logical argument. They drift, from one image to another, by association, like a daydream: a poem that begins with the poet sick on Corfu moves to his memories of leaving Delia, to the Golden Age, to his own death, to the Elysian Fields, to Tartarus, and finally to a fantasy of coming home unexpectedly and finding Delia waiting for him. Each sentence is clear; the problem is understanding why it follows the previous one. Add the usual poetic word order of the couplet, some enjambment, and the conventions of the elegiac genre (the locked door, the guardian, the rival, the lover as slave), and Tibullus is not trivial. But compared with what comes next, Propertius, he is a gentle walk in the country. **Syntactic complexity: MEDIUM; Stylistic figures: MEDIUM.**\n\n## Latin Excerpts\n\n### Excerpt 1: Elegiae I.3.1-10 and 53-58 -- Sick on Corfu\n\n**Source:** Tibullus, *Elegiae*, Book I, poem 3, lines 1-10 and 53-58, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/tibullus1.html). The Latin Library capitalises the first word of every line, as many printed editions do; this has been kept.\n\n**Context:** Around 30 BC, Messalla set off for the East, and Tibullus went with him, but fell ill on Corcyra (Corfu), which he calls Phaeacia, after the island of King Alcinous in the *Odyssey*. Messalla and his staff sailed on. Alone and feverish, Tibullus writes to his patron, and then to Death itself, convinced that he is about to die far from home. In the lines between the two parts of the excerpt, he remembers how reluctant he was to leave Delia in Rome, and confesses that he invented every excuse to delay the departure: bad omens, birds, and even \"the sacred day of Saturn\", one of the earliest references in Latin literature to the Jewish Sabbath. Then he imagines his own death and writes his own epitaph.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Ibitis Aegaeas sine me, Messalla, per undas,\n> O utinam memores ipse cohorsque mei.\n> Me tenet ignotis aegrum Phaeacia terris,\n> Abstineas avidas, Mors, modo, nigra, manus.\n> Abstineas, Mors atra, precor: non hic mihi mater\n> Quae legat in maestos ossa perusta sinus,\n> Non soror, Assyrios cineri quae dedat odores\n> Et fleat effusis ante sepulcra comis,\n> Delia non usquam; quae me cum mitteret urbe,\n> Dicitur ante omnes consuluisse deos.\n>\n> ...\n>\n> Quodsi fatales iam nunc explevimus annos,\n> Fac lapis inscriptis stet super ossa notis:\n> 'Hic iacet inmiti consumptus morte Tibullus,\n> Messallam terra dum sequiturque mari.'\n> Sed me, quod facilis tenero sum semper Amori,\n> Ipsa Venus campos ducet in Elysios.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nYou will sail across the Aegean waves without me, Messalla:\noh, if only you and your staff would remember me!\nPhaeacia holds me here, sick, in an unknown land.\nKeep your greedy hands away from me, black Death, I beg you;\nkeep them away, dark Death, I pray. I have no mother here\nto gather my burnt bones into her grieving lap,\nno sister to pour Assyrian perfumes on my ashes\nand weep before my tomb with her hair let loose,\nand nowhere is Delia, who, when she sent me off from the city,\nis said to have consulted every god first.\n\n...\n\nBut if I have already completed the years that fate allotted me,\nlet a stone stand over my bones, with these words carved on it:\n\"Here lies Tibullus, taken by cruel death\nwhile he was following Messalla over land and sea.\"\nBut because I have always been an easy prey for tender Love,\nVenus herself will lead me to the Elysian Fields.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe opening is a send-off poem in reverse, like Horace's Epode 10, but in a completely different key: here it is the poet who stays behind, and the one in danger. The first line already sets the tone: *Ibitis... sine me*, \"you will go without me\", with *sine me* placed right in the middle, between the Aegean and the waves. Then comes the plea to Death, repeated twice with anaphora, *Abstineas... abstineas*, a jussive subjunctive softened into a prayer by *modo* and *precor*. The image of Death with \"greedy hands\" (*avidas... manus*) is typical Tibullus: concrete, physical, almost a figure from a painting.\n\nThen the heart of the passage: what frightens him is not death itself but dying alone, without the women who would perform his funeral rites. A Roman funeral needed family: a mother to gather the bones from the pyre, a sister to pour perfumes and mourn with her hair unbound. The list builds up in a series of negatives, *non hic mihi mater... non soror... Delia non usquam*, each clause longer and more detailed than the last, until the climax: the woman he loves is nowhere near. Notice the relative clauses of purpose with the subjunctive (*quae legat*, *quae dedat... et fleat*): \"no mother who might gather\", \"no sister who might give\". *Assyrios... odores* is a typical poetic touch: Eastern perfumes, the most precious kind.\n\nThe second part, after the gap, is Tibullus's epitaph for himself, written in the form of a real Roman tomb inscription, beginning with the formula *Hic iacet*, \"here lies\". It is a moving and slightly self-pitying piece of self-promotion: the poet dies in the service of his patron, *Messallam terra dum sequiturque mari* (with *-que* attached, as poets often do, to the second word of the clause rather than the first). And then the typical Tibullan turn: because he has always been \"easy\" (*facilis*) for Love, Venus herself will guide him to the Elysian Fields. Love has a paradise of its own, and he has earned his place in it. Grammatically, note *fatales... annos*, \"the years given by fate\", *explevimus*, a poetic plural for \"I have completed\", and *Fac... stet*, an imperative of *facio* followed by a subjunctive without *ut*: \"make a stone stand\".\n\nHe did not die, of course. He went home, and wrote the poem.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 2: Elegiae I.10.1-14 -- Who Invented the Sword?\n\n**Source:** Tibullus, *Elegiae*, Book I, poem 10, lines 1-14, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/tibullus1.html)\n\n**Context:** The last poem of Book I, and one of the most unusual poems in Latin: an elegy against war. Tibullus has apparently been called up for military service, and does not want to go. He begins by asking who invented the sword, blames gold for the end of the peaceful Golden Age, and prays to the household gods of his childhood to protect him. Coming from a man who had actually served with distinction in Messalla's campaigns, and written in an age that celebrated Actium and the conquests of Augustus, it is a quietly remarkable piece of writing.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Quis fuit, horrendos primus qui protulit enses?\n> Quam ferus et vere ferreus ille fuit!\n> Tum caedes hominum generi, tum proelia nata,\n> Tum brevior dirae mortis aperta via est.\n> An nihil ille miser meruit, nos ad mala nostra\n> Vertimus, in saevas quod dedit ille feras?\n> Divitis hoc vitium est auri, nec bella fuerunt,\n> Faginus adstabat cum scyphus ante dapes.\n> Non arces, non vallus erat, somnumque petebat\n> Securus sparsas dux gregis inter oves.\n> Tunc mihi vita foret, volgi nec tristia nossem\n> Arma nec audissem corde micante tubam;\n> Nunc ad bella trahor, et iam quis forsitan hostis\n> Haesura in nostro tela gerit latere.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nWho was the first to bring out the horrifying sword?\nHow savage he was, how truly made of iron!\nThen slaughter was born for the human race, then battles,\nthen a shorter road was opened to grim death.\nOr is that poor wretch not to blame at all, and was it we who turned to our own ruin\nwhat he gave us to use against savage beasts?\nThis is the fault of rich gold: there were no wars\nwhile a beechwood cup still stood beside the meal.\nThere were no citadels, no palisades, and the leader of the flock\nlooked for sleep without a care among his scattered sheep.\nThat was the time I should have lived, and never known\nthe grim weapons of the crowd, nor heard the trumpet with a pounding heart.\nNow I am being dragged off to war, and perhaps some enemy\nis already carrying the weapon that will lodge in my side.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe poem opens with a question and a pun. *Quam ferus et vere ferreus ille fuit!*: \"how savage, and truly made of iron, he was\". *Ferus* (\"wild, savage\") and *ferreus* (\"made of iron\") sound almost the same, and the man who invented iron weapons is himself iron-hearted. The pun also hides a myth, without naming it: in the ancient story of the ages of the world, the age of iron was the last and worst, the age of war and greed. Tibullus never says so; he lets the adjective do the work.\n\nThe next lines build on the same idea with anaphora (*Tum... tum... tum*, \"then... then... then\"), and then the poet corrects himself: perhaps the inventor is innocent, and it is we who turned against each other a weapon meant for wild animals. *Nos ad mala nostra / Vertimus*: the guilt moves from one man to all of us. The real culprit is named in line 7: *Divitis hoc vitium est auri*, \"this is the fault of rich gold\". In the old days, when people drank from a simple beechwood cup (*faginus... scyphus*), there were no wars, no fortresses, and the shepherd slept peacefully among his sheep. This is the Tibullan ideal in four lines: poverty, simplicity and peace, the countryside against the city, the beechwood cup against gold.\n\nThen comes the turn from the general to the personal, which is the emotional key of the poem. *Tunc mihi vita foret*, \"that was when I should have lived\" (an imperfect subjunctive of wish, *foret* for *esset*), followed by two pluperfect subjunctives of unreal past condition, *nossem* (for *novissem*) and *audissem*: \"I would not have known, I would not have heard\". And then, with a brutal present tense, reality: *Nunc ad bella trahor*, \"now I am being dragged off to war\". The last couplet is one of the most striking images in Augustan poetry: somewhere, at this very moment, an enemy is already carrying the weapon that will stick in the poet's side. *Haesura* is a future participle, \"destined to stick\", placed at the head of the line, so that the weapon is in the reader's mind before the enemy who carries it. Note also *volgi*, the old spelling of *vulgi*, and *quis*, here the indefinite \"some\", as it often is after *si*, *nisi* and *ne*, and here after *forsitan*.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nIn antiquity Tibullus was admired more than he is today. Quintilian ranked him first among the Roman elegists, \"most polished and elegant\", ahead of Propertius and Ovid. Ovid himself, who loved him, placed him in the line of the great elegists between Gallus and Propertius, mourned him in one of his finest poems, and saw him as the model of the gentle, refined love poet. His influence on the countryside of later Latin poetry, on its rustic festivals, its simple gods and its dream of peace far from war and wealth, was deep and lasting.\n\nSince then, he has suffered the fate of the quiet. In the Renaissance and in the eighteenth century, readers who found Propertius too difficult and Ovid too cynical loved Tibullus for his purity and his gentle melancholy, and he was imitated by poets writing in Latin and in the modern languages. But the nineteenth and twentieth centuries preferred Propertius's passion and Ovid's wit, and Tibullus slipped into the background, read mostly by specialists. He also has the strange distinction of being the poet whose name protected someone else's work: it is only because the poems of Sulpicia were copied as part of the *Corpus Tibullianum* that the only surviving poems by a Roman woman reached us at all.\n\nHe deserves better. His anti-war elegy, written in the age of Augustus's triumphs, still reads as one of the most humane poems of antiquity, and his dream of the small farm and the beechwood cup is the ancestor of every poem that has ever preferred a quiet life to a glorious one.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: C (Manageable)**\n\nTibullus is the most accessible of the Augustan poets. His vocabulary is pure and classical, his sentences are mostly short and clear, he uses almost no mythology, and his couplets are smooth and regular. He is still verse, with the normal poetic word order and the conventions of the elegiac genre, and his habit of drifting from one image to another by association means that the logic of a whole poem can be harder to follow than any single sentence in it. But he never plays games with the reader the way Horace does, and he never buries him under learned allusions the way Propertius will. A solid C: the right place to begin with Latin elegy, before Ovid, and long before Propertius.\n\n---\n\n# SULPICIA (fl. late I century BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nSulpicia is a small entry with an enormous distinction: she is almost certainly the only woman of classical Rome whose poetry has survived, and the only female elegist we know. Hundreds of Roman men wrote poetry about women; Sulpicia is the one woman whose own voice comes back to us. We have six short poems by her, about forty lines in all, tucked away in the third book of the collection that goes under the name of Tibullus. That is the whole of her surviving work, and very probably the whole of what she ever published. It is enough to show that she was a poet worth reading, and a woman worth knowing.\n\n**A girl from the best family in Rome.** Unlike most elegists, Sulpicia tells us exactly who she is, and in one poem signs herself, proudly, *Servi filia Sulpicia*, \"Sulpicia, daughter of Servius\". Her father was Servius Sulpicius Rufus, son of the great jurist of the same name who had been consul in 51 BC and a friend of Cicero (it was this grandfather who wrote Cicero one of the most famous letters of consolation in Latin when his daughter Tullia died). Her mother was Valeria, the sister of Messalla Corvinus, Tibullus's patron. Her father seems to have died early, and her uncle Messalla became her guardian. So Sulpicia grew up at the centre of the most brilliant literary circle in Augustan Rome, surrounded by poets, and she decided to become one herself.\n\n**The overprotective uncle.** Two of her poems are about a birthday, and they are the funniest pages she wrote. In the first she complains that her \"hateful birthday\" is coming, and that she is going to have to spend it in the boring countryside near Arretium (Arezzo), on her uncle's estate, with a cold river, and without Cerinthus. \"What is sweeter than the city? Is a country villa a fit place for a girl?\" And then, to Messalla directly: \"You are far too anxious about me, uncle. Calm down. Journeys are not always well timed.\" In the second poem, the trip has been cancelled: \"Have you heard? That dreary journey has been lifted from your girl's mind. Now she can be in Rome for your birthday.\" Every teenager who has ever been dragged to a relative's house on a holiday will recognise the tone.\n\n**Cerinthus.** The man she loves is called Cerinthus, a Greek pseudonym, as elegiac convention required, which may hide a pun on *kerinthos*, the Greek word for \"bee-bread\", the food bees make from pollen. Who he really was, nobody knows. Her poems to him run through the whole emotional range of elegy in a few lines: the triumphant declaration that love has finally arrived and she does not care who knows it (the first excerpt); jealousy, when Cerinthus apparently prefers a common slave girl \"weighed down with her wool basket\" to the daughter of Servius; illness, when she lies sick with fever and asks whether he even cares; and regret, when she apologises for having left him alone one night because she wanted to hide how much she felt (the second excerpt). In elegy the man speaks and the woman is spoken about. Sulpicia turns the genre round: she is the one who loves, suffers, burns and writes, and Cerinthus is the silent beloved.\n\n**A scandal, deliberately.** All of this was more daring than it looks. A Roman aristocratic woman was expected above all to have *pudicitia*, sexual modesty, and her reputation was her family's honour. Sulpicia announces in her very first poem that she is glad to have \"sinned\", and that she is tired of composing her face to protect her reputation. That is not the voice of a woman who expected her poems to stay in a drawer.\n\n**A cycle about her.** Just before her own six poems, the *Corpus Tibullianum* contains five more poems about Sulpicia and Cerinthus, written by someone else: a poet of Messalla's circle, perhaps even Tibullus himself, who tells the same love story from the outside, in a more conventional and polished style. Scholars call him the *amicus Sulpiciae*, \"Sulpicia's friend\". Reading the two side by side is the best way to see what is special about her: his poems are elegant; hers are alive.\n\nA word of honesty: a few modern scholars have argued that Sulpicia's poems were in fact written by a man, impersonating a woman. Most reject the idea, and the poems themselves, with their very personal details of family, birthdays and names, make it hard to believe. But because the question has been raised, it is fair to say \"almost certainly\" rather than \"certainly\".\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n- **Six elegies** (late I century BC), preserved as poems 13-18 of the third book of the *Corpus Tibullianum* (poems IV.7-12 in editions that divide the book in two): the declaration of love (13), the unwanted birthday in the country (14), the cancelled trip (15), the rival (16), the fever (17) and the apology (18). About forty lines in all, the shortest poems in this entire project and almost certainly all she wrote. [Complete]\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nSulpicia's poems are very short, and that is the best thing about them for a student: a whole poem can be translated in an afternoon, with all the satisfaction of having finished a complete work. They are written in elegiac couplets, with ordinary vocabulary, almost no mythology, and none of the long allusive digressions of Propertius. **Lexicon: LOW; Allusion density: LOW.**\n\nBut \"short\" does not mean \"simple\". Sulpicia's Latin is famously compressed, and for a long time it was dismissed as clumsy: nineteenth-century scholars, assuming that a woman could not write as well as a man, called her style awkward and amateurish. Today most readers see the compression as deliberate. She packs a great deal into very few words, uses unusual constructions (double negatives, comparisons built inside comparisons, abstract nouns in the dative), and likes to delay the decisive word until the end of a long sentence. Her last poem is a single sentence of six lines in which the actual confession only arrives in the fifth line. Line for line, she can be harder than Tibullus. **Syntactic complexity: MEDIUM to HIGH; Stylistic figures: MEDIUM.**\n\n## Latin Excerpts\n\n### Excerpt 1: Corpus Tibullianum III.13 -- Love Has Come at Last\n\n**Source:** Sulpicia, *Corpus Tibullianum* III.13, complete, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/tibullus3.html), where her poems are printed under the heading *Sulpiciae Elegidia*, \"the little elegies of Sulpicia\". The text writes consonantal *v* as *u* (*uenit*, *uelim*), which has been kept.\n\n**Context:** The first of Sulpicia's poems, and her manifesto. Love has finally arrived, and instead of hiding it, as a well-brought-up Roman girl should, she announces it to the world. The poem works like a prayer of thanksgiving: she prayed to Venus with her poems, Venus answered, and now Sulpicia pays her debt by telling everyone. Behind it stands the most famous poem by a woman in all of Greek literature, Sappho's prayer to Aphrodite, in which the goddess comes down from heaven to help the poet in love.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Tandem uenit amor, qualem texisse pudori\n> quam nudasse alicui sit mihi fama magis.\n> Exorata meis illum Cytherea Camenis\n> attulit in nostrum deposuitque sinum.\n> Exsoluit promissa Venus: mea gaudia narret,\n> dicetur si quis non habuisse sua.\n> Non ego signatis quicquam mandare tabellis,\n> ne legat id nemo quam meus ante, uelim,\n> sed peccasse iuuat, uultus componere famae\n> taedet: cum digno digna fuisse ferar.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nAt last love has come, and of such a kind that a rumour I had hidden it\nwould bring me more shame than a rumour I had laid it bare to anyone.\nWon over by my Muses, the goddess of Cythera\nbrought him to me and laid him in my arms.\nVenus has kept her promises: let my joys be told\nby anyone who is said to have had no joys of their own.\nI would not want to entrust anything to sealed tablets,\nso that no one could read it before my love does;\nno, I am glad to have sinned, and sick of composing my face\nfor my reputation: let them say I was a worthy woman with a worthy man.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe first couplet is the most discussed sentence Sulpicia wrote, and a perfect illustration of her compression. *Qualem texisse pudori / quam nudasse alicui sit mihi fama magis*: literally, \"such that the report of having covered it would be to me a cause of shame more than of having bared it to someone\". The pieces are *qualem... sit*, a relative clause of result (\"such that\"); *pudori*, a dative of purpose, \"a cause of shame\"; and two perfect infinitives, *texisse* and *nudasse* (for *nudavisse*), compared with *magis... quam*. Put together, it means: hiding this love would be more shameful than revealing it. The images are physical: *tegere* is to cover, *nudare* is to strip naked. It is a startling way for a senator's niece to begin.\n\nThe second couplet is the story of the prayer: *Cytherea* is Venus (from the island of Cythera, where she came ashore after her birth from the sea), and *Camenae* are the Italian Muses, so \"my Muses\" are Sulpicia's own poems. Venus was *exorata*, \"won over by entreaty\", and she brought Cerinthus *in nostrum... sinum*, into the poet's lap or embrace (with a poetic plural, *nostrum* for *meum*). The third couplet turns the prayer into a public thanksgiving: Venus has \"paid off\" her promises (*exsoluit*, the technical word for fulfilling a vow), so Sulpicia will publish her joy, and anyone who has none of their own can tell it for her. Notice the order: *mea gaudia narret, / dicetur si quis...*, with the *si* clause after the main verb and *si quis* split by *dicetur*.\n\nThe fourth couplet is a famous puzzle. *Non ego signatis quicquam mandare tabellis, / ne legat id nemo quam meus ante, uelim*: \"I would not want to entrust anything to sealed tablets, so that nobody might read it before my man\". The double negative, *ne... nemo*, is the difficulty: she is refusing to write love letters on sealed wax tablets meant only for Cerinthus's eyes. She wants her poems to be read by everyone. And then the climax, in the last couplet: *peccasse iuuat*, \"I am glad to have sinned\", *uultus componere famae / taedet*, \"I am sick of arranging my expression for the sake of my reputation\", and finally *cum digno digna fuisse ferar*, a jussive subjunctive: \"let me be spoken of as a worthy woman who was with a worthy man\". The polyptoton *digno digna*, the same adjective in masculine and feminine side by side, places the two lovers on exactly the same level. In the language of elegy, where the poet is usually the slave of his mistress, this is a declaration of equality.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 2: Corpus Tibullianum III.18 -- The Apology\n\n**Source:** Sulpicia, *Corpus Tibullianum* III.18, complete, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/tibullus3.html)\n\n**Context:** The last of Sulpicia's six poems. The night before, she left Cerinthus alone, probably at a party or a dinner, because she did not want him (or the other guests) to see how much she was in love. Now she regrets it, and writes to tell him so. The whole poem is a single sentence, built like a Roman oath: \"may I no longer be... if I have ever done anything I regret more than this\".\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Ne tibi sim, mea lux, aeque iam feruida cura\n> ac uideor paucos ante fuisse dies,\n> si quicquam tota commisi stulta iuuenta\n> cuius me fatear paenituisse magis,\n> hesterna quam te solum quod nocte reliqui,\n> ardorem cupiens dissimulare meum.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nMay I no longer be, my light, as burning a passion for you\nas I seem to have been just a few days ago,\nif, in all my foolish youth, I have ever done anything\nthat I would confess I regretted more\nthan leaving you alone last night,\nbecause I wanted to hide my own desire.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThis is Sulpicia's compression at its most extreme, and a superb exercise in following a Latin sentence to the end. The structure is an oath, or a curse on oneself: *Ne tibi sim... si quicquam commisi*, \"may I not be... if I have done anything\". The main clause comes first, a negative wish in the subjunctive (*ne... sim*), with a comparison inside it: *aeque... feruida cura / ac uideor... fuisse*, \"as burning a passion (*cura*, literally 'care', the standard elegiac word for the beloved) as I seem to have been\". Then the condition, *si quicquam... commisi*, then a relative clause of characteristic inside the condition, *cuius me fatear paenituisse magis*, built on the impersonal verb *paenitet*, which takes the accusative of the person who regrets (*me*) and the genitive of the thing regretted (*cuius*). And only then, in the fifth line, the second half of the comparison, *magis... quam*: what she regrets most is *quod te solum hesterna nocte reliqui*, \"that I left you alone last night\". The confession is held back until the very end of the sentence, exactly as she held back her feelings the night before.\n\nThe final line gives the reason: *ardorem cupiens dissimulare meum*, \"wanting to hide my passion\". *Ardor*, \"burning\", picks up *feruida*, \"boiling\", from the first line, so the poem begins and ends in fire. Notice also the tender address *mea lux*, \"my light\", a common Roman endearment, and *stulta*, \"foolish\", attached to Sulpicia herself: she is the one who has been silly. Few poems in Latin say so much, so precisely, in six lines.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nFor most of their history, Sulpicia's poems were read as part of Tibullus, and for a long time they were barely noticed. When nineteenth- and early twentieth-century scholars did notice them, they often treated them with condescension, as the charming but unskilled effusions of a young girl, whose Latin was too awkward to be the work of a real poet. In the last fifty years that view has been turned upside down. Sulpicia is now read as a sophisticated poet who knew the conventions of elegy perfectly and bent them to her own purpose, and her poems are among the most studied texts in the whole *Corpus Tibullianum*.\n\nHer real importance is simple and immense. She is the only woman of classical Rome whose poetry has survived in any quantity, and the only female love elegist we know. Other Roman women wrote: a later Sulpicia, in the time of Domitian, wrote love poems to her husband, of which two lines survive; a few letters attributed to Cornelia, the mother of the Gracchi, are quoted by Nepos; the speech of Hortensia against a tax on women survives only in a Greek paraphrase. Only in late antiquity do we have complete works by Latin-writing women: the prison diary of the martyr Perpetua, the travel journal of the pilgrim Egeria, and Proba's retelling of the Bible in lines borrowed from Virgil. In all the centuries of classical Latin poetry, Sulpicia's forty lines are the one place where a woman speaks for herself, about her own love, in her own words. They survived only because they were copied next to the poems of a man. That is reason enough to read every one of them.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: C (Manageable)**\n\nSulpicia's poems are short, complete and written in plain vocabulary, with almost no mythology and no learned digressions: with all forty lines in front of you, you can finish her entire works, which is a rare pleasure. That is what pulls her towards D. But her syntax is genuinely compressed, with double negatives, comparisons inside comparisons, abstract datives and decisive words held back until the end of a long sentence, and line for line she can be harder than Tibullus. So C, at the easy end of it: the shortest exam in the project, but not a free pass.\n\n---\n\n# SEXTUS PROPERTIUS (c. 50 -- after 16 BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nIf Tibullus is the daydreamer of Roman elegy, Propertius is its storm. He is passionate, obsessive, jealous, self-pitying, funny, learned, morbid, proud and completely unpredictable, often all within the same poem. He spent five years writing about one woman, Cynthia, and he made her the most vivid woman in Latin poetry: we see her asleep, drunk, furious, sick, beautiful, unfaithful, dead and, in his most extraordinary poem, back from the dead to tell him off. He is also, by common consent, the most difficult of the Roman elegists: a poet so dense, so allusive and so badly preserved that even professional Latinists argue over what he is saying in half his lines. And he is worth every bit of the effort.\n\n**The Umbrian.** Sextus Propertius was born around 50 BC, perhaps a little later, at Asisium, modern Assisi, in Umbria: the same hill town that would later give the world Saint Francis. He was proud of it, and calls Umbria \"the homeland of the Roman Callimachus\", that is, of himself. His family were local landowners of some standing, but the civil wars hit them hard. His father died when he was young. In 41-40 BC, during the Perusine War, when Octavian besieged Lucius Antonius in nearby Perusia (Perugia), a relative of Propertius was killed and left unburied in the Etruscan hills, and the family lost much of its land in the confiscations that followed: \"the grim surveyor's rod took away our well-tilled wealth\". Like Virgil and Tibullus, he grew up knowing exactly what the new regime had cost.\n\n**No lawyer.** His mother brought him to Rome and expected him to do the sensible thing: study law, speak in the Forum, start a public career. He tried. Then, as he tells it in his last book, the god Apollo himself forbade him to \"thunder out words in the insane Forum\", and told him to write elegies instead, adding, helpfully, that he would always be at the mercy of a girl. Apollo was right on both counts.\n\n**Cynthia.** The girl was Cynthia, and she is the first word of his first book: *Cynthia prima suis miserum me cepit ocellis*, \"Cynthia was the first to capture me, poor wretch, with her eyes\". According to Apuleius her real name was Hostia; *Cynthia* is a learned pseudonym, from Mount Cynthus on Delos, the birthplace of Apollo, the god of poetry. She seems to have been a sophisticated, independent woman of the demi-monde, probably older than he was, beautiful, witty, a reader and even a writer of poetry, and, by his account, magnificently impossible. The affair lasted about five years, broke down several times, and ended for good at the close of Book III, where Propertius bids her a bitter farewell. She then returned in Book IV, twice, once as a ghost.\n\n**Maecenas comes calling.** His first book, published around 29-28 BC and known simply as the *Cynthia* or the *Monobiblos* (the \"single book\"), was an immediate success, and it brought him an invitation into the circle of Maecenas. His next books are dedicated to him, and they show Propertius trying to stay himself inside the official culture of Augustan Rome. When Maecenas asks him for epic, he answers with the most famous *recusatio* in elegy: he cannot sing of Caesar's wars, because his only war is in bed. When Augustus withdraws a law that would have forced men of his class to marry, Propertius celebrates in verse, and adds the most un-Augustan line of the age: \"no son of mine will ever be a soldier\". He got away with it. It is a measure of how much freedom the poets of Maecenas still had in the 20s BC.\n\n**Horace's rival?** Horace and Propertius belonged to the same circle and never once mention each other, which, for two poets so fond of naming their friends, is a silence that says a lot. Many scholars think Horace was mocking him in a letter of around 19 BC, where he describes two poets in Rome who flatter each other shamelessly at recitations: \"I come away an Alcaeus by his vote; and what is he by mine? Who but Callimachus?\" Propertius was the only poet in Rome who called himself the Roman Callimachus. If the jab was meant for him, it is one of the best literary insults of antiquity.\n\n**Ovid's friend.** Ovid, much younger, was his friend and admirer, and remembered him reciting his love poems (his \"fires\", as Ovid puts it) to their circle. In Ovid's list of Roman elegists, Propertius is the third, between Tibullus and Ovid himself.\n\n**Death, and a descendant.** His last datable poem was written in 16 BC; after that he disappears, and we do not know when he died. But his family went on writing. A century later, Pliny the Younger describes a recitation by a certain Passennus Paulus, a descendant of Propertius who wrote elegies in the family style. He began his first poem, as poets do, with an address to a friend in the audience: *Prisce, iubes...*, \"Priscus, you bid me...\". Whereupon Priscus, a distinguished jurist sitting in the front row, called out: \"I most certainly do not bid you!\" The audience collapsed, and the recitation never quite recovered. Pliny adds, in fairness, that Priscus was generally thought to be a little unhinged.\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n- **Elegiae, Book I** (*Cynthia* or *Monobiblos*, c. 29-28 BC) -- twenty-two elegies, almost all about Cynthia: falling in love, jealousy, a night spent outside her locked door, her plan to leave him for a trip abroad, and two final poems on the dead of the Perusine War. [Complete]\n- **Elegiae, Book II** (c. 26-25 BC) -- the longest book, and possibly two books run together in the manuscripts: more Cynthia, the *recusatio* to Maecenas, the celebration of the withdrawn marriage law, and a famous poem on the image of Love as a winged boy. [Complete, but the text is in poor condition]\n- **Elegiae, Book III** (c. 23-22 BC) -- a more varied book, in which Propertius presents himself as the Roman Callimachus, writes about politics, money, death and the Parthian expedition, and ends with his farewell to Cynthia. [Complete]\n- **Elegiae, Book IV** (c. 16 BC) -- a new kind of poetry: alongside the last Cynthia poems, a series of \"Roman\" elegies explaining the origins of Roman cults, places and names (the god Vertumnus, the traitor Tarpeia, Hercules at the site of Rome, the temple of Jupiter Feretrius, Apollo at Actium), modelled on the *Aetia* of Callimachus, and the speech of the dead noblewoman Cornelia from her tomb, often called the \"queen of elegies\". [Complete]\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nThe best way to understand Propertius's style is to compare him with Tibullus. Tibullus avoids mythology; Propertius cannot get through ten lines without it. When Cynthia falls asleep, she is like Ariadne abandoned on Naxos, Andromeda freed from her rock and a Bacchant collapsed beside a river, all in the first six lines of the poem. His model is not Tibullus but Callimachus, the most learned poet of Hellenistic Alexandria, and he fills his poems with rare myths, obscure place names, and allusions that are never explained, because explaining them would spoil the pleasure for a learned reader. For a modern student, they are an obstacle course. **Allusion density: HIGH.**\n\nHis Latin is just as demanding. Propertius compresses ruthlessly: he leaves out connectives, jumps from one idea to the next without warning, changes the addressee halfway through a poem, and lets speeches begin and end without saying who is speaking. His word order is bold even by the standards of verse, his use of cases stretches the grammar (an ablative can be means, cause, place and manner at once), he uses words in unusual senses, and he loves sudden shifts of tone, from tragedy to farce and back again in a single couplet. Where Tibullus drifts gently from image to image, Propertius leaps. **Syntactic complexity: HIGH; Stylistic figures: HIGH; Lexicon: MEDIUM to HIGH.**\n\nAnd then there is the text. Propertius survives in a small number of late medieval manuscripts, the oldest from the twelfth century, and they are full of errors. Words are corrupted, lines are missing, couplets have migrated from one poem to another, and the boundaries between poems are often uncertain. Modern editors disagree on hundreds of readings, move couplets around, and even divide Book II into two. The great Latin scholar A. E. Housman devoted years of his life to Propertius and never finished his edition. So when you are stuck in Propertius, it is not always your fault: sometimes the Latin is genuinely broken.\n\nHow much harder is he than Tibullus? Two full tiers. Tibullus is a C, the most accessible poet of the age; Propertius sits in the upper part of A, alongside the hardest parts of Virgil, and only Horace's *Odes* are clearly worse.\n\n## Latin Excerpts\n\n### Excerpt 1: Elegiae IV.7.1-18 -- Cynthia's Ghost\n\n**Source:** Propertius, *Elegiae*, Book IV, poem 7, lines 1-18, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/prop4.html). The text writes consonantal *v* as *u* (*uisa*, *uestis*), which has been kept.\n\n**Context:** Years after the farewell at the end of Book III, Cynthia is dead, and has only just been buried. One night, as Propertius lies awake in his cold bed, she appears to him: the same hair, the same eyes, but with her dress scorched by the pyre and her lips worn by the water of Lethe, the river of the underworld. And she is not there to forgive him. In the rest of the poem she accuses him of neglecting her funeral, accuses her slaves of having poisoned her, orders him to burn the poems he wrote about her, tells him which of her maids to reward and which to punish, and finally announces that soon she will have him all to herself again, in the underworld: \"Others may have you now; soon I alone will hold you.\" It is one of the strangest and most powerful poems in Latin, a love poem, a ghost story and a domestic quarrel all at once. These are its first eighteen lines.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Sunt aliquid Manes: letum non omnia finit,\n> luridaque euictos effugit umbra rogos.\n> Cynthia namque meo uisa est incumbere fulcro,\n> murmur ad extremae nuper humata uiae,\n> cum mihi somnus ab exsequiis penderet amoris,\n> et quererer lecti frigida regna mei.\n> eosdem habuit secum quibus est elata capillos,\n> eosdem oculos; lateri uestis adusta fuit,\n> et solitum digito beryllon adederat ignis,\n> summaque Lethaeus triuerat ora liquor.\n> spirantisque animos et uocem misit: at illi\n> pollicibus fragiles increpuere manus:\n> \"perfide nec cuiquam melior sperande puellae,\n> in te iam uires somnus habere potest?\n> iamne tibi exciderant uigilacis furta Suburae\n> et mea nocturnis trita fenestra dolis?\n> per quam demisso quotiens tibi fune pependi,\n> alterna ueniens in tua colla manu!\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nThe spirits of the dead are something real: death does not end everything,\nand a pale ghost escapes the pyre that has overcome its body.\nFor Cynthia seemed to lean over the head of my bed,\nCynthia, buried only lately by the roar at the edge of the road,\nwhen, after my love's funeral, sleep was hanging over me\nand I was lamenting the cold kingdom of my bed.\nShe had the same hair as when she was carried out to burial,\nthe same eyes; her dress was burnt along her side,\nthe fire had eaten into the beryl ring she always wore on her finger,\nand the water of Lethe had worn away the edges of her lips.\nShe breathed out a living spirit and a voice; but\nher brittle hands cracked their knuckles at me:\n\"Traitor, from whom no girl should ever hope for anything better,\ncan sleep already have power over you?\nHave you already forgotten our stolen nights in the sleepless Subura,\nand my window-sill, worn smooth by our nightly tricks?\nHow often I let down a rope through that window and hung from it for you,\ncoming down hand over hand into your arms!\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe poem opens with a philosophical statement, and a provocative one: *Sunt aliquid Manes*, \"the spirits of the dead are something\". Any Roman reader would have heard in it a contradiction of Lucretius and the Epicureans, for whom death was simply the end of everything. Propertius is about to prove them wrong with an experience of his own. Notice the emphatic word order: *Sunt* first, then *aliquid*, then *Manes*, and the denial in the second half of the line, *letum non omnia finit*.\n\nThen the vision, and it is here that Propertius shows his genius for the concrete detail. Cynthia is not an idealised ghost but a body that has been burnt: her dress is scorched on one side, the fire has eaten into the beryl ring she always wore, and the water of Lethe, which the dead drink to forget, has worn away her lips. The repetition *eosdem... eosdem*, \"the same hair... the same eyes\", makes the horror more intimate: she is exactly as he remembers her, except where the fire has touched her. And then the gesture, *pollicibus fragiles increpuere manus*: her brittle hands crack, or snap their fingers, at him, a gesture of anger or contempt, and the word *fragiles* makes it horribly physical: these are the hands of a corpse.\n\nThe grammar is classic Propertius, and a good example of his compression. *Murmur ad extremae nuper humata uiae* means \"recently buried by the murmur of the furthest stretch of the road\": Roman tombs lined the roads out of the city, and the \"murmur\" is the noise of the traffic on it, a sad and slightly ironic place for a woman who loved the city's nightlife. *Cum mihi somnus ab exsequiis penderet amoris*: literally \"when sleep was hanging over me from the funeral of my love\", where *ab* means \"after\" and *amoris* stands for the beloved herself. *Quibus est elata capillos* is a relative clause with the antecedent *capillos* placed inside it, after the verb: \"the same hair with which she was carried out\". *Lateri*, a dative of place without a preposition, is poetic. *Beryllon* keeps its Greek accusative ending. And *sperande*, in Cynthia's first words, is a vocative gerundive: \"you, who are to be hoped for\", so *nec cuiquam melior sperande puellae* means \"you, from whom no girl should ever hope for anything better\". Then she accuses him of falling asleep so soon, and reminds him of their secret nights in the Subura, the noisy, \"sleepless\" district of Rome where she lived, and of the rope she used to climb down from her window into his arms. Grief, reproach, nostalgia and comedy, in eighteen lines: that is Propertius.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 2: Elegiae IV.8.49-70 -- Cynthia Returns\n\n**Source:** Propertius, *Elegiae*, Book IV, poem 8, lines 49-70, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/prop4.html). The source prints *Lygdame,nil* without a space after the comma; the space has been restored.\n\n**Context:** The very next poem in the book, and the strangest pairing in Latin poetry: in IV.7 Cynthia is a ghost, in IV.8 she is gloriously alive, and the poem is pure comedy. Cynthia has gone out of town to the festival of Juno at Lanuvium, riding in a carriage with a rich rival. Propertius decides to console himself, and invites two girls, Phyllis and Teia, to a little dinner party on the Esquiline, with his slave Lygdamus serving the wine, an Egyptian flute player, Phyllis on the castanets, and a dwarf clapping along to the music. It is a disaster: the lamps keep going out, the table collapses, he keeps throwing the worst possible score at dice, and he cannot stop thinking about Cynthia. \"They were singing to a deaf man, baring their breasts to a blind man: I was at the gates of Lanuvium.\" And then the door opens.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> cum subito rauci sonuerunt cardine postes,\n> et leuia ad primos murmura facta Laris.\n> nec mora, cum totas resupinat Cynthia ualuas,\n> non operosa comis, sed furibunda decens.\n> pocula mi digitos inter cecidere remissos,\n> palluerantque ipso labra soluta mero.\n> fulminat illa oculis et quantum femina saeuit,\n> spectaclum capta nec minus urbe fuit.\n> Phyllidos iratos in uultum conicit unguis:\n> territa uicinas Teia clamat aquas.\n> lumina sopitos turbant elata Quiritis,\n> omnis et insana semita nocte sonat.\n> illas direptisque comis tunicisque solutis\n> excipit obscurae prima taberna uiae.\n> Cynthia gaudet in exuuiis uictrixque recurrit\n> et mea peruersa sauciat ora manu,\n> imponitque notam collo morsuque cruentat,\n> praecipueque oculos, qui meruere, ferit.\n> atque ubi iam nostris lassauit bracchia plagis,\n> Lygdamus ad plutei fulcra sinistra latens\n> eruitur, geniumque meum protractus adorat.\n> Lygdame, nil potui: tecum ego captus eram.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nwhen suddenly the doorposts groaned on a creaking hinge,\nand faint murmurs were heard at the entrance of the house.\nIn an instant Cynthia flings both folding doors wide open,\nher hair not done, but beautiful in her fury.\nThe cup fell from between my slackened fingers,\nand my lips, loosened by the wine itself, went pale.\nHer eyes flash lightning, she rages as only a woman can,\nand the sight was no less than a city being sacked.\nShe hurls her angry nails at Phyllis's face;\nTeia, terrified, shouts \"Water!\" to the neighbours.\nLamps held high wake the sleeping citizens,\nand the whole street echoes with the madness of the night.\nThe two girls, their hair torn and their tunics undone,\nfind refuge in the first tavern of the dark street.\nCynthia exults in her spoils and runs back in triumph,\nand slashes my face with the back of her hand,\nleaves her mark on my neck, draws blood with her teeth,\nand above all strikes my eyes, which deserved it.\nAnd when she had tired her arms beating me,\nLygdamus, who was hiding behind the left leg of the couch,\nis dragged out, and, hauled forward, begs my guardian spirit to save him.\nLygdamus, I could do nothing: I had been taken prisoner along with you.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThis is Propertius in comic mode, and the comedy is built out of epic. Cynthia's entrance is a military assault: she throws the doors open (*resupinat*, \"flings back\", a vivid present), her eyes \"flash lightning\" (*fulminat*, a verb that belongs to Jupiter), and the scene is compared to *capta... urbe*, the sack of a city, the most tragic image in all of epic poetry. Then come the casualties: Phyllis's face, Teia's screams, the neighbours woken up, the two girls fleeing half-dressed to a tavern. And then the victor returns to deal with the real enemy: *Cynthia gaudet in exuuiis*, \"Cynthia exults in her spoils\", *exuuiae* being the spoils stripped from a defeated enemy, here the hair and clothes torn from her rivals. The whole passage is a mock-epic battle, and Propertius plays the part of the defeated city.\n\nThe details are wonderfully precise. *Non operosa comis, sed furibunda decens*: she had not taken any trouble over her hair, but was beautiful in her fury, an oxymoron that sums up Cynthia. Teia does not scream \"help\" but *uicinas... clamat aquas*, \"calls for water to the neighbours\": the cry Romans used in case of fire, because fire was the emergency everyone would answer, a very practical piece of Roman street life. The punishment is lovingly catalogued: a backhand to the face (*peruersa... manu*), a bite on the neck, and above all blows to the eyes, *qui meruere*, \"which deserved it\", because they were the ones that looked at the other girls. And the last couplet turns the whole scene into a joke at the poet's expense: poor Lygdamus, hiding behind the couch, is dragged out and begs his master for protection, and the master can only answer *tecum ego captus eram*, \"I had been taken prisoner along with you\". The great lover is no more free than his slave.\n\nGrammatically, look at the tenses: Propertius switches between historic presents (*resupinat*, *fulminat*, *conicit*, *clamat*, *turbant*, *excipit*, *gaudet*, *recurrit*, *sauciat*, *ferit*), perfects (*sonuerunt*, *cecidere*, *fuit*, *meruere*, *lassauit*) and pluperfects (*palluerant*, *captus eram*), which give the scene its breathless, jerky rhythm. Note the archaic or poetic forms *cecidere* and *meruere* (for *ceciderunt*, *meruerunt*), the Greek genitive *Phyllidos*, the accusatives plural in *-is* (*unguis*, *Quiritis*), the syncopated *spectaclum* for *spectaculum*, and the ablatives absolute *direptisque comis tunicisque solutis*. And then, after the excerpt, the peace treaty: Cynthia dictates her terms, fumigates the whole house to purify it, changes the sheets, and the two of them make up. In the very next lines, the poem ends with them in bed.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nIn antiquity Propertius was read, loved and imitated, especially by Ovid, whose own love elegies would be unthinkable without him. Martial wrote that Cynthia owed her fame to Propertius's poetry, but had given him no less fame in return. Then, for centuries, his difficulty and his damaged text kept him in the background. He was rediscovered by the humanists, and Petrarch read him, but he never became a school author like Virgil or Ovid: he was too hard.\n\nThe modern world has been kinder. Goethe, writing his *Roman Elegies* in the 1790s, took the Roman elegists, and Propertius above all, as his models, and made the genre live again in German. And in 1919 the American poet Ezra Pound published *Homage to Sextus Propertius*, a free and inventive adaptation that turned Propertius into a modern poet resisting empire and official culture. Classicists were horrified. One professor of Latin wrote that if Mr. Pound were a professor of Latin, there would be nothing left for him but suicide. Pound was not, and did not; his *Homage* is now considered one of the landmarks of modernist poetry, and it made Propertius famous among readers who would never open a Latin dictionary.\n\nToday he is widely regarded as the most original of the Roman elegists, and in many ways the most modern: a poet of obsession and irony, who writes about love not as a pleasant pastime but as a kind of madness that takes over a whole life, and who laughs at himself even while he suffers.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: A (Very Difficult)**\n\nPropertius is high in almost every criterion. His mythological and Alexandrian allusions are the densest of any elegist; his syntax is compressed, abrupt and full of unannounced shifts of speaker, addressee and tone; his use of words and cases is bold; and his text is so damaged that some passages are uncertain even for experts. Compared with Tibullus, he is two full tiers harder: Tibullus drifts, Propertius leaps, and he expects you to leap with him. He stops short of S only because, unlike Horace in the *Odes*, his word order is not built as a deliberate puzzle in every line, and because his narrative poems, like the two excerpts here, carry you along with their energy. Upper A, then, and knocking on S's door.\n\n---\n\n# PUBLIUS OVIDIUS NASO -- OVID (43 BC -- 17/18 AD)\n\n## Biography\n\nOvid is the youngest of the great Augustan poets, the only one born after the death of Caesar, and the only one who never knew the civil wars as an adult. That shows. Virgil and Horace had lived through the end of the Republic and wrote about peace as something won at a terrible price; Ovid grew up in the peace, took it for granted, and spent it having fun. For twenty years he was the most fashionable poet in Rome: witty, charming, prolific, endlessly inventive, adored by the public and imitated by everyone. Then, in AD 8, at the height of his fame, Augustus sent him to the end of the world, and he never came back. Few lives in literature have such a sharp turn in the middle.\n\n**Born the year the Republic died.** Publius Ovidius Naso was born on 20 March 43 BC at Sulmo, modern Sulmona, a small town in the mountains of the Abruzzo, \"rich in ice-cold streams\", as he describes it. He was born in a symbolic year, and he knew it: it was the year, he says, \"when both consuls fell by the same fate\", that is, Hirtius and Pansa, killed at Mutina. His family was old and of equestrian rank, comfortably off. He had a brother exactly one year older, born on the same day of the year, so that, as Ovid puts it, the family celebrated a single birthday with two cakes; the brother died at twenty. *Naso* is a family cognomen meaning \"nose\", and Ovid uses it constantly to refer to himself, so that the most fashionable poet in Rome went through life calling himself, more or less, \"Big Nose\".\n\n**The father who wanted a lawyer.** His father sent both boys to Rome to be trained in rhetoric for a political career. The brother was a natural orator; Ovid was a natural poet, and his father despaired. \"Why do you try a useless pursuit?\", he would say. \"Even Homer left no money.\" Ovid tried to obey. He gave up verse and attempted to write prose, but, in the most famous line of his autobiography, \"whatever I tried to write came out as verse\". The elder Seneca, who heard him declaim as a young man in the schools of rhetoric, confirms it: even then, he says, Ovid's prose speeches \"seemed to be nothing other than poetry let loose\". Seneca also tells a wonderful story about his stubbornness. His friends once asked him to remove three lines from his poems that they considered too much; he agreed, on condition that he could name three lines that were exempt. Each side wrote its three lines secretly on a tablet, and when the tablets were opened, the lines were the same. Ovid used to say that a face is more beautiful with a mole.\n\n**No career, thank you.** He began the career his father wanted: he held a couple of junior judicial posts, and was entitled to stand for the Senate. Then he refused. He did not have the body or the temperament for public life, he says, and he was afraid of ambition. Instead he travelled, to Athens, to the cities of Asia Minor and to Sicily, with the poet Macer, and came back to Rome to do nothing but write.\n\n**The most fashionable man in Rome.** And Rome loved him. He began to recite his poems in public when he was barely past adolescence, with his beard shaved for the first or second time, and his first love poems, about a mistress called Corinna, made him famous almost overnight: everyone in Rome, he says, was trying to guess who Corinna was. He was friendly with Propertius, had heard Horace recite, and had seen Virgil, but only from a distance (*Vergilium vidi tantum*, \"I only saw Virgil\"); Tibullus died before they could become friends. By the time he was thirty he was the leading poet of the new generation, and the first generation of Romans who had never known anything but Augustus adored him. His poems were sung at parties, quoted in conversation, and even performed as ballets in the theatres. He married three times: the first two marriages ended quickly, the third, to a woman from a distinguished family, lasted the rest of his life, and she stayed loyal to him through everything that followed. He had a daughter, and grandchildren. He had a villa with gardens outside Rome. For twenty years his life was, by any standard, a success.\n\n**A poem and a mistake.** In AD 8, at the age of fifty, he was on the island of Elba with a friend when the news reached him: Augustus had personally ordered him to leave Rome and go to Tomis, a Greek town on the western shore of the Black Sea, modern Constanța in Romania, at the very edge of the empire. There was no trial and no Senate decree: it was the emperor's own decision, and technically a *relegatio*, so Ovid kept his citizenship and his property. He tells us the reason himself, in two words: *carmen et error*, \"a poem and a mistake\". The poem was the *Ars Amatoria*, published some eight years earlier, a cheerful manual of seduction that sat very badly with Augustus's laws on marriage and adultery. The mistake he never explained, except to say that he had seen something he should not have seen, and to compare himself to Actaeon, who was destroyed for accidentally seeing the goddess Diana naked. It was almost certainly connected to the scandal around Augustus's granddaughter Julia, exiled for adultery that same year. Before leaving, in despair, Ovid burned his own copy of the *Metamorphoses*, which was finished but not yet revised. Fortunately, as with Virgil and the *Aeneid*, his friends already had copies.\n\n**At the end of the world.** Tomis was everything Rome was not. It was cold: Ovid describes the Danube freezing so hard that carts could drive across it, and wine freezing so solid that it kept the shape of the jar after the jar was broken, so that people were served it not by the cup but by the chunk. It was dangerous: the town was regularly raided by the Getae and Sarmatians, horsemen who rode across the frozen river and shot poisoned arrows over the walls. And almost nobody spoke Latin. Ovid, the master of the Latin language, found himself the barbarian, unable to make himself understood: *barbarus hic ego sum, quia non intellegor ulli*, \"here I am the barbarian, because nobody understands me\". So he learned the local languages, Getic and Sarmatian, and eventually wrote a poem in Getic, in praise of Augustus, which he recited to the townspeople; they listened, murmured their approval, and shook their quivers full of arrows in applause.\n\n**Begging to come home.** For the rest of his life he wrote letters in verse to Rome, to his wife, to his friends, to Augustus himself, begging to be allowed home, or at least to be moved somewhere less terrible. Augustus died in AD 14 without relenting; Tiberius did not relent either. Ovid died in Tomis in AD 17 or 18, around the age of sixty, and was buried there. Today a statue of him stands in the main square of Constanța, looking out towards the sea. And in December 2017, two thousand years late, the city council of Rome passed a motion formally revoking his banishment.\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n- **Amores** (*Love Poems*, c. 20 BC, second edition c. 1 BC) -- three books of love elegies, mostly about a mistress called Corinna. They originally made up five books; Ovid cut them down to three for the second edition, and opens the collection with a little epigram in which the books themselves speak: \"We who were once Naso's five little books are now three: he preferred this to the old version. Even if you take no pleasure in reading us, at least the punishment will be two books lighter.\" The *Amores* take all the conventions of Roman elegy, from Gallus, Tibullus and Propertius (the lover as slave, the locked door, the rival, the jealous husband, the soldier of love) and play with them: Ovid is the first elegist who is clearly having more fun with the genre than he is suffering in it. In one poem he complains that he is in love with two women at once; in another he lectures a doorkeeper; in another he sends a parrot to its grave with a full funeral elegy. [Complete]\n- **Heroides** (*Heroines*) -- letters in elegiac couplets written by the abandoned women of mythology to the men who left them: Penelope to Ulysses, Dido to Aeneas, Ariadne to Theseus, Medea to Jason, Sappho to Phaon, and others, plus a later set of paired letters from lovers and their replies. Ovid claimed to have invented the genre. [Complete]\n- **Ars Amatoria** (*The Art of Love*, c. 2 BC -- AD 1) -- a parody of a didactic poem, in three books of elegiac couplets: Books I and II teach men how to find a girlfriend and how to keep her, and Book III, added afterwards, teaches women the same. It is written with the full apparatus of a serious instruction manual, like Virgil's *Georgics*, but its subject is where to pick up women in Rome (the theatre, the races, dinner parties, even the law courts), what to say, how to write letters, how to make your partner jealous, and how to behave when you are caught. It is very funny, very clever, and it was published while Augustus was trying to make adultery a criminal offence. It was followed by the **Remedia Amoris** (*Cures for Love*), a poem on how to fall out of love, and the **Medicamina Faciei Femineae** (*Cosmetics for the Female Face*), of which a hundred lines survive, with actual recipes for face packs. [Complete; the Medicamina fragmentary]\n- **Metamorphoses** (*Transformations*, c. AD 2-8) -- Ovid's masterpiece, an epic in fifteen books of hexameters, around 12,000 lines, telling some two hundred and fifty myths of transformation, from the creation of the world out of chaos to the transformation of Julius Caesar into a star. It is not a single story but a continuous chain of stories, each running into the next, often told by characters inside other stories, so that tales are nested inside tales. Gods turn humans into trees, animals, stones, birds, springs and stars, usually because of love, jealousy, pride or punishment. Its themes are the instability of everything, the power of desire, the cruelty and caprice of the gods, the dangers of artistic talent (Arachne, Marsyas), and the way bodies change while something of the self survives. And it ends with a proud prophecy that Ovid, through this poem, will live on and be read wherever Rome's power extends. [Complete]\n- **Fasti** (*The Calendar*, c. AD 2-8) -- a poem in elegiac couplets on the Roman calendar, explaining the origins of Roman festivals, rituals and anniversaries day by day. It was planned in twelve books, one for each month; only six (January to June) were written before the exile interrupted it. [Incomplete -- 6 of 12 books]\n- **Tristia** (*Sorrows*, AD 9-12) and **Epistulae ex Ponto** (*Letters from the Black Sea*, AD 12-16) -- nine books in all of elegies and verse letters written from exile, describing the journey, the cold, the barbarians and his loneliness, defending his poetry, and begging for pardon. Book IV of the *Tristia* contains his autobiography in verse, the source of most of what we know about his life. [Complete]\n- **Ibis** -- a long curse poem against an unnamed enemy in Rome, in the tradition of Callimachus, packed with obscure mythological punishments. [Complete]\n- **Medea** -- a tragedy, praised by Quintilian and Tacitus as proof of what Ovid could have done if he had controlled his talent. [Lost]\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nOvid is the easiest of the three great Augustan poets, and it is not close. The elder Seneca's verdict on his early speeches, \"poetry let loose\", works just as well in reverse: Ovid's poetry is the closest thing in Latin to prose written in verse. His syntax is light, logical and transparent. His sentences usually follow the natural order of thought, his clauses are short and balanced, and his couplets almost always contain a complete idea, with the hexameter making a point and the pentameter turning or completing it. Where Virgil weighs every word and Horace builds a mosaic, Ovid simply talks, beautifully and very fast. His hexameter in the *Metamorphoses* is the most fluent in Latin, full of dactyls and almost never held back by heavy spondees. **Syntactic complexity: LOW to MEDIUM.**\n\nThat does not mean he is without tricks, and the tricks are what make him a B rather than a C. He was trained in the schools of rhetoric, and it shows on every page: antithesis, paradox, wordplay, puns on names, epigrammatic endings, and a love of repeating a word in a slightly different form or sense. His learning is enormous, and the *Metamorphoses* in particular is full of rare Greek names, patronymics, epithets and allusions to myths that are told elsewhere, or not at all. He alludes constantly to Virgil, to the elegists, and to Greek poetry, often in order to parody them. And he is ironic almost everywhere, so that the translator always has to ask whether a line is serious or a joke. Quintilian, who admired him, put his finger on the problem: Ovid was \"too much in love with his own talent\", and could never resist one more clever turn. **Lexicon: MEDIUM; Stylistic figures: MEDIUM to HIGH; Allusion density: MEDIUM to HIGH.**\n\nThe result is a poet who is still very readable. After Virgil and Horace, Ovid feels almost easy, and that is exactly why he is worth reading: because the Latin does not stand in the way, you can actually follow what he is doing, enjoy the wit, and understand the serious things he says under the jokes, about love, about power, about art, and about how fragile everything is.\n\n## Latin Excerpts\n\n### Excerpt 1: Amores I.9.1-20 -- Every Lover Is a Soldier\n\n**Source:** Ovid, *Amores*, Book I, poem 9, lines 1-20, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/ovid/ovid.amor1.shtml). The dash after *uterque* in the source has been printed as a colon.\n\n**Context:** One of the great themes of Roman elegy is the *militia amoris*, the idea that love is a kind of military service. Tibullus and Propertius use it as a metaphor in passing; Ovid takes it and builds a whole poem out of it, a mock-philosophical proof, addressed to a friend called Atticus, that the lover and the soldier are exactly the same thing. Point by point, he compares them: their age, their courage, their sleepless nights, their long marches, their sieges and their spies. At the end of the poem he concludes that he himself used to be lazy, but love has made him an active man, and anyone who wants to stop being idle should fall in love. This is the first half of the argument. It is not exactly an unknown poem, but it is the best possible demonstration of how clearly Ovid's Latin is built.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Militat omnis amans, et habet sua castra Cupido;\n> Attice, crede mihi, militat omnis amans.\n> quae bello est habilis, Veneri quoque convenit aetas.\n> turpe senex miles, turpe senilis amor.\n> quos petiere duces animos in milite forti,\n> hos petit in socio bella puella viro.\n> pervigilant ambo; terra requiescit uterque:\n> ille fores dominae servat, at ille ducis.\n> militis officium longa est via; mitte puellam,\n> strenuus exempto fine sequetur amans.\n> ibit in adversos montes duplicataque nimbo\n> flumina, congestas exteret ille nives,\n> nec freta pressurus tumidos causabitur Euros\n> aptaque verrendis sidera quaeret aquis.\n> quis nisi vel miles vel amans et frigora noctis\n> et denso mixtas perferet imbre nives?\n> mittitur infestos alter speculator in hostes;\n> in rivale oculos alter, ut hoste, tenet.\n> ille graves urbes, hic durae limen amicae\n> obsidet; hic portas frangit, at ille fores.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nEvery lover is a soldier, and Cupid has a camp of his own:\nbelieve me, Atticus, every lover is a soldier.\nThe age that is fit for war is also suited to Venus:\nan old soldier is a disgrace, an old lover is a disgrace.\nThe spirit that generals look for in a brave soldier\nis what a pretty girl looks for in the man who shares her bed.\nBoth stay awake all night; both rest on the bare ground:\none guards his mistress's door, the other his general's.\nA soldier's duty is the long march; send the girl away,\nand her lover will follow her tirelessly, to the ends of the earth.\nHe will climb the mountains in his way, cross rivers swollen with rain,\nand tramp through the drifted snow;\nabout to set sail, he will not plead the swollen east winds\nas an excuse, or wait for stars suited to skimming the waves.\nWho but a soldier or a lover will endure the cold of the night,\nand snow mixed with driving rain?\nOne is sent out as a scout against the hostile enemy;\nthe other keeps his eyes on his rival, as on an enemy.\nThe one besieges mighty cities, the other the threshold\nof a hard-hearted mistress; one breaks down gates, the other doors.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThis is the clearest possible illustration of Ovid's syntax. Almost every couplet contains one complete idea, and almost every idea is built on a parallel: soldier and lover, war and Venus, *ille* and *ille*, *alter* and *alter*, *hic* and *ille*. The poem opens and closes its first couplet on the same three words, *militat omnis amans*, a frame that announces the thesis like the title of a lecture. Then each couplet adds a point of comparison, in an order so logical that a student can almost predict the next line: age (3-4), courage (5-6), sleepless nights (7-8), long marches (9-14), endurance of cold (15-16), spying (17-18) and sieges (19-20).\n\nLook at how the parallels are built into the grammar. Line 4 is a perfect balance: *turpe senex miles, turpe senilis amor*, the same adjective twice, with the verb *est* left out both times. Lines 5-6 use a correlative pair, *quos... animos... hos*, \"the spirit that... this same\". Line 8 uses *ille... ille*, \"the one... the other\", with a single verb, *servat*, for both halves, and the genitives *dominae* and *ducis* placed in matching positions. And the last couplet is a small masterpiece of symmetry: *ille graves urbes, hic durae limen amicae / obsidet; hic portas frangit, at ille fores*. The verb *obsidet* serves both subjects, and the second half reverses the order of *ille* and *hic*, a chiasmus, ending on the most deflating word of all: *fores*, the door of a girl's house, against *portas*, the gates of a city.\n\nThere are a few things to watch. *Petiere* is the short form of *petiverunt*. *Exempto fine*, \"with the limit removed\", is an ablative absolute: \"without end\". *Pressurus*, a future participle, means \"about to press the sea\", that is, to sail. *Causabitur* is a deponent verb, \"to plead as an excuse\", from *causa*. And *verrendis... aquis*, \"for the waters that are to be swept\", uses a gerundive in the dative: the stars that sailors wait for are the ones suited to the voyage. But none of this gets in the way. That is the point: the Latin is so clear that the reader is free to enjoy the joke, which is that Ovid is applying the solemn Roman virtues of the soldier, discipline, endurance, courage, to a young man trying to get into his girlfriend's house.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 2: Ars Amatoria I.135-162 -- A Day at the Races\n\n**Source:** Ovid, *Ars Amatoria*, Book I, lines 135-162, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/ovid/ovid.artis1.shtml). The Latin Library capitalises the first word of every line, which has been kept.\n\n**Context:** In the first book of the *Ars Amatoria*, Ovid tells his male readers where in Rome they can find a girlfriend: in the porticoes and temples, in the theatres, at the triumphs, at dinner parties, at the seaside resort of Baiae, and here, at the chariot races in the Circus Maximus. The Circus is the perfect place, he explains, for one simple reason: in the theatre men and women sat in separate sections, but at the Circus they sat together, packed on the same benches. What follows is a step-by-step guide to making the most of it, written with the solemnity of a farming manual.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Nec te nobilium fugiat certamen equorum;\n> Multa capax populi commoda Circus habet.\n> Nil opus est digitis, per quos arcana loquaris,\n> Nec tibi per nutus accipienda nota est:\n> Proximus a domina, nullo prohibente, sedeto,\n> Iunge tuum lateri qua potes usque latus;\n> Et bene, quod cogit, si nolis, linea iungi,\n> Quod tibi tangenda est lege puella loci.\n> Hic tibi quaeratur socii sermonis origo,\n> Et moveant primos publica verba sonos.\n> Cuius equi veniant, facito, studiose, requiras:\n> Nec mora, quisquis erit, cui favet illa, fave.\n> At cum pompa frequens caelestibus ibit eburnis,\n> Tu Veneri dominae plaude favente manu;\n> Utque fit, in gremium pulvis si forte puellae\n> Deciderit, digitis excutiendus erit:\n> Etsi nullus erit pulvis, tamen excute nullum:\n> Quaelibet officio causa sit apta tuo.\n> Pallia si terra nimium demissa iacebunt,\n> Collige, et inmunda sedulus effer humo;\n> Protinus, officii pretium, patiente puella\n> Contingent oculis crura videnda tuis.\n> Respice praeterea, post vos quicumque sedebit,\n> Ne premat opposito mollia terga genu.\n> Parva leves capiunt animos: fuit utile multis\n> Pulvinum facili composuisse manu.\n> Profuit et tenui ventos movisse tabella,\n> Et cava sub tenerum scamna dedisse pedem.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nDon't miss the races of the noble horses, either:\nthe Circus, with room for a vast crowd, offers many advantages.\nThere is no need for fingers to talk in secret,\nnor do you need to catch a signal from her nods:\nsit right next to your lady, nobody will stop you,\nand press your side against hers as close as you can;\nand it is a good thing that the seating line forces you together, even if you didn't want it,\nthat by the rules of the place you simply have to touch the girl.\nHere look for a way to start a friendly conversation,\nand let small talk produce your first words.\nMake sure you ask, very keenly, whose horses are coming in,\nand without a moment's delay, whoever she supports, support him too.\nAnd when the crowded procession of ivory gods goes by,\nclap your hands enthusiastically for Lady Venus;\nand if, as happens, a speck of dust should fall\non the girl's lap, you must brush it off with your fingers;\nand even if there is no dust, brush off the nothing:\nany excuse will do to be of service.\nIf her cloak is trailing too low on the ground,\ngather it up and carefully lift it from the dirty earth:\nstraight away, as the reward for your service, if the girl allows it,\nyour eyes will get a glimpse of her legs.\nKeep an eye, besides, on whoever is sitting behind you,\nso that he doesn't press his knee into her soft back.\nLittle things win light hearts: many have found it useful\nto plump up a cushion with a deft hand.\nIt has helped, too, to fan her with a thin writing tablet,\nand to slip a hollow footstool under her dainty foot.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThis passage is the *Ars Amatoria* in miniature: a didactic poem, with all the grammar of instruction, applied to a completely frivolous subject. Watch the verbs. Ovid uses every form of the imperative and the jussive that Latin possesses: the negative jussive subjunctive (*Nec te... fugiat*, \"don't let it escape you\"), the future imperative (*sedeto*, \"you shall sit\", *facito*, \"see to it\"), the ordinary imperative (*iunge*, *fave*, *plaude*, *excute*, *collige*, *effer*, *respice*), the jussive subjunctive (*quaeratur*, *moveant*, *sit*), and the gerundive of obligation (*accipienda... est*, *tangenda est*, *excutiendus erit*). It is the language of Cato's farming manual and of Virgil's *Georgics*, used to explain how to sit next to a girl.\n\nThe comedy is in the details, and the best line in the passage is one of the funniest in Latin: *Etsi nullus erit pulvis, tamen excute nullum*, \"even if there is no dust, brush off the nothing\". The word *nullum*, used as a noun, \"nothing\", turns the whole couplet into a joke about pretexts, and the next line states the moral with perfect seriousness: *Quaelibet officio causa sit apta tuo*, \"let any reason be good enough for your service\". Notice also how the lover is told to support whichever team she supports (*quisquis erit, cui favet illa, fave*, with the play on *favet... fave*), to applaud Venus when the statues of the gods are paraded round the track before the races, and to protect her from the knees of the man behind. The whole passage is a portrait of Roman social life, the noise, the crowd, the dust, the cushions and the footstools, seen through the eyes of a very attentive young man.\n\nGrammatically, it is all clear and straightforward, with just a few points to watch: *capax populi*, \"capable of holding the people\", with an objective genitive; *Et bene, quod...*, \"and it's a good thing that...\", an idiom; *linea*, the line marking the seats; the conditional *si... deciderit*, a future perfect, \"if it should have fallen\"; *officii pretium*, in apposition, \"as the price of your service\"; and *videnda*, a gerundive used almost as an adjective, \"legs to be seen\". Note too the perfect infinitives with *fuit utile* and *profuit* (*composuisse*, *movisse*, *dedisse*), which give the advice the air of tried and tested experience.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 3: Metamorphoses VIII.823-842 and 875-878 -- The Hunger of Erysichthon\n\n**Source:** Ovid, *Metamorphoses*, Book VIII, lines 823-842 and 875-878, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/ovid/ovid.met8.shtml). The story is told by a character, and the source opens line 823 with a quotation mark that is closed elsewhere; it has been left out here. The dash at the end of line 878 has been printed as a full stop.\n\n**Context:** Erysichthon was a king of Thessaly who despised the gods. He took an axe to the sacred grove of Ceres, the goddess of grain, and cut down an ancient oak so holy that it was hung with offerings, killing the nymph who lived inside it. Ceres punished him with the cruellest possible sentence for an offence against the goddess of food: she sent Fames, Hunger personified, from the frozen wastes of Scythia, and Hunger breathed herself into him as he slept. The story is told, in Book VIII, by the river-god Achelous, to the hero Theseus. These lines describe what happens next: the dream, the waking, and the hunger that nothing can fill. In the lines between the two parts of the excerpt, Erysichthon eats his way through his whole fortune and then sells his own daughter to buy food; she escapes by changing her shape, so he sells her again and again. The last four lines are the end of the story.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Lenis adhuc Somnus placidis Erysicthona pennis\n> mulcebat: petit ille dapes sub imagine somni,\n> oraque vana movet dentemque in dente fatigat,\n> exercetque cibo delusum guttur inani\n> proque epulis tenues nequiquam devorat auras;\n> ut vero est expulsa quies, furit ardor edendi\n> perque avidas fauces incensaque viscera regnat.\n> nec mora; quod pontus, quod terra, quod educat aer,\n> poscit et adpositis queritur ieiunia mensis\n> inque epulis epulas quaerit; quodque urbibus esse,\n> quodque satis poterat populo, non sufficit uni,\n> plusque cupit, quo plura suam demittit in alvum.\n> utque fretum recipit de tota flumina terra\n> nec satiatur aquis peregrinosque ebibit amnes,\n> utque rapax ignis non umquam alimenta recusat\n> innumerasque trabes cremat et, quo copia maior\n> est data, plura petit turbaque voracior ipsa est:\n> sic epulas omnes Erysicthonis ora profani\n> accipiunt poscuntque simul. cibus omnis in illo\n> causa cibi est, semperque locus fit inanis edendo.\n>\n> ...\n>\n> vis tamen illa mali postquam consumpserat omnem\n> materiam derantque gravi nova pabula morbo,\n> ipse suos artus lacerans divellere morsu\n> coepit et infelix minuendo corpus alebat.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nGentle Sleep was still soothing Erysichthon with his peaceful wings:\nin his dream he reaches for a banquet,\nmoves his mouth on nothing, grinds tooth against tooth,\nworks his throat, cheated by imaginary food,\nand instead of a feast devours, in vain, the thin air.\nBut when rest had been driven away, a raging desire to eat\ntakes over his greedy jaws and his burning entrails.\nAt once he demands everything that the sea, the earth and the air produce,\ncomplains of hunger with the tables laid before him,\nand in the middle of a feast looks for more feasts; what could have been enough\nfor whole cities, enough for an entire people, is not enough for one man,\nand the more he sends down into his belly, the more he wants.\nAnd as the sea receives the rivers of the whole earth\nand is never satisfied with water, and drinks up streams from distant lands,\nand as a greedy fire never refuses fuel\nand burns countless logs, and the more it is given\nthe more it wants, and grows hungrier from its very abundance:\nso the mouth of impious Erysichthon receives every feast\nand demands more at the same time. All food, in him,\nis a cause for more food, and by eating he is always making empty space.\n\n...\n\nBut when the violence of his disease had consumed all\nhis substance, and there was no new fodder for his grievous sickness,\nhe began to tear his own limbs apart with his teeth,\nand, wretched man, fed his body by consuming it.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThis is the *Metamorphoses* at its most unsettling, and a good example of how Ovid's clarity can be used for horror as well as for comedy. The dream comes first, and it is a cruel joke: Erysichthon eats in his sleep, moves his empty mouth, grinds his teeth together and swallows air. Every verb is physical (*movet*, *fatigat*, *exercet*, *devorat*), and every object is empty (*ora vana*, *cibo inani*, *tenues... auras*). Then he wakes up, and the hunger \"reigns\" (*regnat*) in his jaws and entrails, like a tyrant.\n\nThe central section is built on two devices Ovid loves. The first is paradox, stated in the most epigrammatic form possible: *inque epulis epulas quaerit*, \"in the middle of feasts he looks for feasts\", with the same word repeated in two cases (polyptoton); *plusque cupit, quo plura... demittit*, \"and he wants more, the more he sends down\", a correlative with *quo* and a comparative; and finally *cibus omnis in illo / causa cibi est, semperque locus fit inanis edendo*, \"all food in him is the cause of food, and by eating he always makes empty space\". The second device is the epic simile, and here Ovid gives us two in a row, the sea that swallows all the rivers of the world and is never full, and the fire that burns more fiercely the more wood you give it, each introduced by *utque*, \"and as\", and resolved by *sic*, \"so\". They are borrowed from the grand style of Virgil, and applied to a man who cannot stop eating. Notice also the tricolon *quod pontus, quod terra, quod educat aer*, which runs through the three elements of the world to show that nothing in creation can fill him.\n\nThe last four lines are the end of the story, and its most famous paradox. When there is nothing left to eat, Erysichthon begins to eat himself: *ipse suos artus lacerans divellere morsu / coepit*, \"he began to tear his own limbs apart, ripping them with his bite\". And the final line is a perfect Ovidian epigram: *infelix minuendo corpus alebat*, \"the wretched man fed his body by diminishing it\". The gerund *minuendo*, \"by diminishing\", against the verb *alebat*, \"he was feeding\", is a contradiction in two words. Grammatically the passage is straightforward: note *Erysicthona*, a Greek accusative; *derant*, a contracted form of *deerant*; and the historic presents in the dream (*petit*, *movet*, *fatigat*). The difficulty is not the grammar but the density of wit, which never lets up even in the most horrible moments. That, more than the transformations themselves, is the real Ovid.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nOvid is one of the clearest examples in this whole project of an author who had every reason to be forgotten. He was a pagan, he wrote poems that taught adultery, he mocked the gods, and he had been condemned by the emperor himself. And yet he became one of the most read, copied and imitated authors of the Christian Middle Ages. The twelfth and thirteenth centuries have even been called the *aetas Ovidiana*, the \"age of Ovid\". What saved him was reinterpretation. Christian readers learned to read the *Metamorphoses* as an allegory, in which each myth concealed a moral or religious truth: Phaethon falling from the sky could be read as Lucifer, Pyramus and Thisbe as Christ and the soul, and the transformations themselves as images of sin and redemption. In the fourteenth century a huge French poem, the *Ovide moralisé*, retold every myth in the *Metamorphoses* with a Christian moral attached, and a Latin version did the same for the schools. Even the *Ars Amatoria* was copied, studied and imitated, in the courtly love literature of the Middle Ages. A pagan poet of love and transformation became, against all odds, a moral teacher.\n\nHis influence on the Renaissance and on Western culture as a whole is almost impossible to exaggerate, and it comes above all from the myths of the *Metamorphoses*, which became the standard handbook of classical mythology for Europe. A few examples among hundreds:\n\n- **Dante** places him among the four great poets of antiquity in Limbo, and in the *Inferno* challenges him directly, telling Ovid to be silent, because Dante's own transformations of sinners outdo his.\n- **Chaucer** and **Boccaccio** retold his stories, and the *Heroides* shaped the medieval and Renaissance tradition of the abandoned woman's lament.\n- **Shakespeare** knew him intimately, probably through Arthur Golding's English translation of 1567: the comic play of *Pyramus and Thisbe* in *A Midsummer Night's Dream*, the narrative poem *Venus and Adonis*, and the tragedy *Titus Andronicus*, in which a copy of the *Metamorphoses* is actually brought on stage, all come from Ovid, and a contemporary wrote that the \"sweet witty soul of Ovid lives in mellifluous and honey-tongued Shakespeare\".\n- In painting and sculpture, **Titian**'s great mythological canvases for Philip II of Spain, which he called his *poesie*, **Bernini**'s *Apollo and Daphne* in the Galleria Borghese in Rome, and countless works by Botticelli, Correggio, Rubens and Velázquez are illustrations of the *Metamorphoses*.\n- In music, the very first operas, including **Monteverdi**'s *Orfeo*, drew on his myths, and so did centuries of opera after them.\n- In the modern world, the myth of Pygmalion became George Bernard Shaw's *Pygmalion* and then *My Fair Lady*; **Pushkin**, himself exiled to the shores of the Black Sea, wrote a poem to Ovid; and in 1997 the English poet **Ted Hughes** published *Tales from Ovid*, which became a bestseller.\n\nAnd yet, as with Virgil, fame has narrowed the man. Today most people who know Ovid's name know him as \"the Metamorphoses guy\": the poet of people turning into trees and birds. That is a great deal, but it is far from all of him. The *Amores* are the wittiest love poems in Latin; the *Ars Amatoria* is one of the funniest poems ever written and the reason he spent the rest of his life at the end of the world; the *Fasti* are a unique guide to Roman religion; and the *Tristia* and the letters from the Black Sea are among the first great literature of exile, written by a man who had lost everything except his language, and who kept writing in it, beautifully, until the end.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: B (Difficult)**\n\nOvid is the easiest of the three great Augustan poets, and much the easiest to enjoy. His syntax is light and logical, his couplets make one point at a time, his hexameters run fast, and his sentences follow the natural order of thought: his poetry is the closest Latin comes to prose in verse. What makes him a B rather than a C is everything he adds on top: rhetorical wit, paradox and wordplay on every page, a constant layer of irony, a huge stock of Greek names and mythological allusions, especially in the *Metamorphoses*, and a love of showing off that, as Quintilian said, he could never control. So he sits between Tibullus and Propertius: harder than the first, much easier than the second, and still very readable. And because he is readable, his messages come through: which is exactly why he deserves to be read for much more than the transformations.\n\n---\n\n# GAIUS JULIUS HYGINUS (c. 64 BC -- 17 AD)\n\n## Biography\n\nHyginus is the librarian of the Augustan Age, and the most frustrating kind of author for a project like this one: a man whose own books are almost all lost, and whose name is attached to two books he very probably did not write. But his life is a good story, and the books that go under his name are among the most useful and most charming handbooks of mythology to survive from antiquity.\n\n**A boy brought from abroad.** Almost everything we know about the real Hyginus comes from a short entry in Suetonius's *Lives of the Grammarians*. Gaius Julius Hyginus was a freedman of Augustus, which means he had been a slave of the imperial family before being freed; his name, Gaius Julius, is simply the name of his former master. Nobody could agree where he came from: most said he was a Spaniard, but others thought he was from Alexandria, and that Julius Caesar had brought him to Rome as a boy after the capture of the city in 47 BC. It is a fitting start for a man nobody can quite pin down.\n\n**The pupil of a walking encyclopedia.** In Rome he studied with Alexander of Miletus, a Greek scholar so learned that people called him *Polyhistor*, \"Know-It-All\", and some simply called him *Historia*, \"History\" itself. Hyginus became a scholar in the same mould: an antiquarian, a grammarian and a collector of facts about everything.\n\n**The emperor's librarian.** Augustus put him in charge of the Palatine Library, the great public library built next to the temple of Apollo on the Palatine Hill, beside the emperor's own house, with one hall for Greek books and one for Latin. It was one of the most prestigious posts a scholar could hold in Rome: imagine being made director of the national library, and having the head of state as your neighbour. He combined it with teaching, and had many pupils.\n\n**The man who corrected Virgil.** Hyginus wrote a commentary on Virgil, one of the very first, and he was not afraid to criticise him. Aulus Gellius reports that Hyginus pointed out historical and geographical mistakes in the *Aeneid*, and that he claimed to have consulted a copy of the *Georgics* that came \"from the house and family of Virgil\" himself, in order to settle a disputed reading. The first critic of the most perfect poet in Latin was the man who looked after the books.\n\n**Ovid's friend, and a poor man.** Suetonius adds that Hyginus was a very close friend of Ovid, and of the historian Clodius Licinus, who recorded that Hyginus died very poor, and that Licinus had supported him out of his own pocket for as long as he lived. Some scholars think that one of Ovid's letters from exile, addressed to an unnamed \"guardian and high priest of learned men\" in Rome, asking him to look after his books, was written to Hyginus; it would be exactly the right person to ask. The librarian of the emperor, the friend of poets, the teacher of many, died penniless. Some things about academic life never change.\n\n**The two books with his name.** All of Hyginus's genuine works are lost. What survives under the name of \"Hyginus\" are two handbooks: the **Fabulae**, a collection of short summaries of Greek myths, and the **De Astronomia**, a guide to the constellations and the myths behind them. Neither sounds like the work of a great Augustan scholar. Their Latin is plain and sometimes plainly wrong, and they contain mistakes that a learned man would hardly make: in the *Fabulae*, for instance, Deucalion and Pyrrha survive the great flood by escaping to Mount Etna in Sicily, rather than to Mount Parnassus. Most scholars think they were written much later, probably in the second century AD, by a schoolmaster or compiler who either was also called Hyginus or borrowed the famous name, perhaps drawing on the real Hyginus's lost works. So the books are \"Hyginus\" in the way that a modern textbook might carry the name of a famous editor long dead.\n\n**A manuscript cut into pieces.** The *Fabulae* came even closer to disappearing than most ancient texts. They survived in a single manuscript, written around AD 900 in southern Italian script and kept at Freising in Bavaria. In 1535 the German humanist Jacob Micyllus used it to print the first edition. And then, its job apparently done, the manuscript was taken apart, and its parchment leaves were reused to bind other books. Today only a few scraps of it survive, recovered from the bindings of volumes in Munich and Regensburg. For almost everything in the *Fabulae*, the first printed edition is now our only witness.\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n- **Lost works of the real Hyginus** -- a commentary on Virgil; books on the cities of Italy, on the Trojan families from which Roman clans claimed descent, on the nature of the gods, on agriculture and on bees (which Columella still used as authorities a generation later), on famous men, and collections of moral examples. [Lost, apart from quotations]\n- **Fabulae** (or **Genealogiae**) -- around 270 very short chapters retelling Greek myths, preceded by genealogies of the gods, and followed by lists, which are the most entertaining part: \"those who were nursed by animals\", \"the most beautiful young men\", \"women who killed their husbands\", \"men who killed their fathers\", \"the seven wonders of the world\", \"the inventors of things\", \"the greatest friendships\". It is, in effect, an ancient encyclopedia of mythology for students, and one of our most important sources for the plots of lost Greek tragedies. [Survives, through the first printed edition]\n- **De Astronomia** (also called the *Poeticon Astronomicon*) -- four books on astronomy and the constellations: their shapes, their stars and above all the myths that explain them, such as how the Great Bear was once the nymph Callisto, or why the Scorpion chases Orion across the sky. [Complete]\n- **De Munitionibus Castrorum** -- a technical manual on how to lay out a Roman army camp, attributed to \"Hyginus\" in the manuscripts but written in the imperial period by a land surveyor, and certainly not by our librarian. [Complete; spurious]\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nThe Latin of the *Fabulae* is the simplest in this whole chapter, and possibly in the whole project. It is a schoolbook: short sentences, a subject, a verb and an object, one event after another, with almost no ornament and no attempt at style. Most chapters follow the same pattern: \"X, son of Y and Z, did this; for this reason the gods did that; therefore he was turned into the other.\" For a student who has just finished the grammar, it is an ideal first text: the myths are familiar, the sentences are short, and every chapter can be finished in a few minutes. **Syntactic complexity: LOW; Stylistic figures: LOW.**\n\nThere are only two catches. The first is that the Latin is not always classical: the compiler sometimes uses forms and constructions a careful Augustan writer would have avoided, such as *Iovis* as the nominative of \"Jupiter\" instead of *Iuppiter*, and occasionally the sentences are clumsy or the facts are wrong. A student should not take everything in Hyginus as a model of good Latin. The second is the mythological names: hundreds of Greek gods, heroes, nymphs and monsters, often in Greek forms, which the reader needs to recognise or look up. **Lexicon: LOW to MEDIUM; Allusion density: MEDIUM, but every myth is explained.**\n\n## Latin Excerpt\n\n### Excerpt: Fabulae 220 -- Cura\n\n**Source:** Hyginus, *Fabulae* 220, *Cura*, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/hyginus/hyginus5.shtml)\n\n**Context:** Most chapters of the *Fabulae* retell myths that we know from other sources. This one is different: it is a little allegorical fable that survives nowhere else in ancient literature. Cura, \"Care\" or \"Worry\" personified, shapes the first human being out of clay, and then has to argue with Jupiter and Earth about who it belongs to. It is only a few lines long, it is written in the simplest Latin, and it has had an extraordinary afterlife: the German philosopher Martin Heidegger quoted it in full in his masterpiece *Being and Time* (1927), as evidence that the ancients already understood human existence as fundamentally a matter of care.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\nCura cum quendam fluvium transiret, vidit cretosum lutum, sustulit cogitabunda et coepit fingere hominem. Dum deliberat secum quidnam fecisset, intervenit Iovis; rogat eum Cura, ut ei daret spiritum, quod facile ab Iove impetravit. Cui cum vellet Cura nomen suum imponere, Iovis prohibuit suumque nomen ei dandum esse dixit. Dum de nomine Cura et Iovis disceptarent, surrexit et Tellus suumque nomen ei imponi debere dicebat, quandoquidem corpus suum praebuisset. Sumpserunt Saturnum iudicem; quibus Saturnus aequus videtur iudicasse: \"Tu, Iovis, quoniam spiritum dedisti, animam post mortem accipe; Tellus, quoniam corpus praebuit, corpus recipito. Cura quoniam prima eum finxit, quamdiu vixerit, Cura eum possideat; sed quoniam de nomine eius controversia est, homo vocetur, quoniam ex humo videtur esse factus.\"\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nWhen Care was crossing a certain river, she saw some clayey mud; deep in thought, she picked it up and began to shape a human being. While she was wondering what she had made, Jupiter came along. Care asked him to give it breath, and she easily obtained this from Jupiter. When Care wanted to give it her own name, Jupiter forbade it, and said that his own name should be given to it. While Care and Jupiter were arguing about the name, Earth rose up too, and kept saying that it should be given her name, since she had provided her own body for it. They took Saturn as their judge, and Saturn, it seems, judged fairly: \"You, Jupiter, since you gave it breath, shall receive its soul after death; Earth, since she provided its body, shall take back its body. Since Care was the first to shape it, let Care possess it for as long as it lives; but since there is a dispute about its name, let it be called *homo*, since it seems to be made from *humus*, the earth.\"\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThis is the *Fabulae* at their best, and almost all the grammar of a first-year Latin course is here, in the simplest possible form. There are *cum* clauses with the subjunctive (*cum... transiret*, *cum vellet*); *dum* clauses, one with the present indicative in the classical way (*dum deliberat*) and one, less classically, with the subjunctive (*dum... disceptarent*); an indirect question (*quidnam fecisset*); an indirect command after *rogat* (*ut ei daret spiritum*); indirect statements with a gerundive (*suumque nomen ei dandum esse dixit*) and with *debere*; a causal clause with *quandoquidem*; and then, in Saturn's verdict, three kinds of command one after the other: an ordinary imperative (*accipe*), a future imperative (*recipito*) and jussive subjunctives (*possideat*, *vocetur*). It is almost a grammar exercise in narrative form.\n\nIt also shows the oddities of the *Fabulae*'s Latin. Jupiter is called *Iovis* in the nominative three times, a late and popular form for the classical *Iuppiter*, although the ablative *ab Iove* is normal. Saturn begins by addressing Jupiter as \"you\" and then talks about Earth in the third person while apparently speaking to her (*Tellus, quoniam corpus praebuit, corpus recipito*). And the tenses wander between present and past (*rogat... impetravit*). None of this makes the passage hard; it just reminds us that this is not Cicero.\n\nThe fable itself is a small philosophical myth built on a pun. The human being is a mixture of three things, breath from the sky, a body from the earth, and the anxious care that shapes it, and each part goes back to its owner in the end: the soul to Jupiter, the body to Earth, while Care keeps the human being *quamdiu vixerit*, \"for as long as it lives\". And it is named *homo* because it is made from *humus*, earth: an etymology that ancient grammarians genuinely believed. Heidegger saw in it a profound idea, that human existence is defined by concern and worry from beginning to end, and he was not the first: Herder had turned it into a poem, and Goethe may have had it in mind for the figure of Care in *Faust*. Not bad for a few lines in a schoolbook.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nThe real Hyginus's influence survives mostly at second hand, through the later scholars who used his lost works, and through his place in the story of the Palatine Library and of the first criticism of Virgil. The books that go under his name had a much longer life. The *Fabulae* became one of the basic handbooks of mythology for the Middle Ages and the Renaissance, and they remain indispensable for modern scholars, because many of their summaries preserve the plots of Greek tragedies that are otherwise lost. The *De Astronomia* was one of the first classical scientific texts to be printed, in Venice in 1482, with woodcut illustrations of the constellations, and its pictures and myths shaped the way Renaissance artists and astronomers drew the sky: many of the figures in old star atlases go back, through it, to Hyginus. And the little fable of Cura, through Herder, Goethe and Heidegger, has had a career in modern philosophy that its unknown compiler could never have imagined.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: D (Good Exercise)**\n\nThe *Fabulae* are as easy as Latin prose gets: short sentences, simple syntax, a limited vocabulary and stories most readers already know. Their only obstacles are the many Greek mythological names and a handful of non-classical forms (*Iovis* for *Iuppiter*) that a student should not imitate. Like Augustus's *Res Gestae* at the other end of this chapter, they are a perfect warm-up text, and a pleasant way to learn mythology and grammar at the same time. A clear D.\n\n---\n\n# GRATTIUS (fl. late I century BC)\n\n## Biography\n\nGrattius is the most mysterious author in the whole Augustan Age, and possibly in this whole project. We know one fact about his life, and it comes from a single line of Ovid. Everything else we know, we have to read out of his poem.\n\n**One line in Ovid.** In one of his last letters from exile, Ovid lists the poets who were writing in Rome in his day, a roll-call of names, most of whom are now lost. In the middle of the list comes this line: *aptaque venanti Grattius arma daret*, \"and Grattius would give the hunter the weapons he needs\". That is all. It tells us that Grattius was a contemporary of Ovid, that he was known in Rome, and that he wrote about hunting. It does not tell us when he was born, where he lived or when he died. Even his name is uncertain: the manuscripts and editors spell it both *Grattius* and *Gratius*.\n\n**A Faliscan?** Editors traditionally call him *Grattius Faliscus*, \"Grattius the Faliscan\", and the nickname comes from his own poem. In a passage on the best flax for making hunting nets, he contrasts the Spanish flax of Saetabis with the flax grown by \"our Faliscans\" (*nostris... Faliscis*). The Falisci were the people of Falerii, modern Civita Castellana, a town about fifty kilometres north of Rome. So he may have been from there, or simply proud of the local linen. Either way, it is the only personal detail he lets slip in 541 lines.\n\n**The dog expert.** If there is one thing we do know about Grattius, it is that he loved dogs, and knew a great deal about them. His poem lists the hunting breeds of the known world with the enthusiasm of a modern dog-show judge: the Persian and Median hounds, the Gaulish dogs, the \"Petronian\" dogs, the Sycambrian dogs from the Rhine, the speedy Gaulish greyhound he calls the *Vertraha* (one of the earliest mentions of the Celtic sighthound, ancestor of the modern greyhound), the Umbrian dog with a great nose and no courage, the famous Molossian mastiffs from Greece, and, perhaps most memorably, the dogs of Britain, which are ugly but magnificent in a fight (that is the excerpt). He also explains how to breed them, feed them, train them and cure them of mange and rabies, including a recommendation to take a sick dog to a sulphurous volcanic cave in Sicily sacred to Vulcan.\n\n**A poem that stops mid-sentence.** His *Cynegetica* (\"On Hunting\") survives in 541 hexameters, and breaks off in the middle of a sentence about horses. It was probably longer, perhaps much longer; we do not know whether he ever finished it. It survived the Middle Ages in a single manuscript, copied around AD 800 in France and now in Vienna, which also contains a fragment of a poem on fishing attributed to Ovid. The Neapolitan poet Jacopo Sannazaro found it in France in the early 1500s and brought it back to Italy, and it was first printed in Venice in 1534. Before that, for some seven centuries, the only Roman hunting poet known to the world was a name in a list.\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n- **Cynegetica** (*On Hunting*, probably late I century BC) -- a didactic poem in hexameters, written in conscious imitation of Virgil's *Georgics*. It opens with an invocation to Diana, goddess of the hunt, and a brief history of how hunting began, when men learned to use reason and skill instead of bare strength. It then describes the hunter's equipment: nets and the different kinds of flax used to make them, snares, the *formido* (a long rope hung with brightly coloured feathers, used to frighten deer towards the nets), and spears. The longest section is on dogs: the breeds, their good and bad qualities, how to choose, breed and raise them, and how to treat their diseases. There is a moral digression against luxury and the excesses of the table, and the surviving text ends during a section on horses. [Fragmentary -- 541 lines, breaks off]\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nGrattius is not a great poet, and nobody has ever claimed he was. His model is obviously the *Georgics*, and he borrows Virgil's structures, his solemn invocations, his digressions and his tone, but without Virgil's music or his depth. His hexameters are correct but heavy, his transitions are abrupt, and he has a weakness for showing off his learning with obscure geographical and mythological references. And yet he is not easy.\n\nThe first difficulty is the **subject matter**. A poem on hunting equipment and dog breeding is full of technical terms: kinds of flax and linen, parts of nets and snares, types of spears, names of breeds, symptoms of diseases and the herbs and remedies used to cure them. Many of these words are rare, some appear only in Grattius, and their exact meaning is sometimes uncertain even to specialists. **Lexicon: HIGH.** The second is the **geography**: his dogs come from all over the world, and he names their homelands with learned poetic adjectives, Greek and Latin, that assume a reader who knows exactly where the Morini, the Sycambri, the Athamanes or the Acarnanians lived. **Allusion density: MEDIUM to HIGH.** The third is the **text**. Because it survives in a single manuscript, the *Cynegetica* is full of corruptions, gaps and doubtful readings; modern editions are dotted with angle brackets, where editors have supplied missing words, and with asterisks, where whole lines are lost. **Syntactic complexity: MEDIUM to HIGH; Stylistic figures: MEDIUM.**\n\n## Latin Excerpt\n\n### Excerpt: Cynegetica 171-181 -- The Dogs of Britain\n\n**Source:** Grattius, *Cynegetica*, lines 171-181, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/grattius.html). The source writes *Vmber* with a capital V for *Umber*, and *mavors* with a small m for the god Mars; both are kept as printed.\n\n**Context:** In the middle of his long catalogue of hunting dogs, Grattius has just described the Umbrian hound, famous for its nose, and now he turns his attention to the far north-west. The Morini were a Gallic people on the coast of the Channel, around modern Boulogne, and beyond their dangerous, tidal sea lay Britain, which Caesar had invaded twice only a generation before. Grattius's verdict on the dogs of Britain is one of the earliest descriptions of British dogs in literature. It is also a good example of his style: a slightly pompous Virgilian manner applied to a very practical piece of advice.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> at fugit adversos idem quos repperit hostis\n> Vmber: quanta fides, utinam, et sollertia naris,\n> tanta foret virtus et tantum vellet in armis!\n> quid, freta si Morinum dubio refluentia ponto\n> veneris atque ipsos libeat penetrare Britannos?\n> o quanta est merces et quantum impendia supra,\n> si non ad speciem mentiturosque decores\n> (parvis haec una est catulis iactura Britannis),\n> at magnum cum venit opus promendaque virtus\n> et vocat extremo praeceps discrimine mavors:\n> non tunc egregios tantum admirere Molossos,\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nBut the Umbrian dog runs away from the very enemies it has tracked down:\nif only it had as much courage, and as much will to fight,\nas it has loyalty and keenness of scent!\nWhat if you came to the straits of the Morini, where the sea ebbs and flows uncertainly,\nand chose to go as far as the Britons themselves?\nOh, how great the reward, how far beyond the cost,\nas long as you are not looking for good looks and deceptive charms\n(this is the one flaw of the little British dogs);\nbut when a great task comes, and courage must be shown,\nand the War-god calls you headlong into the final danger,\nthen you would not admire the famous Molossians so much,\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe passage begins with a wish and a complaint. The Umbrian dog is a superb tracker, but a coward: it runs away from the very animals it has found. *Quanta fides... et sollertia naris, / tanta foret virtus*: a correlative pair, *quanta... tanta*, \"as much... so much\", with *utinam* and the imperfect subjunctive *foret* (for *esset*) expressing an unreal wish in the present: \"if only it had as much courage as it has nose\". *Sollertia naris*, \"the skill of its nostril\", is a nicely technical touch.\n\nThen comes the journey to Britain, introduced with an epic flourish. *Freta... Morinum dubio refluentia ponto*, \"the straits of the Morini, flowing back and forth on an uncertain sea\", is a high-style description of the tides of the English Channel, which had famously caught Caesar's fleet by surprise. *Morinum* is a contracted genitive plural (for *Morinorum*), and *veneris* is a perfect subjunctive in the condition. The British dogs themselves are introduced with a frank judgement, set apart in parentheses: *parvis haec una est catulis iactura Britannis*, \"this is the one drawback of the little British dogs\", namely their lack of looks (*speciem mentiturosque decores*, \"appearance and charms that would lie to you\"). But, says Grattius, when real work begins, when *mavors* (an old poetic name for Mars, the war-god) calls you into the final danger, you will stop admiring the famous Molossians, the giant mastiffs from Epirus that were the luxury dogs of the Greek and Roman world. Note the potential subjunctive *admirere* (for *admireris*), \"you would admire\". The sentence goes on in the next lines, but the text there has a gap, which is why the excerpt ends with a comma.\n\nThe grammar here is not especially hard; what makes the passage demanding is the compression of the thought, the learned geographical vocabulary and the slightly overblown style, as if Grattius were describing a military campaign rather than a trip to buy a dog. It is a small, vivid window onto the ancient world: Roman hunters, a generation after Caesar, already knew that if you wanted a dog with real courage, you went to Britain.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nGrattius had very little influence. He is mentioned by Ovid, imitated perhaps by Nemesianus, who wrote another *Cynegetica* in the third century AD, and then forgotten for seven hundred years, until Sannazaro brought his manuscript to Italy. Since then he has been read mostly by specialists in Latin didactic poetry, by historians of hunting, and by historians of dogs, for whom his catalogue of breeds is a precious source. His importance is that of a rare survivor: a minor poet of the Augustan Age who shows what an ordinary, competent imitator of Virgil looked like, and who reminds us how many such poets must have been writing in Rome, all of them lost except for him, and except for the single line in which Ovid remembered that Grattius would give the hunter his weapons.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: B (Difficult)**\n\nGrattius survives in 541 lines, enough for a fair rating. He is not a subtle stylist, but he is a difficult read: dense technical vocabulary on nets, weapons, dog breeds and veterinary remedies, much of it rare or uncertain; learned geographical and mythological references; a heavy, compressed hexameter in imitation of the *Georgics*; and a text that survives in a single, damaged manuscript, with gaps and editorial supplements. He is easier than Virgil, because there is far less depth and far less art to unpack, but considerably harder than Tibullus or Ovid. A B, and an honest one.\n\n---\n\n# POMPEIUS TROGUS (fl. late I century BC -- early I century AD)\n\n## Biography\n\nPompeius Trogus is the great historian of the Augustan Age that almost nobody has read, for the simple reason that nobody can: his work is lost. What we have is a summary of it made centuries later by a man called Justin, who will get his own entry in a later era. Reading Trogus is a bit like reading a famous film through the review in a newspaper: the plot is there, the opinions are there, but the director's voice is mostly gone. And yet what survives of his life, and of his ideas, is surprisingly interesting.\n\n**A Gaul with a Roman name.** Trogus is the only Roman historian who tells us about his own family, and he did it, characteristically, at the very end of his last book. Justin summarises it: his ancestors came from the Vocontii, a Gallic people of Gallia Narbonensis, in what is now Provence, around Vaison-la-Romaine. His grandfather had received Roman citizenship from Pompey the Great during the war against Sertorius in Spain, and, as was the custom, took the name of his patron: that is why a Gaul is called *Pompeius*. His uncle commanded squadrons of cavalry under Pompey in the war against Mithridates, and his father served under Julius Caesar, as his secretary for letters and embassies, and keeper of his seal ring. So in three generations the family went from Gallic tribesmen to the private office of the most powerful man in Rome. Not bad for provincials.\n\n**A historian who looked away from Rome.** Everyone else in Rome wrote the history of Rome. Livy, his contemporary, was writing it from the foundation of the city in 142 books. Trogus decided to write the history of everybody else. His *Historiae Philippicae*, in 44 books, start with Ninus, the legendary first king of Assyria, and move through the empires of the Medes and the Persians, Greece, and above all the Macedonians of Philip and Alexander (hence the title, \"Philippic Histories\") and the Hellenistic kingdoms that came after them. Rome appears mostly from the outside, as the power that eventually swallows them all, and it gets its own narrative only near the end, in the books on its origins, on Gaul and on Spain, where the work ends with Augustus's final conquest of the Spanish tribes. A world history, written by a Gaul in Latin, in which Rome is the last arrival: it is the most original idea in Augustan historiography.\n\n**A bad influence?** Trogus drew heavily on Greek historians, and one of them may have been Timagenes of Alexandria, a brilliant and sharp-tongued Greek who lived at Rome and wrote a history of the world's kings. Timagenes had a famous problem with Augustus: he could not stop making jokes about the emperor and his family. Augustus banned him from his house, and Timagenes, out of spite, publicly burned the books he had written about Augustus's reign. Whether Trogus really used him is debated, but it would explain why his history is so full of the voices of Rome's enemies.\n\n**The man who hated invented speeches.** Ancient historians loved to put long speeches in the mouths of their characters, in direct speech, as if they had taken them down word for word. Livy and Sallust filled their works with them. Trogus thought this was dishonest: Justin tells us that he criticised Livy and Sallust because, by inserting direct speeches and passing off their own eloquence as that of their characters, they had gone beyond the proper limits of history. So when Trogus did include a great speech, he wrote it in indirect speech. Justin admired that one so much that he copied it out almost in full: it is the speech of Mithridates, king of Pontus, to his soldiers before his war against Rome, and it is one of the longest passages of indirect speech in Latin literature, and one of the most violent attacks on Rome ever written by a Roman. That is the excerpt.\n\n**Also an expert on animals.** Trogus also wrote a work on animals and plants, now lost, and Pliny the Elder quotes him several times as an authority on zoology. An historian of empires and a naturalist: the Augustan Age liked its writers versatile.\n\n**Lost, and saved by a summary.** Some time between the second and fourth centuries AD (the date is disputed), Justin produced his *Epitome*, cutting Trogus's 44 books down to a collection of what he himself calls the most pleasant and most instructive parts, a little \"body of flowers\". It was a huge success, and it killed the original: once readers had the short version, nobody bothered to copy the long one. The *Epitome* was read in every medieval school, and survives in some two hundred manuscripts. Of Trogus himself we have only Justin's text, the *prologi* (short summaries of the contents of each of the 44 books, which may go back to Trogus's own edition), and a few quotations. It is the most successful act of destruction-by-abridgement in Latin literature.\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n- **Historiae Philippicae** (*Philippic Histories*) -- a world history in 44 books, from the Assyrian empire to the age of Augustus, centred on Macedon and the Hellenistic kingdoms, with Rome treated mostly from the outside. Survives through Justin's *Epitome* and the *prologi*. [Lost; abridged]\n- **On Animals** (and possibly on plants) -- a work of natural history cited by Pliny the Elder. [Lost]\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nHere we have to be honest: any rating of Trogus is a rating of Justin. The Latin we read is Justin's, though he often keeps Trogus's sentences, especially in the speeches and the moral comments. The result is clear, readable narrative prose: mostly classical in grammar and vocabulary, with long but well-built periods, frequent moralising sentences in the manner of Sallust, and a fondness for antithesis and neat endings. Some later features creep in, and the condensation sometimes leaves gaps in the logic of the story, but on the whole it is easier than Livy, and much easier than Sallust. **Lexicon: LOW to MEDIUM; Syntactic complexity: MEDIUM.**\n\nThe real challenge is the one Trogus chose himself: **indirect speech**. When a whole speech is reported in *oratio obliqua*, every main verb becomes an infinitive, every subordinate verb becomes a subjunctive, every \"I\" becomes *se* or *sibi* and every \"you\" becomes *illi* or *ipsi*, and the reader has to rebuild the original speech in his head as he translates. It is one of the best exercises in Latin syntax, and the Mithridates speech is the classic example. **Stylistic figures: MEDIUM; Allusion density: MEDIUM** (the history of half the ancient world, with names to match).\n\n## Latin Excerpt\n\n### Excerpt: Justin, Epitome 38.6.7-8 -- The Sons of the She-Wolf\n\n**Source:** Justin, *Epitome of the Philippic Histories of Pompeius Trogus*, 38.6.7-8, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/justin/38.html). Section number 8 inside the passage has been removed.\n\n**Context:** In 88 BC Mithridates VI, king of Pontus, is about to begin his first war against Rome. He gathers his army and makes a long speech, which Trogus reports in indirect speech and Justin preserves almost entire. Mithridates has just listed the kings that Rome has used and then betrayed (Eumenes of Pergamum, Masinissa of Numidia), and now he explains why the Romans hate all kings: because their own kings were so embarrassing. Every Roman reader knew the seven kings of Rome; Mithridates turns the proud legend of the city upside down, one king at a time.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\nHanc illos omnibus regibus legem odiorum dixisse, scilicet quia ipsi tales reges habuerint, quorum etiam nominibus erubescant, aut pastores Aboriginum, aut aruspices Sabinorum, aut exules Corinthiorum, aut servos vernasque Tuscorum, aut, quod honoratissimum nomen fuit inter haec, Superbos; atque ut ipsi ferunt conditores suos lupae uberibus altos, sic omnem illum populum luporum animos inexplebiles sanguinis, atque imperii divitiarumque avidos ac ieiunos habere.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\n(He said that) they had laid down this law of hatred against all kings, obviously because they themselves had had kings whose very names made them blush: either shepherds of the Aborigines, or soothsayers of the Sabines, or exiles from Corinth, or slaves and house-born servants of the Etruscans, or, the most honourable name among them, the Proud; and just as they themselves say that their founders were suckled at the teats of a she-wolf, so that whole people had the souls of wolves, insatiable for blood, and greedy and hungry for power and wealth.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThis is a perfect example of *oratio obliqua*. There is no verb of saying in the passage, because the *ait* that governs it came several sections earlier: everything depends on it. So the main verbs are infinitives (*dixisse*, *habere*), with their subjects in the accusative (*illos*, *omnem illum populum*), and the verbs of the subordinate clauses are in the subjunctive (*habuerint*, *erubescant*). The pronouns follow the rules too: *illos* and *ipsi* are the Romans, seen from Mithridates' point of view. To translate it well, a student has to supply \"he said that\" and then decide how much of the indirect construction to keep in Italian or English. One small trap: *quod honoratissimum nomen fuit* is in the indicative, because it is a comment by the narrator, not part of Mithridates' speech.\n\nThe list of kings is a little masterpiece of insult, and every item is a real Roman king described in the worst possible way. The \"shepherds of the Aborigines\" are Romulus and Remus, raised by shepherds; the \"soothsayers of the Sabines\" is Numa Pompilius, the pious Sabine king; the \"exiles from Corinth\" is Tarquinius Priscus, son of Demaratus, a Corinthian refugee; the \"slaves of the Etruscans\" is Servius Tullius, said to be the son of a slave woman; and the last are the Tarquins, whose most famous member was called *Superbus*, the Proud. The sentence then ends with the most famous symbol of Rome, the she-wolf, and turns it into an accusation: a people raised on wolf's milk has the soul of a wolf. Note the *ut... sic* comparison, and the ending, *avidos ac ieiunos*, \"greedy and starving\", with the genitives *imperii divitiarumque* depending on both adjectives. A Gaul whose family owed everything to Pompey and Caesar gave this speech to Rome's greatest enemy, and gave it his best prose: that is either remarkable fairness or remarkable courage.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nThrough Justin, Trogus had an enormous influence on the way the Middle Ages and the Renaissance saw ancient history outside Rome. The *Epitome* was the standard handbook for the history of the Assyrians, the Persians, the Greeks and the Macedonians; it was read by Orosius and by Augustine, quoted throughout the Middle Ages, and used by Renaissance writers looking for good stories and moral examples. Many of the most famous anecdotes about Alexander the Great, the Scythians, the Amazons or the foundation of Marseille reached European culture through this book. And the speech of Mithridates, with its picture of Rome as a nation of wolves, has been quoted ever since by everyone who wanted to criticise an empire. The irony is that Trogus is famous through the very book that caused his work to disappear.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: C (Manageable)**\n\nWhat we read of Trogus is clear, classical narrative prose, filtered and simplified by Justin, and easier than Livy or Sallust. Two things lift it above a D: the condensation, which sometimes makes the story jump or the connections unclear, and the long passages of indirect speech, which are an excellent but demanding test of syntax. A solid C, and one of the best places to practise *oratio obliqua*.\n\n---\n\n# MARCUS MANILIUS (fl. early I century AD)\n\n## Biography\n\nManilius is the last author of the Augustan Age, and in some ways the strangest. He wrote the longest surviving poem on astrology in the ancient world, a work of real power and occasional brilliance; and yet no ancient writer ever mentions him. Not Ovid, who mentions everybody; not Quintilian, who lists the Latin poets; not Pliny. He is a man who claimed to read the future in the stars, and could not secure his own.\n\n**A poet without a biography.** We do not even know his name for certain: the manuscripts call him Manilius, Manlius or Mallius, and the first name Marcus is not secure either. Everything we know comes from the poem itself. He addresses an emperor, Caesar, as the \"prince of his country\" and son of a god; he mentions the disaster of Varus, when three legions were destroyed in Germany in AD 9, as a recent event. So he wrote at the end of Augustus's reign, and probably finished the poem, or failed to finish it, under Tiberius. Some scholars, noting a few un-Roman turns in his Latin, have suggested he was a foreigner; others that he was a freedman, like Hyginus. Nobody knows.\n\n**Astrology, the fashion of the age.** Manilius chose his subject well. Astrology was the great intellectual fashion of the early Empire. Augustus, according to Suetonius, published his own horoscope and struck coins with the sign of Capricorn, which he considered his lucky sign; Manilius duly mentions Capricorn shining on the birth of Augustus. Tiberius, the next emperor, was even more devoted: he lived on Capri with his personal astrologer, Thrasyllus. A poem showing that the stars rule everything, and that the emperor's rule was written in the sky, was exactly what the palace wanted to hear.\n\n**The anti-Lucretius.** Manilius is proud, and he tells us so: he is the first, he says, to bring the knowledge of the heavens into Latin song. His great rival is Lucretius, whom he imitates constantly and contradicts on every point. Lucretius had taught that the universe is made of atoms moving by chance, and that the gods do not interfere; Manilius, a Stoic, teaches that the universe is a single living body governed by divine reason, and that everything, from the fall of empires to the job of the man next door, is decided by fate. If Lucretius wanted to free men from fear of the gods, Manilius wants to free them from anxiety about the future, by telling them that it is already decided anyway. That is the first excerpt.\n\n**Horoscopes as comedy.** The poem's most entertaining parts are the descriptions of the characters of people born under each constellation, which read like a Roman comedy of types. The man born under the Ram becomes a wool merchant; under the Ship, a sailor; under the Charioteer, a circus driver or a trick rider. And whoever is born under Orion will be a busybody, rushing around the city at dawn, all over everyone's doorstep, with the same single word of greeting for everybody, \"the friend of all\". Every reader in Rome must have recognised someone.\n\n**An unfinished heaven.** The poem has five books, about 4,200 lines. Book I describes the universe and the constellations; Books II and III the technical apparatus of astrology, the signs and their relations, the houses, the lots and the calculation of the moment of birth; Book IV the characters given by the signs of the zodiac; Book V the constellations that rise alongside the zodiac and the people they produce. Manilius promises more than once to treat the planets, which are essential to any horoscope, but never does. Either the poem is unfinished, or a part of it is lost. In both cases, the astrologer ran out of time.\n\n**Found again, and edited by a poet.** Manilius disappeared for most of the Middle Ages, and was found again in 1417 by Poggio Bracciolini, the great Italian manuscript hunter, during the Council of Constance (the same man who rediscovered Lucretius). His text is so corrupt, and so technical, that it became the great proving ground of Latin textual criticism. Joseph Scaliger and Richard Bentley edited him; and then A. E. Housman, the English poet of *A Shropshire Lad*, spent almost thirty years on him, publishing his edition in five volumes from 1903 to 1930. Housman did not think much of Manilius as a poet, but he thought even less of earlier editors, and his prefaces are famous for some of the most savage and funniest insults in the history of scholarship. One of the mildest: an editor with no judgment, faced with two manuscripts, is like a donkey between two bundles of hay. A minor didactic poet thus became the reason for one of the great masterpieces of philology, and the subject of the best-written scholarly rudeness in English.\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n- **Astronomica** (written c. AD 9-20) -- a didactic poem in five books of hexameters on astronomy and astrology: the structure of the universe and the constellations (I), the zodiac and the technical system of the horoscope (II and III), the characters and destinies given by the signs of the zodiac (IV), and the constellations rising with the signs and the people born under them (V), with mythological episodes such as Andromeda and Perseus, and philosophical passages on fate and divine reason. The promised treatment of the planets is missing. [Survives; probably incomplete]\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nManilius is one of the hardest poets in Latin, and the reason is that he tries to do two impossible things at once. The first is to put mathematics into verse. Large parts of Books II and III are calculations: degrees of the signs, divisions of the circle, the rising times of the constellations, all expressed in hexameters, which have no room for numbers. Manilius loves to show off by turning \"thirty degrees\" into periphrases like \"three times ten parts\", and the reader has to do the arithmetic and know the astrology at the same time. **Lexicon: HIGH** (technical astrology, Greek terms in Latin dress); **Allusion density: HIGH**.\n\nThe second is to be brilliant. When Manilius is not counting, he is writing philosophy, satire or myth, and here he is one of the most pointed poets after Ovid: short, epigrammatic sentences, paradoxes, antitheses, plays on words, chiasmus, a sentence that turns on its last word. It is the style of the coming Silver Age, a generation early. Add a vocabulary stretched to cover a subject it was never made for, a very compressed syntax, frequent hyperbaton, and a text which survives in poor manuscripts and has been emended in hundreds of places, and the result is, frankly, a nightmare. **Syntactic complexity: VERY HIGH; Stylistic figures: HIGH.** The philosophical and mythological passages are where a student should start; the technical ones are for specialists only.\n\n## Latin Excerpts\n\n### Excerpt 1: Astronomica 4.1-16 -- Why Do We Waste Our Lives?\n\n**Source:** Manilius, *Astronomica* IV, lines 1-16, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/manilius4.html)\n\n**Context:** Book IV opens with a philosophical prelude, before the long description of the characters given by the signs of the zodiac. It is the passage in which Manilius explains why astrology should bring peace of mind rather than fear: if everything is already decided by fate, it is pointless to waste our life in anxiety and greed. It reads like a Stoic sermon, and it ends with one of the most famous lines in Latin poetry.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> Quid tam sollicitis vitam consumimus annis\n> torquemurque metu caecaque cupidine rerum\n> aeternisque senes curis, dum quaerimus, aevum\n> perdimus et nullo votorum fine beati\n> victuros agimus semper nec vivimus umquam,\n> pauperiorque bonis quisque est, quia plura requirit\n> nec quod habet numerat, tantum quod non habet optat,\n> cumque sibi parvos usus natura reposcat\n> materiam struimus magnae per vota ruinae\n> luxuriamque lucris emimus luxuque rapinas,\n> et summum census pretium est effundere censum?\n> solvite, mortales, animos curasque levate\n> totque supervacuis vitam deplete querellis.\n> fata regunt orbem, certa stant omnia lege\n> longaque per certos signantur tempora casus.\n> nascentes morimur, finisque ab origine pendet.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nWhy do we consume our lives in such anxious years,\ntormented by fear and by blind desire for things,\nand, grown old in endless worries, lose our time\nwhile we search for it, and, never happy, since our wishes have no end,\nwe always behave as if about to live, and never live at all?\nEach man is the poorer for his riches, because he wants more,\nand does not count what he has, but only longs for what he has not;\nand though nature asks for herself only small needs,\nwe pile up with our wishes the material for a great collapse,\nand with our profits we buy luxury, and with luxury, plunder,\nand the highest reward of wealth is to squander wealth?\nFree your minds, mortals, lighten your cares,\nand empty your life of so many useless complaints.\nThe fates rule the world, all things stand fixed by a sure law,\nand the long ages are marked by sure events.\nAs we are born, we die, and the end hangs from the beginning.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe whole first part is a single rhetorical question, eleven lines long, held together by *-que* and *et*, which a student has to follow to the question mark at the end of line 11. Manilius packs it with paradoxes in the Stoic manner: we lose time while we look for it (*dum quaerimus, aevum / perdimus*); we are always \"about to live\" and never live (*victuros agimus semper nec vivimus umquam*, with *victuros* the future participle of *vivo*, not of *vinco*: a classic trap); each man is poorer because of his riches; the greatest value of wealth is to waste it (*summum census pretium est effundere censum*, with the same word, *census*, in two different cases at the two ends of the sentence). *Nullo votorum fine beati* is the hardest phrase: literally \"happy with no end of wishes\", that is, people who could be happy but whose wishes never stop; editors and translators still argue about it.\n\nThen the tone changes: the imperatives *solvite*, *levate*, *deplete* give the cure, and lines 14-16 give the reason, in three short, absolute statements. The last line is pure Manilius: *nascentes morimur*, \"we die as we are born\", two words that contain a whole philosophy, and *finisque ab origine pendet*, \"and the end hangs from the beginning\", in which the image is of a chain: the moment of birth, read in the stars, already contains the moment of death. It is both a consolation and a rather chilling thought, and that is exactly the ambiguity of astrology.\n\n---\n\n### Excerpt 2: Astronomica 5.567-576 -- Perseus Sees Andromeda\n\n**Source:** Manilius, *Astronomica* V, lines 567-576, from *The Latin Library* (thelatinlibrary.com/manilius5.html)\n\n**Context:** Book V describes the constellations that rise alongside the signs of the zodiac. When he reaches Andromeda, Manilius cannot resist telling her story, and it becomes the longest and most beautiful narrative episode of the poem. Andromeda, daughter of Cepheus and Cassiope, has been chained to a rock as a sacrifice to a sea monster to atone for her parents' guilt. Manilius has just described her, beautiful even in her suffering, with the birds and the sea nymphs weeping for her. Now the hero arrives, flying home after killing Medusa.\n\n**Latin Text:**\n\n> tandem Gorgonei victorem Persea monstri\n> felix illa dies redeuntem ad litora duxit.\n> isque, ubi pendentem vidit de rupe puellam,\n> deriguit, facie quem non stupefecerat hostis,\n> vixque manu spolium tenuit, victorque Medusae\n> victus in Andromeda est. iam cautibus invidet ipsis\n> felicisque vocat, teneant quae membra, catenas;\n> et, postquam poenae causam cognovit ab ipsa,\n> destinat in thalamos per bellum vadere ponti,\n> altera si Gorgo veniat, non territus illa.\n\n**English Translation:**\n\nAt last that happy day brought Perseus, the conqueror\nof the Gorgon monster, back to those shores.\nAnd when he saw the girl hanging from the rock,\nhe froze, he whom the face of his enemy had not turned to stone,\nand he could hardly hold his trophy in his hand: the conqueror of Medusa\nwas conquered by Andromeda. Now he envies the very rocks,\nand calls the chains happy, because they hold her limbs;\nand when he has learned from her the reason for her punishment,\nhe resolves to make his way to the marriage bed through a war with the sea,\nnot afraid even if a second Gorgon were to come.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThis is Manilius in his Ovidian mode, and it is one of the wittiest passages in Latin poetry. The whole joke is about stone and petrification. Medusa turned to stone everyone who looked at her, but not Perseus; now one look at a girl makes him freeze (*deriguit*, \"he grew stiff\"), \"he whom the face of the enemy had not stupefied\" (*facie quem non stupefecerat hostis*, with the relative clause placed before its antecedent, a typical poetic inversion). The trophy he can hardly hold is the head of Medusa itself. Then the famous paradox: *victorque Medusae / victus in Andromeda est*, \"the conqueror of Medusa was conquered in Andromeda\", with the same root (*victor... victus*) set across the line break, a figure called polyptoton.\n\nThe next lines continue the conceit. Perseus envies the rocks and calls the chains lucky (*felicis*, accusative plural in *-is*, the archaic form for *felices*, agreeing with *catenas*), because they get to touch her: the relative clause *teneant quae membra* has the subjunctive because it gives his reason, and *quae* is postponed after *teneant*. The final line returns to the Gorgon: he would face a second one for her (*altera si Gorgo veniat*, a hypothetical subjunctive, with *illa* in the ablative of cause). Grammatically, the passage is not as hard as the technical books; what makes it demanding is the word order, the pointed style, and the need to see the joke. Manilius wrote the dullest pages of Augustan poetry, and some of the cleverest.\n\n---\n\n## Legacy and Impact\n\nManilius had almost no readers in antiquity, as far as we can tell, and was nearly lost in the Middle Ages. His rediscovery by Poggio in 1417 made him a key text for Renaissance astrologers and astronomers, and the first printed edition appeared around 1473, edited by the astronomer Regiomontanus. Since then his legacy has belonged above all to scholarship: Scaliger, Bentley and Housman made him the most famous battlefield of textual criticism, and Housman's edition, with its prefaces, is still read today, partly for the method and partly for the insults. He is also an important source for the history of ancient astrology, and his poetic passages, on fate, on the universe as a living god, on human reason climbing to the sky, influenced later Stoic writing. And one line has escaped from the poem entirely: *nascentes morimur*, quoted on sundials, gravestones and in books of quotations by people who have no idea who wrote it.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: S (GOD PLEASE HELP)**\n\nManilius is the last author of the Augustan Age, and one of the hardest of all. Technical astrology turned into hexameters, with numbers and calculations disguised as poetic periphrasis; a highly compressed, pointed style full of paradox, hyperbaton and wordplay; a vocabulary stretched beyond its natural limits; and a text so corrupt that it took Scaliger, Bentley and Housman to make sense of it. The philosophical and mythological passages are difficult but rewarding; the technical books are a real ordeal. Alongside Horace, he is the peak of difficulty of this era, and he takes it to the end of the Augustan Age in a firm S.\n\n---\n";
